ab }\expndtw-19 547}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1154\margrsxn10755\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720 {\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2629\margrsxn1662\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 INTRODUCTION}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri14\sb454\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Welcome to \b Smalltalk/V \b0 and the world of object-oriented programming systems or,\line \expndtw-3 more often, \b OOPS \b0 for short. You've joined the world's largest community of Smalltalk\line \expndtw-2 users. Owners of Digiralk's \b Smalltalk/V \b0 are people, like you, who want to squeeze\line \expndtw4 maximum power and performance out of their MS-DOS, OS/2 and Macintosh\line \expndtw-4 computers.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You're in good company. \b Smalltalk/V \b0 is found widely in academic and research\line \expndtw-3 laboratories, R&D and product development departments of Fortune 1000 corporations,\line \expndtw-5 systems development agencies in government as well as on home PCs for recreational and\line \expndtw-3 entrepreneurial pursuits. \b Smalltalk/V \b0 applications have been developed in the areas of\line \expndtw-1 simulation, expert systems, intelligent tutoring computer-based instruction, database\line \expndtw-2 query interfaces, computerized typesetting and integrated programming environments.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri18\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V \b0 is selected by so many for such diverse applications because Smalltalk is\line both a \i powerful language\i0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 you can get a lot of activity out of a few lines of code\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 and\line \expndtw-8 a \i powerful program development environment\i0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 software utilities help you to reuse as many\line \expndtw-2 lines of pre-written code as possible and, once copied, to quickly edit and correct errors\line \expndtw1 in such code for your own program.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri22\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To encourage an exploratory "design-prototype-refine" approach to application devel-\line \expndtw0 opment, \b Smalltalk/V \b0 lets you edit and install small code modules without lengthy\line \expndtw-3 compile and link sessions, building a program piece by piece and seeing the results\line \expndtw1 immediately. You experiment with bits and pieces of a program long before it is\line \expndtw-1 complete, exploring ideas, structures and algorithms as the application takes form.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri25\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Except for a small kernel in machine language, \b Smalltalk/V \b0 is written in \b Smalltalk/V.\line \b0\expndtw-2 Commented source code for virtually the entire system is supplied in digestible chunks\line \expndtw0 of source code which you can reuse and modify in your applications.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri25\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V \b0 features pure object-oriented programming, a revolutionary approach to\line data abstraction, providing a new dimension in which to organize the elements of a\line \expndtw-5 software system. For you, this means highly reusable software, truly generic code and the\line \expndtw0 opportunity to use a prototyping style of software development.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri32\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This book is intended for both people who have never used Smalltalk as well as\line \expndtw-2 experienced Smalltalk programmers. It's organized into five parts:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li619\fi-216\ri407\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 \'95\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  \b Part 1, Overview, \b0 introduces object-oriented programming through a\line \expndtw-1 discussion of Smalltalk's big ideas and concludes with a comparison of\line Smalltalk and Pascal versions of an example program.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2176\margrsxn2119\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 2    Introduction}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li601\fi-202\ri0\sb331\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx601 \b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li601\fi-202\ri0\sb331\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx601 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Part 2, The Smalltalk/V Tutorials, \b0 is a series of tutorials that teach the\line \expndtw-1 Smalltalk language through examples you run in the \b Smalltalk/V \b0 environ-\line \expndtw-4 ment.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li601\fi-202\ri0\sb166\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li601\fi-202\ri0\sb166\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Part 3, The Smalltalk/V 286 Reference, \b0 is a complete specification of\line \b\expndtw-4 Smalltalk/V 286. \b0 You'll find summaries of Smalltalk's syntax and semantics,\line \expndtw1 descriptions of windows and menus that make up the environment, and \b a\line \b0\expndtw0 rundown of the major building blocks (classes) included in the system.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li601\fi-202\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li601\fi-202\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Part 4, The Encyclopedia of Classes, \b0 is a comprehensive,  structured\line \expndtw1 description of the classes and methods in \b Smalltalk/V 286.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li601\fi-202\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li601\fi-202\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Appendices \b0 cover advanced features such as writing your own Smalltalk\line \i primitives \i0 and extensions in other languages and conclude with a detailed\line \expndtw-1 cross-referencing Method Index and Index.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb367\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 System Requirements}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V286 \b0 requires an IBM-PC, PS/2 or compatible, with an 80286 or 80386\line \expndtw-1 processor and the following equipment:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 1 Megabyte of RAM}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Hard disk and one diskette drive}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Monochrome or color monitor}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li601\fi-202\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li601\fi-202\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent202\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Graphics controller (either CGA, MCGA, EGA, VGA, Hercules, Toshiba, or\line \expndtw-3 AT&T)}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 PC-DOS or MS-DOS, Version 2.0 or later}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The following items are optional:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Expansion to 16 Megabytes of RAM (extended memory only)}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Mouse (highly recommended, Microsoft compatible)}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Floating point co-processor (80287 or 80387)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb364\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Before Starting}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Before proceeding, please take a moment to make sure that you have the complete\line \b\expndtw-1 Smalltalk/V 286 \b0 package:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Two diskettes labeled \b Image \b0 and \b Source}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This book}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx601 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent201\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Registration Card}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2528\margrsxn1763\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Introduction    3}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb338\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The diskettes are not copy-protected. Using DOS disk copying utilities, you can make\line \expndtw-4 one or several backup copies, as long as they are for archival purposes so you can protect\line \expndtw-3 your investment.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \b Smalltalk/V \b0 community is growing daily. Digitalk's user newsletter \i SCOOP, \i0 keeps\line \expndtw-2 registered \b Smalltalk/V \b0 users informed of programming hints, product upgrade infor-\line \expndtw1 mation, bug reports, available \b Goodies \b0 packs, and special licensing and pricing\line \expndtw0 information. To make the most of your \b Smalltalk\b0 /V investment, and to enable us to\line \expndtw-1 serve you more quickly when you need support, return the enclosed Registration Card\line \expndtw0 and join the \b Smalltalk/V \b0 community to stay well-informed.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Sign the Registration Card and mail it to:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Digitalk Inc.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 9841 Airport Boulevard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Los Angeles, California 90045}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn4578\margrsxn4192\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li943\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-389\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn7\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs54 Parti}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb857\sa0\sl-623\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex86\expndtw-8\dn10\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs82 Overview}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2615\margrsxn1673\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 1 INTRODUCTION TO THE SMALLTALK LANGUAGE}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri18\sb457\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You do not have to read this chapter to get going with Smalltalk/ V. \i0 One legitimate school\line \expndtw-2 of Smalltalk thought suggests that the best introduction to object-oriented programming\line \expndtw-5 is simply to jump right in\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 to learn Smalltalk by experience. If this notion appeals to you,\line \expndtw2 proceed directly to Chapter 2. You may want to return here to supplement your\line \expndtw-3 experience. But there is nothing in this chapter that you have to know to understand and\line \expndtw1 make effective use of Smalltalk/V.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If this were a book on driving a car, this Overview describes a bit of the "physics" behind\line the car's engine, drive chain and suspension\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 hardly prerequisite knowledge to the act of\line \expndtw-1 driving. But racing drivers will tell you that the more you know about how your car\line \expndtw-4 works, the better you can drive it\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 knowing how to pull maximum performance from the\line \expndtw-2 potential of the car's interacting component parts. If this notion appeals to you, proceed.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb382\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Smalltalk's Big Ideas}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb209\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk grew from a few powerful ideas.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The most important component in a computing system is the individual\line \expndtw-3 human user.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Programming should be a natural extension of thinking.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Programming should be a dynamic, evolutionary process consistent with the\line \expndtw-1 model of human learning activity.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A computing environment is both a language and a productivity enhancing\line \expndtw-2 interface of programmer/user "power tools"\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 utilities to express yourself in\line \expndtw0 that language and to organize and flexibly use both procedural and factual\line \expndtw-5 knowledge.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri22\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk embodies these ideas in a framework for human/computer communication. At\line \expndtw-5 the simplest level, Smalltalk is yet another programming language like Basic, C, Pascal or\line \expndtw-3 Lisp. You will see in this chapter how you can write Smalltalk programs that have the\line \expndtw0 "look and feel" of conventional Pascal or other familiar programming languages.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri25\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You will also see how some thirty lines of Pascal, or less than twenty lines of "Pascalese"\line Smalltalk, can be reduced to five lines of Smalltalk the way it is meant to be written. And\line that's not five lines of dense, cryptic syntaxes like C or APL allows, coding shortcuts that\line \expndtw1 come back to haunt you in application maintenence and enhancement costs.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2169\margrsxn2108\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 6    Chapter 1: Introduction to the Smalltalk Language}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb313\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If we try to build an ideal machine that lives up to the promise of the big ideas above,\line \expndtw-2 we would want a computing environment that is both very hardy and forgiving. If\line \expndtw-3 programming is to be a natural extension of thinking and learning, the system has to take\line programming errors in stride\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 a simple coding error can't crash the system or you'd lose\line \expndtw0 all incentive to use an exploratory prototyping style of application development,}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri14\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk promotes the development of \i safe \i0 systems. Smalltalk "errors" are merely\line \expndtw-2 objects telling you they do not understand how to do what you are asking them to do\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\line \hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 hardly events which blow up the system. And Smalltalk's encapsulation of digestible\line \expndtw0 chunks of program code with their own local data in independently active objects\line \expndtw-3 promotes a "divide and conquer" approach to programming problem solving. Smalltalk\line \expndtw-4 objects are easily inspected, duplicated, modified and, perhaps most importantly, re-used.\line \expndtw-2 Smalltalk lets you get on to the business of solving your problem, not writing the same\line \expndtw0 code over and over.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri22\sb162\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Tutorials will introduce you to the range of programming "power tools" standard in\line \b\expndtw-1 Smalltalk/V \b0 that help you use, re-use and modify the storehouse of Smalltalk source\line \expndtw-2 code which is part of the basic system. But first, it can be helpful to understand that\line \expndtw-4 Smalltalk is both very much like and, at the same time, very much unlike conventional\line \expndtw-3 programming languages.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri22\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We'll then introduce you to some of the special terminology and exciting ideas that\line \expndtw-4 energize object-oriented programming in Smalltalk. From there it's on to the introductory\line \expndtw1 tutorial which gets you up and running and writing your first Smalltalk/V code.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb364\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Smalltalk vs. Conventional Languages}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri25\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This section presents an overview of \b Smalltalk/V \b0 by comparing examples of code in\line \expndtw-2 both Smalltalk and Pascal to help you learn \b Smalltalk/V \b0 more quickly. You don't have\line to be a Pascal programmer to benefit from the comparison as thorough explanations\line \expndtw-3 accompany each example.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri25\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The step-by-step code examples are followed by a complete program written in both\line \expndtw0 languages which solves the same problem. We conclude by rewriting the Smalltalk\line \expndtw-2 version of the algorithm, taking advantage of object-oriented features to significantly\line \expndtw2 reduce the amount of code required to do the same procedure.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri29\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The examples which follow present a series of statements in Pascal and \b Smalltalk/V. \b0 The\line \expndtw-4 left column shows program fragments in Pascal, while the right column shows equivalent\line \expndtw0 code fragments in \b Smalltalk/V.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2512\margrsxn1786\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 1: Introduction to the Smalltalk Language    7}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb360\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Assignment to \i0 a \i Scalar Variable}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0\tx3078 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 a := b + c{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw9 a := b + c}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 These statements look the same in both Pascal and Smalltalk. The assignment operator\line \expndtw-4 is :=. Variable names have the same syntax in both languages. In the example statements,\line \expndtw0 the contents of variable b are added to the contents of variable c and stored in variable\line \expndtw-2 a. In Pascal, the computed value is stored. In Smalltalk, assignment statements always\line \expndtw0 store pointers to objects which contain the values.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb338\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 A Series of Statements/Expressions}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3078 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw14\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 x \b0 := \b 0;{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw15 x \b0 := \b 0.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3078 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 y \b0 := \b 'answer';{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw0 y := 'answer*.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3082 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw20\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 z \b0 := \b w{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw18 z \b0 := \b w}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The statement separator is semicolon in Pascal and period in Smalltalk. Note that in both\line \expndtw-1 languages, the statement separator character is not used after the last statement in the\line \expndtw1 series. The first statement assigns the constant zero to the variable x. The second\line \expndtw-2 statement assigns a literal string to the variable y. In both languages, a string is an array\line \expndtw0 of characters. The third statement assigns the contents of variable \b w \b0 to variable \b z.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb338\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 A Function Call with One Argument}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa0\tx3078 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 a \b0 := \b size(array){\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw1 a \b0 := \b array size}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri4\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The function \b size \b0 is called with argument \b array \b0 and the value returned is stored in the\line \expndtw-6 variable a. In Smalltalk, calling a function is known as \i sending a message. \i0 In this case, the\line \expndtw-1 message \b size \b0 is sent to the contents of variable \b array.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb335\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Function Calls with Two Arguments}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0\tx3078 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 x := max(xl, x2);{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw1 x := xl max: x2.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tx3078 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 y := sum(p, q){\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw14 y \b0 := \b p + q}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In Pascal, the arguments to the function call are enclosed in parentheses. In Smalltalk, for\line \expndtw-4 a two-argument message, the arguments precede and follow the message name. Note that\line \expndtw-3 in Smalltalk, the standard arithmetic operations are performed via messages. In the first\line \expndtw-4 example, the message \b max: \b0 is sent to the contents of variable xl (the first argument), with\line \expndtw-3 the contents of x2 as the second argument. The result returned is assigned to the variable\line \expndtw-1 x. In the second example, the message + is sent to the contents of variable \b p \b0 with the\line \expndtw-2 contents of variable q as the second argument, and the result returned is assigned to the\line \expndtw-4 variable y.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1985\margrsxn2299\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li40\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 8    Chapter 1: Introduction to the Smalltalk Language}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb331\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex80\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A Function Call with Three Arguments}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li421\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 b \b0 := \b between(x, xl, x2)         b := x between: xl and: x2}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When a message has three or more arguments in Smalltalk, the name of the message is\line \expndtw1 split into pieces, and a piece of the message name appears preceding each of the\line \expndtw-1 arguments after the first. This distribution of the message name helps to describe the\line \expndtw1 message arguments. In the example, the message name is \b between:and: \b0 and the\line \expndtw-1 arguments are variables x, \b xl, \b0 and x2. This example could be used to test whether \b the\line \b0\expndtw-4 value of \b x \b0 is between the values of \b xl \b0 and x2, and assign the Boolean result (true or false)\line \expndtw0 to the variable \b b.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb306\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex80\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Subscripted Variable Access}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3085 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 x \b0 := \b a[i];{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-2 x := a at: i.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3082 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 a[i + l] := y;{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-3 a at: i + 1 put: y.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3082 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 a[i + l] : = a[i]{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-3 a at: i + 1 put: (a at: i)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri11\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Pascal uses square brackets to specify subscripting, whereas Smalltalk uses \b at: \b0 and \b at:put:\line \b0\expndtw-4 messages. In the first example, the value of variable \b i \b0 is used to index the array identified\line \expndtw0 by variable a, and the value obtained is stored in variable x.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri11\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The second example shows replacing an element of an array with a new value. Note that\line \expndtw-4 a Pascal assignment may store into an array element, whereas in Smalltalk only scalar\line \expndtw-1 variables appear to the left of an assignment statement, so an \b at:put: \b0 message is used.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri14\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The third example shows accessing and changing array elements. Parentheses are used in\line \expndtw-1 the Smalltalk example to specify evaluation order.}\par \pard\plain\fs2\sa346\sl-1\slmult0\par\trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-310 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2750 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4550 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx5904 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex88\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If Statements}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-353 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2750 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4550 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx5904 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li277\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 if a \b0 <C \b b then}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li202\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw34\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 a <b}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-353 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2750 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4550 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx5904 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li673\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 a := a + 1;}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li598\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifTrue: [a : =}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 = a + l].}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-353 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2750 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4550 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx5904 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li274\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 if \i0 atEnd(stream) then}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li202\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 stream atEnd}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-238 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2750 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx5904 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li673\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 reset(stream)}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li594\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifTrue: [stream reset]}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-238 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2750 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4550 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx5904 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li270\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 else}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li594\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifFalse: [c :-'}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 = stream next]}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-230 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2750 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4550 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx5904 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li673\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 c := next(stream)}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \pard\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb162\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Pascal and Smalltalk provide similar capabilities for the conditional execution of a series\line \expndtw-3 of statements based on the result of evaluating a boolean expression. In Smalltalk, the\line conditional statements are enclosed in square brackets. In the first example above, \b the\line \b0\expndtw-5 variable a will be incremented by one if the value of variable \b a \b0 is \b less than the value \b0 of\line \expndtw-4 variable \b b.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2329\margrsxn1955\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 1: Introduction to the Smalltalk Language    9}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri14\sb299\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The second example illustrates conditionally executed code during file access. The file\line \expndtw-4 being accessed is identified by the variable stream. If the file is positioned at the end; the\line \b\expndtw-5 reset \b0 message is sent to reposition it at the beginning. Otherwise, the variable \b c \b0 is assigned\line \expndtw0 the next character in the file.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb346\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Iterative Statements}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3074 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 while i < 10 do begin{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw5 [i < 10 ]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li810\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3478 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 sum := sum + a[i];{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-1 whileTrue: [}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3870 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw21\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 i \b0 := \b i + 1{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw8 sum \b0 := \b sum + (a at: \b0 i).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3874 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 end;{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw23 i \b0 := \b i + l].}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb184\sa0\tx3107 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 for i \b0 := \b 1 to 10 do{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw2 1 to: 10 do: \b0 [ :i I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tx3474 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 a[i] \b0 := \b 0{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\expndtw1 a \b at: i put: o]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Pascal and Smalltalk provide similar capabilities for repeated execution of a series of\line \expndtw-2 statements. In the first example, the two statements in the loop will be executed as long\line as the value of the variable \b i \b0 is less than 10. In the second example, the single statement\line in the loop will be executed with the variable \b i \b0 taking on the values 1 through 10 in\line \expndtw-5 succession.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb342\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Returning Function Results}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa0\tx3103 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 functionName :={\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\scaps\expndtw-13 a \scaps0\b answer}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi389\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 answer;\line \expndtw-8 return}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri25\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Pascal and Smalltalk both provide for specifying the result of function (or in Smalltalk,\line method) evaluation. In Pascal, the function result expression is assigned to the function\line \expndtw-1 name, which serves as a variable for containing the result. In Smalltalk the caret \scaps (a)\line \scaps0\expndtw-3 appears before an expression that is the method result. This causes method execution to\line \expndtw-4 cease and the value of the expression to be returned as the method result. In the example,\line \expndtw0 the value of the variable \b answer \b0 is the function (and method) result.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb338\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Storage Allocation and De-allocation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0\tx3071 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 new(p){\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw3 p := Array new: 5}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 dispose(p)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri29\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Pascal and Smalltalk both provide for the dynamic allocation of variables (in Smalltalk\line \expndtw-4 terminology, objects). In the first line of the example above, both languages assign to the\line \expndtw-3 variable \b p \b0 a pointer to the newly allocated object. In Pascal, however, it is necessary to\line \expndtw-2 explicitly de-allocate objects when they are no longer needed in order to reclaim their\line \expndtw-4 space. This is done via the dispose function call. In Smalltalk, space reclamation (garbage}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2190\margrsxn2079\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li61\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 10    Chapter 1: Introduction to the Smalltalk Language}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li25\fi0\ri0\sb328\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 collection) is automatic and consequently, there are no language facilities for specifying\line \expndtw-2 object de-allocation. This simplifies programming by eliminating a potential source of\line \expndtw0 error, de-allocating at the wrong time.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-439\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A Complete Program}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi-389\ri0\sb0\sa194\sl-439\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 What follows is a complete program with Pascal code on the left, Smalltalk on the right.\line \b\expndtw-4 program frequency;}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2579\margrsxn3206\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw2458\colsr230\colno2\colw3434 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 const\line \expndtw6 size = 80;\line \expndtw-11 var}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 s: string [size];\line \expndtw-2 i: integer;\line \expndtw-8 c: char;\line \expndtw1 f: array[l..26]\line \expndtw0 of integer;\line \expndtw-2 k: integer;\line \expndtw1 begin}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 writeln('enter line');\line \expndtw-5 readln(s);}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 for i := 1 to 26 do}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 f[i] := 0;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi-400\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 for i : = 1 to size do\line \expndtw2 begin\line c : =}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 asLowerCase(s [i ]);\line \expndtw-1 if isLetter(c) then\line \expndtw1 begin}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1202\fi-396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 k := ord(c)\line \expndtw-3 - ord('a')}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 f[k] :- f[k] + 1\line \expndtw-5\fs22 end\line \expndtw-8 end;\line \expndtw9 for i := 1 to 26 do}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 write(f[i], ' ')\line \expndtw-5 end.}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb698\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw69\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Iscf\expndtw-1 kI}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb457\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 f := Array new: 26.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb713\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 s := Prompter}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 prompt: 'enter line'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi374\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 default: ".\line \expndtw0 1 to: 26 do: [ :i I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi371\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 f at: i put: 0].\line \expndtw0 1 to: s size do: [ :i I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 c := (s at: i) asLowerCase.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi-396\ri0\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 c isLetter\line \expndtw-3 ifTrue: [}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1174\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 k := c ascii Value\line \expndtw-4 - $a asciiValue\line \expndtw14 + 1.\line \expndtw5 f at: k put: (f at: k) + 1}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb238\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 ]\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f2\cchs0 \'95}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2190\margrsxn2079\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri40\sb162\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The programs above ask the user to enter a line of text from the keyboard. It then\line \expndtw-3 computes the frequency of occurrence of each alphabetic character in the input text. All\line \expndtw-2 characters are treated as lower-case letters.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2503\margrsxn1784\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 1: Introduction to the Smalltalk Language    11}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb295\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The example emphasizes the similarities of Pascal and Smalltalk syntax. The algorithm\line \expndtw-5 used is identical in both cases. The input characters are examined one at a time and if they\line \expndtw0 are characters, the frequency counter for that letter is incremented.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 None of the powerful built-in building blocks of Smalltalk were used in the above\line example. The example below shows the same program written using some of these\line \expndtw-1 built-in building blocks.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li421\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex64\expndtw63\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Isf\expndtw0  \expndtw-6 I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 s \b0 := \b Prompter prompt: 'enter line' default: ".}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx5476 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 f := Bag new.{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-42 -***"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 s do: [ :c I c isLetter ifTrue: [f add: \b0 c \b asLowerCase]].}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li421\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Af}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A \b Prompter \b0 is used to get the input string from the user. A \b Prompter \b0 is a special type\line of window. An empty \b Bag \b0 is then created to hold the character frequencies. Bags are a\line \expndtw1 type of collection that count occurrences of objects. The input string is then iterated\line \expndtw-3 over, and each character is examined. If the character is a letter, its lower case equivalent\line \expndtw2 is added to the \b Bag. \b0 The resultant \b Bag \b0 is then returned.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Already, Smalltalk is revealing its expressive power. The considerably shorter rewrite uses\line \expndtw-2 a few of Smalltalk's pre-defined objects, each comes with its own highly developed\line behavior. In the hundred forty or so classes of \b Smalltalk/V \b0 object types, there are over\line two thousand methods you can call upon. Each new object and its methods, which you\line \expndtw-1 create, will be added on equal footing with the generic objects which come in\line \b Smalltalk/V.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft104\shptop-72\shpright853\shpbottom-72\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Objects obviously have something to offer\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 a tremendous source of Smalltalk program-\line \expndtw-2 mer productivity. A greater appreciation of what objects are and how they behave is in\line \expndtw-4 order.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb371\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 The World According to Objects}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk is built on the simple yet powerful model of "communicating objects" as\line \expndtw-3 shown in Figure 1.1. What could be more natural. We experience our world largely as a\line \expndtw0 vast collection of discrete objects, acting and reacting in a shared environment.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li6606\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2180\margrsxn2112\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li43\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa1235 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 12    Chapter 1: Introduction to the Smalltalk Language}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2594\margrsxn4012\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw2048\colsr2452\colno2\colw802 \pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx2826\posnegy0\absw0\absh972\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw1701\pich1714\picwgoal964\pichgoal972\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx5864\posnegy0\absw0\absh972\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw2374\pich1714\picwgoal1346\pichgoal972\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb558\sa0\sl-223\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 1.1\line Communicating Objects}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb198\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Message}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2180\margrsxn2112\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri4\sb446\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 At the human social level we are a \i society of \i0 doctors, lawyers, beggars and thieves, etc.\line \expndtw-2 Although we are a population of unique individuals, we cluster in occupational groups\line \expndtw-5 based on the behavioral skills and knowledge we each develop and exhibit as seen below:}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2572\margrsxn6828\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li767\fi130\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx2012\posnegy1879\absw1814\absh-1088\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Barbara\line \expndtw-3 Truckers}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li353\fi0\ri0\sb76\sa0\tx1472 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx2012\posnegy1879\absw1814\absh-1088\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Fred{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-13 Elton}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb115\sa0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx2012\posnegy1879\absw1814\absh-1088\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 "BlueCollar"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1102\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-270\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx4345\posnegy2077\absw2296\absh-814\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 George}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1170\fi-1170\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-270\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx4345\posnegy2077\absw2296\absh-814\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 "White Collar     \b0 ' Roberta\line \b\expndtw-13 Accountants}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft1760\shptop976\shpright7239\shpbottom4389\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr5\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 75}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn pib}{\sv {\pict\picw9664\pich6019\picwgoal5479\pichgoal3412\jpegblip
}}}{\sp{\sn pictureGray}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn pWrapPolygonVertices}{\sv 8;9;(0,0);(0,19230);(2980,19230);(2980,21600);(21600,21600);(21600,19230);(21600,19230);(21600,0);(0,0)}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fIsBullet}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fAllowOverlap}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fEditedWrap}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fBehindDocument}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fPrint}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistLeft}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistRight}{\sv 0}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 1.2}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Human Occupational Classes}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2180\margrsxn2112\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri14\sb446\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Break a leg, call in a doctor and tell him or her about your condition. You trust the\line \expndtw-2 doctor's special knowledge and skills to help make you better. Self communicates with\line \expndtw0 Doctor Black Box.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb162\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Want to become a lawyer? You learn the law and how to behave like a lawyer. Then as\line \expndtw-1 corporate counsel in response to the MegaCorp CEO's question, "What's our exposure\line \expndtw-2 on this new project?", your answer is couched in legal considerations while the chief\line \expndtw-3 financial officer reflects on fiscal impacts.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2473\margrsxn1840\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 1: Introduction to the Smalltalk Language    15}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb299\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In Smalltalk's object-oriented terms, occupational abstractions like doctor, lawyer,\line \expndtw-8 programmer, etc., are \i classes \i0 of which we individuals are \i instances. \i0 To become a lawyer, we\line \expndtw-4 learn legal \i methods. \i0 Communications between individuals are comparable to Smalltalk\line \i\expndtw-8 messages, \i0 their content equivalent to Smalltalk \i selectors \i0 as shown in Figure 1.3. Correspon-\line \expndtw0 dence between our perception of the world and its representation in machine terms\line through Smalltalk gets at the heart of Smalltalk's power.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2843\margrsxn2517\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2876\shptop374\shpright7246\shpbottom1353\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr5\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 75}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn pib}{\sv {\pict\picw7708\pich1727\picwgoal4370\pichgoal979\jpegblip
}}}{\sp{\sn pictureGray}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn pWrapPolygonVertices}{\sv 8;13;(18432,0);(18432,635);(854,635);(854,2858);(0,2858);(0,21600);(21600,21600);(21600,2858);(21600,2858);(21600,635);(21600,635);(21600,0);(18432,0)}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fIsBullet}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fAllowOverlap}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fEditedWrap}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fBehindDocument}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fPrint}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistLeft}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistRight}{\sv 0}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb202\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Tax advantage of $120,000 available.}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2869\shptop673\shpright4201\shpbottom1580\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr5\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 75}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn pib}{\sv {\pict\picw2349\pich1600\picwgoal1332\pichgoal907\jpegblip
}}}{\sp{\sn pictureGray}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn pWrapPolygonVertices}{\sv 8;13;(0,0);(0,2742);(0,2742);(0,20400);(2918,20400);(2918,21600);(13543,21600);(13543,20400);(21600,20400);(21600,2742);(14711,2742);(14711,0);(0,0)}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fIsBullet}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fAllowOverlap}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fEditedWrap}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fBehindDocument}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fPrint}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistLeft}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistRight}{\sv 0}}}}{\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft5893\shptop673\shpright7239\shpbottom1652\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr5\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 75}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn pib}{\sv {\pict\picw2374\pich1727\picwgoal1346\pichgoal979\jpegblip
}}}{\sp{\sn pictureGray}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn pWrapPolygonVertices}{\sv 8;9;(6121,0);(6121,3176);(0,3176);(0,21600);(21600,21600);(21600,3176);(21600,3176);(21600,0);(6121,0)}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fIsBullet}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fAllowOverlap}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fEditedWrap}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fBehindDocument}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fPrint}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistLeft}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistRight}{\sv 0}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4619\fi-778\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-616\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Fiscal impact of incorporation?\line \expndtw-1 'Jane Doe'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 1.3}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Human and Smalltalk}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tx4172 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Objects Communicating{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-13 prompt: 'New Name?'}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2473\margrsxn1840\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb508\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 What are objects?}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb205\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A Smalltalk object is simply related pieces of code and data. The pieces of code are\line \expndtw-3 Smalltalk \i methods\i0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 a library of self-contained subroutines unique to each class giving\line each class of object its specific behaviors. An object's data structure is described by its\line \expndtw-6 collection of \i instance variables.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When you create a specific \i instance \i0 of a class, the initial values of the object's instance\line \expndtw-3 variables are assigned. The object's methods are its know-how. If we were to create a\line \expndtw-4 Smalltalk "car driver object", it would likely include "brake", "steer", "watch for traffic"\line \expndtw-3 and "shift gears" methods. Instance variables of such a car driving object would include\line \expndtw0 "reaction time", "temperament" and "visual acuity" of the driver.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Related data and program pieces are encapsulated within a Smalltalk object, a\line communicating black box. The black box can send and receive certain messages.\line \expndtw-3 Message passing is the only means of importing data for local manipulation within the\line \expndtw1 black box. And \i if \i0 an object needs something done that it does not know how to do\line \expndtw-3 within its own set of methods, it sends a message to another object, in effect, asking for\line \expndtw0 assistance in the completion of a task.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In Smalltalk, objects communicate to objects just as lawyers talk to accountants in our\line \expndtw-1 occupational analogy in Figure 1.3. A professional's know-how is comparable to a\line \expndtw0 Smalltalk object's collection of methods. People communicate using their know-how.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2277\margrsxn2021\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li47\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 14    Chapter I: Introduction to the Smalltalk Language}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri4\sb324\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Know-how does not communicate to know-how. The lawyer's knowledge used to\line \expndtw0 prepare a client's will does not include a "direct memory access" to the accountant's\line \expndtw-1 ability to compute financial implications of the settlement of an estate. Similarly, a\line \expndtw1 Smalltalk object's methods do not call other objects' methods directly. Rather, the\line \expndtw-5 lawyer's methods include knowing when to send a message requesting financial services,\line \expndtw0 just as the CPA knows when and how to ask for legal services.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri4\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In OOPS terms, \i information hiding\i0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 as this encapsulation of code and data is known in\line \expndtw-5 computer science\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 makes for highly portable, easily modifiable and safe software. Large\line \expndtw-3 applications may be easily maintained since objects may be updated, recompiled, tested\line \expndtw-2 and called immediately back into service with their new behavioral capabilities on line.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb317\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 What kinds of objects can be described?}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb198\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Like their physical counterparts, Smalltalk objects have attributes and exhibit behaviors.\line Since everything in Smalltalk is an object\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 including the Smalltalk environment itself\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\line \expndtw-1\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 then what you can do with the language becomes a question of what objects can be\line \expndtw-2 described and manipulated.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If the encapsulation of information hiding provides the means for creating objects, then\line \expndtw-5 a language's \i data abstraction \i0 capabilities determine what objects can be described. Marco\line \expndtw-3 Polo called upon his powers of data abstraction daily as he traveled to parts unknown.\line \expndtw-4 Things which could not be understood or named within his current world view required\line \expndtw0 invention, new words for new objects.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri14\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You need the same powers of an extendible language capable of describing arbitrary data\line \expndtw0 structures if you are to tailor the generic Smalltalk environment to your purposes.\line \expndtw-3 Smalltalk lets you create arbitrary new data structures, compound objects which can be\line thought of as an array whose elements can be any combination of numbers, symbols or\line \expndtw-2 character strings as well as another array making nested data structures possible. Where\line \expndtw-3 it is generally an exception or nuisance in conventional languages, creating new data\line \expndtw0 structures is done routinely when you define a new class or subclass of objects in\line \expndtw-7 Smalltalk.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb331\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 How do objects communicate and behave?}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb205\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk objects take responsibility for their own actions, responding individually to\line \expndtw-4 every message. Your application may have occasion to print an integer, a floating point\line \expndtw-3 number, an ASCII character or a string of symbols. Since each of these elementary data\line \expndtw-4 types is defined as a Smalltalk class, instances of these classes come with a bundle of\line \expndtw-2 behavioral features built-in, its methods. Each of the elementary data types knows how\line \expndtw1 to perform generally required behaviors such as print, duplicate and comparison\line \expndtw-4 operations.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2451\margrsxn1815\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 1: Introduction to the Smalltalk Language    15}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri25\sb295\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 So when it comes time to print, your Smalltalk application simply sends the near universal\line \expndtw-4 message \b printString \b0 to each of the variety of data type objects to be included in a report:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'This is a string' printString.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 423 printString.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li443\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 #(123) printString.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 $A printString.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li439\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 #('array of 3 'strings and' 2 'numbers') printString.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri25\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Integers, arrays and characters take care of getting themselves represented on paper. This\line \expndtw-1 Smalltalk characteristic of having different objects responding uniquely to the same\line \expndtw-3 message is known as \i polymorphism. \i0 It means you won't have to memorize a unique\line \expndtw-1 vocabulary for each class used in building your applications.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri25\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Because Smalltalk objects take responsibility for their own behavior, you won't have to\line \expndtw-2 litter your application with conditional checks through case statements to see that the\line proper type of function is called to operate on a piece of data. This feature saves much\line \expndtw-1 time and significantly reduces software maintenance costs since only affected objects\line \expndtw0 need be edited and re-compiled to enhance a Smalltalk application.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Among the many reasons objects communicate, a frequent objective is to \i change the state\line \i0\expndtw-4 of the receiver object. Sending a message to store a new value in a variable (a Smalltalk\line \expndtw-2 variable is an object that stores other objects) is an obvious example of state changing\line \expndtw-4 messaging. A bit more subtle is a user request to resize a window which results in a\line \expndtw-2 mouse-based interaction which sends new screen coordinates to the window's instance\line \expndtw-5 variables which store its size and location on the screen. The window's state changes upon\line \expndtw-2 receipt of the resize message.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri36\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk guarantees that there will be a response by a message recipient. If an object\line \expndtw-2 determines that it does not know how to perform a requested behavior, it will at least\line \expndtw-1 answer with a "Message not understood" response message. The method which sends\line this response also kicks in Smalltalk's debugging utilities to help you determine and\line \expndtw0 correct the failure to communicate.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri32\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 So even program errors are detected and resolved within the object-oriented messaging\line \expndtw-2 framework of Smalltalk. This makes for a very exploratory environment in which to\line develop application software. A working prototype can be constructed quickly and\line enhancements integrated easily into the evolving system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb353\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 How does Smalltalk organize objects and their methods?}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri18\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk organizes its classes into a hierarchy of classes and subclasses, a portion of\line \expndtw-5 which is shown in Figure 1.4. For example, the \b Integer \b0 class is a subclass of the \b Number\line \b0\expndtw-2 class which is a subclass of \b Magnitude \b0 which is a subclass of class \b Object, \b0 the most\line \expndtw-1 general Smalltalk class and parent of all other classes.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1974\margrsxn1417\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li349\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa130 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 16    Chapter 1: Introduction to the Smalltalk Language}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn5175\margrsxn1417\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols4\colno1\colw1267\colsr14\colno2\colw1411\colsr72\colno3\colw1245\colsr122\colno4\colw1184 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-1584\shptop187\shpright-1584\shpbottom10584\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft1274\shptop1750\shpright1274\shpbottom2348\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 6985}}}}{\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li68\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx1624\posnegy554\absw796\absh-281\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs10 Animation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb50\sa0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx1624\posnegy554\absw796\absh-281\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs10 Commander}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-526\shptop439\shpright1656\shpbottom1318\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr5\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 75}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn pib}{\sv {\pict\picw3848\pich1549\picwgoal2181\pichgoal878\jpegblip
}}}{\sp{\sn pictureGray}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn pWrapPolygonVertices}{\sv 8;21;(0,0);(0,1239);(0,1239);(0,4072);(0,4072);(0,6904);(0,6904);(0,8675);(0,8675);(0,21600);(13401,21600);(13401,8675);(21100,8675);(21100,6904);(21600,6904);(21600,4072);(9908,4072);(9908,1239);(5346,1239);(5346,0);(0,0)}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fIsBullet}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fAllowOverlap}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fEditedWrap}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fBehindDocument}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fPrint}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistLeft}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistRight}{\sv 0}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qc\li0\fi0\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-166\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs10 - Olass}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qc\li0\fi0\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-166\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs10 - MetaClass}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qc\li0\fi0\ri7\sb4\sa0\sl-166\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs10 - CharacterScanner}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb554\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-5\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs10 - IndexedCollection\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f2\cchs0 \'97}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li86\fi0\ri0\sb389\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs10 FixedSizeCotlection -}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb464\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\super\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-6\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs10 1\nosupersub\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f2\cchs0 \'97\hich\af0\dbch\af2\loch\f0\cchs0  OrderedCollection -}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li252\fi0\ri0\sb1332\sa0\sl-166\slmult0\tlhyph\tx432 \f3\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs10 Array \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f2\cchs0 \'97}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li252\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-166\slmult0\tx400 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs10  \'97\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li252\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-166\slmu