375\sa0\tlhyph\tx187 \f3\fs20{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs10 FileStream}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb50\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex140\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs10 -Terminal Stream}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2498\margrsxn1783\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 1: Introduction to the Smalltalk Language    1\i0 7}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri0\sb299\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Inheritance \i0 provides a mechanism for both organizing and maintaining the collection of\line \expndtw-5 Smalltalk object classes. Inheritance recognizes similarities among objects, capitalizing on\line \expndtw-1 the fact that similar objects often behave similarly.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri18\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A subclass inherits all the methods known to the parent. If you define a new customer\line \expndtw0 database class to be a type of Smalltalk \b SortedCollection, \b0 your application will\line \expndtw-4 automatically know how to add, copy, edit, remove, print and sort customer records. You\line need only create methods which describe the new behaviors the customer database must\line \expndtw-1 exhibit beyond those of the generic \b SortedCollection.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri25\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If a selected parent's behavior is inappropriate, you simply define a method by the same\line \expndtw-3 name to override the parental way of doing things. For instance, to protect confidential\line \expndtw-1 customer information, the print method of the Customer Record class could be written\line \expndtw-3 to perform a password checking operation before printing. The customer record would\line \expndtw-2 then enforce security when asked to print itself while a less rigorously defined object\line \expndtw-1 simply prints itself upon receipt of the same print message.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri11\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk's inheritance features encourage "programming to exception and modifica-\line \expndtw-3 tion". Without inheritance, you would have to spend a good bit of your time telling new\line classes of objects how to do elementary things like print. And if you later revamp the\line \expndtw-2 procedures for printing specific data types, you would have potentially hundreds of\line \expndtw0 individual print methods to update and re-compile if it were not for inheritance.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri14\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Only slight reflection is needed to appreciate the programmer productivity potential of\line \expndtw-5 Smalltalk's inheritance mechanism. A little more reflection will confirm that the hierarchy\line of Smalltalk classes is consistent with the model of classification systems we routinely lay\line \expndtw0 over our perceptions of the world.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb356\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 How do you maintain a Smalltalk world of objects?}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri36\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Each Smalltalk object is an encapsulated program operating on its own local data, a little\line self-contained computer. The user extendable \b Smalltalk/V \b0 system comes with over two\line thousand pre-defined methods in over one hundred classes. With time, your personalized\line \b\expndtw0 Smalltalk/V \b0 environment will contain hundreds, maybe thousands, of new objects,\line classes of objects and their associated methods.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri40\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If you had to explicitly manage program and data file handling for each Smalltalk object\line \expndtw-5 as well as act as traffic cop to the messaging activity, there would be no incentive to adopt\line \expndtw-1 object-oriented programming. The power of Smalltalk's natural human thinking model\line \expndtw-3 at the design level would be lost at code writing. \b Smalltalk/V, \b0 therefore, is designed to\line \expndtw-5 manage the "dirty work" of \i object storage \i0 and \i memory management \i0 for you.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri47\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Imagine the task for your hardware and operating system software if your \b Smalltalk/V\line \b0 directory were strewn with:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2347\margrsxn1956\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li47\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 28    \i Chapter 1: Introduction to the Smalltalk Language}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li410\fi0\ri936\sb320\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 objl.exe\line \expndtw3 objl.prg\line \expndtw4 objl.dat\line \expndtw-5 obj2.exe\line \expndtw-1 ob|2.prg\line \expndtw-2 obj2.dat\line \expndtw-5 obj3.exe\line \expndtw-3 obj3.prg\line \expndtw-2 obj3.dat\line \expndtw1 . . . and so on for hundreds or thousands of objects if it were possible.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 That's what it would take if Smalltalk's objects managed themselves independently under\line \expndtw0 the conventional model of computing. Smalltalk solves this problem with a global\line solution, an object-oriented model for storage.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When you install \b Smalltalk/V \b0 at the beginning of \b The Tutorial, \b0 you will note two large\line \expndtw-5 files. These are the \b Smalltalk/V \b0 source and image files. The source file contains an ASCII\line \expndtw0 representation of the Smalltalk source code. The image file is a computer readable\line \expndtw-1 snapshot of the current state of your \b Smalltalk/V \b0 environment, a kind of group photo\line \expndtw0 preserving the state of each object.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Saving the image to permanent disk storage allows you to end a Smalltalk session, saving\line \expndtw-3 your objects in "suspended animation" to be revived exactly as you left them when you\line \expndtw-1 restart Smalltalk. In this global and efficient manner, Smalltalk takes care of storing\line \expndtw-3 objects.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Having an efficient approach to storage management might only serve to preserve chaos\line \i\expndtw-4 if \i0 RAM memory were to become cluttered with persistent though unused and unwanted\line \expndtw4 objects, therefore taking up valuable real estate in RAM if no longer needed.\line \b\expndtw-3 Smalltalk/V \b0 lets you focus on programming while automatic memory management,\line \expndtw-4 sometimes unglamorously referred to as "garbage collection", maximizes RAM available\line \expndtw1 for the creation of new objects and keeps accumulated "clutter" from crashing the\line \expndtw0 system. Automated object storage and memory management further insure \b a \b0 safe\line \expndtw-2 enviroment for Smalltalk application development.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb364\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Ideas into Action}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V \b0 is a prescription for an exciting programming experience ... the rare\line combination of an open and safe system inviting you to shape its capabilities to your\line needs. The first several chapters of The \b Tutorial \b0 introduce you to the \b Smalltalk/V\line \b0\expndtw1 programming environment and to the range of objects and their behavior in the\line \b\expndtw-1 Smalltalk/V \b0\i system.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2435\margrsxn1867\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter I: Introduction to the Smalltalk Language    19}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb299\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 But this is only the beginning. Smalltalk really shows its stuff in real world application\line \expndtw-3 development. The expanding collection of re-usable Smalltalk objects and their methods\line \expndtw-5 when used in an evolutionary, prototyping development cycle result in quick and efficient\line \expndtw-3 development of complex applications. \b The Tutorial \b0 concludes with an application\line \expndtw-4 development example which showcases the "design-test-improve" cycle of development\line \expndtw0 encouraged by the fully object-oriented character of \b Smalltalk/V.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3762\margrsxn2718\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qc\li79\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-389\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-23\dn7\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs50 Part 2}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1278\fi-1278\ri0\sb760\sa0\sl-889\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-30\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs80 Smalltalk/V 286\line \expndtw-55 Tutorials}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2490\margrsxn1824\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-364\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 INTRODUCTION TO THE SMALLTALK/V 286\line \expndtw-4 ENVIRONMENT}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb454\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In this chapter, you will learn how to install \b Smalltalk/V, \b0 start up its environment, and\line \expndtw1 edit and evaluate some of its most basic expressions. You'll learn how to move the\line \expndtw-4 cursor, use windows and enter and edit text. By the end of this chapter, you'll be familiar\line \expndtw-1 enough with \b Smalltalk/V \b0 to run the tutorials, which make up the remainder of \b Part 2\line \b0\expndtw2 of this book.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If you have worked in a "windows" environment before, you may already be familiar with\line \expndtw-4 some ideas in this chapter. After \b Installing \b0 and \b Starting Up Smalltalk/V, \b0 you may want\line \expndtw-3 to take the \b Quick Tour \b0 and read the \b Starting Out \b0 sections of this chapter, then scan the\line \expndtw-8 rest.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If the \b Smalltalk/V \b0 operating environment is new to you, be sure that you understand the\line \expndtw-1 ideas in this chapter before moving on. You can learn more about the \b Smalltalk/V\line \b0 operating environment in \b Part 3: Smalltalk/V 286 Reference. \b0 Chapters 15 and 16\line \expndtw1 provide an in-depth discussion of many of the topics introduced in this chapter.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb371\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Installing Smalltalk/V}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri7\sb223\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Before you begin installing \b Smalltalk/V, \b0 make sure that you have made backup copies\line \expndtw1 of the \b Source \b0 and Image diskettes included in your \b Smalltalk/V \b0 package.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb353\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Copying Smalltalk/V to a Hard Disk}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Copying \b Smalltalk/V \b0 to the hard disk involves two steps: l) creating a subdirectory on\line \expndtw0 your hard disk and 2) copying the \b Smalltalk/V \b0 files.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb292\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex80\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Creating a Subdirectory}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb191\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V \b0 must have its own subdirectory on your hard disk. You can create a\line \b\expndtw-4 Smalltalk/V \b0 subdirectory as a child of any directory already residing on your disk. Here\line \expndtw3 we are going to create a subdirectory called \b smaltalk, \b0 but you can name your\line \expndtw-1 subdirectory any name you chose.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb144\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To create the subdirectory, enter the following command at the DOS prompt then hit the\line \b return \b0 or \b enter \b0 key:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2376\margrsxn1930\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 22    Chapter 2: Introduction to the Smalltalk/ V286 Environment}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb295\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 C> mkdir \\smaltalk}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb166\sa0\sl-230\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This creates a subdirectory called \b smaltalk, \b0 with the root directory as its parent on Drive\line "C". You may create a subdirectory from any directory on your disk. Refer to your DOS\line \expndtw-2 manual for instructions.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb349\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Copying Smalltalk/V Files}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb205\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To copy \b Smalltalk/V \b0 files to your newly created subdirectory, insert the \b Source \b0 diskette\line in Drive A. Enter the following command at the DOS prompt and hit the \b return \b0 or \b enter}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 key:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 C> copy a:*.* \\smaltalk}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This copies all files on the Drive A Source diskette to the hard disk subdirectory called\line \b\expndtw-1 smaltalk. \b0 After each file is copied to the hard disk, DOS reports back with a screen\line \expndtw-3 message. When the message \b\i "xx \i0 files copied" \b0 appears, DOS is finished copying the\line \expndtw-4 diskette.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Remove the \b Source \b0 diskette from Drive A, and replace it with the \b Image \b0 diskette. \b Repeat\line \b0\expndtw-1 the procedure.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 With the \b Smalltalk/V \b0 files copied to the hard disk, you are ready to run the installation\line \expndtw-4 program.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb353\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Running the Installation Program}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri14\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To install \b Smalltalk/V, \b0 you need only know the type \i of \i0 video adapter board and\line \expndtw-1 monitor your system is using.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb335\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Supported Video Adapters}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb187\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 At time of publication, \b Smalltalk/V 286 \b0 supports the following:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2434\margrsxn1868\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri4\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 2: Introduction to the Smalltalk/ V286 Environment    23}\par \pard\plain\fs2\sa547\sl-1\slmult0\par\trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-310 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4198 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx5976 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Video Adapter Type}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li547\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Monitor Type}\cell 