ineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft6404\shptop-752\shpright7326\shpbottom-752\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft7124\shptop-695\shpright8074\shpbottom-695\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft6908\shptop-551\shpright8161\shpbottom-551\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft6289\shptop-500\shpright8103\shpbottom-500\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft3582\shptop-349\shpright6181\shpbottom-349\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4100\shptop-320\shpright5986\shpbottom-320\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb3010\sa0\sl-302\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex33\expndtw28\dn7\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs42 liL}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Debugger. \b0 The Debugger gives an expanded view of the Walkback in four panes. It is\line \expndtw0 a high-level debugging aid to help you correct programming errors.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2445\margrsxn1840\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 2: Introduction to the Smalltalk/ V286 Environment   \i0 37}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb317\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Inside the Window Pane}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri18\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Most of what you do in \b Smalltalk/\b0 V will involve activities inside a window pane. For\line \expndtw-1 many of the programs that you write, you will be able to find program code that has\line \expndtw0 already been typed into \b Smalltalk/V. \b0 You can copy this code and edit the text strings\line \expndtw-3 to produce the new code you are trying to write. This saves you from having to re-invent\line \expndtw1 source code each time you want to do something in \b Smalltalk/V.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri22\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In this section you will learn how to enter and edit text strings and how to make the most\line \expndtw-3 of the powerful \b cut, \b0 copy and \b paste \b0 methods found in the pane menu. For more detailed\line \expndtw-1 discussion of these subjects, see Chapter 15.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb353\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Selecting Text}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri22\sb209\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To do almost anything in \b Smalltalk/V, \b0 you must select some text to work with. This\line simply means marking any piece of data, such as a block of code, an expression, some\line \expndtw-1 text lines, etc.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri22\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Once you select text you can copy or \b cut \b0 it from its current location then \b paste \b0 it\line \expndtw-1 elsewhere using the relevant menu selection from the pane menu.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri18\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When working with text, you will see an I-beam in the \i text \i0 pane. The I-beam is the\line \expndtw-5 special pointer used when selecting or editing text strings. The I-beam marks the selection\line \expndtw-2 point for working in text. You can position the I-beam anywhere you like in the pane,\line \expndtw0 even between letters in a word.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb364\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Selecting Text Using the Mouse}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To select text using the mouse, first activate the window. Move the cursor to one end of\line \expndtw0 the text string. Press and hold down the left mouse button. While continuing to hold\line \expndtw-2 down the button, move the cursor to the other end of the text you wish to select. As you\line \expndtw-4 move the cursor, text is reversed. When the text you want to select is reversed, release the\line \expndtw-2 left mouse button. The selected text remains reversed until you deselect it. To \b deselect\line \b0\expndtw1 text, click the left mouse button somewhere else in the window pane.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb356\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Selecting Text Using the Keyboard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri18\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To select text using the keyboard, make sure the window is active. Move the cursor to\line \expndtw-2 one end of the text you wish to select and press the + key on the numeric keypad. Then\line \expndtw-3 move the cursor to the other end of the \i text, \i0 and press the \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  key on the numeric keypad.\line \expndtw-2 The selected \i text \i0 is reversed. If you did not select exactly what you wanted, move the\line \expndtw-1 cursor and press the \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  key again.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2379\margrsxn1912\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-778\shptop3985\shpright-778\shpbottom4669\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-770\shptop6577\shpright-770\shpbottom7124\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 38    Chapter 2: Introduction to the Smalltalk/ V286 Environment}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri11\sb320\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Selecting a different piece \i of text is \i0 done in exactly the same way. \b Smalltalk/\b0 V deselects\line \expndtw0 the old selected text and reverses the newly selected text strings.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb353\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Quick Selection of Single Words and Single Lines}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To quickly select a whole word in a text string, position the cursor anywhere on the word\line \expndtw-2 to be selected and click the left mouse button twice or press the + key twice in the same\line \expndtw-1 place\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 a \b double click. \b0 The whole word is reversed without having to drag the cursor\line \expndtw3 from one end of the word to the other.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To quickly select a whole line of text, place the cursor just inside the window border to\line \expndtw-1 the left of the line you wish to select and click the left mouse button twice or press the\line \expndtw-3 + key twice. The whole line is selected. If you continue holding down the mouse button\line \expndtw-1 while you drag the mouse either up or down, line after line of text is selected until you\line \expndtw3 let go of the mouse button.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb353\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Scrolling Text}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb209\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The text inside of a pane can be larger than the pane itself. Scrolling moves different \i text\line \i0\expndtw-1 into the pane for viewing. To scroll text into the pane you can use either the mouse or\line the keyboard.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb338\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Scrolling Text Using the Mouse}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb205\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Make sure that the window is active. Then press the right mouse button and hold it\line \expndtw-3 down. The cursor changes from an arrow to a scroll cursor and the scroll bar appears on\line \expndtw-2 the right side of the pane. The shaded area in the scroll bar shows where the text in the\line \expndtw-1 window is generally located in the overall document.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Continue to hold down the button while dragging the mouse downward. The scroll\line \expndtw-3 cursor changes to an arrow. Drag the arrow downward outside the text pane and the text\line \expndtw-2 in the pane scrolls up. Drag the arrow up beyond the top of the pane and the text scrolls\line \expndtw-1 downward. You can use the same technique to scroll text left and right across the pane\line \expndtw0 by dragging the arrow beyond the pane frame in either direction.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb335\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Scrolling Text Using the Keyboard}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb202\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To scroll text in a pane with the keyboard, first make sure that the window is active. Then\line \expndtw-2 press the \b Home \b0 and \b End \b0 keys to scroll the text left and right, respectively, or the \b PgUp\line \b0 and \b PgDn \b0 keys to scroll the text up and down, respectively. To scroll in large amounts,\line \expndtw0 hold down the \b Shift \b0 key while pressing one of the scrolling keys.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2444\margrsxn1847\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 2: Introduction to the Smalltalk/ V286 Environment   39}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb356\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Scrolling to Select Large Text Areas}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If the text you want to select is too long to fit in the pane at one time, there are two ways\line \expndtw-1 to select large areas of text, l) Move the cursor to one end of the text string. Press and\line \expndtw-2 hold down the right mouse button while dragging the arrow downward as described in\line \expndtw-1 scrolling above. Move the cursor back into the pane when the other end of the desired\line \expndtw-4 text is visible. Then adjust the selection until it is just what you want before releasing the\line \expndtw-1 mouse button. Or 2) you can place the I-beam at one end for selection, then scroll the\line \expndtw0 pane until the other end of the desired text is visible. Move the cursor over the last\line \expndtw-2 character to be selected. To select the text, press the \b shift \b0 key and click the left mouse\line \expndtw0 button or press the \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  key on the numeric keypad. The entire section is selected.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb349\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Inserting Text}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To insert text into a pane, first activate the window. Then move the cursor to the location\line \expndtw0 where you want to insert text. Click the left mouse button or press the + key on the\line \expndtw-3 numeric keypad. Notice the I-beam in the pane. The I-beam marks the insertion point for\line \expndtw-2 new text.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You can insert text in two ways: either by directly typing in the new text string or by\line \expndtw-4 using the \b paste \b0 option from the pane menu to bring in text you have selected and copied\line \expndtw-5 from another place. If you make a mistake, use the \b backspace \b0 key to delete your mistakes.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb353\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Deleting Text}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The easiest but slowest way to delete \i text \i0 is to place the I-beam after the last character\line \expndtw-4 you want to delete and press the backspace key until all the characters you wish to delete\line \expndtw0 disappear from the screen. Remember that you place the I-beam by clicking the left\line \expndtw3 mouse button or pressing the + key on the numeric keypad.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri11\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To delete whole words or strings of words, select the text to be deleted and either press\line \expndtw-5 the \b backspace \b0 key once, or begin typing new text, or \b paste \b0 in copied text from elsewhere.\line \expndtw0 All three actions delete the selected text.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb364\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Zooming in on a Text Pane}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb205\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When \b zoom \b0 is selected using either the \b zoom button \b0 on the window \b label bar \b0 or \b zoom\line \b0\expndtw0 from the \b next menu \b0 pane menu, \b Smalltalk/V \b0 zooms in on the text window so that it\line \expndtw-2 fills the whole screen. You can then have a full display text view for any text entry or\line \expndtw-3 editing you need to do. Alternatively, you can press the F8 function key to activate the\line \expndtw-2 zoom feature.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1114\margrsxn1809\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-122\shptop61\shpright-122\shpbottom4086\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 27305}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-22\shptop6959\shpright-22\shpbottom7355\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 M'   \i0 .}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1382\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 40    Chapter 2: Introduction to the Smalltalk/V286 Environment}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1368\fi0\ri0\sb310\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To return the display to normal, reselect zoom, or press the F8 function key again, or\line \expndtw1 click the left mouse button when the cursor is inside of the window label bar.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1372\fi0\ri0\sb382\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Starting Out}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1368\fi0\ri7\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now that you are familiar with the objects and methods of the \b Smalltalk/V\line \b0\expndtw-4 environment, you can jump right in and get to work. As you work your way through this\line \expndtw1 last section of the chapter and the tutorials that follow, you will quickly come to\line \expndtw3 understand and be able to use Smalltalk/V.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1372\fi0\ri0\sb356\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Evaluating Text}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1364\fi0\ri14\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To evaluate a \b Smalltalk/V \b0 expression, you must first enter the text into a pane and select\line \expndtw-1 it. Make sure that the cursor is inside of the window, and then pop up the \b pane menu.\line \b0\expndtw3 Select either do it or \b show \b0 it from the menu.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1357\fi0\ri14\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When you select \b do it, Smalltalk/V \b0 executes the selected text. When you select \b show\line \expndtw-3 it, Smalltalk/V \b0 executes the selected text and displays the result of the expression on the\line \expndtw-7 screen.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1361\fi0\ri18\sb162\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 For example, place the cursor into any active window pane. Type in the following\line \expndtw-2 expression and evaluate it:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1764\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw60\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 3+4}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1357\fi0\ri22\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When you evaluate this expression with \b do it, Smalltalk/V \b0 executes the expression but\line \expndtw0 shows no results on the screen. When you evaluate this expression with \b show it,\line \expndtw-1 Smalltalk/V \b0 both executes the expression and shows the results. In the tutorials that\line \expndtw0 follow, you will usually use the \b show \b0 it method for evaluation.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1354\fi0\ri0\sb356\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Compilation Errors}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1346\fi0\ri22\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Type the following expression into a window, select it, and then evaluate it, using the do\line \expndtw2 it choice on the pane menu:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2142\fi-392\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Turtle\line \expndtw-6 home;\line \expndtw-5 north;\line \expndtw-8 black;\line \expndtw-4 mandela: \b0 x2 \b diameter: \b0 300}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1346\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The result should be:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2403\margrsxn1910\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3157\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa742 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 2: Introduction to the Smalltalk/ V286 Environment    41}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn5082\margrsxn4300\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw774\colsr882\colno2\colw871 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-310\shptop79\shpright-310\shpbottom2858\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2657\shptop-317\shpright2657\shpbottom2858\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 Turtle}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li266\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 home;\line \expndtw-11 north;\line black;\line \expndtw-10 mandela:}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb533\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1031\fs12  \lang1033 diameter: 380}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2403\margrsxn1910\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li320\fi0\ri0\sb1919\sa0\sl-212\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 2.15\line Compilation Error}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb335\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The compiler detected an error. The error message is inserted in front of the error and\line \expndtw-1 is selected. To delete the error message, press the \b backspace \b0 key. Now correct the\line \expndtw1 expression by changing the letter x to the number 1 so that the line reads:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 mandela: 12 diameter: 300}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb356\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Runtime Errors and Walkback Windows}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb209\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Select the corrected expression from the above example and evaluate it. (Make sure that\line \expndtw0 you select the entire expression, not just the corrected line.) The result should be:}\par \pard \plain \li2405\ri468\sb475\sa0{\pict\picw8331\pich2933\picwgoal4723\pichgoal1663\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3665\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-130\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs14 'en( Obj ect) \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 error \dbch\af3\loch\af1\hich\f1\cchs0 \u9632\'3f\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 '\line \expndtw-2 Pen(Object)\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 doesNotUnderstand:\line \expndtw-3 Jndef inedObj ect\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 Do it}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li338\fi0\ri0\sb842\sa0\sl-220\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 2.16\line \expndtw-7 Runtime Error}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2161\margrsxn2123\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 42    Chapter 2: Introduction to the Smalltalk/ V286 Environment}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri0\sb320\sa0\sl-230\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V \b0\i detected \i0 a runtime error. In this case, the problem is a misspelled word:\line \b\expndtw-5 mandela \b0 should be \b mandala. \b0 The window notifying you of the error is called a walkback\line \expndtw-6 window.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri4\sb166\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To correct the error, simply activate the window with the expression and fix the\line \expndtw0 misspelling. You can now select and evaluate the text again.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb317\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex81\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Prompters}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri4\sb205\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V \b0 uses prompters to ask questions to which you respond by entering a string\line \expndtw-1 of characters. For example, the code for this prompter shows that it asks you to enter\line \expndtw1 your name, and then it prints it:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Prompter prompt: 'your name ?' default: \b0 ' '}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri11\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To see how this works, enter the above expression into a pane, select it, and then evaluate\line \i\expndtw2 it \i0 using the \b show it \b0 choice on the pane menu. The result should be:}\par \pard \plain \li2326\ri0\sb256\sa0{\pict\picw9347\pich5702\picwgoal5299\pichgoal3232\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 2.17\line \expndtw-3 Prompter}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri36\sb320\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You enter your response in the pane of the prompter. A prompter lets you enter, delete\line \expndtw-3 and edit the text in the usual ways, except that \i it \i0 accepts only a single line of text. When\line you press the \b return \b0 or \b enter \b0 key, the line of text is returned to \b Smalltalk/V \b0 as your\line \expndtw0 response and the prompter is closed.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2043\margrsxn2255\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 2: Introduction to the Smalltalk/ V286 Environment    43}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb349\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Reusing Text}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 One of the nice things about \b Smalltalk/V \b0 is that you can \b edit \b0 text that you have\line \expndtw0 previously evaluated, and then \b evaluate \b0 the expression again. For example, in the\line \expndtw-4 prompter example above, change \b 'your name?' \b0 to \b 'your age?' \b0 Evaluate the expression\line \expndtw1 using \b show it \b0 from the pane menu.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb353\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Browsing}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri4\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Some windows, like the Disk Browser have several panes. Some of these panes have lists\line \expndtw0 from which you can select items.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa364\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Pop up the system \b menu \b0 by placing the cursor on the screen background and clicking\line \expndtw0 the left mouse button or using the + key on the numeric keypad. Select \b browse disk\line \b0\expndtw-1 from the system menu. You are presented with a menu of devices. Choose one to open\line \b\expndtw-3 a \b0 Disk Browser window for that device. The pane in the upper left corner lists all of the\line \expndtw-4 directories on the disk. You can scroll this list both horizontally and vertically as you can\line \expndtw-2 with any window pane.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2396\margrsxn3339\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1191\colsr936\colno2\colw4046 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2012\shptop4\shpright2012\shpbottom3194\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}{\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx2376\posnegy0\absw0\absh-158\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs14 Saalltalk/U Transcript}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx2261\posnegy1202\absw0\absh-141\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 ttmac}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx2128\posnegy1361\absw0\absh-140\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 Directory by nane}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2084\shptop40\shpright7160\shpbottom1472\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr5\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 75}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn pib}{\sv {\pict\picw8953\pich2527\picwgoal5076\pichgoal1432\jpegblip
}}}{\sp{\sn pictureGray}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn pWrapPolygonVertices}{\sv 8;13;(7506,0);(7506,1953);(0,1953);(0,17366);(7506,17366);(7506,21600);(21600,21600);(21600,17366);(21600,17366);(21600,1953);(21600,1953);(21600,0);(7506,0)}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fIsBullet}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fAllowOverlap}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fEditedWrap}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fBehindDocument}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fPrint}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistLeft}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistRight}{\sv 0}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa0\sl-576\slmult0\tlhyph\tx832 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 directory\line \expndtw-2 file{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb277\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 file contents}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb1033\sa0\sl-220\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 2.18\line Disk Browser}\par \column {\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phcol\posnegx3074\posnegy180\absw0\absh1404\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw1181\pich2476\picwgoal669\pichgoal1404\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-266\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 TUTORIAL EXAMPLES FOB CHAPTER 2 - INTRODUCTION TO SMALLTALKS\line "Evaluating an Expression"\line "Evaluate this expression \loch\af16\hich\af16\dbch\f17\cchs0 \'ab\hich\af16\dbch\af17\loch\f16\cchs0 ith do it"\line \expndtw-5 Deno run}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 "Evaluate this expression with show it"}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2043\margrsxn2255\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb270\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To select an item from the directory list, move the cursor over the desired item and click\line \expndtw2 the left mouse button or press the + key on the numeric keypad. The item is then\line \expndtw0 reversed. The upper right pane then displays a list of files in the directory you just\line \expndtw1 selected. If you select a file in this list, the large pane at the bottom of the window\line \expndtw-4 displays the file contents. \b Part \b0 3 of this manual explains the Disk Browser in much more\line \expndtw-6 detail.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2563\margrsxn1714\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 44    Chapter 2: Introduction to the Smalltalk/ V286 Environment}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb313\sa310\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now pop up the system \b menu \b0 by placing the cursor on the screen background and\line \expndtw-2 clicking the left mouse button or using the + key on the numeric keypad. Select \b browse\line \expndtw-1 classes \b0 from the system menu to open the Class Hierarchy Browser window.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2919\margrsxn3680\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1375\colsr749\colno2\colw3186 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft1937\shptop1634\shpright1937\shpbottom3204\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}{\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx4673\posnegy410\absw1311\absh-684\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 displayFon*: at:rule:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx4673\posnegy410\absw1311\absh-684\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 d isplayForn:fron:to:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx4673\posnegy410\absw1311\absh-684\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 font}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx4673\posnegy410\absw1311\absh-684\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 fraae}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx4673\posnegy410\absw1311\absh-684\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 gray:}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft1375\shptop0\shpright7243\shpbottom1556\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr5\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 75}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn pib}{\sv {\pict\picw10350\pich2743\picwgoal5868\pichgoal1555\jpegblip
}}}{\sp{\sn pictureGray}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn pWrapPolygonVertices}{\sv 8;17;(0,0);(0,6000);(0,6000);(0,15000);(0,15000);(0,15100);(0,15100);(0,21600);(21467,21600);(21467,15100);(12721,15100);(12721,15000);(12138,15000);(12138,6000);(21600,6000);(21600,0);(0,0)}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fIsBullet}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fAllowOverlap}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fEditedWrap}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fBehindDocument}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fPrint}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistLeft}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistRight}{\sv 0}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-569\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 methods\line \expndtw0 class hierarchy\line \expndtw4 source code \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f2\cchs0 \'97}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb828\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 2.19\line \expndtw-6 Class Hierarchy\line \expndtw-5 Browser}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 initialize: aRectangle font: aFont}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li454\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 ' \i0 "Initialize the instance variables of\line \expndtw-5 the receiver such that its clipping\line rectangle is aRectangle and the font is\line aFont.  The destination \i farm \i0 is assuaed\line \expndtw-6 to be the display screen."\line \expndtw0 f}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li515\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 initialize: a Rectangle\line \expndtw-5 font: aFont\line \expndtw-6 dest: Display}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2563\margrsxn1714\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-875\shptop1854\shpright-875\shpbottom2243\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb299\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Here you can find program code for objects you may want to use \i in \i0 your own\line \b\expndtw0 Smalltalk/V \b0 program. By bringing the code into any edit or text pane, you can then\line \expndtw-2 select it, copy it and paste it in your own program. Once the copied code \i is \i0 in your own\line \expndtw1 program, you can edit and evaluate it until \b Smalltalk/V \b0 does what you want \i it to do.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb364\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Tutorial Files}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri11\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In the tutorials that follow, you'll be seeing many examples and programs. We've already\line provided these examples for you in several disk files. To save yourself the time and effort\line \expndtw-3 of typing in these examples, you can use the Disk Browser to access them. Open a Disk\line Browser for the device that contains \b Smalltalk/V \b0 and select the directory in which\line \b\expndtw-1 Smalltalk/V \b0 is installed.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri14\sb162\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The example files are organized by chapter. For example, the examples for Chapter 2 are\line \expndtw-5 in the file \b Chapter. \b0 2. You can see the examples at the beginning of the chapter displayed\line \expndtw-3 on the screen. To see the rest, just scroll the pane. You can then treat these examples by\line \expndtw1 evaluating and editing them just as \i if \i0 you had entered them yourself.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri18\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You should now be familiar enough with the \b Smalltalk/V \b0 environment to proceed to\line \expndtw-3 the following tutorials. If you want to review any topics covered in this tutorial, you can\line \expndtw0 either repeat the corresponding section of the tutorial or refer to a detailed description\line in \b Part 3, The Smalltalk/V 286 Reference.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1411\margrsxn5372\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw720\colsr245\colno2\colw4161 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 3 OBJECTS AND MESSAGES}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1411\margrsxn1923\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-43\shptop382\shpright267\shpbottom382\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li950\fi0\ri29\sb432\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now that you have toured the \b Smalltalk/V \b0 environment, you are ready to learn about\line \expndtw-4 the Smalltalk language itself. This chapter concentrates on the concepts of \i objects \i0 and\line \i messages, \i0 the basis of the Smalltalk language. You'll also be introduced to global and\line temporary variables.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li961\fi0\ri18\sb130\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Throughout the tutorials, you will be asked to evaluate sample pieces of code. As\line \expndtw-5 described in the previous chapter, you can find these examples stored in a disk file. Simply\line \expndtw-4 use the Disk Browser to access these files. The examples for this chapter are stored in the\line \expndtw-6 file \b chapter.\b0 3.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li965\fi0\ri14\sb133\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Even if you are an experienced Smalltalk programmer, use these examples. They will help\line \expndtw0 you to understand the \b Smalltalk/V \b0 environment, as well as introduce you to some\line \expndtw-4 advanced Smalltalk applications. Of course, if you are new to Smalltalk, these examples\line \expndtw-2 will be even more valuable.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li972\fi0\ri0\sb385\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Simple Objects}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li968\fi0\ri11\sb212\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Objects \i0 are the basic building block of the Smalltalk language. They are analogous to\line \expndtw0 pieces of data in other languages. For example,}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1372\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'this is a string'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li976\fi0\ri11\sb137\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 is a Smalltalk object, a string of characters. It's very much like a string in any other\line \expndtw-3 language. Here are some other simple Smalltalk objects that have counterparts in most\line \expndtw-6 languages:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1390\fi0\ri0\sb184\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx4046 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 1234{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-4 "an integer"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1364\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx4050 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 $A{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-3 "the single character A"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1397\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx4050 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw64\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 #(123){\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-3 "an array of three integer objects"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li976\fi0\ri7\sb137\sa0\sl-248\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Look at the last example, the array. It's an object which itself contains other objects. Let's\line \expndtw0 look at some more examples:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1404\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 #( \b 'array' 'of 'four* 'strings')}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1404\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 #( 'array' 'of 5 'strings' 'and' \b0 2 \b 'integers' \b0 )}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li983\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As you can see from the last example, all of the objects contained inside of an object do\line not have to be of the same type or size. Part of the power of Smalltalk comes from this\line \expndtw-1 capability. Consider this more complex object:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1408\fi0\ri0\sb126\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 #( \b 1 \b0 ( \b 'two' 'three' \b0 ) 4 )}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2437\margrsxn1833\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 46    Chapter 3: Objects and Messages}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri29\sb324\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This is an object (an array) with three objects inside of it. The second object in the array\line \expndtw0 is another array of two strings.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb367\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Simple Messages}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri25\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Of course, an object can do nothing by itself. In Smalltalk, you send \i messages \i0 to objects\line to make things happen. Messages are similar to function calls in other languages. For\line \expndtw-1 example, look at this Smalltalk expression, composed of a single message:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 20 factorial}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri29\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This sends the message \b factorial \b0 to the object 20. To evaluate this expression, either find\line \expndtw-2 it in the tutorial file \b chapter. \b0 3 or type it into a pane and select it. Then use the \b show it\line \b0\expndtw-3 choice on the pane menu to compile and evaluate it. The result should be a very large\line \expndtw-6 integer:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 2432902008176640000}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri29\sb162\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Let's try another simple message. When you select and evaluate the following expression,\line \expndtw1 the result should be the integer 15, the size of the string:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'now \b is the \b0 time' \b size}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri29\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A message is composed of three parts: a \i receiver object, \i0 a \i message selector, \i0 and zero or more\line \i\expndtw-4 arguments. \i0 In the above example, the string is the receiver object, the message selector is\line \expndtw0 size, and there are no arguments. Or consider:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li428\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 #( 1 3 5 7 ) \b at: \b0 2}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri29\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In this example, the array \i is \i0 the receiver, \b at: \b0 is the message selector, and the 2 \i is \i0 the\line \expndtw-4 argument.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri29\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A message always returns a single object as its result, just like functions in most other\line \expndtw-2 languages. Similarly, the message selector is like the function name, and the receiver\line \expndtw-4 object is like the first function parameter. The above example asks for the second element\line \expndtw0 of the array; the result is the integer 3.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi-392\ri0\sb29\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now try evaluating this example:\line \b '20' factorial}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A walkback window appears. Since 20 is enclosed in quotes, it is a string, to which\line \b\expndtw-7 factorial \b0 makes no sense. As this example illustrates, \i objects always know the messages that\line \expndtw-5 are appropriate for them. \i0\b Part 4 \b0 of this manual lists all of the different kinds of objects\line \expndtw1 provided by \b Smalltalk/V, \b0 and the messages that they respond to.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2262\margrsxn2007\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri18\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter \i0 3: \i Objects and Messages    47}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri18\sb320\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To get rid of the walkback window, select the \b close \b0 button from the walkback window\line \expndtw-3 label bar by positioning the cursor over the button and clicking the left mouse button or\line \expndtw2 pressing the numeric keypad + key.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb360\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Unary Messages}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri18\sb209\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Messages with no arguments are called \i unary \i0 messages. Try evaluating these unary\line \expndtw-8 messages:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 #( \b 'array' 'of 'strings' ) size\line \expndtw-3 'now is the time' asUpperCase\line \expndtw-2 'hello there' reversed\line \expndtw6 #( 4 'five' 6 7 ) reversed\line \expndtw-3 $A asciiValue\line \expndtw-8 65 asCharacter}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb374\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Keyword Messages}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri22\sb212\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Messages with one or more arguments are called \i keyword messages. \i0 Try evaluating these\line keyword messages:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'now is the time' at: 6\line \expndtw0 Hello' includes: $e\line \expndtw1 'hello' at: 1 put: $H\line \expndtw0 'The quick brown' copyFrom: 4 to: 9}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri22\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In the last two examples, the message selectors \b at:put: \b0 and \b copyFronv.to: \b0 are divided up\line \expndtw-3 by the arguments. In these examples, \b ar.put: \b0 and \b copyFronv.to: \b0 work with strings, but\line \expndtw-2 these same messages work for arrays as well:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 #( 9 8 7 6 5 ) \b at: \b0 3\line \expndtw75\fs20 #(1(23)45)\expndtw0  \b\expndtw11 includes: \expndtw76 #(23)\line \expndtw62 #(1045)\expndtw0  \expndtw13 at: 2 put: \expndtw74 #(23)\line \expndtw76 #(98765)\expndtw0  \expndtw11 copyFrom: 1 to: \b0 2}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 From these examples, you may have noticed another important point: different kinds of\line \expndtw1 objects can respond to the same message in different ways. These arrays respond\line \expndtw-3 differently to the \b ar.put: \b0 and \b copyFrom-.to: \b0 message selectors than the strings in the\line \expndtw-4 previous examples.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2311\margrsxn1987\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 48    Chapter 3: Objects and Messages}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb331\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Arithmetic Messages}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi-403\ri0\sb97\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk arithmetic looks the same as in most other languages. For example:\line \expndtw68 3+4}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb130\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 But, like other Smalltalk expressions, this is a message. The integer 3 is the receiver, +\line \expndtw-4 is the message selector, the integer 4 is the argument, and the integer 7 is the result. Here\line \expndtw-1 are some more common arithmetic messages you can evaluate.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb194\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3046 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw42\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 5*7{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-4 "multiplication"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3046 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw21\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 5 // 2{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw0 "integer division (truncation)"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3046 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw23\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 4 \\\\ 3{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-5 "integer remainder"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3046 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw63\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 2/6{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-4 "rational division"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The last expression illustrates \i rational arithmetic, \i0 or the arithmetic of fractions. In\line \expndtw-3 Smalltalk, rational arithmetic is exact; there is no rounding or truncation. This is because\line \expndtw-1 Smalltalk stores the result as a numerator and a denominator (reduced to its simplest\line \expndtw0 form), rather than computing an approximate real number.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Arithmetic expressions in Smalltalk also differ from most other languages in their order\line \expndtw-4 of evaluation. Smalltalk evaluates an arithmetic expression strictly from left to right, with\line \expndtw-1 no precedence among operators. For example, evaluate this expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw67\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 3+4*2}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb144\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The result is 14, not 11. You can, however, use parentheses to control evaluation order,\line as in this example:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb29\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw14\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 3 + \expndtw68 (4*\expndtw14  2 )}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi-414\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If your computer has a coprocessor, you can perform floating point arithmetic as well:\line \expndtw7 1.5 + 6.3e2}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb122\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If a walkback window appears, your computer does not have a coprocessor. Close the\line \expndtw-1 walkback window and continue.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb374\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Binary Messages}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Arithmetic messages are examples of \i binary \i0 messages, messages with one argument and\line \expndtw1 one or two special characters (other than digits and letters) as the selector. Binary\line \expndtw-6 messages are always evaluated strictly from left to right, unless you have used parentheses.\line \expndtw-2 For example, evaluate these non-arithmetic messages:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2176\margrsxn2122\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 3: Objects and Messages    49}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb313\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'hello', ' there'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li428\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw18\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 #(1 \b0 2 3), #(4 \b 5 \b0 6)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In these examples, the special character is the comma. It concatenates the argument with\line \expndtw-1 the receiver object.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb374\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Messages inside of Messages}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As we stated before, messages are like functions, in that they return an object. This means\line \expndtw-1 that anywhere an object appears in an expression, you can use a message which returns\line a similar kind of object. For example, evaluate:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'hello' size + 4}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'now' size + \expndtw75 #(1234)\expndtw6  size}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li428\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 #( 1 12 24 36) includes: 4 factorial}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The last expression above is really two messages. \b 4 factorial \b0 is a unary message, and is\line \expndtw-2 computed first. The result then becomes the argument for the \b includes: \b0 message. Now\line evaluate this more complex expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb180\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw25\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 4 factorial between: \expndtw97 3+4\expndtw25  and: 'hello' size \b0 * 7}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb169\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This expression is composed of five messages. The five message selectors are \b factorial,\line \expndtw-6 +, size, *, \b0 and \b between:and:. \b0 As you can see, unary messages are always evaluated before\line binary messages, which in turn are evaluated before keyword messages. As usual, you can\line \expndtw-2 override this precedence by using parentheses:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'hello' at: \b0 ( #( 5 3 \b 1 \b0 ) \b at: \b0 2 )}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri14\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This expression is composed of two \b at: \b0 messages: one to an array, and one to a string.\line \expndtw0 To see what happens when there are no parentheses, try:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'hello' at: \b0 #( \b 5 \b0 3 \b 1 \b0 ) \b at: \b0 2}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri11\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This expression is a single message with two arguments. The message selector becomes\line \b\expndtw-1 af.at:, \b0 clearly not what we had in mind.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb367\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Expression Series}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri18\sb216\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You can't do much with just a single expression. Here's a series of expressions, which you\line \expndtw-1 can evaluate as a single unit. Select the entire series before popping up the pane menu:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2428\margrsxn1856\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li40\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 50    Chapter 3: Objects and Messages}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb306\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Turtle black.\line \expndtw-2 Turtle home.\line \expndtw1 Turtle go: 100.\line \expndtw-2 Turtle turn: 120.\line \expndtw1 Turtle go: 100.\line \expndtw-2 Turtle turn: 120.\line \expndtw1 Turtle go: 100.\line \expndtw-1 Turtle turn: 120}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb162\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Each message in the series is separated from the next by a period. We put each message\line on a separate line purely for appearance. The receiver of all the messages is the object\line \b\expndtw-2 Turtle, \b0 one of the objects supplied in the \b Smalltalk/V \b0 environment. To get a different\line \expndtw0 picture, change the word \b black \b0 to \b white \b0 and evaluate the expression series again.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb126\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To clean up the screen, select \b redraw screen \b0 from the system menu.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb367\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Cascaded Messages}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A \i cascaded message \i0 is a shorthand way of writing a series of messages that are sent to the\line \expndtw0 same receiver. For example, the following expression draws the same figure as the\line \expndtw-2 previous example (only in a different color):}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi-396\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Turtle\line \expndtw-4 white;\line \expndtw-7 home;\line \expndtw1 go: 100;\line \expndtw-5 turn: 120;\line \expndtw1 go: 100;\line \expndtw-5 turn: 120;\line \expndtw1 go: 100;\line \expndtw-2 turn: \b0 120}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri22\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The receiver is written only once, and each message (except for the last) is terminated with\line \expndtw-3 a semi-colon instead of a period. The indentation, again, is optional; it simply makes the\line \expndtw0 code easier to read.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb374\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Simple Loops}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We can simplify the above example by using a message that loops a specified number of\line \expndtw-8 times:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2191\margrsxn2101\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 3: Objects and Messages    31}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb310\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Turtle}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 black;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li421\fi378\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 home.\line \expndtw0 3 timesRepeat: [ Turtle go: 100; turn: 120 \b0 ]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In this example, the argument to the timesRepeat: message is a block of code. Blocks\line \expndtw-3 of code are written as a series of messages enclosed in square brackets, [ and ]. We'd\line \expndtw-1 normally write the above example as:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi-403\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Turtle\line \expndtw-8 black;\line \expndtw-5 home.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi-382\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 3 timesRepeat: [\line \expndtw-3 Turtle}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1116\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 go: 100;\line \expndtw-1 turn: \b0 120]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This makes the cascaded message inside the block easier to see.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb367\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Objects and Messages Are Safe}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The previous series of expressions illustrates another point about objects and messages.\line \expndtw-2 Objects have a state; they can remember things. Messages change an object's state. The\line \b\expndtw-4 Turtle \b0 object remembers its position, heading, and color. The messages \b black \b0 and \b white\line \b0\expndtw-3 change its color, the messages \b go: \b0 and \b home \b0 change its position, and the message \b turn:\line \b0\expndtw0 changes its heading. Let's look at another expression that emphasizes this point:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'hello' at: \b0 1 \b put: \b0 23}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When you evaluate this expression, a walkback error window pops up because 23 is not\line a character. Since you can only change the state of the string by sending messages, the\line \expndtw-5 string can check the validity of the arguments. This makes Smalltalk a very safe language.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb364\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Temporary Variables}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Temporary variables \i0 are so called because Smalltalk discards them as soon as you are done\line \expndtw-2 using them. Temporary variables are declared by enclosing them in vertical bars in the\line \expndtw-3 first line of an expression series. Temporary variable names must start with a lower case\line letter, while the rest of the name can be any combination of upper and lower case letters\line \expndtw-1 and digits. For example, look at this short program that uses three temporary variables\line \expndtw0 and a loop to compute an array of several factorials:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2636\margrsxn1629\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 52    Chapter 3: Objects and Messages}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb317\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I temp index factorials \b0 I\line \b\expndtw24\fs18 factorials \b0 := \expndtw73 #(3456).\line \b\expndtw8\fs22 index := 1.\line \expndtw-4 factorials size timesRepeat: [}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 temp := factorials at: index.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 factorials at: index put: temp factorial.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li421\fi382\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 index :== index + l].\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-10 a \scaps0\b factorials}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri25\sb166\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The first line declares three temporary variables: \b temp, index, \b0 and \b factorials. \b0 A temporary\line variable can hold any type of object. To give it a value, you use an \i assignment expression.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb356\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Assignment Expressions}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb205\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The above example uses four assignment expressions:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 factorials := #( 34 5 6 ).\line \expndtw8 index := 1.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 temp := factorials at: index,\line \expndtw11 index := index + 1.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri25\sb162\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The first two assign objects to the temporary variables, while the last two assign the\line \expndtw-4 results of messages. Since the result of a message is always a single object, they actually\line \expndtw-1 assign objects to the temporary variables.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb364\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Return Expressions}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li421\fi-418\ri0\sb97\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The last expression in the factorial example above is:\line \b\expndtw-8 Afactorials}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri29\sb130\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The caret \scaps (a) \scaps0 indicates that this is the value to be returned as the result \i of \i0 the expression\line \expndtw-2 series. Such a statement beginning with a caret is called a \i return expression.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb367\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Global Variables}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V \b0 has many objects built into it, many of which are contained in \i global\line \expndtw-4 variables. \i0 Unlike temporary variables, Smalltalk does not automatically dispose \i of \i0 global\line \expndtw-2 variables when you are finished using them, and their use is not confined to a single set\line of expressions. For example, \b Smalltalk/V \b0 provides (among others) these three global\line \expndtw-8 variables:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2312\margrsxn1982\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx-965\posnegy0\absw0\absh914\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw1333\pich1612\picwgoal756\pichgoal914\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb364\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 3: Objects and Messages    53}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb324\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Turtle}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Transcript}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Disk}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We have been using the global variable, \b Turtle. \b0 Global variables always contain a single\line \expndtw0 object. For example, select \b Turtle, \b0 and then use the \b show it \b0 choice on the pane menu\line \expndtw1 to see its current contents. The result is itself a single object.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Global variable names always begin with an upper case letter, with the remainder upper\line \expndtw0 and lower case letters and digits. Type in the following, select \i it, \i0 and show \i it:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Sammy}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When the global variable does not currently exist, you get a menu which allows you to\line choose whether or not you want to create it. If you do not create the global variable,\line \b\expndtw-4 Smalltalk/V \b0 assumes you made an error, and displays an error message. This keeps you\line \expndtw-1 from accidentally creating global variables when you misspell something. As with\line \expndtw-3 temporary variables, you use assignment statements to assign values to global variables:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Sammy := 'Sammy Jones'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb378\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Putting It All Together}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri11\sb220\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To conclude this first tutorial, here's a graphical program that draws flowers composed\line \expndtw-3 of several polygons.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Draw a polygon flower"\line \expndtw-3 I sides length I\line \expndtw6 sides := 5.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 length := 240 // sides.\line \expndtw-3 Turtle\line \expndtw-8 black;\line \expndtw-6 home;\line \expndtw-4 north.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi-400\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 sides timesRepeat: [\line \expndtw2 Turtle go: length,\line \expndtw-2 sides - 1 timesRepeat: [\line \expndtw-4 Turtle}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1483\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 turn: 360 // sides;\line \expndtw6 go: length]]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The first line is a \i comment. \i0 Comments are any string of characters enclosed in double\line \expndtw2 quote marks ("comment"). \b Smalltalk/V \b0 ignores comments when it compiles the\line \expndtw-3 program; they simply add clarity to the code. Comments can appear anywhere in an\line \expndtw-5 expression series.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2564\margrsxn1741\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 54    Chapter 3: Objects and Messages}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb299\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Evaluate the above example, and note the results. For a slightly different flower, change\line \expndtw0 the number of sides. To make the polygons spread further apart, change:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Turtle go: length}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 to the following expression (you might want to change the color to \b white \b0 as well):}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi-403\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Turtle\line \expndtw-11 up;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 go: length // 2;\line \expndtw-2 down;\line \expndtw3 go: length.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 which produces:}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn5275\margrsxn4592\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft317\shptop360\shpright1887\shpbottom360\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-65\shptop382\shpright1937\shpbottom382\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst