ain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri112\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tqr\tx6948 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw16\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 indexl := indexl + l].{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\expndtw0 [}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri112\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tqr\tx7718 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\scaps\cf0\charscalex133\expndtw-44\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 aO{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\scaps0\expndtw0 I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri986\sb126\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Try the more general pattern matcher by evaluating the following examples using strings\line and arrays.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'the time has come' indexOfCollection: 'tim'\line \expndtw3 #($c $a $n $ $y $o $u $ ) indexOfCollection: 'you'\line \expndtw8 #(1 2 3 (4 5) 'abc' 6) indexOfCollection: #(2 3)\line \expndtw4 #(1 2 3 (4 5) 'abc' 6) indexOfCollection: 'abc'}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2016\margrsxn2282\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 6: Inheritance    87}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb356\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Processing Recursive Data Structures}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As an example of polymorphism and the processing of recursive data structures, consider\line \expndtw-2 the following method for equality (=), which appears in class \b IndexedCollection. \b0 This\line \expndtw-1 method compares an instance of class IndexedCollection or one of its subclasses (e.g.,\line \expndtw-2 an array) to another similar object by sending the = message to corresponding elements\line of both objects. If the element is a kind of indexed collection, then the method performs\line \b\expndtw-1 a \b0 recursive send, invoking the = method. If the element is an object such as a number,\line \expndtw1 the method performs a non-recursive send, invoking a different = method.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li428\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 = \b aCollection}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li821\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Answer true if the elements contained by\line \expndtw-2 the receiver are equal to the elements\line contained by the argument aCollection."\line \charscalex86\expndtw7 I index I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 self \i =\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  \i0 aCollection\line \expndtw0 ifTrue: \scaps [a\scaps0 true],\line \expndtw-2 (self class \super =s=:\nosupersub  aCollection class)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifFalse: \scaps [a\scaps0 false].\line \expndtw4 index := self size,\line \expndtw5 index ~= aCollection size}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifTrue:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1192\fi-371\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw15\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 [index <= 0]\line \expndtw-4 whileFalse: [}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1602\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (self at: index) = (aCollection at: index)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li817\fi392\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifFalse: [Afalse].\line \expndtw6 index := index - 1.]\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-13 a \scaps0\b true}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb166\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This method also demonstrates the difference between equality (=) and equivalence\line \expndtw-3 (==). Equality tests whether two objects contain the same elements. Equivalence, on the\line \expndtw-2 other hand, tests whether two objects are, in fact, the same object. For example, the\line \expndtw-5 expression}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 self \b0 == \b aCollection}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tests whether the receiver object is the same actual object as the argument. If they are,\line \expndtw0 then they are obviously equal, and the method returns \b true.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To help see this difference, evaluate the following statement:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li421\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex120\expndtw12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 labl}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex110\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 a := #( 1 2 3 4 ).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex110\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 b := #( 1 2 3 4 ).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex110\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Aa = b}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2425\margrsxn1874\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 88    Chapter 6: Inheritance}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb313\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This expression returns \b true, \b0 because the two objects contain the same elements. Now\line \expndtw2 substitute = with ===, and re-evaluate the statement. This returns \b false, \b0 because,\line \expndtw-2 although the two objects contain the same elements, they are still two different objects.\line \expndtw-1 As an example of a true equivalence, evaluate the following expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex89\expndtw56\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 label}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex124\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 a \b0 := #( \b 1 2 34 \b0 ).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex150\expndtw-13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 b:=a.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex150\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 c:=b.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li428\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\scaps\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw29\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 ac \scaps0 == a.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To use the inherited = method on recursive data structures, evaluate these expressions:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li432\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw22\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 #(1 (2 \b0 (3))) - \b #(1 (2 \b0 (3)))\line \b\charscalex110\expndtw-5 #(john smith) \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  #(john smith)\line \expndtw7 #(1 'two' \b0 3) = \b #(1 'two' \b0 3)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri4\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Since the \b indexOfCollection: \b0 method defined above compares elements with the =\line \expndtw-4 message, it can be applied to nested (recursive) collections. For example, show the results\line \expndtw-2 of the following expressions:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li432\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 #((1 \b0 2)(3 \b 4)(5 \b0 6)) \b indexOfCollection: #((3 4)(5 6))\line \expndtw6 #(1 2 3 (4 5) 'abc' 6) indexOfCollection: #(3 (4 5) 'abc')\line #(1 2 3 (4 5) 'abc' 6) indexOfCollectioti: #('abc')}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb378\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 A New Class: MonitoredArray}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The final example of this chapter creates a new class to monitor the frequency of access\line \expndtw-2 to the data in an array. For instance, suppose you have an array of sales tax rates for\line \expndtw-4 California, indexed by zip code minus 90000 (California zip codes begin with 9). If you\line \expndtw-3 know how frequently each sales tax rate is looked up by zip code, you can compute the\line \expndtw-1 average sales tax paid, shipments to each region, and several other statistics.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To do this, we create class \b MonitoredArray \b0 as a subclass of class \b Array. \b0 A monitored\line array is like a normal array, except that it also maintains a parallel array containing the\line \expndtw-2 number of times the \b at: \b0 message was used for each index value. A monitored array can\line \expndtw0 be substituted for an array in any application. Like any subclass, it inherits all the\line \expndtw-2 behaviors of its superclass (arrays), and implements some special behaviors of its own.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri11\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To add the new class, select class \b Array \b0 on the Class Hierarchy Browser. You'll find it\line as a subclass of \b FixedSizeCollection, \b0 which is a subclass of \b IndexedCoilection, \b0 which\line \expndtw-3 is a subclass of \b Collection. \b0 With the cursor in the class list pane, pop up the pane menu\line and select \b add subclass. \b0 You'll then see a prompter, asking you for the \b Array Subclass}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2232\margrsxn2074\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 6: Inheritance    89}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb324\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 name; enter \b MonitoredArray. \b0 Finally, you'll see another menu, from which you should\line \expndtw2 choose the \b variableSubclass \b0 entry. The class list is then updated, with class\line \b\expndtw-5 MonitoredArray \b0 selected.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb155\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now you must specify the new class' instance variables. Proceed to the text pane below\line \expndtw0 and edit the class definition to appear as follows:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi-400\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Array variableSubclass: # MonitoredArray\line instanceVariableNames: 'atCounts'\line classVariableNames: "\line \expndtw-1 poolDictionaries: \b0 "}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Pop up the text pane menu and select the \b save \b0 entry. The class definition is updated.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb371\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Class Methods}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Class methods \i0 respond to messages sent to class objects, rather than to instances of the\line \expndtw-1 class. Class methods are often used for creating initialized instances of a class.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As an example, we'll create a class method for class \b MonitoredArray. \b0 Select class\line \b\expndtw-5 MonitoredArray \b0 using the Class Hierarchy Browser. Then select the pane labeled \b Class.\line \b0\expndtw1 This reverses the pane contents, indicating that any new methods added are class\line \expndtw-3 methods. Now pop up the method list pane menu, and select \b new method. \b0 The contents\line \expndtw-2 pane then displays a template reminding you of a new method's required components.\line \expndtw0 Type the following method into the contents pane, replacing the template:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 new: anlnteger}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li821\fi378\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Answer a new MonitoredArray."\line \expndtw-10 I answer I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 answer := super new: anlnteger.\line \expndtw-4 answer initialize.\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-13 a \scaps0\b answer}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now pop up the pane menu, and select \b save, \b0 which adds this class method to class\line \b\expndtw-1 MonitoredArray. \b0 We re-implement \b new: \b0 for class \b MonitoredArray, \b0 because the\line inherited \b new: \b0 method for arrays does not initialize the \b atCounts \b0 instance variable.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri4\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The remaining three \b MonitoredArray \b0 methods are instance methods. Select the Class\line \expndtw-1 Hierarchy Pane labeled \b Instance, \b0 and add these three instance methods one at a time:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 accessCounts}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li821\fi378\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Answer the array of 'at:' counts."\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-12 a \scaps0\b atCounts}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2189\margrsxn504\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 90    Chapter 6: Inheritance}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb324\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 at: anlnteger}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Answer the element in the receiver at\line \expndtw0 index position anlnteger. Increment\line \expndtw-1 the count for accesses to the receiver\line \expndtw1 using anlnteger."\line \expndtw-7 atCounts}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 at: anlnteger}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li821\fi374\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 put: (atCounts at: anlnteger) + 1.\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-4 a \scaps0\b super at: anlnteger}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 initialize}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1202\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Private - Initialize the MonitoredArray by}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 allocating and initializing the parallel}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li821\fi374\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 atCounts array."\line \charscalex76\expndtw10 I size I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 size : = self size.\line \expndtw0 atCounts := Array new: size.\line \expndtw-2 1 to: size do: [ -.index I atCounts at: index put: 0]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As an example of using a \b MonitoredArray, \b0 evaluate and show the results of the\line \expndtw-4 following expressions:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex76\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I array I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 array : = MonitoredArray new: 20.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li421\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 1 to: 10 do: [ :i I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi392\ri0\sb0\sa367\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 1 to: 10 do: [ :j I array at: i + j]].\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-12 a\scaps0\b array}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2189\margrsxn504\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw7621\colsr875\colno2\colw720 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 What You've Now Learned}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb209\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 After completing this tutorial, you should now be familiar with:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 the class hierarchy}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 inheritance, both of methods and of variables}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 polymorphism}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 general pattern matching}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 processing recursive data structures}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 class methods}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As always, you can review any of these topics by repeating the corresponding section of\line \expndtw-2 the tutorial, or by referring to the detailed description in \b Part 3, The Smalltalk/V 286\line \expndtw-6 Reference.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If you exit the environment before beginning the next tutorial, be sure to save the image.}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1865\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1031\fs24  \lang1033 I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0\sl-202\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 t\line \i0\lang1031  \lang1033 |}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2067\margrsxn2218\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 7 STREAMS AND COLLECTIONS}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb443\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This chapter introduces you to two of Smalltalk's most widely used hierarchies: the\line \expndtw-1 stream classes and the collection classes. At the end of this chapter, you'll see four\line interesting examples using both of these hierarchies.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As always, the examples for this tutorial are stored in a disk file, \b chapter. \b0 7. You can use\line \expndtw1 the Disk Browser to retrieve these examples, if you do not want to type them.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb140\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You will also be altering the image during this tutorial, so be sure to save the image when\line \expndtw-1 you exit the environment.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb378\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Streams}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk supports many different kinds of stream objects. You already saw one of them\line \expndtw1 when you accessed disk files using \b FileStream \b0 objects. Streams are also used for\line \expndtw-4 accessing the keyboard and mouse (class \b TerminalStream), \b0 and for accessing internal\line \expndtw-5 collections of objects, such as strings and arrays (classes \b ReadStream, WriteStream, \b0 and\line \b ReadWriteStream). \b0 The stream classes are arranged in a hierarchy with the class \b Stream\line \b0\expndtw-3 as the superclass. You can use the Class Hierarchy Browser (explained in Chapter 6) to\line explore this hierarchy.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This chapter will present a series of examples using streams, which should give you a\line \expndtw-3 good introduction. \b Part 3, The Smalltalk/V 286 Reference, \b0 gives a detailed description\line \expndtw1 of streams and all of the messages that can be used with them.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Streams are frequently used for scanning input or producing edited output. For example,\line \expndtw2 look at this method, which does both:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Replace occurrences of \i % \i0 with the date today"\line \expndtw-4 I input output char dateStamp I\line \expndtw-1 dateStamp := Date today printString.\line \expndtw-2 input := ReadStream on: "The date today is %'.\line \expndtw2 output := WriteStream on: String new.\line \expndtw-3 [input atEnd]\line \expndtw-2 whileFalse: [}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1210\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (char := input next) = \i $%}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1595\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifTrue: [output nextPutAU: dateStamp]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li428\fi1166\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifFalse: [output nextPut: char]].\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-4 a \scaps0\b output contents}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This example creates two streams. The \b on: \b0 message is sent to the class \b ReadStream \b0 to\line create a stream on the argument string \b "The date today is \b0 %'. The \b on: \b0 message is also\line \expndtw2 used to create a \b WriteStream \b0 on an empty string, to hold the edited output.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2480\margrsxn1789\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 92    Chapter 7: Streams and Collections}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri14\sb324\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As the names imply, ReadStream can only be read and \b WriteStream \b0 can only be written.\line As we have seen previously with the disk file examples, streams are read with the \b next\line \b0 message and written with the \b nextPut: \b0 message. The message \b atEnd \b0 tests if there is more\line \expndtw-3 input to be read. The message \b nextPutAll: \b0 writes several objects to a stream at once. In\line \expndtw0 the above example, the argument is a string of characters containing today's date.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri18\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The above example streams over strings of characters. It uses an empty string for the write\line \expndtw-3 stream because streams automatically grow as necessary, to accommodate the objects\line \expndtw0 written to them. The \b contents \b0 message returns a string containing all of the objects\line written to the stream.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To change the above example to use disk files instead of streams on strings, simply\line \expndtw-2 change the messages that create the streams \b input \b0 and \b output. \b0 This illustrates one of\line \expndtw-3 Smalltalk's most powerful features: you can write programs that are dependent on the\line \i\expndtw-2 behavior, \i0 rather than the \i structure, \i0 of data. This means that you can write and test a\line \expndtw-3 program using simple internal objects, such as streams on strings, and then easily extend\line \expndtw-1 it to use external files.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri25\sb155\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Streams are not restricted to reading and writing only characters. For example, this\line \expndtw-1 method reads and writes arrays of numbers:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li436\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Compute several factorials"\line \expndtw1 I input output/i I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 input := ReadStream on: #( 1 5 10 20 ).\line \expndtw-1 output : = WriteStream on: Array new.\line \expndtw-3 [input atEnd]\line \expndtw-2 whileFalse: [}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li428\fi770\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 output nextPut: input next factorial].\line \expndtw-3 Aoutput contents}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri29\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Although these examples do not show it, streams can also be repositioned, much like a\line \expndtw-5 random access file, using the \b position: \b0 message. The argument is an integer. You can also\line \expndtw0 use the \b position \b0 message to access a stream's current position.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb374\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Printer Stream}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri18\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To see how easy it is to make major enhancements to \b Smalltalk/V, \b0 let's add a new class\line \b\expndtw-5 PrinterStream. \b0 This new class will allow you to use all of the stream messages with your\line \expndtw-2 printer. Define \b PrinterStream \b0 as a subclass of \b WriteStream \b0 (which is a subclass of\line \b\expndtw-4 Stream) \b0 with the type \b subclass. \b0 It needs no instance variables, class variables, or pool\line \expndtw-3 dictionaries. (Chapter 6 explains how to make new classes using the Class Hierarchy\line \expndtw-4 Browser.) After the class \i is \i0 created, the class definition displayed in the Class Hierarchy\line \expndtw-2 Browser should be:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2323\margrsxn1964\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 7: Streams and Collections    93}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi-396\ri0\sb324\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 WriteStream subclass: # PrinterStream\line instanceVariableNames: "\line \expndtw-6 classVariableNames: "\line \expndtw0 poolDictionaries: \b0 "}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Class \b PrinterStream \b0 needs only two methods. Again, we've included these methods for\line \expndtw-1 you in a file. Evaluate the following expression to install them:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (File pathName: 'prntrst7.st') fileln}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This installs the following methods:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 nextPut: aCharacter}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li821\fi382\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Write aCharacter to the receiver."\line \expndtw-5 I string I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 string := ' '. "a string with one blank"\line \expndtw-4 string at: 1 put: aCharacter.\line \expndtw-2 string outputToPrinter.\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-16 a \scaps0\b aCharacter}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 nextPutAU: aString}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi407\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Write aString to the receiver."\line \expndtw-3 aString outputToPrinter.\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-12 a \scaps0\b aString}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri29\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Remember to use the \b update \b0 function from the class list pane menu to display the new\line methods.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 PrinterStream \b0 will inherit its other methods (and all of its variables) from the classes\line \b\expndtw-5 WriteStream \b0 and \b Stream. \b0 To create an instance of the \b PrinterStream \b0 class in the global\line \expndtw-3 variable \b Printer, \b0 evaluate the following expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Printer \b0 : = \b PrinterStream new}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb158\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As an example of using a printer stream, produce a printed report by evaluating the\line \expndtw-3 following program:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Print the first 10 even numbers and their factorials"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx522 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 1{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-3 width factorial I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 width := 20 factorial printString size.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx576 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 2{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw2 to: 20 by: 2 do: [:i I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 factorial := i factorial printString.\line \expndtw-6 Printer}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1188\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 next: 4 - i printString size put: Space;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1188\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 nextPutAll: i printString, * ';}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1188\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 next: width - factorial size put: Space;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1184\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 nextPutAll: factorial;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1181\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 cr]}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2385\margrsxn1882\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 94    Chapter 7: Streams and Collections}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri25\sb317\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If you do not have a printer, you can use the global variable \b Transcript \b0 instead of \b Printer\line \b0\expndtw-3 in the above example. This will print the report in the System Transcript window. (The\line \b\expndtw-4 Transcript \b0\i object is \i0 not a kind of stream, but it does support many of the same messages\line as streams.)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb378\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Collections}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Collections \i0 are objects which contain a collection of other objects. You have already seen\line \expndtw-1 two kinds of collections: \b Arrays \b0 and \b Strings. \b0 Strings are fixed sized sequences of\line \expndtw-4 characters, while arrays are fixed sized sequences of arbitrary objects. You have used the\line \expndtw-6 iterator messages \b do:, collect:, select:, \b0 and \b reject: \b0 with arrays and strings. These messages\line \expndtw-2 are understood by all of the collection classes, three of which are \b Dictionary, Bag, \b0 and\line \b\expndtw-10 Set}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb328\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex80\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Dictionaries}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri22\sb202\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Dictionaries \i0 store and retrieve objects by using a key. For example, let's create a simple\line \expndtw-3 phone book. First, create a global variable containing an empty dictionary by evaluating\line \expndtw-2 the following:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb18\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 PhoneBook \b0 : = \b Dictionary new}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To add phone numbers to the phone book, use the \b at:put: \b0 message:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 PhoneBook}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 at: 'Marisa' put: '645-1082';\line \expndtw-3 at: 'Franceses' put: '555-1212';\line \expndtw-4 at: 'Jackie' put: '392-481-5000';\line \expndtw-3 at: 'Rakesh' put: '645-1083';\line \expndtw-2 at: 'Vijay' put: '645-1083'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri22\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In the above expressions, the strings \b 'Marisa' \b0 and \b 'Francesca' \b0 are the keys, and the strings\line \b\expndtw-3 '645-1082' \b0 and \b '555-1212' \b0 are the corresponding values. Notice that \b at:put: \b0 is also used\line \expndtw-4 to access the elements of strings and arrays. With dictionaries, however, the first argument\line \expndtw1 is the key in the dictionary, instead of the position \i in \i0 the array or string.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri29\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To retrieve an object from a dictionary, use the at: message with the key as the argument.\line \expndtw-1 For example the following expression returns the string \b '645-1082':}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 PhoneBook at: 'Marisa'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri29\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To test if an object exists as a key in the dictionary, use the \b includesKey: \b0 message, as\line \expndtw-2 in the following expression:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2271\margrsxn2028\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 7: Streams and Collections    93}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi-392\ri0\sb324\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (PhoneBook includesKey: 'Aaron')\line \expndtw-2 ifTrue: [PhoneBook at: 'Aaron']\line \expndtw2 ifFalse: ['Not in phone book']}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb140\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A \b0 simpler way to do this is to use the \b at-.if Absent: \b0 message. The first argument is the key\line \expndtw-2 and the second argument is a block of code that will be executed if the key is not in the\line \expndtw-3 receiver dictionary. For example.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 PhoneBook at: 'Aaron' if Absent: ['Not in phone book']}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The keys and the values stored in a dictionary can be any kind of object.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Dictionaries are such useful objects \b that \b0 a special inspector window exists called the\line \i\expndtw-4 Dictionary Inspector. \i0 To open a dictionary inspector on the phone book, evaluate the\line following expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 PhoneBook inspect}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The pane on the left of the window is a sorted list of all of the keys in the dictionary,\line in our case the names of people in the phone book. When you select a key, the\line \expndtw-3 corresponding value is displayed in the pane on the right, in our case the person's phone\line \expndtw0 number. By using the pane menus, entries can be edited, added, and removed.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb328\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1031\fs26  \lang1033 Bags}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb191\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1031\fs22  \i\lang1033 Bags \i0 store an arbitrary number of objects of any kind. Unlike arrays, there is no implied\line \expndtw-2\lang1031  \lang1033 order or sequence to the elements (objects) inside the bag. Elements are added to a bag\line \expndtw-3\lang1031  \lang1033 with the \b add: \b0 message. To test if an object is in a bag, use the \b includes: \b0 message. For\line \expndtw-2\lang1031  \lang1033 example, this expression reads a file and reports the frequency with which each letter\line \expndtw-5\lang1031  \lang1033 occurs:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li428\fi1724\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-104\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex72\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1031\fs14  \lang1033 i\line \b0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\lang1031\fs22  \lang1033 I \b input answer f c I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1031\fs22  \lang1033 input := File pathName: 'chapter.7'.\line \expndtw-1\lang1031  \lang1033 answer := WriteStream on: String new.\line \expndtw9 f := Bag new.\line \expndtw-4 [input atEnd]\line \expndtw-2 whileFalse: [}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1210\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (c := input next) isLetter}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi1184\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifTrue: [f add: c asLowerCase]].\line \expndtw3 0 to: 25 do: [ :i I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 c := ($a asciiValue + i) asCharacter.\line \expndtw-7 answer}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 cr; nextPut: c; space;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li428\fi770\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 nextPutAll: (f occurrencesOf: c) printString].\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-6 a \scaps0\b answer contents}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2324\margrsxn1967\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 96    Chapter 7: Streams and Collections}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb317\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Sets}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri22\sb184\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A \b Set, \b0 like a \b Bag, \b0 stores arbitrary objects. The difference is that a \b Set \b0 does not store the\line \expndtw-4 same object more than once. For example, this expression computes the set of characters\line \expndtw2 that occur in one file and not in another:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li421\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I \b setl set2 \b0 I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 setl := Set new.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 set2 : = Set new.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (File pathName: 'chapter.7') do: [:cl setl add: c].}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (File pathName: 'chapter.6') do: [:cl set2 add: cj.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\scaps\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 a \scaps0\b setl reject: [ :c I set2 includes: c]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri18\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The message \b asSet \b0 creates a set out of the receiver collection object. This is a good way\line \expndtw-2 to eliminate duplicates from a collection. For example, to compute the unique vowels in\line \expndtw-1 a string, evaluate the following:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'Now is the time' asSet select: [ :c I c is Vowel ]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb378\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Generic Code}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You've now seen the iterator messages \b select:, reject:, \b0 and \b collect: \b0 used with strings,\line arrays, sets, and bags. These messages can be used with all of the different kinds of\line collections in \b Smalltalk/V. \b0 As such, they are excellent examples of generic code, code\line that is type and data independent. Smalltalk's ability to allow you to write generic code\line \expndtw0 sets it apart from most other languages. Here is the code in class \b Collection \b0 for the\line \b\expndtw-3 select: \b0 message that is inherited by bags, sets, dictionaries, sorted collections, ordered\line \expndtw-1 collections, and other collection classes:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li817\fi-418\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 select: aBlock\line \expndtw-10 I answer I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 answer : = self species new.\line \expndtw0 self do: [ :element I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1202\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (aBlock value: element)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li817\fi770\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifTrue: [answer add: element]].\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-12 a \scaps0\b answer}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri18\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This method assumes nothing about the structure or type of the collections with which\line it deals. It depends only on an object's behavior, the existence of the \b species, do:, \b0 and\line \b\expndtw-3 add: \b0 it sends to them. Smalltalk's polymorphism (discussed in Chapter 6) makes this\line \expndtw-5 possible.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri22\sb140\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 By exploring the \b Collection \b0 classes using the Class Hierarchy Browser, you can see many\line \expndtw0 more examples of the power of generic code.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2187\margrsxn2093\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 7: Streams and Collections    97}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb346\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Blocks as Objects}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb202\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \b select: \b0 message above \b shovfc \b0 another interesting feature of Smalltalk: the use of\line \expndtw1 blocks of code as objects. To illustrate this, look at the following invocation of the\line \b\expndtw-2 select: \b0 method.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'Now is the time' asSet select: [ :c I c is Vowel ]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri18\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The receiver of the select: message is the set of all characters in the string \b 'Now is the\line \expndtw-2 time'. \b0 The argument to the \b select: \b0 message is a block of code with one block argument,\line \b\expndtw0 [ :c \b0 I \b c isVowel ]. \b0 This block of code is as much an object as the string \b 'Now is the\line \expndtw-2 time'. \b0 As such, we can use it as an argument for the \b select: \b0 method, which you saw\line \expndtw-3 previously. When the method is invoked, the block of code is assigned to the argument\line \b\expndtw0 aBlock \b0 in the \b select: \b0 method.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri18\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A block of code executes when it is sent the message \b value, value:, \b0 or \b value:value:,\line \b0\expndtw-1 depending on whether the block has zero, one, or two block arguments, respectively.\line \expndtw-2 Since it uses one argument, the \b select: \b0 message evaluates the block \b aBlock \b0 using the\line \b\expndtw-5 value: \b0 message.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri18\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As you now know, all messages return a result. The result of evaluating a block is the\line \expndtw6 result of the last expression in the block. In the above example, the block \b [ :c \b0 I \b c\line \expndtw-1 is Vowel ] \b0 returns \b true \b0 or \b false, \b0 depending on whether or not the object passed to the\line block, c, is a vowel.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb382\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Patterns}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri18\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Block objects in turn let you build very powerful objects. For example, look at the class\line \b\expndtw-4 Pattern. \b0 Patterns are generalized and efficient pattern matchers. A pattern object consists\line \expndtw-2 of a collection of objects to match, and a block of code to execute when the pattern is\line \expndtw-4 successfully matched. For example, this expression computes the number of occurrences\line \expndtw0 of a phrase in a file:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Compute occurrences of a phrase in a file"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I pattern count input word I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 count := 0.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (pattern := Pattern new: #( 'now' 'is' 'the' ))}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi400\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 matchBlock: [count :== count + l].\line \expndtw0 input := File pathName: 'chapter.7'.\line \expndtw4 [(word := input next Word) isNil]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi371\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 while False: [pattern match: word asLowerCase].\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-10 a \scaps0\b count}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri22\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This example uses an array of strings as the pattern. Any collection of objects can be used\line \expndtw1 as the pattern, as long as it can be indexed using the \b at: \b0 message.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2365\margrsxn1923\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 98    Chapter 7: Streams and Collections}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb349\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Computing Letter Pair Frequencies}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The following example computes the frequency with which letter pairs occur in a file, and\line \expndtw-1 stores the result in the global variable, \b Pairs:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "compute letter pair frequencies"\line \expndtw-7 I last pair I\line \expndtw0 Pairs : = Bag new.\line \expndtw1 last := Space.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li810\fi-392\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (File pathName: 'chapter.7') do: [ :c I\line (last isLetter and: [c isLetter])\line \expndtw-1 ifTrue: [}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1991\fi-385\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (pair := String new: 2)\line \expndtw-3 at: 1 put: last;\line \expndtw-1 at: 2 put: c.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi806\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Pairs add: pair asLowerCase].\line \expndtw10 last \b0 := \b c]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The following expression, in turn, produces a report of the pair frequencies that occur\line more than 60 times in \b Pairs:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "print letter pair frequencies greater than 60}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 in the Transcript"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li428\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I frequent I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Transcript cr.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 frequent : = Pairs asSet select: [ -.pair I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (Pairs occurrencesOf: pair) \i > \i0 60 1.\line \expndtw-4 frequent asSortedCollection do: [:pair I\line \expndtw-6 Transcript}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 nextPutAU: pair;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tab;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1199\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 nextPutAU: (Pairs occurrencesOf: pair)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 printString;\line \expndtw-2 cr]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The message \b asSortedCollection \b0 creates a new kind of collection, a \b SortedCollection.\line \expndtw-2 SortedCollections \b0 are described in detail in \b Part 3, The Smalltalk/V 286 Reference.\line \b0\expndtw-5 Briefly, they are collections in which all of the elements are stored in sorted order. As you\line \expndtw-4 can see from the above example, they are useful for sorting a collection of objects before\line \expndtw1 outputting a report.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2125\margrsxn2152\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3236\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tx4946 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 *{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i\expndtw-1 Chapter 7: Streams and Collections    99}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb346\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Animals Revisited}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri7\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In Chapter 6, we built a simple hierarchy of animal classes. In this section, we will give\line those animals an environment (habitat) in which to live and a way to acquire knowledge\line \expndtw0 and interact with their habitat.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The habitat will have a set of animals that inhabit it. Every animal will store knowledge\line \expndtw-3 as a collection of patterns, instances of class \b Pattern. \b0 In this case a pattern is a sequence\line of words that, when recognized by the pattern, evaluates a corresponding block of code.\line \expndtw-2 This causes the animal to react to a word sequence in some prescribed way. The global\line \expndtw-3 variable \b Script \b0 contains a stream of words to send to all of the animals. Giving many\line \expndtw0 different patterns to a single animal provides that animal with a rich set of behaviors.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb349\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Animal Habitat}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri14\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Create a new class \b AnimalHabitat \b0 as a subclass of class Object, and assign to it five\line \expndtw-3 instance  variables,  \b animals,  replyStream,  animator,  inputString,  \b0 and  \b inputPane.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (Chapter 6 explains how to do this using the Class Hierarchy Browser.) When the new\line \expndtw-5 class is successfully created with the five instance variables, you should see the following\line \expndtw-1 class definition when it is selected in the Class Hierarchy Browser:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi-392\ri0\sb162\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Object subclass: # AnimalHabitat\line \expndtw-7 instanceVariableNames:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'animals replyStream animator\line \expndtw-3 inputString inputPane'\line \expndtw-8 class VariableNames: "\line \expndtw0 poolDictionaries: \b0 "}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri18\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The instance variable \b animals \b0 will contain the set of animals that inhabit the habitat. (The\line \expndtw-6 instance variables \b replyStream, animator, inputString, \b0 and \b inputPane \b0 are used in a later\line \expndtw-3 tutorial.)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb32\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now evaluate the following expression to file in the methods for the \b AnimalHabitat:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi407\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (File pathName: 'habitat7.st') fileln\line \b0\expndtw-2 Click the cursor over the \b instance \b0 label to view the methods list. The new methods are:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 add: anAnimal}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Add anAnimal as an inhabitant of the receiver.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi403\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Notify anAnimal of its new habitat."\line animals isNil}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi400\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifTrue: [animals := Set new].\line \expndtw-6 animals add: anAnimal.\line \expndtw-4 anAnimal habitat: self}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1665\margrsxn1654\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw720\colsr256\colno2\colw7614\linebetcol \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11779\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 111}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li65\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 100    Chapter 7: Streams and Collections}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi0\ri0\sb313\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 play}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li824\fi382\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Play the Script to all of the animals."\line \charscalex83\expndtw10 I word I\line \charscalex100\expndtw-5 Script reset.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li846\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 animals do: [ :animal I animal reset ].\line \expndtw-5 [Script atEnd]\line \expndtw-2 whileFalse: [}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1613\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 word :\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  Script next asLowerCase.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qc\li0\fi0\ri86\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 animals do: [ :animal I animal reactTo: word]]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li421\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 script: aString}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li842\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Change Script to the stream on the\line words in aString."\line \expndtw-6 I stream word I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li817\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 stream : = ReadStream on: aString.\line \expndtw-3 Script := ReadWriteStream on: Array new.\line \expndtw3 [(word := stream next Word) isNil]\line \expndtw-2 whileFalse: [}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1613\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Script nextPut: word]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now evaluate the following expression to create a global variable, \b Habitat, \b0 containing an\line \expndtw-2 instance of the \b AnimalHabitat \b0 class:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Habitat := AnimalHabitat new}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb360\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Animal Knowledge}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To put animals inside of the habitat, you must first add some methods to the \b Animal\line \b0\expndtw0 class. Evaluate the following expression to add the required methods:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi410\ri0\sb18\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (File pathName: 'animal7.st') rileln\line \b0\expndtw-2 The new methods in class \b Animal are:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 habitat: aHabitat}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi414\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Change habitat to aHabitat"\line \expndtw-1 habitat := aHabitat}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2543\margrsxn1770\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx-1048\posnegy3712\absw0\absh662\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw927\pich1168\picwgoal525\pichgoal662\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3064\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tx4838 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 >{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i\expndtw-1 Chapter 7: Streams and Collections    101}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb317\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 learn: aString action: aBlock}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Add a pattern of the words in aString to the}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 receivers knowledge. The action to perform}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi374\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 when the pattern is matched is aBlock."\line \expndtw-5 I words pattern I\line \expndtw4 knowledge isNil}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li785\fi407\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifTrue: [knowledge := Dictionary new],\line \expndtw-4 words := aString asLowerCase asArrayOfSubstrings.\line \expndtw0 pattern := Pattern new:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi407\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (Array with: name asLowerCase), words,\line \expndtw-4 pattern matchBlock: aBlock.\line \expndtw-1 knowledge at: words put: pattern}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 reactTo: aWord}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi400\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Send a word to every pattern in knowledge."\line \expndtw4 knowledge isNil}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifTrue: [Aself].\line \expndtw-4 knowledge do: [-.pattern I pattern match: a Word]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 reset}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi400\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Reset all patterns in knowledge"\line \expndtw4 knowledge isNil}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifTrue: [Aself].\line \expndtw-3 knowledge do: [-.pattern I pattern reset]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb356\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Using the Habitat}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb209\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 First, let's add some animals to the habitat. The following expressions use the animals\line \expndtw-9 that were created in Chapter 6:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Habitat}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 add: Snoopy;\line \expndtw-2 add: Polly}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now, set up a script to work with:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Habitat script:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li785\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'Snoopy is upset about the way that Polly is\line behaving. It is as if whenever anyone asks\line \expndtw2 Polly to talk, Polly will be nasty. Maybe if\line \expndtw1 instead of Snoopy barking at Polly when he\line wants Polly to talk, Snoopy quietly asks Polly\line \expndtw2 to be pleasant for a change, things would go\line \expndtw0 better. Now maybe Snoopy barking quietly will\line \expndtw-1 not make Polly nasty.'}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2493\margrsxn1773\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li72\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 102    Chapter 7: Streams and Collections}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li47\fi0\ri0\sb302\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Before playing the script, we need to give the animals some knowledge:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li436\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Snoopy}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li432\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 learn: 'barking' action: [Snoopy talk];\line learn: 'quietly' action: [Snoopy beQuiet; talk];\line \expndtw0 learn: 'is upset' action: [Snoopy beNoisy; talk].\line \expndtw1 Polly}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li821\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 learn: 'to be pleasant' action:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li817\fi432\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 [Polly vocabulary: 'Have a nice day'; talk];\line learn: '* nasty' action:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1616\fi-367\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 [Polly vocabulary: 'Why are you bothering me';\line \expndtw-3 talk].}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li29\fi0\ri4\sb169\sa0\sl-230\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The asterisk (*) in '* \b nasty' \b0 stands for none or more arbitrary words. To play the script\line to the animals, evaluate the following expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li421\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Habitat play}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Look in the System Transcript to see the responses from the animals.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb342\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 A Network of Nodes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As a final example of streams and collections, we will build a network of nodes, and\line \expndtw0 determine paths through the network. Many problems can be described in terms of\line \expndtw-2 networks of nodes and paths through the network, such as route maps, pert charts, and\line \expndtw-1 many kinds of optimization problems.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb320\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Network}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri32\sb212\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A \i network \i0 is a collection of nodes that are connected to each other. Create the class\line \b\expndtw-4 Network \b0 as a subclass of class \b Object, \b0 and define a single variable named \b connections.\line \b0 When you have created the class and the instance variable, the class specification in the\line \expndtw-3 Class Hierarchy Browser should be:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi-392\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Object subclass: #Network\line \expndtw-9 instance VariableNames:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi407\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'connections'\line \expndtw-10 class VariableNames: "\line \expndtw-1 poolDictionaries: \b0 "}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri50\sb155\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The instance variable \b connections \b0 will hold a dictionary of connections between nodes.\line \expndtw0 The key to the dictionary will be a node, and the value stored under that key will be a\line \expndtw-1 set of all of the nodes to which it is connected. Use the following expression to file in\line \expndtw0 the methods for class \b Network:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2302\margrsxn2007\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 7: Streams and Collections    103}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb299\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (File pathName: 'network7.st') fileln}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The methods are:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 connect: nodeA to: nodeB}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Add a connection from nodeA to nodeB."\line \expndtw-2 (connections\line \expndtw-4 at: nodeA\line \expndtw-1 if Absent: [connections at: nodeA put: Set new])}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 add: nodeB.\line \expndtw-2 (connections\line \expndtw-4 at: nodeB}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1588\fi-396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 if Absent: [connections at: nodeB put: Set new])\line \expndtw-4 add: nodeA}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb162\sa0\sl-230\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 initialize}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi403\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-230\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Initialize the connections to be empty."\line \expndtw2 connections := Dictionary new}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 pathFrom: nodeA to: nodeB avoiding: nodeSet}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li814\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Answer a path of connections that connect nodeA\line \expndtw3 to nodeB without going through the nodes in\line \expndtw-1 nodeSet. This result is returned as a new\line \expndtw1 network. Answer nil if there is no path"\line \expndtw-9 I answer I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 nodeSet add: nodeA.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1184\fi-410\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (connections at: nodeA if Absent: [Anil]) do:\line \expndtw-8 [ -.node I\line \expndtw0 node \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  nodeB\line \expndtw-2 ifTrue: [}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1843\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\scaps\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 a \scaps0 Network new initialize}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1199\fi788\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 connect: nodeA to: node].\line \expndtw-2 (nodeSet includes: node)\line \expndtw-4 ifFalse: [}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1814\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 answer := self}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1818\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 pathFrom: node\line \expndtw-1 to: nodeB\line \expndtw-2 avoiding: nodeSet.\line \expndtw1 answer isNil\line \expndtw-4 ifFalse: [}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li814\fi1735\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\scaps\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 a \scaps0 answer connect: nodeA to: node]]].\line \expndtw-7 Anil}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2199\margrsxn2085\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li40\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 104    Chapter 7: Streams and Collections}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb320\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 printOn: aStream}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Print a description of the receiver on aStream."\line \expndtw-2 connections keys asSortedCollection do: [ -.node I\line \expndtw-5 node printOn: aStream.\line \expndtw-2 (connections at: node) asSortedCollection do:\line \expndtw-5 [ -.neighbor I\line \expndtw-14 aStream\line cr;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1188\fi400\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw20\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 nextPutAU: ' \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  \\\line \expndtw-3 neighbor printOn: aStream].\line \expndtw-7 aStream cr]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Notice the recursion in the \b pathFrom:to:avoiding: \b0 message. This is a simple solution; it\line \expndtw-2 does not find the optimal (shortest) path. If you want to find such an optimal solution,\line \expndtw-3 however, you need only change this one method. This is another of \b Smalltalk/V's\line \b0 characteristics. You can quickly build program fragments to start exploring the nature of\line \expndtw0 the problem being solved. When you better understand the problem, the changes are\line \expndtw-2 quick and localized.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb317\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex83\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Network Nodes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri22\sb205\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Before using the \b Network \b0 class, define the class \b NetworkNode \b0 as a subclass of class\line \b\expndtw-5 Object, \b0 with two instance variables, \b name \b0 and \b position. \b0 After you have created the class\line \expndtw-1 and its instance variables, the class specification should be:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi-392\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Object subclass: # NetworkNode\line \expndtw-9 instance VariableNames:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi400\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'name position'\line \expndtw-9 class VariableNames: "\line \expndtw0 poolDictionaries: \b0 "}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb14\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Then use the following expression to file in the methods for class \b NetworkNode:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi403\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (File pathName: 'nodes7.st') nleln\line \b0 The methods are:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li421\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw18\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 <= \b aNode}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li814\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Answer true if the receiver name is less or\line equal to aNode name."\line \b0\scaps\expndtw4 a \scaps0\b name <= aNode name}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb162\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 hash}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li810\fi385\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Answer receiver's hash."\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-11 a \scaps0\b name hash}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2021\margrsxn2291\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 7: Streams and Collections    105}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li389\fi0\ri0\sb317\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 name}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li810\fi378\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Answer receiver's \b0 name."\line \scaps\expndtw-16 a \scaps0\b name}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 name: aString position: aPoint}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li785\fi400\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Set the receivers name and position."\line \expndtw1 name := aString.\line \expndtw3 position := aPoint}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 printOn: aStream}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li785\fi407\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Print a description of the receiver on aStream."\line \expndtw-14 aStream}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1184\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 nextPutAll: 'Node(', name;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1184\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 space;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1184\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 nextPutAll: position printString;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1184\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 nextPut: \b0 $)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb364\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Building a Network}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb198\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now evaluate the following expression to create an empty network in the global variable}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Net:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Net \b0 := \b Network new initialize}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Then evaluate these expressions, to create six nodes and connect them together into \b a\line \b0\expndtw-5 network:}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2021\margrsxn2291\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx850\posnegy115\absw5346\absh-1437\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 = \b NetworkNode new name: 'one' position: 300 @ 100.\line \expndtw3 = NetworkNode new name: 'two' position: 400 (5) 150.\line \expndtw1 = NetworkNode new name: 'three' position: 500 (5) 120.\line \expndtw2 = NetworkNode new name: 'four' position: 200 (8) 50.\line = NetworkNode new name: 'five' position: 350 (5) 195.\line \expndtw4 = NetworkNode new name: 'six' position: 550 @ 130.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li396\fi0\ri6851\sb162\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw15\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Nl\line \expndtw10 N2\line \expndtw8 N3\line \expndtw10 N4\line \expndtw6 N5\line \expndtw11 N6\line \expndtw5 Net}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 connect: Nl to: N2;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 connect: N2 to: N3;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 connect: N4 to: N5;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 connect: N5 to: Nl;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 connect: N3 to: N6;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 connect: N3 to: N5;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 connect: N3 to: Nl}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You can ask the network to print itself by evaluating the following expression using\line \b\expndtw-2 show it:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Net}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2115\margrsxn2176\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li40\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 106    Chapter 7: Streams and Collections}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb306\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now evaluate the following expression and show the results, to find a path from \b Nl \b0 to}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 N5:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Net pathFrom: Nl to: N5 avoiding: Set new}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi-396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To see if there is a path that does not go through N3, evaluate the following expression:\line \b\expndtw4 Net pathFrom: Nl to: N5 avoiding: (Set with: \b0 N3)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb331\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 What YouVe Now Learned}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb205\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 After having completed this tutorial, you should be familiar with:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 streams, including \b PrinterStream}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 collections, including Dictionaries, Bags, arid Sets}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 generic code}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As always, you can review any of these topics by repeating the corresponding section of\line \expndtw-2 the tutorial, or by referring to a detailed description in \b Part 3, The Smalltalk/V 286\line \expndtw-5 Reference.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If you exit the environment before beginning the next tutorial, be sure to save the image.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1916\margrsxn2353\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 8 DEBUGGING}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri14\sb454\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This chapter uses what you learned in Chapter 7 to build a complete program using\line \expndtw-3 collections and streams. The program, however, purposely contains several errors. This\line \expndtw1 chapter, then, shows you how to locate and correct errors using the \b Smalltalk/V\line \b0\expndtw-3 debugger.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri18\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As always, the examples for this chapter are stored in the disk file, chapter.8. You can\line use the Disk Browser to retrieve these examples.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri22\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Since you will again be making modifications to your \b Smalltalk/V \b0 environment, be sure\line \expndtw1 to save the image when you exit \b Smalltalk/V.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb364\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 A Document Retrieval System}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The first thing we'll do is implement a new class, \b Wordlndex, \b0 which allows you to create\line \expndtw0 a database of documents and locate them based upon the words that they contain.\line \i\expndtw-4 Documents \i0 are ASCII text files, viewed as a series of words containing alphanumeric\line \expndtw-3 characters separated by a series of non-alphanumeric characters. You query the database\line by supplying a collection of word strings, which returns a collection of the file names of\line all the documents that contain all the words. You could use the word index, for example,\line \expndtw-5 to locate resumes in a personnel system, such as all employees whose resumes contain the\line \expndtw3 words C and Unix.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Instances of class \b Wordlndex \b0 have instance variables \b documents \b0 and \b words.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri22\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 documents \b0 is a set of strings of the document file path names whose words have been\line \expndtw1 entered into the word index.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri22\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 words \b0 is a dictionary, with each key containing a string for a word and each value being\line \expndtw1 a set containing the path names of all documents containing the word.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Therefore, the class definition is:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi-389\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Object subclass: # Wordlndex\line \expndtw-6 instanceVariableNames:\line \expndtw-3 'documents words'\line \expndtw-8 class VariableNames: "\line \expndtw-2 poolDictionaries: "}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri29\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Add \b Wordlndex \b0 class definition and methods to your \b Smalltalk/V \b0 image by evaluating\line \expndtw-2 the following expression:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2313\margrsxn2003\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 108    Chapter 8: Debugging}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb295\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (File pathName: \super f\nosupersub wrdindx8.st') nleln}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now select update in the Class Hierarchy Browser's class list so that you can browse the\line \expndtw-5 methods of class Wordlndex. There are six methods defined for class Wordlndex, as\line \expndtw-9 follows:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li389\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 addDocument: pathName}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1192\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Add all words in document described by}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi374\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 pathName string to the words dictionary."\line I word wordStreaml\line \expndtw-2 (documents includes: pathName)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li778\fi403\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifTrue: [self removeDocument: pathName].\line \expndtw-1 wordStream := File pathName: pathName.\line \expndtw-3 documents add: pathName.\line \expndtw6 [(word := wordStream next Word) == nil]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1177\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 whileFalse: [}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li778\fi799\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 self add Word: word asLowerCase to: pathName].\line wordStream close}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li382\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 addWord: wordString for: pathName}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Add wordString to words dictionary for\line \expndtw-2 document described by pathName."\line \expndtw-1 (words at: wordString) add: pathName}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li382\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 initialize}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li774\fi407\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Initialize a new empty Wordlndex."\line \expndtw2 documents := Set new.\line \expndtw4 words := Dictionary new}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li378\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 locateDocuments: queryWords}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li774\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Answer an array of the pathNames for\line \expndtw0 all documents which contain all words\line \expndtw-1 in queryWords."\line \expndtw-5 I answer bag I\line \expndtw7 bag := Bag new.\line \expndtw2 answer := Set new.\line \expndtw1 queryWords do: [ :word I\line \expndtw-4 bag addAll:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li774\fi410\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (documents at: word if Absent: [#()])].\line \expndtw-3 bag asSet do: [ -.document I\line \expndtw4 queryWords size =}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1577\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (bag occurrencesOf: document)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi1170\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifTrue: [answer add: document]].\line \scaps\expndtw-8 a \scaps0 answer asSortedCollection as Array}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2045\margrsxn2247\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 8: Debugging    109}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb317\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 removeDocument: pathName}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1202\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Remove pathName string describing a\line \expndtw-1 document from the words dictionary."}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 words do: [ :docs I docs remove: pathName].}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 self removeUnusedWords}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 removeUnusedWords}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li828\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Remove all words which have empty\line \expndtw-1 document collection."\line \expndtw-7 I new Words I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 new Words := Dictionary new.\line \expndtw-3 words associationsDo: [ :anAssoc I\line \expndtw-4 anAssoc value isEmpty}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi796\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifFalse: [newWords add: anAssoc]].\line \expndtw2 words := new Words}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb382\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 How Class Wordlndex Works}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri7\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Next, we'll describe class \b Wordlndex \b0 in terms of the high-level messages which create\line \expndtw-1 an index and make queries.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We mentioned earlier that we've included some intentional errors; this is the first place\line \expndtw-5 where they occur. For this reason, don't evaluate these messages until the tutorial tells you\line \expndtw4 to do so.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri7\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We'll construct and use the word index in three steps. First, we create an empty word\line \expndtw-2 index in an expression such as the following (remember, don't evaluate this expression\line \expndtw-12 yet):}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Index := Wordlndex new initialize}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri22\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \b initialize \b0 method \i initializes \i0 instance variables of the \b Wordlndex; \b0 that is, \b docu-\line \expndtw0 ments \b0 now contains an empty set and \b words \b0 contains an empty dictionary.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri11\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Next, we'll add the words from documents to the \b Wordlndex. \b0 The \b addDocument:\line \b0 method creates a file stream to scan the document, repeatedly sends the \b nextWord\line \b0\expndtw-3 message to the file stream to obtain each word, and then uses the \b addWord:for: \b0 method\line \expndtw1 to enter each word/document pair in the \b words \b0 dictionary. For example, to add the\line \expndtw-4 words from the Chapters 5 and 6 sample files, you would use the following expressions\line \expndtw-2 (again, don't evaluate these yet):}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Index addDocument: 'chapter.5'.\line Index addDocument: 'chapter.6'.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri14\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To query the word index, you use the \b locateDocuments: \b0 message, as in the following\line \expndtw-2 examples (again, do not evaluate them):}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2314\margrsxn1966\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li54\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw21\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 110\expndtw0     Chapter 8: Debugging}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi0\ri0\sb313\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Index locateDocuments: #('show' 'class')\line \expndtw1 Index locateDocuments: #('where' 'the' 'turtle')\line Index locateDocuments: #('each' 'talk')}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Each query above returns an array of strings, containing the document path names for all\line \expndtw1 documents that contain all words in the query. The \b locateDocuments: \b0 method is\line \expndtw-3 somewhat more complex than the other methods in its class. It uses \b a \b0 bag to accumulate\line \expndtw-2 all the path names of all the files that contain each word in the query. (Remember that\line \expndtw-3 bags, unlike sets, can contain multiple occurrences of the same object.) It then examines\line \expndtw-1 the bag to find any documents which are repeated as many times as there are words in\line \expndtw0 the query; these are the documents which contain all the words.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb382\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Debugging Class Wordlndex}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri7\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now that you've seen how this class is supposed to work, let's see if it does. (From this\line \expndtw-4 point, start evaluating the sample expressions again.) First, build a new word index and\line \expndtw0 assign it to the global variable \b Index:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Index := Wordlndex new initialize}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri14\sb162\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now try adding the tutorial files for Chapters 5 and 6 by evaluating the following\line \b\expndtw-5 addDocuments: \b0 messages:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Index addDocument: 'chapter.5*.\line \expndtw-3 Index addDocument: 'chapter\b0 .6'.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Oops! Instead of adding the tutorial files, we get a \b walkback \b0 window:}\par \pard \plain \li2398\ri558\sb691\sa0{\pict\picw8242\pich1943\picwgoal4672\pichgoal1101\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3650\fi122\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs14 irdlndex(0bject)\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 error:\line \expndtw-2 UordIndex(Object)\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 doesNotUnderstand:\line \expndtw-7 UordIndex>>addDocuaent:\line \expndtw1 Undef inedObject\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 Soit}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li389\fi0\ri0\sb1379\sa0\sl-220\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 8.1\line \expndtw-2 Walkback Window}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1965\margrsxn2333\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex68\expndtw16\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Chapter8: Debugging    \expndtw42 111}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb324\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As you saw in Chapter 2, a walkback window describes an error condition. The label\line \expndtw-4 shows the error condition, and the text pane shows the most recently sent messages, with\line \expndtw-1 those most recently sent appearing first.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In the above walkback window, the label says that the \b addWord:to: \b0 message is not\line \expndtw0 understood, while the top line in the text pane shows \b Wordlndex \b0 as the class of the\line \expndtw1 object which did not understand the message.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Whenever you get a walkback window, you generally do one of three things:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li641\fi-230\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx641 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li641\fi-230\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx641 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent230\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You can determine what the problem is from the information contained in\line \expndtw-4 the walkback window. In this case, you normally close the walkback window\line \expndtw1 and then go fix the problem.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li641\fi-230\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx641 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li641\fi-230\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx641 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent230\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You can determine that the walkback window occurred either as a result of\line \expndtw2 you typing the control and break keys simultaneously, or because a \b halt\line \b0\expndtw-2 message was sent. In this case, there is nothing wrong with the program, so\line \expndtw-3 you can pop up the pane menu for the walkback window and select \b resume.\line \b0\expndtw-1 The walkback window closes and execution continues.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li641\fi-230\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx641 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li641\fi-230\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx641 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent230\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You can decide that you need more information, and would like to use the\line \i\expndtw-5 debuggerto \i0 obtain it. In this case, you pop up the pane menu for the walkback\line \expndtw-1 window and select \b debug. \b0 The walkback window closes and the debugger\line \expndtw-2 window opens.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In our case, we probably have enough information in the walkback window to fix the\line \expndtw-4 problem. Look at the code for class \b Wordlndex \b0 using the Class Hierarchy Browser. We\line \expndtw-2 defined a method addWord:fbr:, but sent the message \b addWord:to: \b0 (in the \b addDocu-\line \expndtw0 ment: \b0 method) which was not understood. We used the wrong message!}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Correct the addDocument: method to use \b addWord:for: \b0 instead of \b addWord:to, \b0 and\line \expndtw-3 then try again to add the tutorial files to the word index, using the following expressions.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Index addDocument: 'chapter.5'.\line \expndtw-3 Index addDocument: 'chapter.\b0 6\\}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Not fixed yet! This time, you get a new walkback window:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2374\margrsxn1924\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li50\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 112     Chapter 8: Debugging}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2743\fi0\ri0\sb428\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 Saalltalk/V Transcript}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi2668\ri0\sb169\sa0\sl-126\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 C rmex \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'ab\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 (WBocu\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 ent:   'chapter\line \expndtw1 tmtex AdiOtocttwent:    'chantnr}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3654\fi86\ri0\sb389\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex108\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 i ct i onary( ObJ ect) > > error:\line \expndtw2 D ict ionary\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 errorAbsentKey\line \expndtw3 Dictionartj>>at:\line \expndtw1 UordI ndex\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 addllord: for:\line \expndtw-11 Uor d I ndex> > addDocument '\dbch\af3\loch\af1\hich\f1\cchs0 \u9632\'3f\line \expndtw1\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 Undef inedObj ect\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 Do it}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li367\fi0\ri0\sb896\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 8.2\line \expndtw-2 Opening\line the Debugger}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2374\margrsxn1924\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb389\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx5778 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The label of the walkback window says \b Key is missing. \b0 Since the problem is not\line \expndtw-1 obvious, let's see if we can \i get \i0 some more information by using the debugger. Pop up\line \expndtw-3 the walkback window pane menu and select \b debug. \b0 You'll see the following debugger\line \expndtw-9 window:{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\sub\expndtw0 h}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx3442\posnegy1332\absw1714\absh-270\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs14 D ict ionaryl Obj ect) \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 error:\line \expndtw-4 Diet ionary>>errorAbsentXey}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-137\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx3438\posnegy1631\absw1498\absh-382\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs14 UordI ndex\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 addllord: for:\line \expndtw-6 UordIndex\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 addDocuiaent:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1127\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx3438\posnegy1631\absw1498\absh-382\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex164\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs8 Knit}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2354\shptop72\shpright6947\shpbottom2384\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr5\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 75}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn pib}{\sv {\pict\picw8102\pich4076\picwgoal4593\pichgoal2311\jpegblip
}}}{\sp{\sn pictureGray}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn pWrapPolygonVertices}{\sv 8;21;(0,0);(0,1143);(0,1143);(0,1413);(0,1413);(0,3095);(0,3095);(0,20590);(0,20590);(0,21600);(5044,21600);(5044,20590);(21600,20590);(21600,3095);(18112,3095);(18112,1413);(17266,1413);(17266,1143);(17131,1143);(17131,0);(0,0)}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fIsBullet}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fAllowOverlap}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fEditedWrap}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fBehindDocument}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fPrint}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistLeft}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistRight}{\sv 0}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li6350\fi-295\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-194\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 skip\line \expndtw-10 jump}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3438\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-130\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 at: aKey}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3722\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-130\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 "Answer the value of the key/value pair whost\line \expndtw-8 equals aXey froai the receiver dictionary,\line \expndtw-6 not found\i , \i0 report an error."\line \expndtw-10 ! answer !}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3989\fi-281\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-130\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 ^(answer := \ul self loohUpKey: aKey\ul0 ) == nil\line \expndtw-12\fs16 if True: [ggg^^^Q^QQg|}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4558\shptop-32\shpright5832\shpbottom-32\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 29845}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3989\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 ifFalse: [answer value]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li382\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 8.3\line \expndtw-3 Debugger with\line \expndtw-6 Window Buttons}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri11\sb140\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The debugger window gives you an expanded view of the walkback in several panes. The\line \expndtw-1 top left pane (a list pane) repeats the walkback information; you can use this pane to\line \expndtw-4 select walkback lines. When you select a walkback line, the other panes contain related\line \expndtw0 information. Select the entry containing Dictionary > >at:.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri25\sb151\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The bottom pane displays the source code for the selected method, in this case \b at: \b0 from\line \expndtw-4 class \b Dictionary:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2003\margrsxn2295\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex70\expndtw13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Chapter 8: Debugging    \expndtw35 113}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb313\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 at: aKey}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li817\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I answer I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li817\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\scaps\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 a \scaps0\b (answer := self lookUpKey: aKey) == nil}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1192\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifTrue: [self errorAbsentKey]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1192\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifFalse: [answer value]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The text that is reversed is the expression currently being evaluated in this method.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As you can see, this method invokes another dictionary method \b lookUpKey:, \b0 and then\line \expndtw-4 invokes \b errorAbsentKey \b0 if the key is missing, which eventually results in the walkback\line \expndtw-6 window.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The two panes on the top right are an inspector for the receiver, arguments and temporary\line \expndtw-5 variables of the selected method. In this case, you see the receiver \b self, \b0 the argument \b aKey\line \b0 and the temporary \b answer. \b0 Select \b self; \b0 you see that the value is an empty dictionary. Now\line \expndtw-3 select \b aKey; \b0 the value is the string \b 'tutorial', \b0 the first word in the file. We tried to do a\line \expndtw0 dictionary lookup on an empty dictionary, \b self, \b0 with the first word in the file as key.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Select the line containing \b addWord:for: \b0 in the walkback pane on the top left of the\line \expndtw-4 window. Now select the argument \b wordString. \b0 Again, it's the string \b 'tutorial*. \b0 We tried\line \expndtw0 to access the \b words \b0 dictionary with a key, without first testing whether or not the key\line is present! Correct the \b addWord:for: \b0 method in the bottom pane of the debugger to\line \expndtw-2 look as follows:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 addWord: wordString for: pathName}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1199\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Add wordString to words dictionary for}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi385\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 document described by pathName."\line \expndtw-1 (words includesKey: wordString)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi385\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifFalse: [words at: wordString put: Set new],\line \expndtw-1 (words at: wordString) add: pathName}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now pop up the bottom pane menu and select save. Notice what happens. The entries\line \expndtw-3 above \b addWord:for: \b0 in the walkback list are discarded, because a method they would\line return to has been changed. The \b addWord:for: \b0 method is still selected. Now pop up the\line \expndtw-5 menu in the walkback list pane and select \b restart. \b0 Execution resumes by re-sending the\line \expndtw-4 selected message.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The debugger window disappears, and the method builds the index. With the dictionary\line \expndtw0 now built, let's try to make some queries. Try evaluating the expression below.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Index locateDocuments: #('show' 'class')}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Another walkback window pops up, indicating that there is another error. Immediately\line \expndtw1 open a debugger window on this new error. You'll see the following window:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1255\margrsxn1921\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1177\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 114    Chapter 8: Debugging}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn5964\margrsxn2223\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-1325\shptop990\shpright-1325\shpbottom2020\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft3823\shptop1206\shpright3823\shpbottom3733\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 15875}}}}{\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li137\fi-137\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx-396\posnegy1793\absw1764\absh-270\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 ray(IndexedCol lection)\loch\af16\hich\af16\dbch\f17\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af16\dbch\af17\loch\f16\cchs0 do:\line \expndtw-10 I ndex\loch\af16\hich\af16\dbch\f17\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af16\dbch\af17\loch\f16\cchs0  locateDocuaents:}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-1274\shptop544\shpright3824\shpbottom2466\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr5\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 75}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn pib}{\sv {\pict\picw8991\pich3390\picwgoal5097\pichgoal1922\jpegblip
}}}{\sp{\sn pictureGray}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn pWrapPolygonVertices}{\sv 8;17;(0,0);(0,20062);(0,20062);(0,20143);(0,20143);(0,21438);(0,21438);(0,21600);(5155,21600);(5155,21438);(7932,21438);(7932,20143);(8267,20143);(8267,20062);(21600,20062);(21600,0);(0,0)}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fIsBullet}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fAllowOverlap}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fEditedWrap}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fBehindDocument}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fPrint}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistLeft}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistRight}{\sv 0}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li731\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-130\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 alienage}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li68\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-130\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 "Initiate a walkback because a Message Ma* sent which is\line \ul\expndtw-6 not understood, i.e.. there is no Matching Method."}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1255\margrsxn1921\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1490\fi0\ri6354\sb522\sa220\sl-216\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 8.4\line \expndtw-4 Inspecting\line \expndtw-6 Variables}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1255\margrsxn1921\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw720\colsr428\colno2\colw7585\linebetcol \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb1958\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex181\expndtw-27\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 i i}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The message \b at:if Absent: \b0 was sent to an instance of class \b Set, \b0 which did not understand\line \expndtw-1 it. Select the top walkback line containing \b Set(Object)>> doesNotUnderstand:, \b0 and\line then select \b self \b0 in the temporary variable list. The value is:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Setfchapter.6' 'chapter.5')}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now select the third walkback line, representing a block in the \b locateDocuments:\line \b0\expndtw-4 method, and examine the values of the temporary variables. Then look at the source code\line for the method. The \b at:ifAbsent: \b0 message being executed is reversed. It uses instance\line \expndtw-3 variable \b documents \b0 as receiver. The value printed out for the set above confirms this,\line \expndtw0 because it does contain the document path names.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Let's look at this statement. Either we sent the wrong message to \b documents \b0 or\line \b\expndtw-3 documents \b0 is the wrong receiver. This statement is trying to add to variable \b bag \b0 all the\line \expndtw-2 documents that include the string contained in variable \b word. \b0 The receiver is indeed\line \expndtw-1 wrong. This statement should instead use the \b words \b0 dictionary:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 bag addAll:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (words at: word if Absent: \b0 [#()])}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb137\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Change the \b locateDocuments: \b0 method using the debugger, save it, and restart at\line \b\expndtw-2 locateDocuments:. \b0 It works! Try the following queries:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Index locateDocuments: #('where' 'the' 'turtle')\line \expndtw1 Index locateDocuments: #('each' 'talk')}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1981\margrsxn2314\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri11\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex69\expndtw13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Chapter 8: Debugging    \expndtw35 115}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb349\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Hop, Skip and Jump}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now that you have class \b Wordlndex \b0 debugged, let's see how you can use the debugger\line to learn how an application operates by watching it send messages. Open a debugger\line \expndtw-3 window to step through execution of the query you just performed by evaluating the\line \expndtw-4 following expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 self halt.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa497 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Index \b0 locateDocuments: #(each talk)}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2320\margrsxn4114\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1198\colsr1188\colno2\colw3088 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2329\shptop2261\shpright2329\shpbottom3211\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 15875}}}}{\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft1998\shptop0\shpright7247\shpbottom1829\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr5\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 75}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn pib}{\sv {\pict\picw9258\pich3225\picwgoal5248\pichgoal1828\jpegblip
}}}{\sp{\sn pictureGray}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn pWrapPolygonVertices}{\sv 8;13;(0,0);(0,20409);(0,20409);(0,20664);(0,20664);(0,21600);(1540,21600);(1540,20664);(21511,20664);(21511,20409);(21600,20409);(21600,0);(0,0)}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fIsBullet}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fAllowOverlap}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fEditedWrap}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fBehindDocument}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fPrint}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistLeft}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistRight}{\sv 0}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb2650\sa0\sl-220\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 8.5\line \expndtw-4 Debugging\line an Expression}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-130\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs10 locateDocuments: queryUords}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li248\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-130\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs10 "Answer an array of the pathNames for\line \expndtw5 all documents which contain \expndtw20 all\expndtw5  words\line \expndtw4 in queryUords."\line ! answer bag !\line \expndtw-9 bag : \loch\af16\hich\af16\dbch\f17\cchs0 \'97\hich\af16\dbch\af17\loch\f16\cchs0  f^^yVA^^ff.\line \expndtw5 answer := Set new.\line queryUords do: [ :word !\line \expndtw4 bag addAll:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li248\fi540\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-130\slmult0\tlul\tx3089 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs10 (words at: word ifAbsent: [*()])].\line \ul\expndtw-11 hair asRftt \i An'. I \i0 : rtnniimfsnt. !{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1981\margrsxn2314\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb104\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There are 6 buttons on the right side of a debugger window label bar as seen in Figure\line \expndtw-2 8.3. The first three called hop, \b skip \b0 and \b jump \b0 are related to debugging. \b Hop, skip \b0 and\line \b\expndtw1 jump \b0 each cause limited program execution. Hop executes the least amount: one\line \expndtw-4 Smalltalk message send or assignment statement. Skip executes more than hop: up to the\line \expndtw-2 next message send or assignment in the current method or up to the next breakpoint,\line whichever comes first. (Refer to Chapter 16 for a description of breakpoints). \b Jump\line \b0\expndtw4 executes more than skip: up to the next breakpoint or the end of the debugged\line \expndtw-5 expression.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Try selecting the \b hop \b0 button twice and watch the debugger window. Execution state is\line now at the beginning of execution of the expression, shown in Figure 8.5. Select \b hop\line \b0 again. Notice how execution proceeds in small amounts, with the next statement to be\line \expndtw-3 executed highlighted after the step. You can examine the state of objects after each \b hop.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now try selecting the \b skip \b0 button a few times. Notice that the highlighting stays within\line the same method until the method finishes execution. This allows you to concentrate on\line \expndtw-1 a single method activation and ignore lower level messages.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2399\margrsxn1918\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 116    Chapter 8: Debugging}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb317\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 What YouVe Now Learned}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 By the end of this tutorial, you should be familiar with walkback windows and how to\line \expndtw-4 use the debugger. If you want to review, you can either repeat the tutorial, or refer to the\line \expndtw0 detailed description in \b Part 3, The Smalltalk/V 286 Reference.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As always, if you exit \b Smalltalk/V \b0 before beginning the next tutorial, be sure to save the\line \expndtw-8 image.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1989\margrsxn2274\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 9 GRAPHICS}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri32\sb446\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In these tutorials, you have seen some of Smalltalk/V's remarkable graphics. In this\line \expndtw0 tutorial, you will learn how \b Smalltalk/V \b0 produces such graphics.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri32\sb140\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The examples in this chapter repeatedly alter the top half of the screen. For this reason,\line \expndtw-8 you will want to open any windows \i in the bottom half of the screen only. \i0 After each example,\line \expndtw0 you can restore the screen to its previous state by selecting \b redraw screen \b0 from the\line \expndtw-4 system menu. As always, you can find the sample code for this tutorial in the disk file\line \expndtw-2 chapter.9. Use the Disk Browser to retrieve these examples from this file. Since this file\line \expndtw-3 is larger than 10,000 bytes, only the beginning and end of it can be seen in the text pane.\line \expndtw1 Pop up the text pane menu and select \b read it \b0 to make the entire file accessible.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri40\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You will again be adding new methods and classes to the environment in this tutorial,\line \expndtw1 so be sure to save the image when you exit \b Smalltalk/V.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb371\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Some Basic Concepts}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V \b0 owes its graphical capability to \i bit-mapped \i0 graphics (also called \i raster\line \i0\expndtw-3 graphics). A line is drawn with a continuous vector of dots. A cursor is formed with a\line \expndtw1 rectangle of black and white dots. Even a character is formed with a block of dots,\line \expndtw-2 instead of an ASCII value.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri36\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 These dots are displayed on a monitor screen as colored pixels. They are stored internally\line \expndtw-6 as a \i Bitmap, \i0 contained in a \i Form. \i0 A Bitmap is a matrix of bits, with a value 1 representing\line \expndtw-2 white and 0 representing black. To refer to an individual dot within a Bitmap, you use\line \i\expndtw-5 Points. \i0 To move a group of dots from one place to another (either within the same Bitmap\line \expndtw-2 or between different Bitmaps), you use \i Rectangles \i0 to denote the areas involved. Thus\line \b\expndtw0 Point, Rectangle, \b0 and \b Form \b0 are \b Smalltalk/V's \b0 basic graphic data structures.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb353\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Point}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri43\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A Point refers to a position within a two dimensional array. It has two instance variables:\line \expndtw1 x, the column coordinate, and y, the row coordinate. To create a \b Point, \b0 you use the\line \expndtw-2 binary message @. For example, the expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li896\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 10}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 creates a \b Point \b0 referencing column 5 and row 10. Evaluate the following expressions:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (5 @ 10) x\line (5 @ 10) y}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1420\margrsxn1889\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-324\shptop3402\shpright-324\shpbottom8320\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 31750}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-209\shptop5965\shpright-209\shpbottom6260\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-115\shptop6944\shpright-115\shpbottom8816\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1019\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw14\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 118\expndtw0     \expndtw-3 Graphics}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li990\fi0\ri0\sb317\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 These expressions return the values 5 and 10, respectively. You can also add, subtract,\line \expndtw-1 multiply, divide, or compare Points, as in these examples:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1390\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw52\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (l@2)+(-l@-2)\line \charscalex140\expndtw12 (1@2)-(1@2)\line \expndtw8 (l@2)*(l/2@(l/2))\line \expndtw1 (1@2) <(3@4)\line \expndtw-21 (3 @ 5) > (3 @ 4)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li990\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 These expressions combine or compare \b x \b0 of the receiver with \b x \b0 of the argument and y\line \expndtw-3 of the receiver with y of the argument. This is why the last expression returns \b false; \b0 the\line \expndtw1 first x, 3, is not greater than the second, also 3.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li986\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You can mix a Point with a scalar:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1404\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 1 (3) 2 + -1\line \b\expndtw15 1 \i @ 2 II \i0 2}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li986\fi0\ri4\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 which applies the scalar to both \b x \b0 and y of the Point. You cannot, however, compare\line point coordinates and a scalar. For example, try evaluating this expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1400\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw16\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 1 @ 2 < 3}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li983\fi0\ri7\sb140\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To alter one of the two coordinates, simply use the messages x: and y:. For example,\line \expndtw-1 evaluate the following expressions as a group:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1400\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I \b aPoint I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1375 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 aPoint := (5 @10).\line \expndtw0 aPoint x: 1.\line \sub\expndtw-27 ;;{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\nosupersub\expndtw1 aPoint \b0 y: \b 2.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li990\fi0\ri0\sb562\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex83\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Rectangle}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li983\fi0\ri7\sb202\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A \b0\i Rectangle \i0 is represented by two points: an \i origin \i0 (the top left point) and a \i corner \i0 (the\line \expndtw-3 bottom right point). With this information, Smalltalk can determine its extent (the width\line \expndtw0 and height of the elements contained within the rectangle) as:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1375\fi0\ri0\sb22\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 corner - origin}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li976\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To create a Rectangle, you normally send messages to a point, as in this example:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li979\fi421\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 1 @ 1 \b corner: \b0 100 @ 100\line \expndtw-4 Or equivalently:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1393\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 1 \b0\i (Sb \i0\b 1 extent: 99 \b0 @ 99}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2039\margrsxn2235\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri22\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex70\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Graphics    \expndtw30 119}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb324\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A Rectangle includes the bits \i inside \i0 of the rectangle. The rectangle itself is imposed on\line \expndtw0 gaps between bits. For example, the Rectangle}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 1(5)1 \b corner: \b0 4 @ 3}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 contains 6 bits (3 horizontal and 2 vertical) as illustrated below:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2480\fi0\ri0\sb1505\sa655 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 rectangle}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2432\margrsxn6270\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw903\colsr1584\colno2\colw720 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft5407\shptop-259\shpright6861\shpbottom-259\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 8890}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 9-1\line \expndtw-2 Pixels and\line \expndtw-8 Rectangles}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb331\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 pixel}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2039\margrsxn2235\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb914\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There are many operations you can perform on Rectangles. For example, try evaluating\line \expndtw0 each of the following:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (0 @ \b 0 extent: 100 @ 100) center\line (0(5)0 extent: 100 @ 100) insetBy: 10\line \expndtw7 (-5 \i @ \i0 -10 extent: 20 \i @ \i0 20)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi385\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 intersect: (1 @ 2 extent: 20 \i @ \i0 20)\line \expndtw6 (-5 \i @ \i0 -10 extent: 20 \i @ \i0 20)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 containsPoint: 0 (8) 0}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 These operations are not the focus of this tutorial, however; refer to \b Part 3, The\line \expndtw0 Smalltalk/V 286 Reference, \b0 for more detailed descriptions.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb342\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Form}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As we said before, a Point or a Rectangle simply refers to a position or an area of\line \expndtw-2 positions. The object that actually holds the graphical image is the \i Form. \i0 A Form has\line \expndtw0 many instance variables, but only three concern you: \b bits, width, \b0 and \b height.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2261\margrsxn2041\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li40\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 120    Graphics}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb317\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex107\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The variable \b bits \b0 contains a Bitmap, which is the content of the Form. This Bitmap\line \expndtw2 contains bits for the area represented by a Rectangle:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb14\sa0\sl-396\slmult0\tx594 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex107\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 0{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\charscalex114\expndtw1 @ 0 \b extent: (width @ height)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi-392\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex107\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 So, for example, to create a Form containing all 1 bits, evaluate the following expression:\line \b\expndtw6 F \b0 := \b Form width: 100 height: 50}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex107\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 To display a Form, use the message \b displayAt: \b0 with a point for the argument. For\line example, to display the rectangle created above, you would use:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex107\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 F displayAt: 0 (8) 0}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex107\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 This displays a white rectangle at the top left corner of the screen. You can also display\line \expndtw2 only a portion of a Form. For example, evaluate the following expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li421\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx533 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex107\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 1{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b black white aRect I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex107\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 white : = Form width: Display width height: Display height,\line black := (Form width: Display width height: Display height)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex107\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 reverse.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex107\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 aRect := Display boundingBox. "screen rectangle"\line \expndtw9 Display height // 2 // 8 timesRepeat: [}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex107\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 black displayAt: 0(2)0 clippingBox: aRect.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex107\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 aRect := aRect insetBy: 4.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex107\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 white displayAt: 0 (3) 0 clippingBox: aRect.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi392\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex107\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 aRect := aRect insetBy: 4J.\line \expndtw0 Menu message: 'continue'.\line \expndtw-3 Scheduler systemDispatcher redraw}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex107\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 The message \b displayAtxlippingBox: \b0 restricts the area on the screen to be changed.\line (You'll learn more about clipping boxes later in this tutorial.) The last two lines pop up\line \expndtw3 a menu and then redraw the screen.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex107\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 You can also display a Form on a printer by evaluating the following expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi-385\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex107\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 (Form new width: 100 height: 100 initialByte: l6rF0)\line \expndtw0 outputToPrinter}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri11\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex107\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Class \b Form \b0 has three immediate subclasses: \b BiColorForm, ColorForm, \b0 and\line \b\expndtw-3 DisplayScreen.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex107\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 A BiColorForm \b0 allows a foreground color to be assigned to the 1 pixels, and a\line \expndtw-1 background color to the 0 pixels in the bitmap. This allows a single bitmap to represent\line \expndtw4 two colors other than just black and white.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex107\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 A \b ColorForm \b0 has an array of bitmaps. The bits in the same position of each bitmap\line \expndtw2 collectively represent the color of the pixel at that position.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1969\margrsxn2326\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri14\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Graphics    121}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri14\sb328\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Class \b DisplayScreen \b0 and its subclass \b ColorScreen \b0 represents a monochrome screen and\line \expndtw-5 a color screen respectively. Their bitmaps have fixed physical addresses, as well as fixed\line \expndtw1 sizes, dictated by the mode of the graphics adapter being used. A global variable,\line \b\expndtw-3 Display, \b0 contains an instance of either \b ColorScreen \b0 or \b DisplayScreen \b0 depending on\line \expndtw1 whether or not your monitor and graphics adaptor supports color.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb367\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 The Basic Class of Graphics: BitBIt}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri18\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 BitBlt \b0 ("bit block transfer") is the fundamental class of all \b Smalltalk/V \b0 graphics. Classes\line like \b Pen \b0 (for drawing) and \b CharacterScanner \b0 (for writing text) are subclasses of \b BitBlt.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The basic function of \b BitBlt \b0 is to move a rectangular area of bits from one portion of\line \expndtw-3 a \b Form \b0 or \b DisplayScreen \b0 to another. The simplest form of the move requires \b a \b0 source\line \expndtw-4 form, a destination form, a rectangle on the source form to be moved, and an origin point\line \expndtw1 on the destination form. Smalltalk can then calculate the corner of the destination\line \expndtw0 rectangle by adding the source rectangle extent to the destination origin.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 For example, consider the following code, which copies the top left quarter of the screen\line \expndtw1 to the right:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (BitBlt destForm: Display sourceForm: Display)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 sourceRect: (0 (S> 0 extent: Display extent // 2);\line \expndtw4 destOrigin: (Display width // 2 (3) 0);\line \expndtw-3 copyBits}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The first line creates a \b BitBlt \b0 instance with \b Display \b0 as both the source and destination\line form. The second line specifies the top left quarter of \b Display \b0 as the source rectangle.\line \expndtw-3 The third line specifies the top center point as the destination origin. The last line moves\line \expndtw-1 the bits.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri14\sb155\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Let's look at another example. This code copies a white form in its entirety to \b Display,\line \b0\expndtw1 while storing the old contents of the screen in another form, \b F:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 F \b0 := \b Display compatableForm new extent: Display extent // 2.\line \expndtw-2 (BitBlt destForm: F sourceForm: Display) copyBits.\line \expndtw-3 (BitBlt destForm: Display sourceForm:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li810\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (Form new extent: F extent)) copyBits.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The first line creates a form, F, with a size one quarter of the display screen. \b Display\line \expndtw-5 compatableForm \b0 returns either class \b Form \b0 or class \b ColorForm \b0 depending on \b whether\line \expndtw-4 Display \b0 is an instance of class \b DisplayScreen \b0 or \b ColorScreen, \b0 respectively. The second\line \expndtw0 line copies the top left quarter of the screen onto F. When, as in this example, you do\line not specify a source rectangle and destination origin, the \b destFornv.sourceForm:\line \b0\expndtw-2 message uses the entire area of the source form and point 0 @ 0 as the source rectangle\line and destination origin, respectively.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2384\margrsxn1896\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li40\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 122    Graphics}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri14\sb320\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In this copy operation, \b BitBlt \b0 copies a larger rectangle onto a smaller form. This poses\line no problem, however; \b BitBlt \b0 never touches any bits beyond those contained in the\line \expndtw-2 destination form.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri14\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The last two lines create a new white form with the same size as F, and then copies it onto\line \expndtw-2 the screen. Here, we move a smaller form to a larger one. Again, \b BitBlt \b0 moves only as\line \expndtw-3 many bits as are contained in the source form. Now evaluate the above expressions; the\line \expndtw-2 upper left corner is blanked. But, since the old contents are stored in F, you can restore\line \expndtw-1 the old screen by evaluating:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (BitBlt destForm: Display sourceForm: F) copyBits}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The rest of this section describes the concepts of \b BitBlt \b0 with color.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When bits are moved from a \b BiColorForm \b0 to either a \b ColorForm \b0 or \b ColorScreen, \b0 the}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 bits of value 1 in the BiColorForm become the foreground color specified by the mask\line \expndtw-1 form and the bits of value 0 become the background color. Then these color pixels are\line \expndtw-2 moved to the destination with four bits of each color pixel occupying the same position\line \expndtw0 on the four destination bitmaps. Try the following:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li421\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I aForm I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 aForm : \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  BiColorForm width: 100 height: 100.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (BitBlt destForm: aForm sourceForm: nil)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 mask: Form black;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 extent: 50 (S) 100;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 copyBits;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 destForm: Display;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 sourceForm: aForm;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 mask: (BiColorForm foreColor: 1 backColor: 4);}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 extent: 100 (S) 100;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 copyBits}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This example first initializes \b aForm \b0 to be a \b BiColorForm \b0 with all bits set to 1. Then it\line \expndtw-2 copies zeros into the bits comprising the left half of \b aForm. \b0 Finally it copies \b aForm \b0 to\line \expndtw-3 the display screen assigning color 1 (blue) to 1 bits and color 4 (red) to 0 bits. The result\line \expndtw-2 shown on the screen is a 100 x 100 pixel block with the left half in red and the right half\line \expndtw-3 in blue.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri18\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When bits are moved between a \b ColorForm \b0 and a \b ColorScreen, \b0 bits are moved between\line \expndtw1 corresponding bitmaps. In other words, this can be viewed as four separate moves\line \expndtw-1 between corresponding single bitmaps. Try the following:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2124\margrsxn2174\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri4\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Graphics    123}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi0\ri0\sb324\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex83\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I aForm I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 aForm \b0 := \b ColorForm width: 100 height: 100.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (BitBlt destForm: aForm sourceForm: Display)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 copyBits;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 destForm: Display;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 sourceForm: aForm;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 mask: Form gray;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 copyBits}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This example copies a block from \b DisplayScreen \b0 to a \b ColorForm \b0 and then copies it\line \expndtw1 back to the screen with a gray halftone.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When bits are moved from a \b ColorForm \b0 or \b ColorScreen \b0 to a \b BiColorForm, \b0 the colors\line \expndtw-2 on the source bitmaps that are the same as the foreground color of the mask form are\line \expndtw-3 turned into 1 bits on the destination \b BiColorForm \b0 and the rest of the bits are set to zero.\line For example,}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex83\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I aForm I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 aForm := BiColorForm}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 width: Display width}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi385\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 height: Display height.\line \expndtw-4 (BitBlt destForm: aForm sourceForm: Display)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 mask: (BiColorForm color: 11);}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 copyBits;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 destForm: Display;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 sourceForm: aForm;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 copyBits}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 extracts the area with color 11 from the screen to \b aForm, \b0 and blacks out the rest when\line \b\expndtw0 aForm \b0 is copied back to the screen.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When the source form is nil, if the destination is a \b BiColorForm, \b0 then bits in the mask\line form will be tiled over the destination. If the destination object has multiple bitmaps then\line \expndtw-1 1 bits in the mask form will take on the foreground color of the mask form, 0 bits the\line \expndtw0 background color, and the colors will be tiled over the destination area. For example,}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi-385\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (BitBlt destForm: Display sourceForm: nil)\line \expndtw-2 mask: (BiColorForm color: 4);\line \expndtw4 destRect: (0(8)0 extent: 100 @ 100);\line \expndtw-3 copyBits}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 paints a red rectangle in the top left corner of the screen.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2255\margrsxn2043\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li47\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 124    Graphics}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb360\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Halftone (Mask)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb205\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A \i halftone, \i0 or \i mask, \i0 is a \b Form \b0 which combines with the source form to create the effect\line \expndtw-2 of gray tone. This mask Form is restricted to have a width and height of 16. To copy a\line \expndtw-4 mask over a larger area, \b BitBlt \b0 repeatedly applies (tiles) the content throughout the entire\line \expndtw-3 affected area. Since a bit value 1 represents white and 0 represents black, a white mask\line \expndtw-4 form contains all 1 bits while a black one contains all 0 bits. There are four possible ways\line to combine source and mask forms; evaluate each of the following expressions to see the\line \expndtw-7 results:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "no source, no halftone (displays solid white)"\line \expndtw-2 (BitBlt destForm: Display sourceForm: nil)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 destRect: (0 @ 0 extent: 100 @ 100);}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 copyBits}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "halftone only (gives halftone tiling)"\line \expndtw-3 (BitBlt destForm: Display sourceForm: nil)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 mask: Form gray;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 destRect: (0 @ 0 extent: 100 @ 100);}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 copyBits}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In this case, the message \b Form gray \b0 returns a prebuilt mask form which yields a gray\line \expndtw-3 tone effect. Other prebuilt mask forms can be obtained by sending the message \b black,\line \expndtw-1 darkGray, gray, lightGray, \b0 or \b white \b0 to class \b Form.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "source only (gives source bits)"\line \expndtw-4 (BitBlt destForm: Display sourceForm:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1192\fi-389\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (Form new width: 100 height: 100 initialByte: l6rF0))\line \expndtw-3 copyBits}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Where \b0 the \b initialByte \b0 argument, l6rF0, specifies the initial value for all bytes in the new\line \expndtw-1 Form. The result will be white and black vertical strips (each four pixels wide).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "both specified (bits in mask are ANDed with bit in source)"\line \expndtw-3 (BitBlt destForm: Display sourceForm:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (Form new width: 100 height: 100 initialByte: l6rF0))}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1188\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 mask: Form darkGray;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1184\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 copyBits}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri18\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When a mask having colors other than black and white is desired, a \b BiColorForm \b0 mask\line \expndtw-2 should be used to control which color is painted on the screen or on a \b ColorForm. \b0 The\line \expndtw0 following messages are often used to create such a mask form:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 BiColorForm color: aColor}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Creates a 16 x 16 \b BiColorForm \b0 with all 1 bits, with \b aColor \b0 as its foreground\line \expndtw0 color and 0 (black) as its background color.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2045\margrsxn2272\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri11\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Graphics    125}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb324\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 BiColorForm foreColor: fColor backColor: bColor}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Creates a 16 x 16 \b BiColorForm \b0 with all 1 bits, \b fColor \b0 as its foreground color\line \expndtw0 and \b bColor \b0 as its background color.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 BiColorForm gray foreColor: fColor backColor: bColor}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Creates a 16 x 16 \b BiColorForm \b0 with alternating 0 and 1 bits, with \b fColor \b0 as its\line \expndtw1 foreground color and \b bColor \b0 as its background color.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This last mask form can be used to mix two colors together. For example,}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (BitBlt destForm: Display sourceForm: nil)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 mask: (BiColorForm gray foreColor: 1 backColor: 4);}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 extent: 100 \b0\i @ \i0\b 100;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 copyBits}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri11\sb140\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 paints a purple block (mixing blue and red) on the screen. By using this technique, you\line \expndtw-1 can expand the number of simultaneously displayable colors from 16 toL20.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb281\sa0\tx5620 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex71\expndtw14\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Combination Rules   V-   .        ^   \i\expndtw38 ?,J\expndtw14  Ac{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\charscalex152\expndtw-26 -^   t\super s\nosupersub ^}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb155\sa187\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A \i combination rule \i0 is an Integer which specifies how the source form bits (after being\line \expndtw-1 merged with the mask form) are combined with the destination form bits. Since a bit\line \expndtw-3 value of 1 represents white and a bit value of 0 represents black, if you OR white with\line \expndtw-4 black, the result is white. If you AND them together, the result is black. To specify what\line \expndtw0 you want to happen in different situations, send a message to class \b Form:}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2451\margrsxn3212\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1328\colsr1332\colno2\colw3585 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Form over\line \expndtw-3 Form orRule\line \expndtw-6 Form andRule\line \expndtw-5 Form under\line \expndtw-8 Form erase\line \expndtw-6 Form reverse\line \expndtw-4 Form orThru}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 destination becomes source}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 source OR into destination}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 source AND into destination}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 source AND into destination}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 if source is 1 then destination becomes 0}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 source XOR into destination}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 first erase without specifying mask form,}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 then OR with mask form specified}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2045\margrsxn2272\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb126\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 All the examples we've used so far in this chapter have assumed the combination rule\line \expndtw-4 over. To see the effect of different combination rules, evaluate the following expression,\line \expndt