\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \b over \b0 rule is used to copy the source rectangle over the destination rectangle\line \expndtw0 regardless of which colors are contained in the destination area. When you create a\line \b BitBlt \b0 instance, it defaults to this rule. For example:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi-389\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (BitBlt destForm: Display source Form: Display)\line \expndtw4 destOrigin: (Display extent // 2);\line \expndtw-3 copyBits}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \b opaque \b0 rule (called \b orThru \b0 for black and white) first blanks (zeroes) out the\line \expndtw-3 destination area corresponding to the 1 bits of the source area, then combines the source\line \expndtw-2 area with the repeated bits of the mask form by logical AND, and finally ORs the result\line \expndtw-4 to the destination area. In short, the 0 bits of the source form will appear to be transparent\line \expndtw-1 after the move. For example:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex83\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I aForm I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 aForm := (Bi Color Form width: 100 height: 100)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi382\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 foreColor: 5.\line \expndtw-4 (BitBlt destForm: aForm sourceForm: nil)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 mask: Form black;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 destRcct: (20 \i @ \i0 20 extent: 60 @ 60);}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 copyBits;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 destForm: Display;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 sourceForm: aForm;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 mask: nil;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 destRect: Display boundingBox;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 combinationRule: Form opaque;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 copyBits}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 first makes an inner rectangle of 0 bits in \b aForm \b0 and then copies \b aForm \b0 to the screen.\line You can see that the portion of 0 bits is transparent. This rule also has many other uses.\line \expndtw-5 For example:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2199\margrsxn2092\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li40\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 128    Graphics}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb317\sa0\sl-230\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I aForm I\line \charscalex100\expndtw-1 (BitBlt}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 destForm:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi414\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (aForm := BiColorForm new extent: Display extent)\line \expndtw-3 sourceForm: Display)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 mask: (BiColorForm color: 5; "extract blue color"\line \expndtw-9 copy Bits.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 aForm foreColor: 4. "change foreground color to red"\line (BitBlt}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 destForm: Display\line \expndtw-8 sourceForm: aForm)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 combinationRule: Form opaque; "change only 1 bits"\line \expndtw-4 copyBits}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 changes blue to red on your screen.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \b orRule \b0 merges two colors together. For example, combining blue (l) with green (2)\line yields cyan (3). Try the following:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li421\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I aForm I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 aForm := (BiColorForm width: 100 height: 100)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 foreColor: 1. "a blue single-bitmap form"\line \expndtw-5 aForm display At: 0 (3) 0.\line \expndtw-3 aForm foreColor: 2.\line \expndtw-4 (BitBlt destForm: Display sourceForm: aForm)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 combinationRule: Form orRule;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 copyBits}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \b andRule \b0 is usually used to extract a base color. For example:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li421\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I aForm I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 aForm := (BiColorForm width: Display width height: Display height)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi382\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 foreColor: 1. "a blue single-bitmap form"\line \expndtw-4 (BitBlt destForm: Display sourceForm: aForm)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 combinationRule: Form andRule;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 copyBits}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 causes any pixel in the destination rectangle having an odd numbered color (a trace of\line \expndtw1 blue) to become blue and all other pixels to become black.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \b reverseRule, \b0 as its name implies, \i is \i0 usually used \i to \i0 reverse the destination color, for\line \expndtw-8 example:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1888\margrsxn2403\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Graphics    129}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi-385\ri0\sb331\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (BitBlt destForm: Display sourceForm: nil)\line \expndtw-4 combinationRule: Form reverse;\line \expndtw-7 extent: 100 <S> 100;\line \expndtw-3 copyBits}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 reverses the color in the top left comer of your screen.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb320\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex81\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Clipping Rectangle}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb202\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 BitBlt lets you use \i clipping rectangles \i0 to restrict your bit transfer to a designated rectangle\line \expndtw-2 on the destination Form. For example, each pane within a window sets up a clipping\line \expndtw-1 rectangle every time the window is opened or moved or reframed; you never have to\line \expndtw0 worry about writing or drawing beyond that rectangle.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 For example, look at the following code, which expands all the black areas in your active\line \expndtw-2 window to the left by one pixel. Thus black characters in the window will appear to be\line \expndtw0 bolded, while white ones appear to be thinned:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi-389\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (BitBlt destForm: Display sourceForm: Display)\line \expndtw-4 clipRect: Scheduler topDispatcher pane frame;\line \expndtw-3 combinationRule: Form andRule;\line \expndtw-1 destOrigin: -1 (3) 0;\line \expndtw-3 copyBits}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In the example, \b Scheduler topDispatcher \b0 returns the Dispatcher controlling the active\line \expndtw0 window, the \b pane \b0 message returns the pane associated with the Dispatcher, and the\line \b\expndtw-2 frame \b0 message asks its receiver pane to answer the rectangle surrounding the active\line \expndtw-4 window. \i \{Dispatchers \i0 control keyboard and mouse input; they are described in detail in\line \b\expndtw-3 Part \b0 3.) This final rectangle is used as the clipping area. Now evaluate the expression;\line \expndtw-2 you'll see that only the content of the active window is affected, even though the entire\line screen is copied.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You should note two things from the above example. First, this example copies a form\line to itself, overlapping a large portion of the source and destination rectangles. \b BitBlt\line \b0\expndtw2 handles this situation properly as if a copy of the source form was made before\line \expndtw-1 transferring the bits.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Second, both the source and destination origin can be somewhere outside their forms.\line \expndtw-2 The only restriction is that the coordinates of origin points must be small integers. The\line \expndtw-3 final rectangular area affected by the BitBlt is the intersection of the destination form,\line \expndtw-4 source form (with the source origin aligned with the destination origin), source rectangle,\line \expndtw-1 destination rectangle, and clipping rectangle.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2331\margrsxn1957\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 130    Graphics}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb335\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Extension of BitBIt}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You've now seen some of \b BitBlt's \b0 raw power. It still, however, needs to be extended to\line \expndtw-1 provide an easier user interface for handling tasks like drawing lines or displaying\line \expndtw-4 characters. \b Smalltalk/V \b0 therefore provides four \b BitBIt \b0 subclasses: \b CharacterScanner,\line \expndtw-3 Pen, Commander, \b0 and \b Animation.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb320\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 CharacterScanner}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri7\sb205\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Class \b CharacterScanner \b0 converts a character's ASCII representation into its graphical,\line \expndtw-1 readable form. For example, a message such as:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'Hello' displayAt: 0 <S> 0}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb155\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 actually creates a \b CharacterScanner \b0 and tells it to display the String 'Hello' at screen\line \expndtw3 location 0 @ 0.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 One of CharacterScanner's major additions to \b BitBIt \b0 is an instance variable containing\line the font to use when displaying characters. It is an instance of class \b Font, \b0 containing a\line \expndtw1 particular version of the bitmap representation of all characters, which provides\line \expndtw-3 information about how to retrieve each character. When a \b CharacterScanner \b0 is told to\line \expndtw-5 display a string, it literally uses the ASCII value of each character in the string as an index,\line \expndtw-2 then uses its own \b BitBIt \b0 to copy the bitmap pointed to by the index onto the destination\line form. For example:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 CharacterScanner new}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 initialize: Display boundingBox}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li785\fi400\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 font: Font eightline;\line \expndtw2 display: 'Hello' at: 0 \i @ \i0 0;\line \expndtw-1 setFont: Font fourteenline;\line \expndtw1 display: 'Hello' at: \b0 40 @ 0}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 displays \b Hello \b0 at the top left corner of the screen using a font 8 lines high, switches to\line \expndtw1 a font of 14 lines, and displays the same string next to the previous one.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri14\sb155\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You can use the \b setForeColor.backColor: \b0 message to set the foreground and\line \expndtw0 background colors for the characters to be displayed:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 CharacterScanner new}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li781\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 initialize: Display boundingBox}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1184\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 font: SysFont}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li785\fi396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 dest: Display;\line \expndtw-3 setForeColor: (Display compatibleMask color: 1)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li785\fi403\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 backColor: (Display compatibleMask color: 14)\line \expndtw0 display: 'abc' at: 0 \i @ \i0 0. "display on screen"}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2043\margrsxn2260\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Graphics    131}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb324\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In a monochrome system, the colors numbered 0-7 are mapped into black and colors\line \expndtw-1 numbered 8-15 are mapped into white.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb335\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-17\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Pen}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In the previous chapters, you have seen many examples of turtle graphics. Although you\line \expndtw0 may not have known it, you were using an instance of class \b Pen. \b0 When you tell a pen\line to draw a line from one place to another, the pen actually uses its \b BitBlt \b0 to copy from\line \expndtw-1 its source form to its destination form at each position along the line. For example, to\line draw from 0 @ 0 to 9 @ 0, the pen copies from its source form to its destination form\line 10 times, starting at 0 @ 0 and moving right by one pixel for each of the successive\line \expndtw-2 copies. The end result looks like a straight horizontal line. When you tell a pen to draw\line \expndtw-5 diagonally, it moves to successive positions in as straight a line as possible. The algorithm\line \expndtw1 also makes sure that there are no gaps in the line.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Because a pen uses its source form to draw, changing the shape or tip size of a pen simply\line \expndtw-3 means changing its source form. To change the color of a pen, use a mask form with the\line \expndtw-2 desired gray tone. Of course, you can also change the combination rule and clipping\line \expndtw-1 rectangle to get different effects.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A pen always remembers its current \i location, direction, \i0 and \i downState. \i0 Location tells it\line \expndtw-2 where to start for the next movement. Direction allows it to calculate the ending point\line \expndtw-3 when the go: message is used, where only units of movement are specified. The angle is\line \expndtw-1 expressed in degrees, with east equal to 0 and north equal to 270. If downState is true,\line \expndtw-5 the pen draws while it moves; otherwise, it moves without drawing. The mandala drawing\line \expndtw-2 method illustrates this:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2358\margrsxn1948\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li43\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 132    Graphics}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb320\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 " Pen method "}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 mandala: sides diameter: diameter}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li828\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I vertices radius center angle color I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li810\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "initialize local variables"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 center : = self location.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 vertices := Array new: sides.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 radius := diameter // 2.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 angle := 360 // sides.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li810\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "set down state to false in order to use Pen to}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 locate vertices without drawing"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 self direction: 270; up.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li824\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 1 to: sides do: [ :i I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1199\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 self go: radius, "move to next new vertex"\line \expndtw-2 vertices at: i put: self location, "remember it"\line \expndtw-3 self place: center; "change location to center"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi785\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 turn: angle], "increase direction by angle"\line \expndtw-1 "draw from each vertex to every later one"\line \expndtw1 self down,\line \expndtw8 color := 1.\line \expndtw1 1 to: sides - 1 do: [ :j I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1591\fi-396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 j + 1 to: sides do: [ :i I\line \expndtw-3 halftone foreColor:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1591\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (color := 5 - color),\line \expndtw-2 self place: (vertices at: j);\line \expndtw2 goto: (vertices at: i)]]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi-403\ri0\sb29\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To execute this method, evaluate the following expression:\line \b\expndtw-3 Pen new mandala: 30 diameter: 300}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When you send the message \b go: \b0 to a pen, be aware that the actual pixels drawn may not\line be the same as you specified. This is because the number of pixels drawn are adjusted by\line \expndtw0 a global variable, \b Aspect, \b0 which describes the aspect ratio of your display. \b Aspect\line \b0\expndtw-2 contains a fractional number, determined by the video adapter board your system uses.\line The \b go: \b0 message multiplies the vertical distance of its argument by \b Aspect \b0 to yield the\line \expndtw-3 real number of pixels to draw. Because of this adjustment, you are able to draw squares\line and circles without worrying about your screen's aspect ratio. To find the correct \b Aspect\line \b0\expndtw0 to use with your video adapter board, evaluate the following expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Display black: (2 \i @ \i0 2 extent: 150 \b0 @ \b 150)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 With a ruler, measure the width and height of the black rectangle; then set:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2144\margrsxn2144\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri11\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Graphics    133}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb317\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Aspect \b0 := widthMeasured / heightMeasured}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Aspect must be an integer or a fraction, not a floating point. For example, if the black\line \expndtw1 rectangle measured 1 3/4" by 2 1/4" you would set:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Aspect \b0 : = (7/4) / (9/4)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb335\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 The Network Example Revisited}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb202\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 With all the graphics techniques we have learned so far, we can now actually draw the\line \expndtw-1 network system you saw in Chapter 7.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In class \b NetworkNode, \b0 add the following methods to draw the node itself:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 draw}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1202\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Draw the receiver node with a}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi378\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 circle around its name."\line \expndtw-2 I font r aspect pen I\line \expndtw7 font := Font eightline.\line (pen := Pen new)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1192\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 defaultNib: 2;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi400\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 mask: Form white,\line \expndtw-1 pen place: position,\line \expndtw6 pen solidEllipse: (r := name size * font width + 15 // 2)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi392\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 aspect: (aspect := font height + 16 / r / 2 / Aspect),\line \expndtw-3 pen mask: Form black,\line \expndtw-1 pen centerText: name font: font,\line \expndtw-2 pen ellipse: r aspect: aspect}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 position}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li817\fi382\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Answer the position of the receiver node."\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-6 a \scaps0\b position}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb140\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The method \b draw \b0 draws a solid white ellipse large enough to contain the node name,\line displays the text for the name in black, and finally redraws the border of the ellipse in\line \expndtw1 black. The method \b position \b0 answers the position of the node.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2237\margrsxn2057\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li50\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 134    Graphics}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb302\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Then, in class \b Network, \b0 add another \b draw \b0 method to first draw arcs and nodes:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 draw}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Draw the network. For each node, it draws\line all the arcs and then the node. All the\line \expndtw-1 nodes visited are remembered to avoid double\line \expndtw-2 drawing."\line \expndtw-3 I visited pen I\line \expndtw2 pen -.^Pen new}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 defaultNib: 4 \i @ \i0 3.\line visited := Set new.\line \expndtw-1 connections keys do: [ :nodeA I\line \expndtw-2 visited add: nodeA.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1602\fi-396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (connections at: nodeA) do: [ :nodeB I\line (visited includes: nodeB)\line \expndtw-3 ifFalse: [}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2383\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 pen place: nodeA position;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi1580\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 goto: nodeB position]].\line \expndtw-1 nodeA draw]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb162\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In Chapter 7 you assigned a network of nodes to the global variable \b Net. \b0 Display the\line \expndtw0 network by evaluating all of the following:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Display white.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Net draw.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Menu message: 'continue'.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Scheduler systemDispatcher redraw}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The result should be as follows.}\par \pard \plain \li3708\ri587\sb842\sa0{\pict\picw5854\pich2438\picwgoal3319\pichgoal1382\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi0\ri0\sb1044\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 9.2\line \expndtw-7 Displaying\line \expndtw-4 a Network}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2020\margrsxn2279\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri11\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Graphics    135}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb374\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Commander}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Commander \b0 is a subclass of \b Pen. A Commander \b0 commands an array of \b pens. \b0 It \b has\line \b0\expndtw-5 messages to either fan out or line up all the pens under its command. \b It \b0 also \b reimplements\line \b0\expndtw-1 messages like \b place:, turn:, down, up, go:, \b0 and \b goto: \b0 to pass \b the \b0 message to all of \b its\line \b0\expndtw-4 pens. When the units of movement are small, this creates an illusion \b of all pens drawing\line \b0\expndtw-5 simultaneously. For example, evaluate the following example, which \b draws five dragons\line \b0\expndtw-1 fanning out:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Display white: Display boundingBox. "blank screen"\line \expndtw-3 (Commander new: 5)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 fanOut; "set fan out direction"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 up;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 go: 60; "set starting point"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 down;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi392\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 dragon: 9. "draw dragon"\line \expndtw-3 Menu message: 'continue'.\line \expndtw-6 Scheduler systemDispatcher redraw}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The result should be as follows.}\par \pard \plain \li3805\ri1386\sb1728\sa0{\pict\picw4267\pich4368\picwgoal2419\pichgoal2476\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb22\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 93\line \expndtw-6 Dragon Curves}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1267\margrsxn1959\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1080\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 136    Graphics}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1040\fi0\ri0\sb328\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex80\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Animation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1044\fi0\ri0\sb205\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw14\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Animation \b0 is another subclass of \b Pen. \b0 An \b Animation \b0 contains several collections of\line \expndtw11 images of objects in motion. Each collection of images (each image is an instance of class\line \b Form) \b0 represents the motion of a single object as a series of still frames. When the forms\line are displayed continuously, it creates the illusion of a moving object, as in a cartoon. You\line \expndtw15 can send messages to an \b Animation \b0 to tell any of its objects to move, or change its\line \expndtw14 position or direction.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1040\fi0\ri18\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw14\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Movement in class \b Animation \b0 differs from the one in \b Pen \b0 in that an animation always\line \expndtw15 erases the old image of an object before moving it to a new position. The following\line \expndtw13 example illustrates four walking dogs. The pictures of these dogs were drawn using the\line \b\expndtw14 FreeDrawing \b0 tool included on your \b Smalltaik/V \b0 diskettes. Evaluate these expressions\line \expndtw17 to create an animation called \b Animator, \b0 with four dogs under your command:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1440\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw17\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I \b doglmages \b0 I\line \b\expndtw14 dog Images \b0 : =}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1440\fi0\ri1897\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw16\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Array "build an Array of pictures of a walking dog"\line \expndtw17 with: (GraphDictionary at: 'dogl')\line \expndtw16 with: (GraphDictionary at: 'dog2')\line \expndtw15 with: (GraphDictionary at: 'dog3')\line with: (GraphDictionary at: 'dog2').\line \expndtw20 Animator := Animation new}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1436\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw15\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 initialize: Display bounding Box. "ink clipping rectangle"\line \expndtw17 Animator add: doglmages "add first dog"\line name: 'Snoopy' " with a name Snoopy"\line \expndtw19 color: #lightGray. " and a lightGray color"\line \expndtw17 Animator add: doglmages}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi1836\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1829 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 name: 'Lassie'\line \expndtw-19 j!\super ij{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\nosupersub\charscalex100\expndtw1 color: # black.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1436\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Animator add: doglmages\line \expndtw-4 name: 'Bow'\line \expndtw2 color: #darkGray.\line \expndtw-3 Animator add: doglmages\line \expndtw0 name: 'Wow'\line \expndtw3 color: # white.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2082\margrsxn2220\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Graphics    137}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb317\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now evaluate the following expressions, to issue commands to your dogs:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "issue commands"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Display gray, "make whole screen gray"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Animator}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 speed: 16; "each picture is displayed 8 pixels apart"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 shiftRate: 2; "display picture twice before going}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1188\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 to the next (slows down the leg and tail motion)"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 setBackground; "use current screen as background"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: 'Snoopy' place: 0@0;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: 'Snoopy' direction: 45;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: 'Lassie' place:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1192\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 0 @ (Display height - 60);}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: 'Lassie' direction: -45;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: 'Bow' place:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1202\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (Display width - 100) @ 0;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: 'Bow' direction: 135;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: 'Wow' place:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1192\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Display extent - (100 @ 60);}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: 'Wow' direction: -135;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: 'Snoopy' go: 350;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: 'Lassie' go: 350;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: 'Bow' go: 350;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: 'Wow' go: 350.\line \expndtw-3 Menu message: 'continue'.\line \expndtw-6 Scheduler systemDispatcher redraw}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The above expressions command the four dogs to move towards the center of the screen.\line \expndtw-2 The following commands cause Snoopy to bounce around the screen at a quicker pace:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Display gray, "make whole screen gray"\line \expndtw-6 Animator}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 speed: 24;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 shiftRate: 1;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: 'Snoopy' bounce: 4000.\line \expndtw-3 Menu message: 'continue'.\line \expndtw-6 Scheduler systemDispatcher redraw}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2269\margrsxn2008\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li43\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 138    Graphics}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb306\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The following commands make ail four dogs bounce together.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi-389\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 80 timesRepeat: [\line \expndtw-6 Animator}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: 'Lassie' bounce: 16;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1199\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: 'Bow' bounce: 16;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1199\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: 'Wow' bounce: 16;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi796\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: 'Snoopy' bounce: 16].\line \expndtw-2 Menu message: 'continue'.\line \expndtw-5 Scheduler systemDispatcher redraw}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri11\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Global variables like \b Animator \b0 have varying amounts of memory assigned to them. To\line \expndtw-4 free this space after you have finished exploring the \b Animator \b0 variable here, type the\line \expndtw-2 following and evaluate it:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Animator \b0 := \b nil. "get rid of the instance"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri18\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This will set the global variable to \b nil, \b0 the undefined object, freeing up space as you\line \expndtw-3 continue.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb371\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 What YouVe Now Learned}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb209\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 By the end of this tutorial, you should be familiar with:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1031\fs22  \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033 \'95{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b\expndtw-1\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 Point, Rectangle, Form, \b0 and \b Bitmap}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 \'95{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-2\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 BitBlt}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1031\fs22  \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033 \'95{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-3\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 Halftone (mask)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1031\fs22  \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033 \'95{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-2\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 Combination Rules}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Clipping rectangles}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 BitBlt subclasses \b CharacterScanner, Pen, Commander, \b0 and \b animation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As always, you can review any of these topics by repeating the corresponding section of\line \expndtw-2 the tutorial, or by referring to a detailed description in \b Part 3, The Smalltalk/V 286\line \expndtw-6 Reference.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri22\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If \b0 you are going to \i exit \i0\b Smalltalk/V \b0 before proceeding on to the next tutorial, be sure\line to save the image.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1973\margrsxn2315\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs32 10 WINDOWS}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb450\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As you've seen throughout these tutorials, windows provide the major interface between\line \expndtw-1 you and \b Smalltalk/V. \b0 For example, you use the Class Hierarchy Browser window to\line \expndtw-4 enter programs into the system, and the Disk Browser window to browse and manipulate\line \expndtw-1 files. In the previous chapters, we used standard, supplied windows for the tutorial\line \expndtw1 examples. In this chapter, you'll find out how to make your own windows.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As always, the examples for this tutorial are stored in a disk file. To retrieve these\line \expndtw-1 examples, simply use the Disk Browser to load the contents of the file \b chapter. 10.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You will also again be making modifications to the \b Smalltalk/V \b0 environment in this\line \expndtw-3 tutorial. Be sure to save the image when you exit the environment; if you want to repeat\line \expndtw1 any section of this or any tutorial, \i it \i0 will already be there for you.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This tutorial builds on several of the previous tutorial examples in chapters 6, 7, and 9.\line If you have not done the tutorials in those chapters and saved the image, you should\line evaluate the following expression to install the needed classes:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (File pathName: 'classlO.st') nleln}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The later part of this tutorial extends the animal and animal habitat classes with several\line \expndtw0 new methods. Evaluate the following expression to file in all of the new methods:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (File pathName: 'animallO.st') fileln}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We will explain the new methods as they are used below.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb328\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 The Prompter}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri11\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Prompters \i0 are a special kind of window which lets you ask a question and wait for a single\line \expndtw-2 response. For example, evaluate the following expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1346\fi-947\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Prompter prompt: 'Do \b0 you \b know Smalltalk/V?'\line \expndtw-1 default: 'Yes, I"ve done a tutorial.'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A window pops up with the \b prompt: \b0 argument as the window label, and the \b default:\line \b0\expndtw-3 argument shown below it. The line containing the default is a standard text pane, which\line \expndtw-2 means that its contents can be edited. The user can either accept the response, or edit it.\line \expndtw-3 When you press the \b return \b0 or \b enter \b0 key, or select \b accept \b0 on the text pane menu, the\line \expndtw1 prompter accepts your answer and displays it as the result of \b show it.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A unique characteristic of a prompter is that as long as it lives, no other windows can be\line \expndtw0 activated. You must either accept or cancel the answer (which closes the prompter\line \expndtw-1 window) before you can activate another window.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2305\margrsxn1971\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li40\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 140    Chapter 10: Windows}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb331\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Single Pane Window}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Other windows in \b Smalltalk/V \b0 are not as restrictive as prompters. They are more under\line \expndtw0 user, rather than program, control. For instance, often a window may ask a question\line which you cannot answer without first consulting another window. You would then\line \expndtw-4 simply select or open another window, \i get \i0 your answer, then switch back to the previous\line \expndtw-1 window and enter the answer.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Let's start with a window with only a single text pane. Evaluate the following expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi-396\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 LearnDispatcher : =\line \expndtw-4 TextEditor}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1199\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 windowLabeled: 'Learning Status'\line \expndtw3 frame: (0 @ 0 extent: 400 \i @ \i0 100).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 TextEditor \b0 is one of the \b Dispatcher \b0 classes whose instance variables, when paired with\line \expndtw-1 a \b TextPane, \b0 provide text editing capabilities. Sending the \b windowLabeled:frame:\line \b0\expndtw-2 message to it creates a window occupying a rectangular area (0 @ 0 extent: 400 @ 100)\line \expndtw1 on the screen, with the specified label on the top.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To write text from your current window into the \i text \i0 pane of this new window, evaluate\line \expndtw-2 the following expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 LearnDispatcher}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 nextPutAll: 'I have learned everything about Smalltalk.';\line \expndtw-7 cr.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 LearnDispatcher \b0 is used like a \b Stream, \b0 which you saw in Chapter 7. As you recall, the\line \expndtw-2 message \b nextPutAll: \b0 adds its \b String \b0 argument to the end of the receiver contents. The\line \expndtw-1 message \b cr \b0 then adds a line feed. For a \b TextEditor, cr \b0 also displays the buffered\line \b\expndtw-4 nextPutAll: \b0 strings immediately; without it, the display will be delayed until the next line\line \expndtw-3 feed is received.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You can now move the cursor over the new window and select it, which activates the\line \expndtw2 window and lets you edit the text in it.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri11\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To retrieve the contents of this window from another window, activate a different\line \expndtw-2 window and evaluate the expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 LearnDispatcher contents.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri14\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To close the window, select \b close \b0 from the window menu or select the \b close button \b0 on\line \expndtw0 the label bar. Or, if you are in another window, simply evaluate the expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 LearnDispatcher closelt.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1983\margrsxn2312\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri11\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 10: Windows    l4l}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb356\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Single Pane Window with More Interaction}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The window you just created automatically inherits a window's standard text editing\line \expndtw-4 capabilities. In many cases, however, you'll want to customize a window to suit your\line \expndtw-2 application's needs.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In Chapter 7, you developed an animal habitat with a script to command animals. In this\line \expndtw-4 section we will build a custom window for editing and playing animal habitat scripts that\line \expndtw0 uses its own menus. We will build the window in stages adding features as we go.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb356\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Opening the Animal Habitat}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The new window we are creating is for editing and playing scripts for animals. Since the\line \expndtw-2 class \b AnimalHabitat \b0 contains the methods for playing scripts we will add the methods\line \expndtw0 for the new window to this class rather than create a new class. By convention, the\line \expndtw-3 message usually used to open a window is either \b open \b0 or \b open On:. \b0 Since we will want\line \expndtw-2 to pass a default or initial script to the window, we will implement a method for the\line \b\expndtw-3 openOn: \b0 message. The following expression files in the first version of the \b openOn:\line \b0\expndtw-2 method for the \b AnimalHabitat \b0 class:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (File pathName: 'windowl.st') fileln}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This method opens a window that behaves in the same way as a workspace or System\line \expndtw-3 Transcript window.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 openOn: aString}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li821\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Create a single pane window with aString\line \expndtw-2 as its initial script."\line \expndtw-8 I topPane I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 inputString := aString.\line \expndtw-5 topPane :== TopPane new label: 'Habitat*.\line \expndtw-4 topPane addSubpane: TextPane new.\line \expndtw-5 topPane dispatcher open schedule Window}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Every window has a \b TopPane \b0 which encompasses the entire window, including the label.\line \expndtw-3 In addition to initializing the window label, it acts as a local scheduler inside the window\line \expndtw0 to pick the active pane. To do this, it needs to know the existence of every subpane.\line \expndtw-1 Hence, we send the \b addSubpane: \b0 message to the \b TopPane.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The last line first sends the \b open \b0 message to the dispatcher associated with the \b topPane,\line \b0\expndtw4 which prompts for the window area and displays the window. It then sends the\line \b\expndtw-5 schedule Window \b0 message to let the \b Scheduler \b0 activate the window. The global variable\line \b\expndtw-3 Scheduler \b0 is an instance of class \b DispatchManager; \b0 only the windows known to it can\line \expndtw-2 be activated.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2286\margrsxn2009\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 142    Chapter 10: Windows}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb184\sa0\sl-392\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now, to open the window, evaluate:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-392\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 AnimalHabitat new openOn: 'Snoopy be quiet'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-392\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A \b0 blank window with the label "Habitat" will appear.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Congratulations, you have created your first customized window! However, this window\line \expndtw-2 cannot do much, except some text editing. It does not even display the initial script, the\line \expndtw4 argument \b aString, \b0 since we have not defined a mechanism for the habitat to\line \expndtw-1 communicate with the window.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Before proceeding, close the window by selecting \b close \b0 from the window menu or select\line \expndtw2 the \b close button \b0 on the label bar.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb356\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Connecting the Habitat to the Window}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb209\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Our next version of the \b openOn: \b0 method adds a means of communication between the\line \expndtw0 habitat and the window. The following expression files it in.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi396\ri0\sb18\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (File pathName: 'window2.st') fileln\line \b0 Here is the new code:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 openOn: aString}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li814\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Create a single pane window with aString\line \expndtw-2 as its initial script."\line \expndtw-7 I topPane I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 inputString := aString.\line \expndtw-1 topPane := TopPane new label: 'Habitat'.\line \expndtw-7 topPane addSubpane:\line \expndtw-1 (TextPane new\line \expndtw-3 model: self;\line \expndtw5 name: #input).\line \expndtw-5 topPane dispatcher open schedule Window}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In this method, we've sent two additional messages to the newly created \b TextPane:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri11\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 model: self \b0 tells the text pane the identity of the controlling application (in our case, an\line instance of \b AnimalHabitat) \b0 so that the text pane can send messages to the application.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 name: \b0 # \b input \b0 lets the text pane identify itself with the name # \b input. \b0 The text pane also\line \expndtw-2 uses its name as a message to the controlling application to retrieve the pane contents\line \expndtw-5 when \i it \i0 is first opened. Hence we implemented the message \b input \b0 in the application class,\line \b\expndtw0 AnimalHabitat, \b0 to initialize the contents of the text pane:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2007\margrsxn2288\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 10: Windows    143}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb306\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 input}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li824\fi378\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-230\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Initialize inputPane with inputString."\line \expndtw-6 AinputString}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb29\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We can now open the new window with the following expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi389\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 AnimalHabitat new openOn: 'Snoopy be quiet.'\line \b0 Now the initial script appears when the window is opened.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb313\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Customizing the Habitat Pane Menu}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If you pop up the pane menu of the above window, you still see a standard text editor\line \expndtw1 menu. In order to play the script, you'll want to customize this menu.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb22\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The following expression files in the final version of the \b openOn: \b0 method:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi403\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (File pathName: 'window3.st') fileln\line \b0\expndtw-2 We now present the final version of the \b openOn: \b0 message for \b AnimalHabitat:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 openOn: aString}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li824\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Create a single pane window with aString\line \expndtw-2 as its initial script."\line \expndtw-8 I topPane I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 inputString :\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  aString.\line \expndtw-1 topPane := TopPane new label: 'Habitat'.\line \expndtw-7 topPane addSubpane:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (inputPane := TextPane new\line \expndtw-3 model: self;\line \expndtw-1 name: # input;\line \expndtw4 menu: #inputMenu).\line \expndtw-5 topPane dispatcher open schedule Window}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We have made two changes to the \b openOn: \b0 method. We send the additional message\line \b menu: \b0 to the text pane to inform it that the controlling application has a method called\line \b inputMenu \b0 to create the menu for the input pane. We also assign the instance variable\line \b\expndtw2 inputPane \b0 to point to the input pane, so that we can communicate with the pane.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Here is the \b inputMenu \b0 method which returns the new menu for the input pane.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2233\margrsxn2065\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li43\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 144    Chapter 10: Windows}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb317\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 inputMenu}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li821\fi385\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Answer a Menu for the input Pane."\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-13 a \scaps0\b Menu}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1199\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 labels: 'copy\\cut\\paste\\play selection\\play all' withCrs\line \expndtw11 lines: #(3)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1595\fi-396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 selectors: # (copySelection cutSelection\line \expndtw-5 pasteSelection playSelection play All)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The method \b inputMenu \b0 simply returns a menu. In this menu, the first three selectors\line \b copySelection, cutSelection, \b0 and \b pasteSelection \b0 are the standard \b TextEditor \b0 messages\line for copying, cutting, and pasting, which we do not need to implement again in class\line \b\expndtw-1 AnimalHabitat. \b0 We do, however, need to implement the following two new methods:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 playSelection}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Accept selected string as the script\line \expndtw-2 and play it to animals."\line \expndtw-4 CursorManager execute change,\line \expndtw-2 self script: inputPane selectedString.\line self play.\line \expndtw-4 CursorManager normal change}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 playAll}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Accept the entire content of the pane\line \expndtw-2 as the script and play it to animals."\line \expndtw-4 CursorManager execute change,\line \expndtw-2 self script: inputPane contents,\line self play.\line \expndtw-4 CursorManager normal change}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \b playSelection \b0 method plays only the selected text in the input pane, while \b playAll\line \b0\expndtw0 plays the entire text in the pane. Notice that we change the cursor shape to \b execute\line \b0\expndtw-1 (shown as an hour glass) while playing the script, and then change it back to \b normal\line \b0\expndtw-4 when finished.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb162\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The window is now defined. To try it, first set up the global variable \b Habitat \b0 and teach\line \expndtw0 the animals commands in the same way as you did in Chapter 7, and then open the\line \expndtw-7 window:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Snoopy \b0 := \b Dog \b0 new \b name: 'Snoopy'.\line Polly := Parrot \b0 new \b name: 'Polly'.\line \expndtw0 Habitat := AnimalHabitat new}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 add: Snoopy;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 add: Polly.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1882\margrsxn2409\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 10: Windows    143}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb324\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Snoopy}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 learn: 'barking' action: [Snoopy talk];\line learn: 'quietly* action: [Snoopy beQuiet; talk];\line \expndtw0 learn: 'is upset' action: [Snoopy beNoisy; talk].\line \expndtw1 Polly}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 learn: 'to be pleasant' action:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi418\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 [Polly vocabulary: 'Have a nice day'; talk];\line \expndtw-4 learn: '* nasty' action:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1591\fi-371\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 [Polly vocabulary: 'Why are you bothering me';\line talk].}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Habitat openOn:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'Snoopy is upset about the way that Polly is\line behaving. It is as if whenever anyone asks\line \expndtw2 Polly to talk, Polly will be nasty. Maybe if\line \expndtw1 instead of Snoopy barking at Polly when he\line wants Polly to talk, Snoopy quietly asks Polly\line \expndtw2 to be pleasant for a change, things would go\line \expndtw0 better. Now maybe Snoopy barking quietly will\line \expndtw-1 not make Polly nasty.'.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 After the window is displayed, pop up the pane menu and select the \b play all \b0 choice.\line \expndtw-3 You'll then see the dialogue in the Transcript window. Next, try selecting only \b a \b0 portion\line \expndtw0 of the script; pop up the same menu, pick the \b play selection \b0 choice, and watch the\line \expndtw-4 dialogue.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb382\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Multi-Pane Windows}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Multi-pane \i0 windows, such as the Class Hierarchy Browser or the Disk Browser, group\line \expndtw-3 related functions into several panes within a single window. This gives the user a clear\line picture of the application, since panes within one window do not overlay each other.\line \expndtw-1 Multi-pane windows are also ideal when panes within the window interact with one\line \expndtw-4 another to a high degree. For example, when you select a directory in the Disk Browser's\line \expndtw-3 directory list pane, the contents of two other panes (the file list pane and the bottom text\line \expndtw-2 pane) are automatically updated.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We'll now create a window which groups the animal pattern matching you saw in \b Chapter\line \b0\expndtw-2 7 with the animation from Chapter 9- In this window, we add two more panes to \b the\line \b0\expndtw-5 previous example:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2352\margrsxn1903\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa281 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 146    Chapter 10: Windows}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2727\margrsxn1921\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw943\colsr1724\colno2\colw4593 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb2779\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 10.1}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Animation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Window}\par \pard\plain\fs2\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\column\par\trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-1217 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2779 \clvmgf\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx4593 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li328\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 reply pane}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li212\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 input pane}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-2174 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2779 \clvmrg\clvertalt\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx4593 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li281\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 animation pane}\cell {\par \cell }\pard\intbl\row \pard\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2352\margrsxn1903\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb428\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \i input pane \i0 is the same as the one in the previous example, where you can edit the\line \expndtw-4 script to be played to the animals. The \i reply pane \i0 contains the dialogue from animals, so\line \expndtw-1 that we don't have to use the System Transcript window any more. The \i animation pane\line \i0\expndtw1 is the stage where animals can perform in response to commands in the script.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri29\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The following expression files in a new \b openOn: \b0 method which opens the above\line \expndtw-1 multi-paned window and \b initWindowSize \b0 which establishes its default si2e:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (File pathName: 'window4.st') fileln}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Here is the new code:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1937\margrsxn2336\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 10: Windows    147}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb320\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 openOn: aString}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Create a kennel window with aString\line \expndtw-2 as its initial script."\line \expndtw-5 I topPane replyPane I\line \expndtw2 inputString := aString.\line \expndtw0 (topPane : = TopPane new)\line \expndtw21 label: 'KENNEL';\line \expndtw-2 model: self.\line \expndtw-7 topPane addSubpane:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1598\fi-389\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (replyPane := TextPane new\line \expndtw-3 model: self;\line \expndtw-1 name: # reply;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 framingRatio: (0@0 extent: 2/3 @ (1/4))).\line \expndtw-7 topPane addSubpane:\line \expndtw-4 (GraphPane new\line \expndtw-3 model: self;\line \expndtw2 name: #graph:;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi792\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 framingRatio: (0 \i @ \i0 (1/4) extent: 2/3 @ (3/4))).\line \expndtw-7 topPane addSubpane:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1591\fi-382\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (inputPane := TextPane new\line \expndtw3 menu: #inputMenu;\line \expndtw-3 model: self;\line \expndtw-1 name: # input;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi788\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 framingRatio: (2/3 (8) 0 extent: 1/3 @ 1)).\line \expndtw-3 replyStream := replyPane dispatcher.\line \expndtw-2 topPane dispatcher open schedule Window}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri11\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We \b0 call this window "KENNEL" because it will contain only dogs. (Your \b Smalltalk/V\line \b0\expndtw-2 diskettes include the \b FreeDrawing \b0 utility, with which you can draw your own pictures\line \expndtw-3 of different animals and try them out.) The window consists of three panes: a text pane\line \expndtw0 called \b reply, \b0 a graph pane called \b graph, \b0 and another text pane called \b input.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri14\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We set the instance variable, \b inputPane, \b0 to point to the input pane, so that later we can\line \expndtw4 use it to reference the pane's contents. We also set another instance variable,\line \b\expndtw-2 replyStream, \b0 to point to the dispatcher of the reply pane, so that we can later use it like\line \b\expndtw2 a \b0 stream and output things to it.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Since the size of each subpane depends on the size of the whole window, the message\line \b framingRatio: \b0 defines the position and size of each pane relative to its window. The\line \expndtw-1 coordinates of the rectangle argument to \b framingRatio: \b0 are a fraction of the width or\line \expndtw0 height of the window. For example, if the window rectangle is:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li421\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 100 @ 100 extent: 300 @ 200}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 then a framing ratio of (2/3 @ 0 extent: 1/3 @ l) yields the rectangle:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2322\margrsxn2009\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 148    Chapter 10: Windows}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi-396\ri0\sb324\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (100 <S) 100) + ((300 <8> 200) * (2/3 \i @ \i0 0)) extent:\line \expndtw12 (300 \b0 @ \b 200) \b0 * \b (1/3 \b0 @ \b 1)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 which is equivalent to:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 300 \b0\i @ \i0\b 100 extent: 100 \b0 @ \b 200}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Since we want the window to open with a particular size, we provide the \b metho4\line \expndtw0 initWindowSize \b0 which is used when the window is first opened:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 initWindowSize}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li817\fi389\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Answer the initial window extent"\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-1 a \scaps0\b (Display bounding Box insetBy: 16 @ 16) extent}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If you do not supply an \b initWindowSize \b0 method, the system uses a standard default \b siza}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Several other new methods need to be added and existing methods need to be modifiei\line before we can try out the new window. The following code files in the changes an\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'ab\line \expndtw-4\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 additions:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi407\ri0\sb29\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (File pathName: 'window5.st') fileln\line \b0\expndtw-1 The following two methods initialize the two additional panes:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb115\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 reply}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li817\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Initialize reply pane with an\line \expndtw-2 empty String."\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-4 a \scaps0\b String new}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 graph: aRect}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li785\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Initialize graph pane area to aRect and the\line \expndtw-3 animation associated with it."\line \expndtw-11 I aForm I\line \expndtw2 aForm := Form}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1192\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 width: aRect width\line \expndtw-1 height: aRect height.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li785\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 aForm display At: aRect origin, "background"\line \expndtw-2 animator :\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  Animation new initialize: aRect.\line \expndtw-4 animals do: [:anAnimal I\line \expndtw-9 animator}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li810\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 add: anAnimal picture\line \expndtw-7 name: \b0 an \b Animal name\line \expndtw-1 color: anAnimal color].\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-8 a\scaps0\b aForm}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1893\margrsxn2384\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 10: Windows    149}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri7\sb328\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now, we'll slightly modify the previous example's \b playSclection \b0 and \b playAll \b0 methods by\line \expndtw-4 adding the message \b changed:, \b0 which asks the pane identified by its \b Symbol \b0 argument to\line \expndtw-2 reinitialize the pane contents:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 playSelection}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Accept selected string as the script\line \expndtw-2 and play it to animals."\line \expndtw1 self changed: # reply.\line \expndtw-4 CursorManager execute change,\line \expndtw-2 self script: inputPane selectedString.\line self play.\line \expndtw-4 CursorManager normal change}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 playAll}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Accept the entire content of the pane\line \expndtw-1 as the script and play it to animals."\line \expndtw1 self changed: # reply.\line \expndtw-4 CursorManager execute change,\line \expndtw-2 self script: inputPane contents,\line self play.\line \expndtw-4 CursorManager normal change}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri11\sb140\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Next, we'll change the \b answer: \b0 method to write to the reply pane instead of the System\line \expndtw-3 Transcript window:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 answer: aString}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi400\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Output aString to the reply Pane."\line \expndtw-8 replyStream}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 nextPutAll: aString;\line \expndtw-9 cr}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri18\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Next, we add a new method to clean up objects that are no longer useful after the\line window is closed. The message \b super \b0 release releases the objects needed by the\line \b\expndtw-1 changed: \b0 message in methods \b playSelection \b0 and \b playAll:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 release}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Window is closed, release all the\line \expndtw-3 objects created by this habitat."\line \expndtw2 inputPane := replyStream := nil.\line \expndtw-5 super release}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb324\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex83\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Changes to Animal Classes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb198\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 At the beginning of this tutorial we filed changes and additions to the \b Animal \b0 methods.\line \expndtw0 We present them in this section.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2554\margrsxn1741\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 150    Chapter 10: Windows}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb317\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Since we are going to animate the animal objects, we need to provide methods for\line \expndtw-4 initializing and accessing an animal's color and picture attributes. We are storing the color\line \expndtw-2 as a symbol representing the desired color and the picture as a series of images of the\line animal in motion that can be used by the \b Animation \b0 class described in Chapter 9. Here\line are these methods:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 color}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li814\fi378\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Answer the receivers color."\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-8 a \scaps0\b color}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 name: aString picture: images color: aColor}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Initialize the receivers name, pictures,}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li785\fi400\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 and color."\line name : = aString.\line \expndtw1 picture := images,\line \expndtw-6 color : \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  aColor}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 picture}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li814\fi382\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Answer the receiver's array of pictures."\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-9 a \scaps0\b picture}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 We need to provide behavior that lets the animals move about the graph pane. Since we\line \expndtw1 are only using dogs for our example, we present the new methods for class \b Dog:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 run: distance}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi407\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Run for distance."\line \expndtw-4 self answer:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'I am running ',}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1188\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 distance printString,}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi407\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'feet',\line \expndtw-8 habitat animator}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1188\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 speed: topSpeed;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1188\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: name go: distance}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 turn: anAngle}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi403\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Turn direction with anAngle."\line \expndtw-5 self answer:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi410\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'I am turning ', anAngle printString, ' degrees',\line \expndtw-7 habitat animator}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1188\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: name turn: anAngle}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2055\margrsxn2240\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 10: Windows    151}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb324\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 walk: distance}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi407\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Walk for distance."\line \expndtw-4 self answer:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1199\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'I am walking ' ,}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi400\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 distance printString , ' feet',\line \expndtw-7 habitat animator}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1192\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 speed: self topSpeed // 2;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1192\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: name go: distance}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In \b0 addition there are methods implementing the following new messages: \b bounce:,\line \expndtw-5 topSpeed:, direction:, \b0 and \b home. \b0 The implementations are very similar to the ones given\line \expndtw0 above. You can use the Class Hierarchy Browser to view them.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb356\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Running the Animation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb202\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now that the methods are defined, we can initialize the animals:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qc\li0\fi0\ri5522\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I \b doglmages \b0 I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qc\li0\fi0\ri5483\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 doglmages : =}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qc\li0\fi0\ri5504\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Array}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1188\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 with: (GraphDictionary at: 'dogl')}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1188\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 with: (GraphDictionary at: \super f\nosupersub dog2')}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1188\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 with: (GraphDictionary at: 'dog3')}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi792\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 with: (GraphDictionary at: 'dog2').\line \expndtw1 Kennel := AnimalHabitat new.\line \expndtw-5 Kennel add:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (Snoopy := Dog new}