}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li374\fi0\ri0\sb3049\sa0\sl-220\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 12.4\line \b\expndtw-2 SalesCom}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-220\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Network\line Screen}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb43\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The preceding code drew directly on the display. What we want is to draw it to a form\line \expndtw-2 so we can display it in a window. We need to edit the methods \b draw \b0 in class \b Network\line \b0\expndtw-1 and \b NetworkNode \b0 to produce an additional method \b drawOn: \b0 for each class. You can\line \expndtw-4 either directly edit these methods using the Class Hierarchy Browser as indicated below\line \expndtw-3 (note the ** \b changed \b0 ** lines) or copy the methods from the file \b chapter. 12 \b0 and paste\line \expndtw1 them over the new method template.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The method \b drawOn: \b0 for class \b Network \b0 is:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2059\margrsxn2229\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 12: Application Development: Case Study    169}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb299\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3935 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 drawOn: aForm{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw1 "** changed **"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3935 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Draw the network. For each node, it draws all\line the arcs and then the node. All the nodes visited\line \expndtw-2 are remembered to avoid double drawing."\line \expndtw-4 I visited pen I\line \expndtw1 (pen := Pen new: aForm){\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }"** changed **"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 defaultNib: 4 @ 3.\line \expndtw4 visited := Set new.\line \expndtw-1 connections keys do: [ :nodeA I\line \expndtw-2 visited add: nodeA}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1602\fi-396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (connections at: nodeA) do: [ :nodeB I\line (visited includes: nodeB)\line \expndtw-4 ifFalse: [}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2380\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 pen place: nodeA position;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi1584\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 goto: nodeB position]].\line \expndtw-3 nodeA drawOn: aForm]      "** changed **"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb374\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The method drawOn: for class NetworkNode is:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3931 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 drawOn: aForm{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw1 "** changed **"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1206\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Draw the receiver node with a circle}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi378\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3931 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 around its name."\line \expndtw-2 I font r aspect pen I\line \expndtw7 font := Font eightline.\line \expndtw1 (pen := Pen new: aForm){\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-1 "** changed **"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1199\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 defaultNib: 2;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi403\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 mask: Form white,\line \expndtw-2 pen place: position,\line \expndtw6 pen solidEllipse: (r := name size * font width + 15 // 2)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 aspect: (aspect : = font height + \expndtw78 l6/r/2/\expndtw2  Aspect),\line \expndtw-3 pen mask: Form black,\line \expndtw-1 pen centerText: name font: font,\line \expndtw-3 pen ellipse: r aspect: aspect.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Note that the draw methods could now by changed to use the new drawOn: methods,\line \expndtw-4 but we leave this as an excercise for you. (Hint: it can be done in a single line.)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To test the new methods, evaluate the following:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2214\margrsxn2070\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 170    Chapter 12: Application Development: Case Study}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb547\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 f \i0 : = Form width: 600 height: 300.\line \expndtw-4 (SalesStrategy at: 'StratNet') drawOn: f.\line \expndtw4 f displayAt: 0 \i @ \i0 0.\line \expndtw-2 Menu message: 'continue'.\line \expndtw-4 Scheduler systemDispatcher redraw}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 That's it. You have roughed out one of the two main components of the visual\line \expndtw-1 presentation of the SalesCom application.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Take a moment to inspect the \b ComEvent \b0 class. Although the \b Net \b0 drawing of \b ComEvent\line \b0 nodes is visually similar to the random network tutorial example, notice the impact of \b the\line \expndtw-3 initialize \b0 and \b name:position: \b0 methods. While a \b ComEvent \b0 can be drawn by the same\line \expndtw4 code that draws the original \b NetworkNode \b0 of the network tutorial example, a\line \b\expndtw-1 ComEvent \b0 has a much richer data structure:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 initialize}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1202\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Initialize the dictionary data record of information about the}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi400\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 communication event to be empty."\line \expndtw2 info : = Dictionary new\line \expndtw3 info}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1192\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 at: 'l.Strategy' put: 'NYS'; "not yet specified"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1192\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 at: '2.Type' put: 'L, P or F";}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1188\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 at: '3.Message' put: 'Type your message here.';}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1192\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 at: '4.Next Step' put: 'NYS';}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1188\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 at: '5.Days Till NS' put: 'NYS';}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1188\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 at: '6.MTD' put: 0;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1192\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 at: '7.YTD' put: 0}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 name: aString position: aPoint}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1202\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Set the name and position while adjusting for screen}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi400\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 aspect ratio"\line \expndtw-6 super}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1188\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 name: aString}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi389\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 position: aPoint x \i @ \i0 (aPoint y * Aspect) truncated.\line \expndtw-2 StratNodes at: aString put: self}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The next step is to integrate this graphic network display into the multi-pane window you\line \expndtw-1 are about to create with code copied from the Animal Habitat tutorial example. Divide\line \expndtw1 and conquer. That's a big part of \b Smalltalk/V \b0 programming strategy.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1927\margrsxn2360\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 12: Application Development: Case Study    111}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb324\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Raiding the Animal Habitat}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Before you can "borrow" some code, you need some place to put it. While you can\line \expndtw-2 expect to call on many existing classes in the course of your \b Smalltalk/V \b0 development\line \expndtw0 projects, you can pretty much count on adding a new class for the application model.\line This is the class which defines the user interface of your application.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To run your "program", you create a new instance of your application model class, then\line \expndtw-2 interact with it. In this case, evaluate the following to create the new \b SalesCom \b0 class as\line \b\expndtw-3 a \b0 subclass of \b Object:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi-396\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Object subclass: # SalesCom\line \expndtw-9 instance VariableNames:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi407\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'replyStream'\line \expndtw-8 class VariableNames: "\line \expndtw-5 poolDictionaries: 'SalesStrategy'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The first and most important method in the \b SalesCom \b0 application is the \b openOn:\line \b0\expndtw-4 method which describes the window appearance. This method also associates a collection\line \expndtw-1 of methods with each of the panes. These methods are triggered as needed when the\line \expndtw1 window or any of its subpanes are created or changed.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri4\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You will get much of the code detail and the overall method structure from the \b openOn:\line \b0\expndtw1 method of the \b AnimalHabitat \b0 class. Use a Class Hierarchy Browser to locate the\line method, select all of it and copy it.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Return to the currently empty \b SalesCom \b0 class and add your first method by choosing\line \b\expndtw-3 new method \b0 from the method list pane menu. Select the entire default method template\line \expndtw1 and chose \b paste \b0 from the text pane menu. Then change the method selector from\line \b\expndtw-1 openOn: aString \b0 to \b open. \b0 The method source text pane should look like this at the\line \expndtw0 conclusion of this operation:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2275\margrsxn1995\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 / \i 72    Chapter 12: Application Development: Case Study}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb306\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 open}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Create a kennel window with aString as its initial script."}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li814\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I topPane replyPane I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 inputString := aString.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 topPane := TopPane new label: 'KENNEL';}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi403\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 model self.\line \expndtw-9 topPane addSubpane:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1588\fi-385\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (replyPane := TextPane new\line \expndtw-3 model: self;\line \expndtw-1 name: # reply;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 framingRatio: (0 \i @ \i0 0 extent: 2/3 (3> (1/4))).\line \expndtw-7 topPane addSubpane:\line \expndtw-4 (GraphPane new\line \expndtw-2 model: self;\line \expndtw-1 name: # graph;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi792\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 framingRatio: (0 \i @ \i0 (1/4) extent: 2/3 @ (3/4))).\line \expndtw-7 topPane addSubpane:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1588\fi-385\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (inputPane := TextPane new\line \expndtw3 menu: #inputMenu;\line \expndtw-3 model: self;\line \expndtw0 name: # input;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi792\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 framingRatio: (2/3 @ 0 extent: 1/3 @ 1)).\line \expndtw-2 replyStream := replyPane dispatcher.\line topPane dispatcher open schedule Window}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri29\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Choose \b save \b0 from the method source text pane and you will \i get \i0 an "undefined" message\line \expndtw-2 explaining that the \b SalesCom \b0 class does not have the instance variable, \b inputString,\line \b0\expndtw-6 defined.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri25\sb155\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 What a dilemma. You can't save the method without discarding the changes which, in\line \expndtw0 this case, means losing the entire new method.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Survival Tip \i0 #2/ \i When you are on cut and paste "raids" of existing methods, put the source\line \expndtw-1 method's class instance variable name(s) into the temporary variable declaration of the new\line \expndtw-3 method in your new class. You can then save the method and decide later whether your new class\line \expndtw-5 definition needs to be changed to incorporate the source's instance variable(s) or whether you leave\line \expndtw-3 the temporary declarations.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri29\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Insert \b inputPane, inputString, \b0 and \b aString \b0 into the temporary variable declaration as\line \expndtw-6 follows:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I \b topPane replyPane inputPane inputString aString I}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1966\margrsxn2333\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 12: Application Development: Case Study    173}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb295\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 And \b save \b0 again. This time it works. But you want a \b SalesCom \b0 window to open on a\line \expndtw-2 marketing network, not on an \b inputString. \b0 Edit the comment. You've determined that\line you won't be needing a temporary or instance variable called \b inputString, \b0 so delete it\line \expndtw-3 from the temporary declaration. Also delete the temporary \b aString \b0 and remove the first\line \expndtw0 statement setting \b inputString. \b0 Edit the \b label: \b0 argument now appearing in the first\line \expndtw1 statement of the method. The SalesCom window model now looks like:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 open}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Create a SalesCom window with aNet\line \expndtw-2 as its marketing strategy."\line \expndtw-5 I topPane replyPane inputPane I\line \expndtw1 topPane := TopPane new label:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ('SalesCom: ', Date today printString);\line \expndtw-1 model self.\line \expndtw-7 topPane addSubpane:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1584\fi-382\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (replyPane := TextPane new\line \expndtw-3 model: self;\line \expndtw-2 name: # reply;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 framing Ratio: (0 @ 0 extent: 2/3 @ (1/4))).\line \expndtw-7 topPane addSubpane:\line \expndtw-4 (GraphPane new\line \expndtw-3 model: self;\line \expndtw-2 name: # graph;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi785\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 framingRatio: (0 @ (1/4) extent: 2/3 \i @ \i0 (3/4))).\line \expndtw-7 topPane addSubpane:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1588\fi-382\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (inputPane := TextPane new\line \expndtw-5 menu: inputMenu;\line \expndtw-3 model: self;\line \expndtw-1 name: # input;}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi788\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 framingRatio: (2/3 \i @ \i0 0 extent: 1/3 \i @ \i0 1)).\line \expndtw-3 replyStream := replyPane dispatcher.\line topPane dispatcher open schedule Window}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri4\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Follow the same procedure, copying method \b initWindowSize \b0 from \b AnimalHabitat \b0 to\line \expndtw-3 class \b SalesCom \b0 and save it.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 SalesCom \b0 is shaping up. Evaluate:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Test := SalesCom new.\line Test open}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A \b0 Walkback window informs you that the \b SalesCom \b0 object, \b Test, \b0 does not know how\line \expndtw-3 to \b reply. \b0 Close the Walkback and go back to \b AnimalHabitat \b0 to copy the \b reply \b0 method\line \expndtw0 and paste it in as a new method in the \b SalesCom \b0 class. Save it. Next evaluate:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Test open}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2270\margrsxn758\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw7653\colsr508\colno2\colw720 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb558\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 174    Chapter 12: Application Development: Case Study}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri36\sb306\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 No big deal, another Walkback. A little more careful inspection of \b open \b0 reveals the need\line \expndtw-2 to copy \b graph:, input \b0 and \b inputMenu \b0 methods from \b AnimalHabitat \b0 into \b SalesCom.\line \b0\expndtw-1 Start with \b graph:. \b0 After pasting it into \b SalesCom, graph: \b0 will look like this:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 graph: aRect}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Initialize graph pane area to aRect and the\line animation associated with it."\line \charscalex82\expndtw7 I aForm I\line \charscalex100\expndtw2 aForm := Form}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 width: aRect width\line height: aRect height.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 aForm displayAt: aRect origin, "background"\line \expndtw-2 animator : = Animation new initialize: aRect.\line \expndtw-8 ftrnmftls do: [:anAnimal I\line animator}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li814\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 add: an Animal picture\line \expndtw-6 name: anAnimal name\line \expndtw-1 color: anAnimal color].\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-8 a\scaps0\b aForm}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri29\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Before saving, notice that most of the code relates to animals and not to our problem.\line \expndtw-3 Let's first edit the comment to say what we really want this method to accomplish. Then\line edit the code to do what is necessary. We can always leave the code incomplete and the\line comment will serve as a guide to finish it later. In this case, the actual implementation is\line \expndtw-6 very simple:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 graph: aRect}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li814\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Initialize graph pane area to aRect and the\line \expndtw-1 network strategy graphic associated with it."\line \charscalex82\expndtw7 I aForm I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li785\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 aForm := Form width: 600 height: 400.\line \expndtw-4 StratNet drawOn: aForm.\line \expndtw-3 aForm displayAt: aRect origin clipRect: aRect.\line \expndtw-14 AaForm}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Survival Tip \i0 #3/ \i When re-working a copied method, edit the "comment" first to make it reflect\line \expndtw-2 the changes you will be making. Let the edits you make in the ccmment direct you to lines in the\line \expndtw-3 code which implement that aspect of the method responsible for the behavior referred to in the\line \expndtw-2 comment. This technique can be so useful that you will develop an appreciation for and will,\line hopefully, write clear and complete comments. They aren't "training wheels". Comments are an\line \expndtw2 integral part of the communication among the \b Smalltallk/V \b0 community of programmers.}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 \\}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1907\margrsxn2344\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 12: Application Development: Case Study    175}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri36\sb295\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Continue by copying the \b inputMenu \b0 method into \b SalesCom. \b0 It can be saved without\line modification, though a \b SalesCom \b0 object will not understand if you ask it to perform any\line of its menu selections related to the Animal Habitat. The reps brought in \b inputMenu \b0 to\line \b get \b0 the \b SalesCom \b0 prototype window running as soon as possible. They later used this\line method as a template to create a menu for each of the \b SalesCom \b0 window subpanes as\line \expndtw-1 required by their prototype design.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri40\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Next copy the \b input \b0 method from \b AnimalHabitat \b0 to \b SalesCom. \b0 Before saving it will\line \expndtw-1 look like this:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 input}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li824\fi378\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Initialize inputPane with inputString."\line \expndtw-5 AinputString}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri36\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You can see that this method simply returns the content of \b inputString, an\line \expndtw-1 AnimalHabitat \b0 instance variable, and since you just slipped those instance variable\line \expndtw-3 names into the temporary variable declaration of the \b open \b0 method, you know this won't\line \expndtw-4 work. So to "hardwire" a consistent response from this method until you decide what this\line \expndtw2 method will need to do, change the method to read:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 input}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li821\fi382\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Initialize inputPane with a temporary input string."\line \scaps\expndtw-3 a \scaps0 "This is a test input string.'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 and \b save \b0 the method.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Survival Tip #4: Simplified, interim versions of methods are an excellent way to "divide and\line conquer\super 11\nosupersub  a \b Smalltalk! \b0 V programming problem. Most often this involves writing a return\line \expndtw-5 expression which is a specific instance of the kind of object which will result from the more complex\line \expndtw0 computation that a method is planned to perform. This is especially useful for separating\line \expndtw-4 development progress on the interface from progress on the internal manipulations of the involved\line \expndtw-1 data structures.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi-389\ri0\sb25\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now select and evaluate:\line \b\expndtw0 Test open}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri43\sb122\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You've done it. The first incarnation of a recognizable \b SalesCom \b0 object, as seen in Figure\line \expndtw-4 12.5. The pizzaz of the strategy network GraphPane hints at the developing interface. The\line window resembles but certainly isn't an animal habitat anymore. The two \b TextPanes \b0 are\line \expndtw0 the targets of the next phase of your \b SalesCom \b0 prototype development project.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2354\margrsxn1938\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 176    Chapter 12: Application Development: Case Study}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2739\margrsxn7478\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx1865\posnegy263\absw0\absh3218\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw9207\pich5676\picwgoal5220\pichgoal3218\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb2743\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 12.5\line \b\expndtw-5 SalesCom \b0 Window\line \expndtw-1 Phase One}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2354\margrsxn1938\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb648\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Customers and Events: \b0\i A Matter of State}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri14\sb212\sa0\sl-252\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The reps realized they were on track now. They saw two areas of \b SalesCom \b0 which needed\line \expndtw-3 attention:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-245\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-245\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 They needed to create an initial state, a test case "world" onto which a\line \b\expndtw-1 SalesCom \b0 object could \b open.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 They also needed to add new methods which would make the \b SalesCom\line \b0\expndtw1 window  manage  the  interaction  and   updating  of the  Customers   and\line \expndtw-3 Communication Events databases.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You incorporate many traditional database management operations into the \b SalesCom\line \b0 application simply by making the global variable \b Customers \b0 a new \b Dictionary \b0 object.\line \expndtw-4 The dictionary \i keys \i0 will be the customer company names. The value associated with each\line \expndtw-2 company is an array, its elements the 'fields' in the customer 'database*. A comment\line \expndtw-1 identifies each element in the first case entry. Select and evaluate:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1889\margrsxn2394\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 12: Application Development: Case Study    111}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li421\fi0\ri0\sb277\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Customers := Dictionary new.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li421\fi0\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Customers}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1217\fi-410\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3139 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 at: 'ABC Inc.' put: #(    "Key is company name"\line \expndtw-8 'Fred Smith'{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-2 "Field 1 is Contact Name."}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1217\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3154 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 '123 Maple \i St.'{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\expndtw-2 "Field 2 is Address."}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1217\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3154 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'Boston MA 02055'   "Field 3 is City State and Zip"\line \expndtw-5 '555-4321'{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-1 "Field 4 is Phone."}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1210\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3146 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'Kick-off Ltr'{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-2 "Field 5 is Current Event scheduled."}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1210\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3154 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'Feb 14, 1988'{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-1 "Field 6 is Prep Date for Current Event."}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1213\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3154 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ());{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-2 "Field 7 is an array for the customer history."}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 at: 'DEF Co.' put: #('Jane Doe' '321 Poplar Terr.'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1217\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'Hicksville MD 21202' '555-3476'   'Fol-Up Ltr' 'Feb 15, 1988'\line \expndtw-1 ('Kick-off Ltr: Feb 7, 1988'));}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li810\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 at: 'GHI Ltd.' put: #('Clive Davies' '999 Oak Ave.'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1210\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'Tustin CA 92680' '555-7890'   'Kick-off Ltr' 'Feb 14, 1988'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1213\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-21\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 0);}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 at: 'JKL Inc.' put: #('Bert Jenks' \i ^666 \i0 Sycamore St.'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1210\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'Irvine CA 92680' '555-4734'   'Kick-off Ltr' 'Feb 14, 1988'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1213\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-23\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 0);}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 at: 'MNO Co.' put: #('Bill Rasp' '345 Apple Ave.'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1210\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'New Vists CA 93232' '555-5678'   'Fol-Up Ltr' 'Feb 20, 1988'\line \expndtw-1 ('Kick-off Ltr: Feb 6, 1988'));}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 at: 'PQR Corp.' put: #('Ellie Small' '423 Sassafras Ave.'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1213\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'Tustin CA 92680' '555-2064'   'Phone Call' 'Feb 20, 1988'\line \expndtw0 ('Kick-off Ltr: Feb 1, 1988' 'Fol-up Ltr: Feb 8, 1988'));}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi-403\ri0\sb25\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To see the mini-database which you just created, evaluate:\line \b\expndtw-5 Customers inspect}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri14\sb122\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To inspect a customer\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 to review and modify the fields in a customer's "record"\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 select\line \expndtw-2 a customer name in the left hand list pane of the Customers Dictionary Inspector. Bring\line \expndtw-3 up the pane menu and click on \b inspect \b0 to pop up an Inspector on the selected customer.\line \expndtw-4 Each element, or field, of the customer record is a selectable line in the new Inspector list\line \expndtw-3 pane. Remember to select \b save \b0 in the \b TextPane \b0 whenever you make field changes that\line \expndtw1 you want to keep.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri14\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To explore the many things you can do with \b Customers, \b0 turn to the \b Dictionary \b0 class\line \expndtw2 entry in the \b Smalltalk/V Encyclopedia of Classes. \b0 Try "talking" to the \b Customers}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 dictionary to explore its database-like behaviors.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The reps used the \b Encyclopedia of Classes \b0 like a foreign language phrase book\line \expndtw-2 throughout the development of SalesCom. The reps got the customers to jump through\line \expndtw-4 hoops with \b keysDo:, includes:, occurrencesOf: \b0 and \b ar.put: \b0 messages. Check it out. Flip\line \expndtw1 to the \b Encyclopedia \b0 and explore the things you can do to the \b Customers.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2303\margrsxn1937\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li40\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 178    Chapter 12: Application Development: Case Study}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri0\sb295\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Survival tip #5: Study and understand the format of a class entry in the \b Encyclopedia of\line \expndtw4 Classes \b0 in \b Part 4 of \b0 the \b Smalltalk/\b0 V manual. And learn to actively use the \b Methods Index\line \b0\expndtw-4 in the \b Appendices \b0 to direct your cross-referencing exploration of the behavior of the many classes\line \expndtw5 which make up the \b Smalltalk\b0 /V environment.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri43\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 With customer objects in hand, the reps turned their attention to the communication\line \expndtw-6 events.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri43\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The reps used the same basic object inspection technique to fill in the required\line \expndtw-1 information in each of the \b ComEvent \b0 network strategy nodes. This included typing in\line \expndtw2 the body of the letters and phone call scripts that will eventually be merged with\line \i\expndtw-1 customer \i0 information. To save you time, evaluate:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb29\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (File pathName: 'comevents.in') fileln}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi-407\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To view the new data contained in the \b ComEvent \b0 nodes, select and evaluate:\line \b\expndtw-3 (SalesStrategy at: 'StratNodes') inspect}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri40\sb133\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Open an Inspector on one of the nodes. Use the \b Zoom \b0 button to \i get \i0 a good view of \b the\line \b0 letter bodies and phone script. All these data management and multi-window editing\line \expndtw-5 features of \b Smalltalk/ V \b0 dictionaries are already a part of the capabilities of the \b SalesCom\line \b0 application. The reps realized this was as sophisticated a data management facility as they\line \expndtw1 needed for the prototype.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri25\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 SalesCom \b0 consists now of two databases, \b Customers \b0 and \b StratNodes, a network of\line \expndtw2 ComEvents. It \b0 will be up to new and original methods to manage these objects'\line \expndtw-2 interation and updating. You will do that by enhancing the behavior of the multi-pane\line \b\expndtw-6 SalesCom \b0 window.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb374\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Methods and Messages: \b0\i Bringing the Prototype to Life}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The reps next objective was to get each \b TextPane \b0 working with the \b Customers\line \b0\expndtw1 dictionary. The inspiration was the browser, so they poked around relevant methods,\line \expndtw-2 especially \b open, \b0 in the browser classes and determined that they needed one to function\line \expndtw0 as a \b ListPane.   \b0 Selections in the \b ListPane \b0 would then trigger a method for the \b other\line \expndtw1 TextPane \b0 to display the data record for the selected customer.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The reps edited \b open \b0 to reflect the following changes:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 replyPane \b0 was renamed \b custsPane \b0 and made a \b ListPane.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-212\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-212\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 custsPane \b0 was assigned a \b name: \b0 method, \b customers, \b0 to return the Sorted\line \expndtw-3 Collection of customer company name keys from the \b Customers \b0 dictionary.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-212\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-212\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A \b change: \b0 method, \b viewCust:, \b0 was added to \b custsPane \b0 so list selections\line \expndtw-5 would trigger display of the customer record in the customer detail \b TextPane.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1865\margrsxn2409\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 12: Application Development: Case Study    179}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb295\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb295\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 inputPane  \b0 was  renamed  \b custDetPane  \b0 to  show  that  it  would  display\line \expndtw-2 customer details.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 custDetPane   \b0 was  added   to  the  \b SalesCom   \b0 instance   variable   list  and\line \expndtw-1 recompiled after "killing" the \b SalesCom \b0 object, \b Test.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Each pane was assigned its own menu method.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To see the many changes that the reps made to \b SalesCom \b0 during this next phase of\line \expndtw-2 prototype development, evaluate:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Test := nil.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (File pathName: 'classl2.2in') file In.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Test := SalesCom new.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Test open}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You will probably get a walkback saying \b has instances.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Survival Tip \i0 #6/ \i Finding lost instances. Windows cause dependencies to be stored between panes\line \expndtw-1 and models in the \b Dependents \b0 dictionary. If you have instances of a class that you cannot find,\line they are probably in the \b Dependents \b0 dictionary. This usually happens when you are debugging\line a new window. You remove these instances by executing the expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Scheduler reinitialize}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 All open windows close and the System Transcript reappears.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri29\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 SalesCom's basic behavior is evolving. Note the reps are using a "divide and conquer"\line \expndtw-1 approach to bring the prototype up in stages with the interface taking the lead. The\line \b\expndtw-3 customers \b0 method returns a typical list of customer names and \b viewCust: \b0 simply clears\line \expndtw0 the Customer Detail pane and displays a few lines of sample text.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri29\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Once they got the synchronization between panes and the stream to the Customer Detail\line pane working, they "attached" the \b Customers \b0 dictionary to the \b SalesCom \b0 application by\line \expndtw0 a quick rework to the \b customers \b0 method:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 customers}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li817\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Return the keys of a Customers dictionary as a Sorted\line \expndtw-3 Collection."\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-5 a \scaps0\b Customers keys asSortedCollection}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri29\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 And an enhancement of \b viewCust: \b0 was all that was needed to bring the Customers\line \expndtw-3 Dictionary "on-line":}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2705\margrsxn1590\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li43\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 180    Chapter 12: Application Development: Case Study}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb313\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 viewCust: aCust}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Change: method response for the Customer List pane.\line \expndtw0 It updates the Customer Detail pane to show the\line \expndtw-3 currently selected customer."\line I custData custHist prepDate I\line \expndtw-1 curCust := aCust.\line \expndtw-2 custData := Customers at: curCust.\line \expndtw-4 custDetPane cancel.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1591\fi-371\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 1 to: custData size - 1 do: [:index I\line \expndtw-8 replyStream}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 nextPutAll: (custData at: index);\line \expndtw-8\fs24 cr].\line \expndtw0\fs22 custHist := custData at: 7.    "Now print the history"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tldot\tx4338\tldot\tx6026 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 replyStream nextPutAll:    '{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw1 HISTORY{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-5 '; cr.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1192\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 1 to: custHist size do: [:index I\line \expndtw-8 replyStream}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1192\fi396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-230\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 nextPutAll: (custHist at: index);\line \expndtw-3 cr]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb166\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You can edit the methods to reflect these changes or paste them in from the tutorial\line \b\expndtw-4 chapter. 12 \b0 file. Once these two methods are changed, you have reached another plateau\line \expndtw-3 in the \b SalesCom \b0 prototype development project. Both the Customers and Communica-\line \expndtw-4 tion Events databases are up and accessible interactively in the \b SalesCom \b0 window as in\line \expndtw-1 Figure 12.6. Evaluate the expression \b Test open \b0 to see it.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn3209\margrsxn7008\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx1811\posnegy482\absw0\absh3117\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw9372\pich5499\picwgoal5313\pichgoal3117\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx5368\posnegy716\absw1670\absh-1073\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 Jane Doe}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx5368\posnegy716\absw1670\absh-1073\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 321 Poplar Terr.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx5368\posnegy716\absw1670\absh-1073\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 Hicksville MD 21282}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx5368\posnegy716\absw1670\absh-1073\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 555-3476}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx5368\posnegy716\absw1670\absh-1073\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 N2}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx5368\posnegy716\absw1670\absh-1073\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 Fefa 15, 1988}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi601\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx5368\posnegy716\absw1670\absh-1073\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 HISTORV\line \expndtw-6 Kick-off Ltr: Feb 7, 1988}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb3193\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 12.6\line \b\expndtw-5 SalesCom \b0 Window\line \expndtw-4 Phase Two}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2289\margrsxn1966\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 12: Application Development: Case Study    181}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri36\sb295\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Menu selections foreshadow but don't yet deliver the full \b SalesCom \b0 environment. Menu\line \expndtw-1 selections result in message \b not \b0 understood walkbacks. The rest of the prototype\line \expndtw0 development consisted of writing small methods to perform the actions requested by\line \expndtw1 each of the menu items.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb389\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 It's Getting Better All the Time: \b0\i Evolutionary Development}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri36\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The reps were on a roll. Every method they wrote gave them new insights and ideas for\line \expndtw-1 the next. They found that \b Smalltalk/V \b0 lent itself to "team development" as they took\line \expndtw-4 turns, one on the keyboard, the other scouring the manual. And by experience, they came\line \expndtw1 up with another tip.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Survival Tip \i0 # \i 7: Keep moving. Write the methods that "jump out at you" and practically\line \expndtw-2 write themselves. If you get stuck on a method, put its body in comment quotes and save it. Then\line go on to the next method you need to write. Something you pick up solving another method's\line \expndtw-3 implementation or sometimes just some "creative gestation\super 1\nosupersub ' will bring you back to unfinished\line \expndtw-4 methods. Eventually the toughest methods seem to give way when they are the only thing standing\line \expndtw-1 between you are your application running.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri40\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To see what the reps were able to accomplish with \b SalesCom \b0 on their maiden\line \b\expndtw-1 Smalltalk/V \b0 development project, evaluate:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (File pathName: <classl2.3in') file In.\line \expndtw-1 Test open}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri40\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 While they have a long wish list, the reps now had a prototype that met their original\line \b\expndtw-1 SalesCom \b0 development objective, see Figure 12.7. With the pop-up mail merge and\line \expndtw-2 phone call script windows, \b SalesCom \b0 was a lot more sophisticated a program than the\line \expndtw0 reps ever throught they could have written.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri43\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Take a few minutes to take \b SalesCom \b0 on a shake-down cruise. Add a customer and\line update somebody's contact information. Print a few letters. Make a phone call or two.\line \expndtw-3 Push that initial bunch of prospects through a hypothetical sales campaign. Sell \b a \b0 couple\line \expndtw2 of WORMS. Lose a couple to the Dead Leads bin.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri40\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Notice how the node highlighting pinpoints the position of the selected customer in the\line \expndtw0 marketing campaign. Track how printing a letter or making a call updates both the\line \expndtw-1 customer and communication event records.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri43\sb162\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To see what makes \b SalesCom \b0 tick, poke around the methods which you have just filed\line \expndtw2 in. Note that while a method like viewCust: now looks complex with all the data\line \expndtw-3 handling that has found its way in to support the additional interface features, \b viewCust:\line \b0\expndtw3 became complex in stages. The reps wrote simple methods that became more\line \expndtw1 sophisticated a line or two at a time.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2242\margrsxn2049\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa223 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 182    Chapter 12: Application Development: Case Study}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2257\margrsxn2585\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1292\colsr4518\colno2\colw1256 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2246\shptop-7\shpright7538\shpbottom-7\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 20320}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft7502\shptop43\shpright7502\shpbottom633\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}{\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx2261\posnegy0\absw0\absh3254\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw4787\pich5740\picwgoal2714\pichgoal3254\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx4028\posnegy61\absw0\absh-141\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 SalesCow:   Apr 28,   1988}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-130\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx4543\posnegy554\absw1062\absh-263\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 add customer\line \ul\expndtw-8 import customers}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0\tx472 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx5810\posnegy619\absw1667\absh-436\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex98\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 555-3471char>ge Contact\line \expndtw-11 N2{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw8 change Address}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx5810\posnegy619\absw1667\absh-436\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex98\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 Feb  15.|rtlange City St Zip}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx4972\posnegy1256\absw2556\absh-1073\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 Jane Doe}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx4972\posnegy1256\absw2556\absh-1073\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 DEF Co.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx4972\posnegy1256\absw2556\absh-1073\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 321 Poplar Terr.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx4972\posnegy1256\absw2556\absh-1073\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 Hicksville \i HO \i0 21282}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx4972\posnegy1256\absw2556\absh-1073\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 There is still root* for a busy executi\line \expndtw-5 at our next UORH Uarwer. Free food an\line \expndtw-6 small dose of technoj^SffiHd-pony at:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-137\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx4975\posnegy2455\absw2538\absh-677\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 Vou could add vast aass storage to you\line \expndtw-7 a few dollars a day.  Join us, please.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb122\sa0\sl-137\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx4975\posnegy2455\absw2538\absh-677\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 Best Regards,!\line \expndtw-5 Susan Heath}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4068\shptop562\shpright7588\shpbottom1433\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr5\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 75}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn pib}{\sv {\pict\picw6210\pich1536\picwgoal3520\pichgoal871\jpegblip
}}}{\sp{\sn pictureGray}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn pWrapPolygonVertices}{\sv 8;25;(9806,0);(9806,6961);(0,6961);(0,8568);(0,8568);(0,11781);(0,11781);(0,12138);(0,12138);(0,17494);(0,17494);(0,21600);(5477,21600);(5477,17494);(21600,17494);(21600,12138);(15327,12138);(15327,11781);(10689,11781);(10689,8568);(10159,8568);(10159,6961);(10159,6961);(10159,0);(9806,0)}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fIsBullet}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fAllowOverlap}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fEditedWrap}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fBehindDocument}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fPrint}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistLeft}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistRight}{\sv 0}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb2182\sa0\sl-220\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Figure 12.7\line \expndtw-5\fs22 Full \b SalesCom\line \b0\expndtw-4 Prototype}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 Jane Doe}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 321 Poplar Terr.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 Hicksville HD 21282}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2242\margrsxn2049\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2333\shptop-86\shpright7589\shpbottom-86\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 20320}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4817\shptop7\shpright6941\shpbottom7\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 2540}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft7157\shptop7\shpright7575\shpbottom7\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb648\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 For the record, over the course of the prototype development project, the reps were able\line \expndtw0 to get all the behavior you see exhibited in SalesCom by:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 creating two new classes, and}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 writing about thirty new methods.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The reps began using \b SalesCom \b0 the day after they finished the prototype. They had \b a \b0 long\line \expndtw6 list of anticipated changes but they found that use helped focus their subsequent\line \expndtw-1 development.   Rather than add features that they thought would be useful, they wrote\line \expndtw0 those that their daily use convinced them they needed.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb374\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Where to Go from Here}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Like many \b Smalltalk/V \b0 applications, \b SalesCom \b0 begs extension. While the prototype was\line \expndtw-3 fully functional and positively impacted the reps sales performance, over the next few\line \expndtw1 months they intend to do the following:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Add batch printing of daily correspondence, including envelope printing, to\line the mail merge capabilities of \b SalesCom.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Make the network of strategy nodes dynamic so exits of one strategy network\line \expndtw1 lead to the top of other strategic clusters of communication event nodes.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Add a management report window to show month and year to date totals,\line \expndtw0 sales total, and "body counts" of customers in each node of the network as\line \expndtw-2 well as compute the average days delay between one event and another, etc.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Use \b Smalltalk/V Goodies #2 \b0 to use the \b FieldPane \b0 class to create good\line \expndtw2 looking, easy to use Customer and Communication Event data entry and\line \expndtw-4 editing interfaces. This will allow users to "tab" from field to field rather than\line \expndtw-1 using the \b SalesCom \b0 prototype Inspectors and Prompters.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1882\margrsxn2402\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 12: Application Development: Case Study    183}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb299\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 To take SalesCom to an even higher level of sophistication, the reps (or you) might\line \expndtw3 consider extensions into the realm of discrete event simulation and expert systems:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Use the multi-tasking capabilities provided by the \b Process \b0 and \b Semaphore\line \b0\expndtw5 classes of \b Smalltalk/V \b0 to add an Event Driven Simulation component to\line \b\expndtw2 SalesCom. \b0 Such an extension would take the \b SalesCom \b0 databases' current\line \expndtw0 state and use what it knows about response tendencies to generate simulation\line \expndtw3 data to evaluate future impacts of current marketing decisions.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Use the backward chaining inference capabilities available from the logic\line \expndtw-1 programming extensions of the \b Prolog \b0 class supplied as an example program\line \expndtw-2 to create a \b SalesCom \b0 Expert System. Based on sales management "rules", the\line \b\expndtw4 SalesCom \b0 Expert System should be able to analyze data from \b SalesCom\line \b0\expndtw-1 Event Driven Simulation runs. The result of this analysis would be a series of\line \expndtw0 recommendations as to how to adjust sales activity now to avoid bottlenecks\line \expndtw3 and "feast or famine" cycles in the future.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 As you can see, the potential of \b SalesCom \b0 is great and will be reached by a process of\line \expndtw1 evolutionary development. From prototype to bigger and better. With \b Smalltalk/V \b0 the\line \expndtw0 limits are those of your imagination and the time and energy you have for development.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb364\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 What YouVe Now Learned}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb220\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 In the last chapter, we introduced the \b Smalltalk/V \b0 application development cycle. In this\line \expndtw3 chapter, we've shared a number of tips to help speed your application development:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li612\fi-205\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li612\fi-205\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx612 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 {\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Don't over-plan. Know enough to get going, then do. \b Smalltalk/V \b0 is designed to\line \expndtw-1 work with you in an evolving, incremental process.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li612\fi-205\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li612\fi-205\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 {\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 When on cut and paste "raids" of existing methods, turn source class instance\line \expndtw0 variable references into termporary variable assignments until you determine the\line \expndtw-1 destination class' data structure requirements.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li612\fi-205\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li612\fi-205\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 {\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 When re-working a copied method, edit the "comment" first. Then let the changes\line \expndtw5 made to the comment point the way to lines in the method source code which\line \expndtw-6 implement those aspects of the method whose description in the comment has changed.\line \expndtw1 And remember, comments are a critical means of communication among \b Small-\line talk! \b0 V programmers.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li612\fi-205\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li612\fi-205\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 {\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Simplified, interim versions of methods are an excellent way to "divide and conquer"\line \expndtw8 a \b Smalltalk/\b0 V programming problem.   Often this means writing a  return\line \expndtw1 expression which is a "hardwired" instance of the type of message a method will\line \expndtw-2 return once its more complex computation is written.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li612\fi-205\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li612\fi-205\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx612 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 {\b0\i\cf0\charscalex106\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Use the \b Encyclopedia \b0 of \b Classes \b0 in \b Part 4 of \b0 the \b Smalltalk/ V \b0 manual. And use\line \charscalex100\expndtw-1 the \b Methods Index \b0 in the \b Appendices \b0 to direct your cross-referencing exploration\line \expndtw2 of' \b Smalltalk/V \b0 classes and their methods.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2549\margrsxn1750\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 184    Chapter 12: Application Development: Case Study}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li634\fi-227\ri0\sb317\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx634 \b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li634\fi-227\ri0\sb317\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx634 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent227\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 {\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Keep moving. Keep your productivity high by writing the "easy ones" first. Very\line often the act of writing other methods or incubating on a tough one will result in\line insights to conquer almost any \b Smalltalk/ V \b0 programming problem.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li634\fi-227\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx634 \b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li634\fi-227\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx634 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent227\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 {\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Know how to bounce back from errors, like using \b Scheduler reinitialize \b0 to kill\line \expndtw-3 hard-to-find instances.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Keep these tips in mind as you tackle your first \b Smalltalk/V \b0 programming project. The\line remaining three sections of this manual are provided as reference tools. \b Part 3, The\line \expndtw-2 Smalltalk/V 286 Reference, \b0 covers in greater detail all the material explored in these\line \expndtw-4 tutorials. \b Part 4, The Encyclopedia of Classes, \b0 exhaustively describes the classes in the\line \b\expndtw0 Smalltalk/V \b0 environment, including each classes' instance and class variables and\line \expndtw-1 methods. The \b Appendices \b0 contain a variety of sections on advanced and special topics\line as well as that ever-so-important \b Methods Index.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If you want to review any of the topics covered in this tutorial, you can either repeat the\line \expndtw1 corresponding section of the tutorial, or refer to the detailed description in \b Part 3.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn902\margrsxn11007\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720 {\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1357\margrsxn4641\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw720\colsr3924\colno2\colw1267 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 i \i0 i\super 1}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb126\sa0\sl-382\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-23\dn7\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs50 Part 3}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1357\margrsxn2567\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3568\fi-1004\ri0\sb749\sa0\sl-889\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-21\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs78 Smalltalk/V 286\line \expndtw-35 Reference}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2045\margrsxn2243\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 13 THE SMALLTALK LANGUAGE}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb450\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk is easy to learn and use because it has simple syntaxes and semantics, and few\line \expndtw-5 concepts. The concepts \i object, class, message, \i0 and \i method \i0 form the basis of programming\line \expndtw-1 in Smalltalk. The methodology for using Smalltalk consists of:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx623 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx623 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent213\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Identifying the objects appearing in the problem and its solution.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx623 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx623 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent213\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Classifying the objects according to their similarities and differences.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li623\fi-212\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx623 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li623\fi-212\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx623 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Designing messages which make up the language of interaction among the\line \expndtw-2 objects.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li623\fi-212\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx623 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li623\fi-212\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx623 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Implementing methods which are the algorithms that carry out the interac-\line \expndtw1 tion among objects.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb378\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Objects}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri7\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Objects \i0 are self-describing data structures. Every object is an instance of a class. The class\line \expndtw-1 of an object is determined by sending it the message \b class. \b0 This message is understood\line by all objects.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Objects are protected data structures. The data stored inside of an object is accessible\line \expndtw0 only through messages. Objects can also be shared.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The word \b self \b0 in a method refers to the receiver object of the message that invokes the\line \expndtw-2 method.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb328\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Variables}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb212\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 All Smalltalk \i variables \i0 are containers for objects. A variable contains a single object\line \expndtw-4 pointer.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The variable name can be used in an expression to refer to the object whose pointer it\line contains. A variable may contain different object pointers at different times. The object\line \expndtw-4 pointer contained in a variable changes when an assignment expression is evaluated. An\line \expndtw2 assignment makes a copy of a pointer to an object, not a copy of the object itself.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri11\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Variables are either private or \i shared. \i0 Private variables are accessible only to a single\line \expndtw-1 object. Shared variables are accessible to multiple objects. A private variable has a\line \expndtw-2 lower-case first letter, while a shared variable has an upper-case first letter.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There are three kinds of variables:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li619\fi-209\ri410\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 1. \i Instance \i0 variables are the component parts of an object. They exist for the\line lifetime of the object.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2293\margrsxn1969\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li43\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 188    Chapter 13: The Smalltalk Language}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li634\fi-216\ri0\sb299\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx634 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 2.{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-5 Temporary \i0 variables are created during the activation of a method. They exist\line \expndtw1 for the lifetime of the method activation.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-223\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-245\slmult0\tx688 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-17\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 3.{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i\expndtw2 Shared \i0 variables are shared by many objects. They exist until explicitly\line \expndtw-4 deleted.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb320\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Instance Variables}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri11\sb202\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Each object maintains its own internal state. The private memory of an object consists\line \expndtw-5 of its individually accessible components called \i instance variables. \i0 Instance variables are\line \expndtw-3 similar to fields of a record structure in other languages. Instance variables either have a\line \expndtw-4 name or are referred to with an integer index. \i Named \i0 instance variables are accessed by\line \expndtw-5 using their name. \i Indexed \i0 instance variables are accessed only through messages (usually\line \expndtw-4 using \b ar.put: \b0 with integer indices). Each member of a class has its own separate instance\line \expndtw-8 variables.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri18\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Instance variables have a type\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 they contain either \i pointers \i0 or \i bytes. \i0 All instance variables\line \expndtw2 for objects belonging to the same class are the same type. Most objects' instance\line \expndtw-3 variables contain pointers. The pointers refer to objects. If an object contains bytes, then\line \expndtw-2 its instance variables contain eight-bit values representing elementary data values.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri14\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Classes may specify both named and indexed instance variables for their member objects.\line The number and names of named instance variables are fixed for all members of the class.\line \expndtw-2 The number of instance variables may differ among members of the same class. For\line \expndtw1 example, \b #(1 2 \b0 3) and \b #('up' 'down') \b0 are both objects of class \b Array, \b0 but they have\line \expndtw0 different numbers of indexed instance variables, 3 and 2 respectively. A class with\line \expndtw-5 indexed instance variables creates new members with a message that specifies the number\line \expndtw0 of indexed instance variables to create (usually the message \b new: \b0 with an integer\line \expndtw-1 argument). Many objects return their number of indexed instance variables in response\line \expndtw0 to the message \b size.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri18\sb155\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Only the instance variables of the receiver of the message that invoked the method can\line \expndtw0 be referred to by name.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb328\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Temporary Variables}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li25\fi0\ri0\sb205\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Temporary variables \i0 include \i method arguments \i0 and \i method temporaries, \i0 and contained \i block\line \expndtw-5 arguments.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri18\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Method arguments are assigned the associated message arguments for the message which\line \expndtw0 caused method invocation. Method temporaries are initialized to nil upon method\line \expndtw2 invocation. Block arguments are assigned the associated message arguments for the}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1924\margrsxn2357\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 13: The Smalltalk Language    189}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb313\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 value: \b0 message at the time the block is activated. When a block is invoked while its\line \expndtw-2 containing method is still active, the block and the containing method share the same\line \expndtw-4 temporary variables.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb324\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex83\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Shared Variables}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb205\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Shared variables \i0 are defined in dictionaries called \i pools. \i0 Different kinds of shared variables\line \expndtw-4 are defined in different kinds of pools. All shared variable names start with an upper-case\line \expndtw0 letter. The variable name and the variable value are bound together into an object that\line \expndtw1 is an instance of class Association. This association object is placed in the pool.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb22\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The System Dictionary \b Smalltalk \b0 is a pool which contains all the global variables.\line \expndtw-1 Global variables are accessible from every object.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb130\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Class variables for each class are implicitly collected into a pool for the class. Class\line \expndtw-3 variables are defined as part of the class specification. Class variables are accessible only\line \expndtw0 to the class, subclasses, instances of the class, and instances of the subclasses.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri4\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Pool variables are contained in named pool dictionaries that you explicitly construct.\line \expndtw-4 Pool dictionaries are global variables. To make pool variables accessible to a class and its\line \expndtw-1 instances, you must modify the class specification.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb364\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-16\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Classes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Classes \i0 are the program modules of Smalltalk because they describe data structures\line \expndtw-1 (objects), algorithms (methods), and external interfaces (message protocol). Classes\line provide complete capabilities to solve a particular problem.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Every object is an instance of some class. All objects which are instances of a class are\line \expndtw-4 similar because they have the same structure (i.e., the same instance variables), the same\line \expndtw0 messages to which they respond, and the same available methods.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Classes are also objects contained in global variables which are maintained in the System\line \expndtw-5 Dictionary \b Smalltalk. \b0 As such, class names begin with a capital letter. This allows classes\line \expndtw1 to be referred to in an expression.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2374\margrsxn1911\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li40\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 190    Chapter 13: The Smalltalk Language}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb324\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex83\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 The Class Hierarchy}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb198\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Classes form a hierarchy, consisting of a root class, called \b Object, \b0 and many subclasses.\line Each class inherits the functionality of all its superclasses in the hierarchy. Class \b Object\line \b0\expndtw-2 provides the common behavior for all objects. It includes methods for printing an object\line \expndtw-3 symbolically, for testing the class of an object, and for making a copy of an object. Each\line \expndtw-2 subclass builds on its superclasses by adding its own methods and instance variables to\line \expndtw0 complete the implementation of the subclass's behavior.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb158\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The complete \b Smalltalk/ V \b0 class hierarchy is shown on the next page. Indentation is used\line \expndtw-1 to show subclass relationships.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1951\margrsxn2352\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4619\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa313 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 13: The Smalltalk Language    191}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1951\margrsxn2578\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw3528\colsr796\colno2\colw3056 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Object}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Behavior\line \expndtw-10 Class\line MetaClass\line \expndtw-2 BitBlt}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 CharacterScanner\line \expndtw-5 Pen}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Aoimacioa\line \expndtw-8 Commander\line \expndtw-3 Boolean\line \expndtw-10 False\line \expndtw-4 True}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 ClassBrowser\line ClassHierarchyBrowser\line \expndtw-8 ClassReader\line \expndtw-1 Collection\line \expndtw-6 Bag\line \expndtw-2 IndexedCollection}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1591\fi-385\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 FixedSizeCollection\line \expndtw-9 Array}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1595\fi396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 CompiledMethod\line Bitmap\line \expndtw-11 Byte Array}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1595\fi400\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 FileHandle\line Interval\line String}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1991\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Symbol}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1591\fi-396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 OrderedCollection\line \expndtw-5 Process}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi796\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 SortedCollection\line \expndtw-10 Set}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Dictionary}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1595\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 IdentityDictionary}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi400\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 MethodDictionary\line \expndtw-4 SystemDictionary\line \expndtw-5 SymbolSet\line \expndtw-4 Compiler}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi400\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 LCompiler\line \expndtw-2 Context}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi396\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 HomeContext\line \expndtw-7 CursorManager}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi403\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 NoMouseCursor\line DeletedClass\line \expndtw-5 DemoClass\line \expndtw-1 Directory\line \expndtw-2 DiskBrowser\line \expndtw-3 Dispatcher}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 GraphDispatcher\line \expndtw-2 PointDispatcher\line \expndtw-6 ScreenDispatcher\line \expndtw-3 ScrollDispatcher\line \expndtw-6 ListSelector\line \expndtw-3 TextEditor}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1595\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 PromptEditor}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi392\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 TopDispatcher\line \expndtw-4 DispatchManager\line \expndtw-3 DisplayObject}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi-403\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 DisplayMedium\line \expndtw-8 Form}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 BiColorForm}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1195\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 ColorForm}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1192\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 DisplayScreen}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi1591\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 ColorScreen\line \expndtw-1 DOS\line \expndtw-6 File\line \expndtw-5 Font\line \expndtw-1 Icon}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 InputEvent\line \expndtw-3 Inspector}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Debugger\line \expndtw-4 Dictionary Inspector\line \expndtw-3 Magnitude}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Association\line \expndtw-11 Character\line \expndtw-4 Date\line \expndtw0 Number\line \expndtw-7 Float\line Fraction\line \expndtw-1 Integer}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 LargeNegativelnteger\line \expndtw-1 LargePositivelnteger\line Smalllnteger\line \expndtw-2 Time\line \expndtw-5 Menu\line Message\line \expndtw-9 Pane}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 SubPane}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 GraphPane\line \expndtw-1 IistPane\line \expndtw-4 TextPane\line TopPane\line \expndtw-8 Pattern}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi396\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 WildPattern\line \expndtw-1 Point}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 ProcessScheduler\line \expndtw-5 Prompter\line \expndtw-1 Rectangle\line \expndtw-7 Semaphore\line \expndtw-11 Stream}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 ReadStream\line \expndtw-6 WriteStream}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-198\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 ReadWriteStream\line \expndtw-7 FileStream\line TerminalStream\line \expndtw-2 StringModel\line TextSelection\line UndennedObject}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2370\margrsxn1907\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 192    Chapter 13: The Smalltalk Language}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb364\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Inheritance}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb194\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A class inherits all of its superclasses' instance variables, class variables, and methods.\line \expndtw-3 Inheritance of class variables allows the methods of a class to refer to the class variables\line defined in its superclasses.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri7\sb140\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Inheritance of instance variables allows the methods of a class \i to \i0 refer to the instance\line \expndtw-5 variables defined in its superclasses, but it also means that superclass instance variables are\line \expndtw0 included in objects which are \i instances of \i0 the class.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Determining what method to perform starts with two pieces of information: the message\line \expndtw0 selector and the class of the receiver of the message.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 First, the available methods for the class of the receiver are examined to see if there is a\line \expndtw-2 method which matches the message selector. If so, that method is performed. If not, the\line \expndtw-3 superclass \i of \i0 the class \i of \i0 the receiver is used, and the check for a method matching the\line \expndtw-2 selector is performed again. This checking for a matching method and advancing to the\line \expndtw-1 superclass \i is \i0 repeated until the method is found or until the end of the superclass chain\line \expndtw-4 is reached. In the latter case, a programming error occurs, and a message which describes\line \expndtw1 the error is sent \i to \i0 the receiver \i of \i0 the original message.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There is a special syntax form for a receiver, \b super, \b0 which changes the initial class used\line \expndtw0 for message lookup. The word \b super \b0 has two implications.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li605\fi-194\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx605 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-23\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li605\fi-194\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx605 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent194\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-23\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 It represents the same object as \b self \b0 does, the receiver of the message which\line \expndtw1 caused the method containing \b super \b0 to be performed.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li605\fi-194\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx605 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li605\fi-194\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx605 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent194\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 It causes message lookup to start in the superclass of the class containing the\line \expndtw5 method in which \b super \b0 appears, rather than starting in the class of the\line \expndtw-7 receiver.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb155\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The major purpose of a message to \b super \b0 is to be able to use a method in a superclass\line \expndtw-1 which is redefined in a subclass.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb360\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Class Messages}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Messages to class objects are used for \i creating \i0 instances of the class and for initializing\line \expndtw-4 class variables, the most common messages for creating new instances are \b new \b0 and \b new:.\line \b0\expndtw-1 Some classes define their own messages for creating instances.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Like all objects, classes know to which messages they can respond. For other objects, the\line \expndtw-2 methods available are determined by the object's class. Class objects, too, belong to a\line \expndtw-5 "class," called a \i metadass, \i0 which determines the messages to which the class can respond.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1911\margrsxn2380\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 13: The Smalltalk Language    193}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb184\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There are three important classes relating to metaclasses:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-396\slmult0\tx608 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-396\slmult0\tx608 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent194\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Metaclass\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 the class of all metaclasses.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-396\slmult0\tx608 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-396\slmult0\tx608 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent194\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Class\b0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 the superclass of all instances of \b Metaclass.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-396\slmult0\tx608 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-396\slmult0\tx608 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent194\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Every metaclass has exactly one instance: the class of which it is the metaclass.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb295\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex83\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Specifying a New Class}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In order for you to add a new class, you first choose a superclass on which you will build.\line \expndtw-2 Make the new class a subclass of the chosen superclass, then add the instance variables\line \expndtw-3 and methods necessary to complete the new class's functionality. Classes are normally\line \expndtw-5 specified using a Class Hierarchy Browser. The following describes the information which\line \expndtw-3 defines a class.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Classes are defined by sending a message to the new or modified class's superclass with\line \expndtw-3 class specification information as arguments. The class information that can be specified\line \expndtw-2 is the following:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The class name}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Whether objects of the class contain pointers or bytes}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Whether objects of the class can contain indexed instance variables}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The names of the named instance variables for objects of the class}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The names of the class variables available to all objects of the class}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-212\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-212\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The names of the pool dictionaries which define shared variables available to\line \expndtw0 objects of the class and possibly other classes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The message which specifies a class is sent to its superclass. There are three class\line \expndtw-2 definition messages. They are as follows:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx662 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 1){\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-5 subclass: subclassSymbol}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 instance VariableNames:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 instanceVariableNameString\line \expndtw-9 class VariableNames: class VariableNameString\line \expndtw-3 poolDictionaries: poolDictionaryNameString}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx662 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 2){\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-6 variableSubclass: subclassSymbol}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 instance VariableNames:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi392\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 instance VariableNameString\line \expndtw-9 class VariableNames: class VariableNameString\line \expndtw-3 poolDictionaries: poolDictionaryNameString}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx662 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 3){\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-6 variableByteSubclass: subclassSymbol}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li785\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 class VariableNames: class VariableNameString\line \expndtw-3 poolDictionaries: poolDictionaryNameString}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1306\margrsxn1952\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-36\shptop644\shpright-36\shpbottom874\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-79\shptop3359\shpright-79\shpbottom8860\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-22\shptop3488\shpright-22\shpbottom4654\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-144\shptop8291\shpright-144\shpbottom9738\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1058\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 i94    Chapter 13: The Smalltalk Language}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1033\fi0\ri18\sb320\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The first two messages define classes whose member objects contain pointers. The first\line \expndtw-2 message specifies objects with named instance variables (zero or more of them). The\line \expndtw-1 second message specifies objects with both named and indexed instance variables.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1033 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The third message defines classes whose member objects contain bytes. Objects with\line \expndtw-3 bytes contain only indexed instance variables, so there is no instance variable name string\line \expndtw0 |{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-4 argument. Objects with bytes define elementary data values such as strings of characters.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1044\fi0\ri0\sb353\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Messages and Methods}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1030\fi0\ri18\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 All processing in a Smalltalk system involves sending messages to objects. Messages are\line \expndtw3 the language of interaction which you use in order to express your computing\line \expndtw-4 requirements to objects. Messages request services from an object in terms of its external\line \expndtw-6 interface.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1030\fi0\ri18\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Methods are the algorithms which are performed by an object in response to receiving\line a message. Methods represent the internal details of the implementation of an object.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1033\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Protocol definitions for a class always have two parts\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\i\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 class \i0 methods and \i instance\line \i0\expndtw-3 methods.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1030\fi0\ri22\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1031\fs22  \i\lang1033 Class \i0 methods implement the messages sent to the class. The receiver of a class message\line \lang1031  \lang1033 is always the class object, not an instance of the class. All classes are global variables and\line \expndtw0 can be referred to by their names.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1030\fi0\ri18\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Instance \i0 methods implement messages sent to instances of the class. The receiver of an\line \expndtw0 instance message is always an object that is an instance of the class.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1026\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A method contains a sequence of Smalltalk expressions. There are four types of\line \expndtw-6 expressions:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1426\fi0\ri0\sb25\sa0\sl-396\slmult0\tx1660 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 1.{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-10 Literals:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1847\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 #aSymbol        #(1 2 4 16)        'magic'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1426\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-396\slmult0\tx1660 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 2.{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-8 Variable names:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1818\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk       x   replacementCollection}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1426\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-396\slmult0\tx1660 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 3.{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-5 Message expressions:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1814\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 bag add: stream next}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1836\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 100 factorial}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1811\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 array at: index + 10 put: Bag new}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1942\margrsxn2327\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 13: The Smalltalk Language    195}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb288\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 4. Blocks of code:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li828\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 [ :x :y I x name <C y name ]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The beginning of a method defines its name, arguments, and any temporary variables that\line \expndtw-2 it uses.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Sending a message involves:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li634\fi-223\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx634 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li634\fi-223\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx634 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent223\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Identifying the object to which the message is sent (the  \i receiver \i0 of the\line \expndtw-8 message).}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li634\fi-223\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx634 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li634\fi-223\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx634 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent223\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Identifying the additional objects that are included in the message (the\line \expndtw-9 message \i arguments).}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0\tx634 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0\tx634 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent224\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Specifying the desired operation to be performed (the message \i selector).}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0\tx634 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0\tx634 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent224\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Accepting the single object that is returned as the message \i answer.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The following sections present the syntax of methods and messages both informally with\line \expndtw-4 examples and more precisely using a syntax metalanguage. The metalanguage definition\line \expndtw-5 appears in Appendix 1. If you find that the informal presentation is sufficient, you can skip\line \expndtw-4 over the syntax rules. A complete syntax summary and cross reference are also presented\line \expndtw-2 in Appendix 1.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb346\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 The Syntax of Variable Names and Literals}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri4\sb205\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Variable names \i0 and \i literals \i0 are the elemental building blocks used in higher-level syntax\line \expndtw-2 forms in Smalltalk.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb328\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Variable Names}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri7\sb194\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A variable name identifies a variable in an object. A variable is a container for an object\line \expndtw-1 pointer. A variable name is a sequence of letters and digits, beginning with a letter.\line \expndtw-3 Example variable names are:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 OrderedCollection       aString       elements       \b0 x2}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri4\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Variable names beginning with an upper case letter represent shared variables, while those\line beginning with a lower-case letter represent private variables. The rules for variable names}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 are:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li428\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 <rule> variableName = identifier.\line \expndtw6 <rule> identifier = letter \{letter \b0 I \b digit\}.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1294\margrsxn1860\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-148\shptop-544\shpright-148\shpbottom11948\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 50165}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1166\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 196    Chapter 13: The Smalltalk Language}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1134\fi0\ri0\sb385\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Literals}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1138\fi0\ri7\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A literal defines an object of class \b Number, String, Character, Symbol, \b0 or \b Array.\line \b0\expndtw-4 Examples are given below where each of the possible literal forms is defined. The syntax\line \expndtw-2 rule for literals is:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3359\fi-1800\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 <Crule> literal = number I string I characterConstant\line \expndtw3 I symbolConstant I arrayConstant.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1138\fi0\ri0\sb331\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Numbers}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1134\fi0\ri0\sb205\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Numbers are objects of class \b Float, Fraction, \b0 or \b Integer. \b0 If a number contains a decimal\line \expndtw-1 point, it is an object of class \b Float. \b0 If it contains a negative exponent and no decimal\line point, it belongs to class \b Fraction. \b0 All other numbers belong to class \b Integer. \b0 If the\line \expndtw-2 number includes r, the digits preceding \b r \b0 define the number radix. In this case, capital\line \expndtw2 letters are used to represent digit values greater than 9, with A = 10, B = 11, etc.\line \expndtw-3 Example numbers are:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex41\expndtw-5\dn0\loch\af1\dbch\af3\hich\f1\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 \u9632\'3f\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tx1552 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw21\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 t,{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i0\expndtw0 15        l6rFF       3.1416        le-3       -100}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1130\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The rules for numbers are:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1555\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 <rule> number = [digits "r"] ["-"] bigDigits [". "bigDigits] ["e" ["-"]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3517\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 digits].}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2398\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1031\fs22  \lang1033 digits = digit \{digit\}.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1555\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 <rule> digit = "0" I "1" I "2" I "3" I "4" I "5" I "6" I "7" I "8" I "9".\line \expndtw13 <rule> bigDigits = bigDigit \{bigDigit\}.\line \expndtw6 ^rule^ bigDigit = digit I capitalLetter.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1134\fi0\ri0\sb331\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Strings}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1134\fi0\ri0\sb205\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A string is a sequence of characters enclosed in apostrophes. It is an object of class \b String\line \b0\expndtw-4 which is a sequence of objects of class \b Character \b0 that can be indexed. Strings are not\line necessarily constant; their characters may be changed by sending a message to the string.\line \expndtw-2 Paired apostrophes within a string reduce to a single apostrophe in the resultant string\line object. Example strings are:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1534\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'hello'       \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'b0\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0        \super f\nosupersub isn"t'       ' "comment in string" \b0 '}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1130\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The rules for strings are:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1859\margrsxn2428\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri4\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 13: The Smalltalk Language    197}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi0\ri0\sb281\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex74\expndtw19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 <rule> string = " ' " \{character | \expndtw93\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'ab\'ab\'95\'bb\'95\expndtw19\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  | \expndtw82\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'ab\'bb\'95\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 \}"\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 ".}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li428\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex74\expndtw24\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 <rule> character = letter I digit I selectorCharacter I "[" I "]" I "(" I ")"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2509\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex80\expndtw23\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I \scaps "a" \scaps0 I "\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f2\cchs0 \'95\hich\af0\dbch\af2\loch\f0\cchs0 " I "A" I "#" I \expndtw36 **\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f2\cchs0 \'95\hich\af0\dbch\af2\loch\f0\cchs0 "\expndtw23  I " " I "f" I "I" I \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f2\cchs0 \'95\hich\af0\dbch\af2\loch\f0\cchs0 * "}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li428\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 <rule> selectorCharaaer = "," I "+" I "/" I "\\" I "*" I "~" I ">" I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3229\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex57\expndtw29\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 "<" I "=" I "@" I "%" I "I" I "&" I "?" I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb522\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex80\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Comments}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb191\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A \b0 comment is a sequence of characters enclosed in double quotes. A comment is ignored\line \expndtw-2 anywhere within a method, except when occurring within a string. Example comments\line \b\expndtw-23 are:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb18\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Answer the \b0 size \b of the \b0 receiver"       \b "goodBye"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The rule for comments is:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li428\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex67\expndtw32\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 <rule> comment = ' " ' \{character \b0 I \expndtw94\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 '\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 \}\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'ab\'bb\dbch\af3\loch\af1\hich\f1\cchs0 \u9830\'3f\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 .}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb288\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex80\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Character Constants}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb191\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A character constant is an object of class \b Character. A \b0 character constant appears as a\line \expndtw-1 dollar sign followed by any character. Example character constants are:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb119\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 $$       $a       |<       $       $.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The rule for character constants is:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li428\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 <rule> characterConstant = "$" character \b0 I "$" " ' " I "$" * " '.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb306\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex80\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Symbols}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb187\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A \b0 symbol is an object of class \b Symbol, \b0 a sequence of objects of class \b Character \b0 which\line \expndtw-2 can be indexed. Symbols'differ from strings in that their characters may not be changed.\line \expndtw1 A symbol constant identifies the associated symbol object. The form of a symbol\line \expndtw-3 constant is a number sign, #, followed by the characters of the symbol. Example symbol\line \expndtw-2 constants are:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi432\ri0\sb22\sa0\sl-403\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 #+        #asOrderedCollection        #at:put:        \b0 # = =\line \expndtw0\fs22 The rules for symbols and symbol constants are:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2376\margrsxn1912\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li43\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 198    Chapter 13: The Smalltalk Language}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li432\fi0\ri0\sb317\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1231 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw14\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 <rule>{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw5 symbolConstant = "#" symbol.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li436\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1231 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 <rule>{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-1 symbol = unarySelector I binarySelector I keyword \{keyword\}.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li432\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1231 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 <rule!>{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-1 unarySelector = identifier.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1231 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw15\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 <rule>{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-4 binarySelector = selectorCharacter [selectorCharacter] I "\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  ".}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li428\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1231 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 <Crule>{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw5 keyword = identifier \b0 ":".}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb328\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Arrays}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb202\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 An array is an object of class \b Array \b0 which may be indexed by an integer from one\line \expndtw-3 through the \i size \i0 of the array. An array is a series of literals enclosed in parentheses. An\line \expndtw-1 array constant identifies the associated array object. It consists of an array preceded by\line a number sign. Example array constants and arrays are:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li432\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 #('red' 'blue' 'green')}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li436\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 #(yes no)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li428\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 #(1 'two'three $4 (5))}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The rules for arrays and array constants are:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 <rule> arrayConstant = "#" array.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li432\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 <Crule!> array = "(" \{number I string I symbol I array I characterConstant\}}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb562\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex81\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Expression Syntax}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The actions in a method are specified by a series of expressions separated by periods. A\line \expndtw0 period is optional after the last expression \i of \i0 the series. Each expression computes \b a\line \b0\expndtw-4 single object as its result. The expression may also include assignment of its result to one\line \expndtw-3 or more variables.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The final expression in an expression series may be preceded by a caret, \scaps a. \scaps0 The caret\line \expndtw0 means that method execution terminates and answers the object computed by the\line \expndtw-5 expression.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The rules for expressions and expression series are:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1267\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1031\fs22  \lang1033 expressionSeries = \{expression "."\} \scaps [["a"] \scaps0 expression],\line \expndtw0\lang1031  \lang1033 expression = \{variableName ":="\} (primary I messageExpression}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li432\fi2228\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 \{";" cascadeMessage\}).\line \expndtw0 <Crule> primary = variableName I literal I block I "(" expression \b0 ")".}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A message expression is a request to an object (the receiver of the message) to perform\line \expndtw-2 a computation and return an object as the answer. There are three kinds of message\line \expndtw-4 expressions: \i unary, binary, \i0 and \i keyword\i0 (n-ary). Each has a different precedence and \b a\line \b0\expndtw0 different syntax for its selector, the name of the message.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2036\margrsxn2263\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 13: The Smalltalk Language    199}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb302\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A unary expression sends a series of unary messages which are evaluated from left to\line \expndtw-1 right. A unary message has no arguments.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A binary expression sends a series of binary messages which are evaluated from left to\line \expndtw0 right. A binary message has a single argument following the binary selector. The\line \expndtw-3 traditional arithmetic operators are implemented in Smalltalk using binary expressions.\line \expndtw-1 This gives all arithmetic operators the same precedence. Parentheses may be used to\line specify other than left-to-right evaluation.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A keyword expression sends a single keyword message with one or more arguments. The\line \expndtw0 arguments to a keyword message are evaluated from left to right.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri7\sb144\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The selector of a keyword message is the concatenation of all the keywords in the\line \expndtw-7 message.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Unary expressions have highest precedence, followed by binary and then keyword.\line \expndtw-1 Parentheses may be used to specify a different evaluation order.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Cascaded messages \i0 are a series of messages to the same receiver. Each message after the first\line \expndtw-1 is preceded by a semicolon.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The rules for message expressions are:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1264\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1031\fs22  \lang1033 messageExpression = unary Expression I binary Expression I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3391\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx7394 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 keywordExpression.{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw0 ^}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1260\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1031\fs22  \lang1033 cascadeMessage = unary Message I binaryMessage I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qc\li137\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 keywordMessage.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 <rule> unaryExpression = primary unary Message \{unary Message\}.\line \expndtw-4 ^Crule^ binary Expression = (unaryExpression I primary) binaryMessage}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi2844\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 \{binaryMessage\}.\line \expndtw1 <rule> keywordExpression = (binaryExpression I primary)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1264\fi2200\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 keywordMessage.\line \expndtw-2\lang1031  \lang1033 unaryMessage = unarySelector.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li428\fi842\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1031\fs22  \lang1033 binaryMessage = binarySelector (unaryExpression I primary).\line \expndtw-1 <Crule> keywordMessage = keyword (binaryExpression I primary) I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3254\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 \{keyword (binaryExpression 1 primary)\}.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb328\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Blocks}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb202\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A \b0\i block \i0 is a part of a method enclosed in square brackets. It is an object describing\line \expndtw-1 executable code. Blocks may be nested.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A block may have arguments. These are specified between the left bracket and vertical\line \expndtw0 bar by preceding each block argument variable name with a colon.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1321\margrsxn1865\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw720\colsr378\colno2\colw7624\linebetcol \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs14 Ill}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb414\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 200    Chapter 13: The Smalltalk Language}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb317\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The result of block execution is the final expression in the block. A block with no\line arguments is executed by sending it the message \b value.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb140\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A block with one argument is executed by sending it the message \b value:. \b0 The \b argument\line \b0\expndtw1 to the \b value: \b0 message is assigned to the block argument upon block execution.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A two-argument block is executed by sending it the message \b value:value:. The\line \expndtw-1 value:value: \b0 arguments are assigned to the block arguments.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A block may contain an expression preceded by a caret, \scaps a. \scaps0 Evaluation of such an\line \expndtw-2 expression causes termination of execution for both the block and the method in which\line \expndtw0 the block appears.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Blocks are the basis for control structures in Smalltalk. Since control structures conform\line \expndtw-1 to keyword message syntax, control structures have no special syntax.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The rule for blocks is:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 <rule> block \b0 = "[" [\{":" \b variableName\} \b0 "I"] \b expressionSeries "]".}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb360\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Method Syntax}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A complete \i method specification \i0 includes a message pattern, optional primitive number,\line \expndtw-4 optional temporaries, and an expression series. The message pattern specifies how to send\line \expndtw-3 a message to request method execution. It includes the method selector and the variable\line \expndtw1 names used to refer to arguments within the method.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The rules for method syntax are:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li428\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 <rule> method = messagePattera [primitiveNumber] [temporaries]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2358\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 expressionSeries.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3060\fi-1796\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1031\fs22  \lang1033 messagePattera = unarySelector I binarySelector variableName I\line \expndtw-1 keyword variableName \{keyword\line \expndtw-3 variableName\}.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 <rule> primitiveNumber = "<" "primitive:" number ">".\line \expndtw135 ^!^\expndtw0  temporaries ,= "I" \{variableName\} "I".}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb356\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Control Structures}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Control structures are invoked by sending messages with blocks as arguments. Three\line \expndtw-4 forms, with several variations, are predefined in the Smalltalk language. You may define\line \expndtw-1 additional forms in Smalltalk using these predefined ones.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2223\margrsxn2051\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri25\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 13: The Smalltalk Language    201}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb371\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Conditional Execution}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb194\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The following predefined conditional execution messages are available:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifTrue: aBlock}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifFalse: aBlock}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifTrue: trueBlock ifFalse: falseBlock}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ifFalse: falseBlock ifTrue: trueBlock}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In \b0 all cases, the receiver expression must be of class \b Boolean \b0 and the arguments must be\line \expndtw-2 blocks with no arguments. The ifTrue: argument block (if present) is sent the message\line \b value, \b0 if and only if, the receiver has the value \b true. \b0 The \b ifFalse: \b0 argument block (if\line present) is sent the message \b value, \b0 if and only if, the receiver has the value \b false. \b0 The\line answer of the conditional messages is the last expression in the executed block or nil if\line \expndtw1 no block is executed.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb342\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Iterative Execution}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb191\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The following predefined iterative execution messages are available:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 whileTrue: aBlock\line whileFalse: aBlock}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri18\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Both the receiver and argument of these messages must be no-argument blocks. For\line \b whileTrue:, \b0 the receiver block is sent the message \b value. \b0 If it answers true, the argument\line block is sent the message \b value. \b0 The iteration continues until the answer of the first block\line \expndtw-3 evaluation is false. For \b whileFalse:, \b0 the sequence is the same but the iteration continues\line until the answer of the first block evaluation is true. The answer of both \b whileTrue: \b0 and\line \b\expndtw-6 'whileFalse: \b0 is always nil.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb342\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 Short Circuit Boolean Evaluation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb187\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The following predefined boolean operators are available:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 and: aBlock\line \expndtw-2 or: aBlock}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri22\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The receiver of each of these methods must be of class \b Boolean \b0 and the argument must\line \expndtw-3 be a block. For \b and:, \b0 if the receiver is true, the block is sent the message \b value, \b0 and the\line \expndtw-4 answer of the message is the last block expression. If, however, the receiver of the \b and:\line \b0 message is false, the answer is false, and the block is not evaluated. For or:, if the receiver\line \expndtw-2 is false, the block is sent the message \b value, \b0 and the answer of the message is the last\line \expndtw-3 block expression. If, however, the receiver of the \b or: \b0 message is true, the answer is true,\line \expndtw1 and the block is not evaluated.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1301\margrsxn10608\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720 {\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2115\margrsxn2184\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 14 SMALLTALK/V 286 CLASSES}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri4\sb443\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This section describes the major \b Smalltalk/V \b0 classes. These classes serve as the basic\line \expndtw-1 building blocks for your applications.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb378\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Magnitudes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \b magnitude \b0 classes are the easiest to understand and the most frequently used. They\line \expndtw-2 define objects that can be compared, measured, ordered, and counted. These include\line \expndtw-4 characters, numbers, dates, and times. Many useful messages for comparing, testing, and\line \expndtw-3 ordering these objects are defined. The arithmetic operators and many useful numerical\line \expndtw-2 functions are also defined as messages understood by the numerical magnitude objects.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This chapter presents a quick overview of each of the magnitude classes provided in\line \b\expndtw-4 Smalltalk/V. \b0 Examples are used to demonstrate some of the functionality provided. \b Part\line \expndtw-3 4: The Encyclopedia of Classes \b0 gives a detailed specification of each of the magnitude\line \expndtw-8 classes.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \b Magnitude \b0 class hierarchy shown below lists all of the magnitude classes.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Magnitude}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Association\line \expndtw-12 Character\line \expndtw-4 Date\line \expndtw-1 Number\line \expndtw-8 Float\line Fraction\line \expndtw-2 Integer}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 LargeNegativelnteger\line \expndtw-2 LargePositivelnteger\line \expndtw-3 Smalllnteger\line \expndtw-4 Time}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2318\margrsxn1973\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 204    Chapter 14: Smalltalk/ V 286 Classes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb371\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Magnitude}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 All of the magnitude classes are subclasses of the abstract class \b Magnitude. \b0 Class\line \b\expndtw-4 Magnitude \b0 provides the comparing and ordering protocol inherited by its subclasses. All\line \expndtw-2 magnitudes support comparing, ordering, and interval testing. \b Magnitude \b0 assumes its\line \expndtw3 subclasses implement the ordering relation and comparison methods: =, <=, >=, <,\line \expndtw0 >, ~=s. Based on these methods, \b Magnitude \b0 provides generic methods for interval\line \expndtw-2 testing and max/min computation inherited by all \b magnitude \b0 classes. Some numerical\line \expndtw-5 examples are:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb173\sa0\tx3071 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Expression{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\ul0\expndtw-3 Answer}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb194\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3035 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 46 > 33{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\expndtw-3 true}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3035 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 46 min: 33{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-22 33}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3035 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 46 max: 33{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-13 46}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3035 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 5/4 between: 0.5 and: 1{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\expndtw-7 false}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb310\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Character}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb205\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The instances of class \b Character \b0 are the extended ASCII character set from ASCII value\line 0 to ASCII value 255. Characters are pre-existing objects in Smalltalk, hence they do not\line \expndtw-2 have to be created. References to characters are made in two ways: as literals or by\line \expndtw-3 converting integers into the corresponding ASCII character. There are two conversion\line \expndtw-5 messages. The message \b asCharacter \b0 can be sent to an integer, or the message \b value: \b0 with\line \expndtw-1 an integer argument can be sent to class \b Character. \b0 For example:}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2710\margrsxn3770\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx7\posnegy119\absw0\absh-259\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Character}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx2084\posnegy119\absw0\absh-259\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Literal}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri18\sb173\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Equivalent Expression}\par \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-425 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1411 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2858 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx5428 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 A}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li554\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 $A}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 65 asCharacter}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-238 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1411 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2858 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx5428 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 B}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li551\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 IB}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 66 \i0 asCharacter}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-238 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx1411 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2858 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx5428 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscal