\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  abstract classes, there are no instances}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1098\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Internal \b0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  ordered by the internal contents of the collection\line N.A. \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  not applicable (determined by subclasses)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In the table, the only collections that have the same attribute values are the \b String\line \expndtw-3 Symbol \b0 pair and \b Dictionary IdentityDictionary \b0 pair. The difference between a \b String\line \b0\expndtw-4 and a \b Symbol \b0 is that a \b Symbol \b0 is guaranteed to be unique while a \b String \b0 can have many\line \expndtw-2 copies. The difference between a \b Dictionary \b0 and an \b IdentityDictionary \b0 is that during\line \expndtw2 the key lookup comparison, the former uses the = message while the latter uses \super =ss\nosupersub .}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2318\margrsxn1952\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 224    Chapter 14: Smalltalk/ V 286 Classes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb374\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Conversions}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb209\sa32\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Because the various collection classes have different attributes, being able to convert from\line \expndtw3 one kind of collection to another is useful. \b Smalltalk/V \b0 provides the following\line \expndtw0 conversion protocol in class \b Collection.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2717\margrsxn3413\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1868\colsr821\colno2\colw3088 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-436\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Methods}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4\shptop-25\shpright803\shpbottom-25\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 15875}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-436\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 asArray}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-436\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 asBag}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-436\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 asSet}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-436\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 asOrderedCollection}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-436\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 asSortedCollection}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi-396\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 asSortedCollection:\line \expndtw-1 sortBlock}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-436\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Comments}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft0\shptop-32\shpright972\shpbottom-32\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 18415}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-436\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Ordering is possibly arbitrary.\line \expndtw-1 Duplicates are kept.\line \expndtw-2 Duplicates are eliminated.\line Ordering is possibly arbitrary.\line \expndtw3 Each element is <= its successor.\line \expndtw-2 Ordering is specified by \b sortBlock.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2318\margrsxn1952\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Thus any collection can be converted into an \b Array, a Bag, \b0 a \b Set, \b0 an \b OrderedCollection,\line \b0\expndtw-2 or a \b SortedCollection.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb367\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Instance Creation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb209\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Like other classes, message \b new \b0 can be used to create an instance of any collection.\line \expndtw-3 Message \b new: \b0 can be used to create a fixed-size collection with a specified size and \b a\line \b0\expndtw-2 variable size collection with a specified initial allocation size.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Some collections may be expressed in literal form:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb169\sa187\tx3089 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Class{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-2 Instance in literal form}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2717\margrsxn3878\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw720\colsr1973\colno2\colw2620 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2682\shptop-180\shpright4835\shpbottom-180\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 15875}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 String}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Symbol}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Array}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'John Mary'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 #John}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 #($J 'John' John (John 3))}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2318\margrsxn1952\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb130\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A literal string is enclosed in a pair of quotes, a literal symbol is preceded by a number\line sign (#), and a literal array is enclosed in paired parentheses and preceded by a number\line sign. The \b Array \b0 example contains four elements: a character, a string, a symbol, and\line \expndtw-3 another array which has two elements \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  a symbol and a small integer. Notice that within\line \expndtw-5 a literal array, a symbol or another array element must not be prefixed with \b a \b0 number sign.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb140\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In addition, there is protocol in every collection class to create instances with one, two,\line \expndtw0 three, or four elements which are not necessarily constants. For example,}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1967\margrsxn2332\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri4\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 14: Smalltalk/ V 286 Classes    225}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li994\fi-594\ri0\sb299\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Array with: 'Daughters of John'\line \expndtw5 with: #('Ann' 'Mary')}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 creates an array with two elements, a string and another array of two elements.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb313\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex83\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Common Protocol}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb202\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V \b0 provides common protocol to manipulate collections in \b a \b0 uniform way.\line These can be categorized as adding new elements, removing elements, testing the\line \expndtw-3 occurrences of elements, and enumerating elements. These are all described in \b Part 4:\line \expndtw-2 Encyclopedia of Classes \b0 under class \b Collection.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Suppose you have two global variables, \b Customer \b0 and \b Supplier, \b0 initialized as:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Customer \b0 : = \b Bag with: #John.\line \expndtw6 Supplier := #(John Peter).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Then you send adding, removing, and testing messages to \b Customer:}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2367\margrsxn2991\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx2578\posnegy126\absw0\absh-259\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-