b\cellx331 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx727 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 34}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-187 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx331 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx727 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 4^S}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ) 2}\cell \pard\intbl\row \pard\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1319\margrsxn1929\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\pard\plain \sb122\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1319\margrsxn1929\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw720\colsr331\colno2\colw7610\linebetcol \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex63\expndtw12\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 i I '}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb166\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex83\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Rectangle}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb205\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A Rectangle references a rectangular block of bits contained in a Form. It is represented\line \expndtw2 by two Points: the top left Point, called \b origin, \b0 and the bottom right Point, called\line \b\expndtw1 corner. \b0 Its width and height can then be calculated by:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 width := corner x - origin x\line height := corner y - origin \b0 y}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1948\margrsxn2326\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4644\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 14: Smalltalk/ V 286 Classes    243}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1948\margrsxn2326\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li29\fi0\ri0\sb310\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 which represent the number of columns and the number of rows of bits contained in the\line \expndtw3 Rectangle. The Point represented by \b width @ height \b0 is called the \i extent \i0 of the\line \expndtw-1 Rectangle. A simpler way to calculate the extent is:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 extent := corner - origin.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A \b0 Rectangle is usually created by sending the \b corner: \b0 or \b extent: \b0 message to a Point. For\line \expndtw-2 example, the following two expressions create two Rectangles covering the same area:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li436\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-248\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 1 \b0 @ \b 1 corner: 100 @ 100\line \expndtw5 1 @ 1 extent: 99 \b0 @ 99}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To illustrate the Rectangle instance messages, consider these rectangles, \b Boxl \b0 and \b Box2:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Boxl := 20 @ 0 corner: 150 @100.\line \expndtw4 Box2 := 70 @ 80 corner: 170 @ 120.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi0\ri0\sb176\sa0\tx3827 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Expression{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\ul0\expndtw-4 Result}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb194\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3769 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Boxl top{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw0 0}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3769 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Boxl bottom{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\expndtw-18 100}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3769 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Box2 left{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-16 70}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3769 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Box2 right{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\expndtw-18 170}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx4392\posnegy-32\absw0\absh-230\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 50}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3769 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Boxl center{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw3 85 @}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3769 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Boxl width{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\expndtw-19 130}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3769 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Boxl height{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\expndtw-18 100}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3769 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Boxl origin{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw1 20 @}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx4172\posnegy-58\absw0\absh-263\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 @ 100}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3769 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Boxl corner{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\expndtw-19 150}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3769 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Boxl containsPoint: 50 \b0 @ \b 50{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\expndtw-3 true}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx4370\posnegy-32\absw1966\absh-1922\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 -10 corner: 160 @ 110\line \expndtw-1 90 corner: 160 @ 110}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx4370\posnegy-32\absw1966\absh-1922\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 80 corner: 150 @ 100\line \expndtw-1 0 corner: 170 @ 120\line \expndtw-3 10 corner: 160 @ 110\line 10 corner: 160 @ 110\line \expndtw-1 10 corner: 130 @ 50}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3769 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Boxl expandBy: 10{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\expndtw-1 10 \b @}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3769 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Box2 insetBy: 10{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw3 80 @}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3769 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Boxl intersects: Box2{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\expndtw-3 true}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3769 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Boxl intersect: Box2{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw2 70 @}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3769 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Boxl merge: Box2{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }20 @}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3769 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Boxl translateBy: 10 @ 10{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }30 @}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3769 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Boxl moveBy: 10 @ 10{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw0 30 @}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx3769 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Box2 moveTo: Boxl origin{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\b0\expndtw-1 30 @}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Notice that the last two expressions modify Boxl and Box2 themselves, while others\line \expndtw-3 create new rectangles.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2420\margrsxn1845\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 244    Chapter 14: Smalltalk/ V 286 Classes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb364\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Form}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Class \b Form \b0 is a subclass of \b DisplayMedium, \b0 which in turn is a subclass of\line \b\expndtw-2 DisplayObject. DisplayObject \b0 and \b DisplayMedium \b0 are abstract classes; they do not\line \expndtw-3 hold any data. Their purpose is to group related methods together to be inherited by their\line \expndtw-2 subclasses. \b (Part 4 \b0 lists their protocols.)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri14\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As we said earlier, the main purpose of a \b Form \b0 is to provide a two dimensional view for\line \expndtw-5 a bitmap. It therefore has three instance variables: \b bits \b0 (which contains the bitmap), \b width\line \b0\expndtw-1 (which contains the number of pixels horizontally), and \b height \b0 (containing the number\line \expndtw-4 of pixels vertically).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A Form also has three additional instance variables:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-205\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-205\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 offset is the Point on the DisplayScreen from which this Form was originally\line \expndtw-1 copied by sending message \b fromDisplay: \b0 to class \b Form.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-205\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-205\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 byte Width \b0 is the number of bytes (8 bits) horizontally. For efficiency reasons,\line \expndtw2 each row of a Form is allocated in an integral number of words (16 bits).\line \expndtw-1 Therefore, if its width is not an integral of the word size, the remainder bits\line \expndtw0 in the last word of each row (the right hand side of the Form) are not used.\line This, however, is transparent to the user.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-205\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-205\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx619 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 deviceType denotes the type of device to which this Form belongs. When an\line \expndtw0 instance of class \b Form \b0 is created, its \b deviceType \b0 is automatically set to 0,\line \expndtw-4 indicating that the Form resides in regular memory. When an instance of class\line \b\expndtw-1 DisplayScreen \b0 (a subclass of \b Form) \b0 is created, it is set to 1, indicating that\line \expndtw-2 the Form serves as the buffer for the display screen, whose address and size\line \expndtw3 are dictated by the graphics adapter and graphics mode being used. The\line \expndtw-2 values of \b deviceType \b0 for instances of class \b ColorScreen, ColorForm, \b0 and\line \b BiColorForm \b0 are 1, 3, and 2, respectively.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri18\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A global variable, called \b Display, \b0 contains an instance of either \b ColorScreen \b0 or\line \b\expndtw-4 DisplayScreen. \b0 Every time you change the graphics mode, this variable is reinitialized to\line \expndtw-3 the exact size of the screen. It can be used as any other Form; when pixels are moved to\line \expndtw0 Display, however, they are automatically shown on the screen. In other words, the\line \expndtw-1 contents of Display reflect your monitor's display screen.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri22\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The following messages can be sent to class \b Form, BiColorForm, \b0 or \b ColorForm \b0 to\line \expndtw-3 create new instances:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 fromDisplay: aRectangle}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Answer a new Form whose extent equals \b aRectangle's \b0 extent and whose\line \expndtw0 content is copied from \b aRectangle \b0 area of the display screen.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 width: wlnteger height: hlnteger}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Answer a white Form whose width is \b wlnteger \b0 and height is \b hlnteger.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1915\margrsxn2348\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 14: Smalltalk/ V 286 Classes    245}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri22\sb302\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You can also use the message \b Form new \b0 to create an instance of Form, and then use one\line \expndtw-2 of the following messages to initialize its variables:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 extent: aPoint}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Change the receiver width and height to the coordinates of \b aPoint.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 width: w height: h}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Change the receiver width to \b w \b0 and height to h, and allocate its bitmap with the\line \expndtw-4 appropriate size.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 width: w height: h initialByte: aByte}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Change the receiver width to \b w \b0 and height to h, and initialize every byte in the\line \expndtw-1 bitmap to \b aByte.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Other useful messages are \b copy:from:to:rule:, \b0 which copies the contents of one Form to\line \expndtw-5 another Form; \b display At: \b0 and \b displayOn:at:clippingBox:rule:mask:, \b0 which display the\line \expndtw1 contents of a Form on the screen; and \b outputToPrinter, \b0 which shows the contents of\line \b\expndtw-3 a \b0 Form on a printer. Refer to the description of \b Form \b0 in \b Part 4, The Encyclopedia of\line \expndtw-1 Classes, \b0 for the definition of these messages.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb360\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 BitBIt}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri18\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Class \b BitBIt \b0 ("bit block transfer") describes all the parameters in a basic bitmapped\line operation. This basic bitmapped operation is to move a block of bits from one place to\line \expndtw-4 another. More complicated operations, such as drawing a line, involve a sequence of such\line basic moves. This basic operation can sometimes become rather complicated when all the\line \expndtw-3 parameters are involved.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri25\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This basic operation works as follows. You first define a source rectangle on the source\line form. The bits from the source rectangle are combined with the bits from the mask form\line \expndtw-2 with a logical AND operation. The resulting rectangle of bits are combined into the\line \expndtw0 destination Form rectangle with a specified combination rule and clipping rectangle.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri29\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The following figure shows how the parameters of a BitBIt determine which bits are\line \expndtw-1 involved in the bit transfer:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2368\margrsxn1923\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 246    Chapter 14: Smalltalk/ V 286 Classes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri3708\sb335\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 source form}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn8035\margrsxn2250\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-238\shptop1134\shpright-238\shpbottom2797\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft1915\shptop1134\shpright1915\shpbottom2797\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}{\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx-3420\posnegy76\absw0\absh1692\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw5613\pich2984\picwgoal3182\pichgoal1692\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li72\fi0\ri0\sb817\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 destination form}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-245\shptop79\shpright1937\shpbottom79\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb270\sa630 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 clipping rectangle}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2699\margrsxn2110\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1303\colsr3996\colno2\colw1800 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft5537\shptop-572\shpright6523\shpbottom-572\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft5544\shptop-11\shpright6530\shpbottom-11\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 14.2\line \b\expndtw-7 BitBlt \b0 Clipping}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb86\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 destination rectangle}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2368\margrsxn1923\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb742\sa785\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 And the following figure shows how the resultant bits are produced by combining the\line source bits, mask bits, and destination bits with a combination rule:}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn5770\margrsxn2128\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw950\colsr2243\colno2\colw817 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-140\shptop-385\shpright1084\shpbottom-385\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2984\shptop-364\shpright4208\shpbottom-364\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-140\shptop-378\shpright-140\shpbottom608\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft1062\shptop-385\shpright1062\shpbottom609\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft2992\shptop-364\shpright2992\shpbottom622\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft4187\shptop-364\shpright4187\shpbottom622\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 source bits}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb14\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 mask bits}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2368\margrsxn1923\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft3262\shptop356\shpright4486\shpbottom356\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 15875}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft6394\shptop371\shpright7611\shpbottom371\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 15875}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft6394\shptop1681\shpright7618\shpbottom1681\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 15875}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft3269\shptop1688\shpright4493\shpbottom1688\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft7596\shptop1681\shpright7596\shpbottom2667\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}{\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx4932\posnegy2012\absw0\absh-278\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 -Xfule)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx6588\posnegy2045\absw0\absh-209\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 result bits}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3409\fi0\ri0\sb1987\sa0\sl-187\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 destination\line bits}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li346\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-209\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 14.3\line \b\expndtw-4 BitBlt \b0 Operation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb245\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 For example, suppose}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 source bits = 11110000\line \expndtw4 mask bits = 10101010\line dest bits = 00001111\line \expndtw7 rule = logical OR}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 the resultant bits would be 10101111. These resultant bits are then stored back to the\line \expndtw-2 destination Form.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1891\margrsxn2382\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri22\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 14: Smalltalk/ V 286 Classes    247}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb310\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A mask form has a fixed width and height of 16. If its extent is smaller than the source\line \expndtw-2 rectangle extent, its bits are repeated both horizontally and vertically up to the extent of\line \expndtw2 the source form rectangle so that they can be ANDed with each bit in the source\line \expndtw-2 rectangle. The effect of this is to provide a halftone texture for the 1 bits in the source\line \expndtw0 rectangle. The mask Form, therefore, is also called \i halftone \i0 Form. The following\line \expndtw-5 expressions obtain a prebuilt mask Form whose halftone is the same as the message name:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Form white\line \expndtw-5 Form black\line \expndtw-4 Form gray\line \expndtw-8 Form darkGray\line \expndtw-4 Form lightGray}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri22\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When the destination is a \b ColorForm \b0 or \b ColorScreen \b0 and the source is a \b Form \b0 or\line \b BiColorForm, \b0 a \b BiColorForm \b0 mask can be used to obtain colors other than black and\line \expndtw-5 white. This is not a rare case. For example, both \b Pen \b0 and \b CharacterScanner \b0 instances are\line \expndtw-3 in this category. \b BiColorForm \b0 has two additional instance variables: \b foreColor \b0 and\line \b backColor. \b0 When a \b BiColorForm \b0 is used as a mask form, its \b foreColor \b0 specifies the\line \expndtw-2 color for 1 bits and the \b backColor \b0 the color of 0 bits after source bits are ANDed with\line \expndtw-5 the mask form bits. To obtain a \b BiColorForm \b0 mask, the following messages can be used:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 BiColorForm color: 1.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 BiColorForm gray foreColor: 2 backColor: 4.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri25\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The first message returns a 16 by 16 \b BiColorForm \b0 whose bitmap consists of all 1 bits and\line \expndtw0 whose foreground color is 1 (blue) and background color is 0. The second message\line \expndtw-3 returns a 16 by 16 \b BiColorForm \b0 whose bitmap consists of alternating 0 and 1 bits and\line \expndtw1 whose foreground color is 2 and background color is 4.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa191\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When the ANDed source bits are combined into the destination form rectangle, two\line \expndtw-6 additional parameters must be specified. One is the \i combination rule, \i0 which indicates how\line \expndtw-2 the source bits are to be combined with the destination bits. The other is the \i clipping\line \expndtw1 rectangle, \i0 which limits the affected area on the destination form. To access the\line \expndtw0 combination rules supported by \b Smalltalk/V, \b0 send a message to class \b Form:}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2298\margrsxn3343\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1328\colsr1336\colno2\colw3603 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Form over\line Form orRule\line \expndtw-6 Form andRule\line \expndtw-5 Form under\line \expndtw-7 Form erase\line \expndtw-5 Form reverse\line \expndtw-4 Form orThru}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 destination becomes source}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 source OR into destination}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 source AND into destination}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 source AND into destination}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 if source is 1 then destination becomes 0}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 source XOR into destination}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 first erase without specifying mask Form,}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 then OR with mask Form specified}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1891\margrsxn2382\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb112\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The final affected area on the destination Form can be simulated as follows:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2450\margrsxn1835\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 248    Chapter 14: Smalltalk/ V 286 Classes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb310\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 AffectedRect : = sourceRect intersect:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2380\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 sourceForm boundingBox.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 AffectedRect moveBy: destRect origin - sourceRect origin.\line \expndtw-2 AffectedRect : =}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1192\fi-389\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ((AffectedRect intersect: destRect)\line intersect: destForm boundingBox)\line \expndtw-3 intersect: clipping Rect}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The instance variables of \b BitBlt \b0 are:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 sourceForm destForm halftone rule width height}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 sourceX sourceY destX destY clipX clipY clip Width clipHeight}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri11\sb140\sa0\sl-252\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Refer to \b BitBlt \b0 in \b Part 4, The Encyclopedia of Classes \b0 for a description of all instance\line \expndtw-7 variables.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb18\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In terms of instance variables, the source rectangle is defined as:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi400\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 sourceX @ sourceY extent: width (8) height\line \b0\expndtw-1 The destination rectangle is defined as:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi396\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 destX \b0 @ \b destY extent: width (S> height\line \b0 And the clipping rectangle is defined as:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 clipX \i @ \i0 clipY extent: clip Width @ clipHeight}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri4\sb126\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \b sourceForm \b0 must be either nil or an instance of \b Form \b0 or its subclasses. The \b halftone\line \b0\expndtw-2 must be either nil or an instance of \b Form \b0 or \b BiColorForm \b0 with width and height equal\line to 16. The \b destForm \b0 must be an instance of \b Form \b0 or its subclasses. All other instance\line \expndtw-3 variables must be a Smalllnteger. When \b sourceForm \b0 or \b halftone \b0 is nil, it implies an\line \expndtw0 infinitely large form with contents of all 1 bits.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Note that the source form and destination form can be the same form. In this case, the\line only complication is that the source rectangle may overlay the destination rectangle.\line When such overlay occurs, the operation is carried out as if the source bits were first\line \expndtw-2 copied to an intermediate Form, and then copied to the destination rectangle. The actual\line implementation still amounts to a single move and is as efficient as moving between\line \expndtw-5 different Forms.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi-392\ri0\sb25\sa0\sl-392\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To create an instance of class \b BitBlt, \b0 use the class message:\line \b\expndtw-8 destForm:sourceForm:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li25\fi0\ri7\sb133\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 with only destination and source forms specified. Other instance variables are supplied\line \expndtw-2 with the following default values:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1951\margrsxn2322\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 14: Smalltalk/V 286 Classes    249}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb302\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 sourceX := source Y := 0.\line \expndtw9 destX := destY := 0.\line \expndtw8 clipX := clipY : = 0.\line \expndtw3 width := destForm width,\line \expndtw1 height : = destForm height,\line clip Width := destForm width.\line \expndtw2 clipHeight := destForm height,\line \expndtw4 rule := Form over}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri14\sb155\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If \b0 you want to specify all parameters, use \b BitBlt new \b0 to create an instance, and then send\line \expndtw0 the following message to the new instance to set the instance variables:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 destForm:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 sourceForm:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 halftone:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 combinationRule:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 destOrigin:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 sourceOrigin:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 extent:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 clipRect:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Class \b BitBlt \b0 supplies numerous messages to change a small number of parameters. Refer\line \expndtw0 to \b BitBlt \b0 in \b Part 4, The Encyclopedia of Classes \b0 for a list of all messages.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 After the parameters have been correctly set up, two messages in class \b BitBlt \b0 actually\line \expndtw-1 move the bits:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb25\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 copyBits}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 does exactly what it says: moves bits from one place to another.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi392\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 drawLoopX: xDelta Y: yDelta\line \b0 draws a line from the destination origin to:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (destX \i @ \i0 destY) + (xDelta \i @ \i0 yDelta).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri14\sb126\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The line is drawn by performing a sequence of \b copyBits, \b0 starting from the destination\line \expndtw-3 origin up to the other end of the line. For each \b copyBits, \b0 the destination origin is moved\line \expndtw0 by one pixel horizontally and/or vertically towards the other end.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1332\margrsxn1822\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1148\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa374 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 250    Chapter 14: Smalltalk/ V 286 Classes}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1332\margrsxn1822\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw720\colsr403\colno2\colw7632 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb5281\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex33\expndtw17\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 l   \i <}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 CharacterScanner}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb202\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 CharacterScanner is a subclass of \b BitBlt. \b0 Its main function is to convert a String of\line \expndtw-4 ASCII characters to displayable bitmapped shapes. It therefore adds an instance variable,\line \b\expndtw-3 curFont, \b0 which contains the current Font being used for conversion. A Font provides a\line \expndtw-1 Form containing all the bitmap images of characters. It also has an index table which\line \expndtw-3 gives the source origin of each character within the Form. (Class \b Font \b0 is discussed in\line \expndtw-4 Chapter 15.) To display a Character, you use its ASCII value to obtain the source origin\line \expndtw2 from the index table and then move the bitmap image to the destination form.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To create a CharacterScanner, use either trie expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li796\fi-389\ri5162\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 CharacterScanner new\line \expndtw-3 initialize: clipRect\line font: aFont}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 or}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi-385\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 CharacterScanner new\line \expndtw-2 initialize: clipRect\line \expndtw-3 font: aFont\line \expndtw-7 dest: aForm}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb155\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The former method uses \b Display \b0 as the destination form, while the latter specifies \b aForm\line \b0\expndtw-2 as its destination. Both specify \b aFont \b0 as the current font and \b clipRect \b0 as the clipping\line \expndtw-5 rectangle.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 CharacterScanner \b0 has an instance variable, \b frame, \b0 which is initialized by both of the\line above messages to contain \b clipRect. \b0 This allows you to temporarily change the clipping\line \expndtw0 rectangle and later restore it back to \b frame.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri14\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To specify colors, \b CharacterScanner \b0 has another two variables: \b foreColor \b0 specifies the\line color of the character, and \b backColor \b0 specifies the color of the background. They default\line \expndtw0 to black and white, respectively, with both of the above messages.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To display a string, send the following message to a \b CharacterScanner:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi-396\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 display: aString\line \expndtw-6 at: startPoint}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri14\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 where \b startPoint \b0 is the top left corner, relative to the origin of the frame, of the displayed\line \expndtw1 string. To display a portion of a string, send the message:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi-392\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 display: aString\line \expndtw-9 from: startChar\line \expndtw-6 to: endChar\line \expndtw-5 at: startPoint}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1905\margrsxn2376\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 14: Smalltalk/ V 286 Classes    251}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri0\sb306\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 where \b startChar \b0 and endChar bracket the portion of the string to be displayed. Another\line \expndtw-8 message,}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi-396\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 display: aString\line \expndtw-9 from: startChar\line \expndtw-5 at: startPoint}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb162\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 displays \b aString \b0 from \b startChar \b0 up to its last character at \b startPoint \b0 in the frame. In\line \expndtw-3 addition, it blanks out the rest of the line after the last character. This is often used when\line \expndtw-1 replacing a portion of the line with another string. Blanking out the rest of the line is\line \expndtw1 needed since the length of the line may change after the replacement.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi-392\ri0\sb25\sa0\sl-392\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If you want to display a string without bothering its background, use the message:\line \b\expndtw-3 show: aString from: start at: aPoint}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb126\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 which displays \b aString \b0 from \b start \b0 character up to its last character at \b aPoint \b0 in the frame\line \expndtw0 without changing the background.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Other useful messages are:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 displayForm: aForm at: aPoint rule: aRule}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Display \b aForm \b0 at \b aPoint \b0 in the frame using \b aRule.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 gray: aRect}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Color \b aRect \b0 in frame with gray tone.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 reverse: aRect}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Reverse the color of \b aRect \b0 in frame.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 setFont: aFont}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Change the font to \b aFont.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 setForeColor: fColor backColor: bColor}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Set the foreground color to \b fColor \b0 and background color to \b bColor.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb338\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-17\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Pen}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb209\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Class \b Pen \b0 provides a graphics interface similar to turtle graphics. It adds the following\line \expndtw-2 parameters to \b BitBlt:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li608\fi-205\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx608 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li608\fi-205\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx608 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 location \b0 consists of \b destX \b0 and \b destY, \b0 which represent the integer coordi-\line \expndtw5 nates of a Pen's current location, and \b fractionX \b0 and \b fractionY, \b0 which\line \expndtw0 represent the hundredth fractional part of the coordinates.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li608\fi-205\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx608 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li608\fi-205\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx608 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 direction \b0 is an instance variable containing a number between 0 and 359\line \expndtw-2 degrees, which specifies where the Pen is heading. 0 makes the Pen go east,\line \expndtw0 90 makes it go south, and 270 north.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2376\margrsxn1897\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 252    Chapter 14: Smalltalk/ V 286 Classes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li623\fi-205\ri0\sb328\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 \'95\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  \b downState \b0 is an instance variable containing true or false, indicating whether\line \expndtw0 the Pen draws while it moves.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The source form of a Pen represents its nib. Thus, to draw a thicker line, simply expand\line \expndtw2 the extent of the source form. The mask form provides the color of the Pen. The\line \expndtw-1 destination form is the form on which the Pen is going to draw. The clipping rectangle\line \expndtw0 limits the area on which the drawing can have any effect.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi-403\ri0\sb18\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The expression:\line \b\expndtw1 Pen new}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb126\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 creates a Pen with a nib of size 1 by 1, black color, \b Display \b0 as its destination form, the\line \expndtw-3 entire Display area as its clipping rectangle, the center of the Display as its \b location, \b0 a\line \expndtw1 direction of north, and its downState equal to true. The expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Pen new: aForm}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 creates a similar Pen except, that it draws on \b aForm \b0 rather than \b Display.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The following messages (in addition to the ones provided in BitBlt) can be used to\line \expndtw-1 modify a Pen's parameters:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 black}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Change the Pen color to black.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 gray}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Change the Pen color to gray.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li389\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 white}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Change the Pen color to white.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li385\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 changeNib: aForm}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li788\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Change the source Form (nib) to \b aForm.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li389\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 defaultNib: shape}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li785\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Change the size of the nib to \b shape \b0 which can be either a number or a point.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li385\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 direction: aNumber}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li785\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Set the direction to \b aNumber \b0 degrees.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li382\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 turn: anlnteger}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li785\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Turn the receiver Pen \b anlnteger \b0 number of degrees which can be either positive\line \expndtw-3 or negative.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li382\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 down}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li785\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Set down the pen.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1925\margrsxn2351\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 14: Smalltalk/ V 286 Classes    253}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb274\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 up}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li810\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Lift up the pen.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 home}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Center the pen on the destination form.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 north}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Set the direction to 270 degrees.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 place: aPoint}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Position the receiver Pen at \b aPoint.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri14\sb148\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The following messages move the Pen by a certain pattern. If the Pen is down, the pattern\line \expndtw-2 will be drawn:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 grid: scale}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li810\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Draw a grid within the clipping rectangle where \b scale \b0 is the space between lines.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 bounce: increment}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If the Pen touches the clipping rectangle after going for \b increment, \b0 change its\line \expndtw1 direction so that it looks like bouncing off the wall.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ellipse: r aspect: asp}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Draw an ellipse with pen position as its center, \b r \b0 as half of the width, \b asp \b0 as the\line \expndtw1 ratio of ellipse height to width. The height will be adjusted by \b Aspect.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 nllAt: aPoint}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Color all pixels that are connected to \b aPoint \b0 and have the same color as that of\line \b\expndtw1 aPoint \b0 by the pattern contained in mask form.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 go: distance}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Move the receiver Pen for length distance in the current direction. The y-axis\line will be adjusted by \b Aspect.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 goto: aPoint}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Move the receiver Pen to \b aPoint.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 polygon: length sides: n}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Draw a polygon with \b n \b0 sides, each \b length \b0 long.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri18\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Notice that the \b ellipse:aspect: \b0 and \b go: \b0 messages both adjust their y-axis by the ratio\line \expndtw-3 contained in the global variable \b Aspect. \b0 With this adjustment, you can draw a real circle\line \expndtw-4 or a real square. To find out the correct \b Aspect \b0 ratio for your screen, first display a Form\line \expndtw2 with the same width and height, measure with a ruler the width and height of the\line \expndtw-3 displayed rectangle, and evaluate:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2405\margrsxn1829\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 254    Chapter 14: Smalltalk/ V 286 Classes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb310\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Aspect := measuredWidth / measuredHeight.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Class \b Pen \b0 has a subclass called \b Commander. \b0 An instance of \b Commander \b0 contains an\line Array of Pens. Many messages implemented by \b Pen \b0 are reimplemented in \b Commander\line \b0 so that every Pen in the Commander receives the same message. This creates the illusion\line \expndtw-4 that all pens are moving at the same time. To create a Commander, evaluate the following\line \expndtw-6 expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb22\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Commander new: numberOfPens.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A Commander has two unique messages:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 fanOut}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Turn each Pen's direction by an increment of 360 / (number of Pens).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 lineUpFrom: startPoint to: endPoint}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li806\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Place all pens on a line specified by \b startPoint \b0 and \b endPoint \b0 with pens equally\line \expndtw1 spaced between the two points.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb353\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Animation}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Class \b Pen \b0 also has another subclass, called \b Animation. \b0 Each instance of \b Animation\line \b0\expndtw0 contains an object kept as a Pen with a name, a color, and an Array of pictures. When\line an object moves, the Animation erases its old image before displaying it at the new\line location. To create an Animation, evaluate:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Animation new initialize: aRectangle}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 where \b aRectangle \b0 is the clipping rectangle on Display. To add an object to an\line \expndtw-1 Animation, send the message:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 add: formArray name: aName color: aSymbol}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \b formArray \b0 is an Array of Forms simulating the continuous movement of the object.\line \expndtw1 The argument \b aName \b0 is normally a String or a Symbol to identify the object. The\line \expndtw-2 argument \b aSymbol \b0 is the selector for a unary message which can be sent to class \b Form\line \b0\expndtw3 to return a mask form (such as # black, # white, etc.).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Animation \b0 has the following messages to change the behavior of its objects:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: name bounce: increment}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Tell the object with the \b name \b0 to bounce for \b increment \b0 distance.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: name direction: anlnteger}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Tell the object with the \b name \b0 to change its direction to \b anlnteger.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: name go: distance}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Tell the object with the \b name \b0 to go for \b distance.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1943\margrsxn2333\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri4\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 14: Smalltalk/ V 286 Classes    \i0 2.5.5}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb292\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: name place: aPoint}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li803\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Tell the object with the \b name \b0 to be placed at \b aPoint.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb115\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 tell: name turn: anlnteger}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Tell the object with the \b name \b0 to turn by \b anlnteger \b0 degrees.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 speed: anlnteger}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li803\fi0\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Change the distance between the consecutive copies to \b anlnteger. \b0 The larger\line \expndtw1 the distance, the faster the object moves.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 shiftRate: anlnteger}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li803\fi0\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Specify how many times the current picture will be copied before shifting to the\line \expndtw-1 next one. By increasing the value of \b anlnteger, \b0 the motion within the object\line will appear slower even though it is traveling the same distance.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb360\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Integrating Color and Monochrome Displays}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri4\sb216\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Many applications expect to run on both monochrome and color displays with one\line \b\expndtw-1 Smalltalk/V \b0 image. Following are some guidelines for developing such applications.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi-396\ri0\sb22\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When you want to create a Form that contains a portion of the display, use the message:\line \b\expndtw-3 Display compatibleForm}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb126\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 to obtain the class of the new Form. It returns either class \b Form \b0 or class \b ColorForm\line \b0 depending on whether the class of \b Display \b0 is \b DisplayScreen \b0 or \b ColorScreen, \b0 respec-\line \expndtw-4 tively. For example:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Display compatibleForm fromDisplay: (0 @ 0 extent: 100 \b0 @ \b 100)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 creates a \b Form \b0 or \b ColorForm \b0 containing the top left 100 by 100 area of the screen\line \expndtw1 without worrying about whether the screen has color or not.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When you want to create a mask that is compatible with the destination, use the message\line \b\expndtw-2 compatibleMask. \b0 It returns class \b Form \b0 if the receiver of the message is a \b Form,\line \expndtw-3 BiColorForm, \b0 or \b DisplayScreen; \b0 or returns class \b BiColorForm \b0 if the receiver is a\line \b\expndtw-4 ColorForm \b0 or \b ColorScreen. \b0 This message is often used in conjunction with the message\line \b\expndtw1 color: \b0 to obtain the desired color. For example:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Display compatibleMask color: \b0 1}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 returns either a \b Form \b0 with all 0 bits if \b Display \b0 is a \b DisplayScreen, \b0 or a \b BiColorForm\line \b0\expndtw-3 with all 1 bits and a foreground color 1 if \b Display \b0 is a \b ColorScreen. \b0 The way the \b color:\line \b0\expndtw0 message works is as follows. If the receiver is class \b BiColorForm, \b0 then the \b color:\line \b0\expndtw-2 message returns a 16 by 16 \b BiColorForm \b0 with all 1 bits and its foreground color set to\line \expndtw-3 the argument and background color set to 0. If the receiver is class \b Form, the message\line \b0\expndtw-2 returns a \b 16 \b0 by \b 16 Form \b0 with all bits set to 0 if the argument of the message is between}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2340\margrsxn1934\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 256    Chapter 14: Smalltalk/ V 286 Classes}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb324\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx162 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  0\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb324\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx162 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart0\pnindent155\pnf3}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 and 7, or set to 1 if the argument is between 8 and 15. In other words, for a Form mask,\line \expndtw-2 the \b color: \b0 message maps the first eight colors into 0 bits and the remaining 8 colors into}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx162 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  1\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx162 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart0\pnindent155\pnf3}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 bits. Because of this mapping, when you use a \b Pen \b0 or a \b CharacterScanner, \b0 you had\line better choose the foreground colors from one set of eight colors and background colors\line \expndtw-1 from the other set of eight colors. Otherwise, when you run on a monochrome display,\line \expndtw-2 you will end up drawing white on white, or black on black, making the result invisible.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb364\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Multiprocessing Classes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Object-oriented computing in Smalltalk involves communicating objects which send\line \expndtw1 messages to each other to perform useful work. Although this suggests parallel\line \expndtw-3 computation, it actually is not. An object always waits to receive a response after sending\line \expndtw-4 a message. The situation corresponds to that of using a procedural language, where at any\line point in time there is a stack of incomplete procedure calls, and there is a single procedure\line \expndtw-1 which is active. In Smalltalk, there is a stack of incomplete message sends, and there is\line a single method which is active.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V \b0 provides parallel processing by defining multiple stacks of incomplete\line message sends, where each stack is represented by a separate object of class \b Process.\line \b0\expndtw-4 Since there is a single processor, the parallelism is simulated. At any time, only a single\line \expndtw-2 process is executing. However there may be many processes ready to execute and there\line \expndtw-3 are well defined conditions under which \b Smalltalk/V \b0 switches to a new current process.\line \b\expndtw-1 Semaphore \b0 objects are provided for synchronization among processes.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A new process is created by sending the message \b fork \b0 to a block. For example:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 [Transcript show: 'Hello'; cr] fork.\line \expndtw-2 Transcript show: 'Goodbye'; cr}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri7\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This example creates a separate process to execute the code within the block and\line \expndtw-2 continues execution of the current process in the code following the block. The result\line displayed in the System Transcript is:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0\sl-248\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Hello\line \expndtw-3 Goodbye}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The output shows that the new process is initiated before the current process is\line \expndtw-2 continued, although both processes operate at the same priority. Processes can be given\line \expndtw0 different priorities by sending the \b forkAt: \b0 message to a block. For example:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 [[Transcript show: 'world!'; cr] forkAt: 2.\line \expndtw1 Transcript show: 'Hello '] forkAt: \b0 3}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The example above creates two new processes, one at priority two and the other at\line \expndtw-4 priority three. Since higher priority processes are scheduled first, the output on the System\line \expndtw-3 Transcript is:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1916\margrsxn2353\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 14: Smalltalk/ V 286 Classes    237}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb284\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Hello world!}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri4\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Multiprocessing is especially useful in discrete event simulation because it allows each\line \expndtw-2 simulation object to carry out its behavior as a separate process, using semaphores to\line \expndtw0 synchronize processes. Multiprocessing is implemented in \b Smalltalk\b0 /V by classes\line \b\expndtw-4 Process, ProcessScheduler \b0 and \b Semaphore.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 For some interesting examples of multiprocessing applications in Smalltalk, see the\line following publications:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A Little Smalltalk, \b0 Timothy Budd, Addison-Wesley 1987, page 116, "The\line \expndtw-1 Dining Philosophers Problem".}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk-80, the Language and its Implementation, \b0 Adele Goldberg and\line \expndtw-2 David Robson, Addison-Wesley 1983, page 262, "Resource Sharing".}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb324\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex83\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Semaphore}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb198\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Semaphore \b0 is a subclass of \b Object. \b0 A \b Semaphore \b0 is an object used to synchronize\line multiple processes. A process waits for an event to occur by sending the message \b wait\line \b0 to a semaphore. A process signals that an event has occurred by sending the message\line \b\expndtw1 signal \b0 to a semaphore.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A semaphore has two instance variables:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 signalCount}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li803\fi0\ri14\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Contains an \b Integer \b0 representing the number of \b signal \b0 messages minus the\line \expndtw0 number of \b wait \b0 messages sent to the semaphore during its entire lifetime.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 waitingProcesses}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li799\fi0\ri7\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Contains an \b OrderedCollection \b0 of processes that have sent the message \b wait \b0 to\line \expndtw-3 the semaphore without a corresponding \b signal \b0 message. New waiting processes\line \expndtw2 are added at the end of the collection.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 An example of the use of semaphores is the following:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Isl}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 s \b0 := \b Semaphore new.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 [Transcript show: '1'] fork.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li421\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 [Transcript show: \b0 '2'.s \b wait. Transcript show: \b0 '3']}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li421\fi371\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 forkAt: 3.\line \expndtw-2 [Transcript show: '4'.s signal. Transcript show: '5'; cr]}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 forkAt: 2}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2397\margrsxn1894\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 258    Chapter 14: Smalltalk/ V 286 Classes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri4\sb328\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This example creates three new processes. The output displayed on the System Transcript\line \expndtw-16 is:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi0\ri0\sb166\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 12 4 3 5}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This output is created as follows. The \b fork \b0 message creates a process which shows \b 'I'.\line \b0\expndtw-4 The \b forkAt: \b0 3 message creates a process which shows '2' and then is blocked waiting on\line the semaphore. The \b forkAt: \b0 2 message creates a process which shows '4' and signals the\line \expndtw-1 semaphore. This allows the higher priority process to resume, show '3' and terminate.\line \expndtw-2 Then the process at priority 2 resumes, shows '5' and terminates. The initiating process,\line \expndtw-1 the user interface, is running concurrently with these processes.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb320\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Process}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb198\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Process \b0 is a subclass of \b OrderedCollection. \b0 A \b process \b0 is a sequence of computations in\line \expndtw-2 Smalltalk carried out by objects sending messages to other objects and waiting for the\line \expndtw-3 results. An object of class \b Process \b0 describes such a computation sequence. A process has\line \expndtw-2 a \b name \b0 and a \b priority. A \b0 process exists in one of several states. Figure 14.4 shows the\line \expndtw0 state transitions a process can make.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2768\margrsxn7035\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx2279\posnegy371\absw0\absh3038\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw8763\pich5359\picwgoal4968\pichgoal3038\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb2974\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 14.4}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Process State Transitions}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2397\margrsxn1894\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb464\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The process state transitions occur for the following reasons:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li619\fi-209\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 \'95\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0   A new process is created and becomes \b ready \b0 as a result of sending the \b fork\line \b0\expndtw0 and \b forkAt: \b0 messages to a block.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1884\margrsxn2385\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 14: Smalltalk/ V 286 Classes    259}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li630\fi-212\ri0\sb302\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx630 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 \'95{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-3\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 A \b ready \b0 process becomes \b active \b0 if it is the longest waiting at its priority and\line \expndtw6 there are no higher priority ready processes and: (l) the active process\line \expndtw-1 becomes blocked or dead, (2) the ready process has higher priority than the\line \expndtw1 active process, or (3) the ready process has the same priority as the active\line \expndtw-1 process and the following expression is executed:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li814\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Processor yield}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li630\fi-212\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx630 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li630\fi-212\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx630 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \b active \b0 process becomes \b ready \b0 when it is replaced by a ready process\line \expndtw0 under the conditions described above for transition 2.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li630\fi-212\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx630 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li630\fi-212\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx630 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 An \b active \b0 process becomes \b blocked \b0 when the message \b wait \b0 is sent to a\line \expndtw-2 semaphore which has no excess signals.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li630\fi-212\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx630 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li630\fi-212\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx630 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A \b blocked \b0 process becomes \b ready \b0 when it is the first in the waiting queue of\line \expndtw1 a semaphore and the message \b signal \b0 is sent to the semaphore.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li630\fi-212\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx630 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li630\fi-212\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx630 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent212\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The active process becomes \b dead \b0 when it reaches the end of the block which\line \expndtw-3 caused process creation as a result of the \b fork \b0 or \b forkAt: \b0 messages, or when\line \expndtw-1 the following expression is executed:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li814\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Processor schedule}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb324\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 The User Interface Process}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb202\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalltalk/V \b0 user interface is driven by a single process which responds to all\line \expndtw-3 keyboard and mouse input events for all windows. The user interface process alternates\line \expndtw-1 between (l) responding to an input event, and (2) waiting for the next input by sending\line \expndtw0 the message \b wait \b0 to global variable \b KeyboardSemaphore. \b0 When there is no input\line \expndtw-2 activity, other lower-priority processes can run. The process scheduler guarantees that\line \expndtw-4 there is always a lowest priority \i idle \i0 process to run when there is no other system activity.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Errors are handled by a debugger running under the user interface process whether or not\line \expndtw-3 the error occurs in the user interface process. If the \b error: \b0 message is sent under the user\line interface process, the current process is suspended and a new user interface process is\line \expndtw0 created. This allows the process with the error to be debugged with the debugger. If\line \b\expndtw-2 error: \b0 is sent by a non-user-interface process, an entry describing the error is placed in\line \expndtw-5 the \b PendingEvents \b0 queue (a global variable). \b PendingEvents \b0 is polled for activity by the\line \expndtw0 user interface process when there is no other input activity.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb317\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 ProcessScheduler}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri14\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Class \b ProcessScheduler \b0 is a subclass of \b Object. A ProcessScheduler \b0 controls process\line \expndtw-2 execution. There is a single instance of class \b ProcessScheduler \b0 maintained in global\line \expndtw-4 variable \b Processor. \b0 The process scheduler determines which ready process is the active\line process and maintains a queue of ready but inactive processes. The highest priority ready}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2489\margrsxn1784\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 260    Chapter 14: Smalltalk/V 286 Classes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb310\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 process is selected as the active process. If there is more than one process at the highest\line \expndtw1 priority, the process that has been ready the longest is chosen.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb119\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Process priorities may range between 1 and \b Processor topPriority.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb338\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 Interrupts}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb205\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Interrupts are the mechanism used for communicating asynchronous external events to\line \b\expndtw-3 Smalltalk/V. \b0 Examples of external events are keyboard inputs, mouse movements and\line \expndtw1 clock ticks. The set of interrupt events can be extended by you.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \b Smalltalk/V \b0 interrupt model corresponds to typical computer hardware interrupt\line \expndtw-3 architectures. Interrupts may be explicitly enabled and disabled. When an interrupt event\line occurs and interrupts are enabled, interrupts are disabled and a \b vmlnterrupt: \b0 message is\line \expndtw-2 sent to the object at the top of the execution stack for the current process. The argument\line \expndtw-4 to \b vmlnterrupt: \b0\i is \i0 the selector of the method defined in \b Process \b0 class which services the\line \expndtw0 interrupt. An interrupt routine concludes by enabling interrupts and returning to\line \b\expndtw-2 vmlnterrupt:, \b0 which answers \b self, \b0 leaving the execution stack exactly as before the\line \expndtw-3 interrupt.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When an interrupt event occurs and interrupts are disabled, the \b Smalltalk/V \b0 places the\line \expndtw0 interrupt event in a pending interrupts queue. Each time interrupts are enabled, the\line \expndtw1 pending interrupts queue is examined to see if there are additional interrupts \i to \i0 be\line \expndtw-7 serviced.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb162\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The typical interrupt processing method merely signals a semaphore to resume a process\line \expndtw-2 to handle the event. For eaxmple, the keyboard interrupt handler \i in \i0 class \b Process \b0 is as\line \expndtw-6 follows:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 keyboardlnterrupt}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi407\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'Handle keyboard interrupt."\line KeyboardSemaphore signal}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Note that the semaphore \b signal \b0 message enables interrupts if they are disabled. Interrupts\line are explicitly enabled and disabled by sending the message \b enablelnterrupts: \b0 to \b Process\line \b0\expndtw-2 class. Disabling interrupts should be used with extreme caution, because \b Smalltalk/V\line \b0\expndtw-1 cannot respond to external events while interrupts are disabled. The interrupt state may\line \expndtw0 be preserved around a critical code section as follows:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 I \b oldState I}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 oldState := Process enablelnterrupts: false, "disable and save state"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 " ... critical code ... "}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Process enablelnterrupts: oldState "restore interrupt state"}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2068\margrsxn2220\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri7\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 14: Smalltalk/ V 286 Classes    261}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb310\sa176\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Global variable InterruptSelectors contains an array of selectors corresponding to\line \expndtw-2 interrupt events defined as follows:}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2475\margrsxn2361\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw3034\colsr133\colno2\colw3906 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Interrupt}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tx922 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Number{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-6 Selector}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-7\shptop4\shpright792\shpbottom4\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 18415}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft986\shptop4\shpright1713\shpbottom4\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 15875}}}}{\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li371\fi0\ri0\sb194\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx979 \b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  1\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li371\fi0\ri0\sb194\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx979 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent608\pnf3}\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 undefinedlnterrupt}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li371\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx979 \b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  2\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li371\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx979 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent608\pnf3}\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\