fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Lf}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li547\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Line feed character (ASCII value 10)}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-684 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2023 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6725 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 MouseButton}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li544\fi-4\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Character sent when any mouse button chang\line \expndtw-4 state (ASCII value 254)}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-569 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2023 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6725 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 SetLoc}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li540\fi-4\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Character sent when the mouse moves (ASCII\line \expndtw-6 value 255)}\cell \pard\intbl\row \pard\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2449\margrsxn2558\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1432\colsr1253\colno2\colw4215 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-439\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Space}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-439\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Tab}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-439\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 UpperToLower}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-443\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Space character (ASCII value 32)\line Horizontal tab character (ASCII value 9)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalllnteger of value 32, the numeric difference\line between upper and lower-case ASCII value\line \expndtw-4 characters}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2050\margrsxn2220\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb299\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex79\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 FunctionKeys}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb194\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 FunctionKeys \b0 is a pool dictionary associating names to the function key values such as\line cursor movement keys and menu keys. By assigning a new value to a function key name,\line \expndtw2 a different key on the key board can be associated to the named function.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb378\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Maintaining Smalltalk/V}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb216\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In this section, the procedures for maintaining the system are discussed. These include:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx616 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx616 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 How to keep the files that the system uses from getting too big.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li616\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx616 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li616\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx616 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Some simple steps that you can take to protect yourself from losing all your\line \expndtw1 work in the event of a total failure (crash) of the system.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx616 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx616 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 What to do if the system does crash.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2320\margrsxn1968\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 282    Chapter 15: Smalltalk/ V 286 Environment}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb324\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex81\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Automatic Logging of Changes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb205\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As you define and modify classes or methods, \b Smalltalk/V \b0 is logging all of these\line \expndtw-2 changes to the \b change log. Smalltalk/V \b0 maintains pointers from the compiled methods\line in the environment to the latest version of the source code. If the source code has never\line \expndtw-1 been changed, the pointer goes to the \b source \b0 file. Otherwise, the pointer goes to the\line \b\expndtw-2 change log. \b0 As a result, the disk is accessed as you browse the methods in a class.\line \expndtw-4 Furthermore, since the \b image \b0 file contains pointers to the \b source \b0 file and the \b change log,\line \b0\expndtw0 you must maintain these three files as a group.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 For these reasons, \i you must not edit or modify the change log! \i0 You can view it with the Disk\line \expndtw-1 Browser and you can reinstall methods and class definitions from it into your system.\line \expndtw-4 This capability can be used to facilitate recovery from a system crash and is explained in\line \expndtw0 more detail in the section on \b Surviving a System Crash \b0 in this chapter.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Other important events are automatically logged by \b Smalltalk/V. \b0 Every time the image\line \expndtw-3 is saved, a message with the date and time is written to the \b change log. \b0 Every Smalltalk\line \expndtw-4 expression that you evaluate with either \i do it \i0 or \i show it \i0 is also logged. In addition, every\line \expndtw1 time you remove a method from a class, a message is logged.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The format of the \b change log \b0 is very similar to the one described by Glenn Krasner in\line \expndtw-6 Chapter 3 of \i Smalltalk-80: Bits of History and Words of Advice, \i0 Addison-Wesley, 1983. A\line \expndtw1 brief description of this format using the EBNF notation described in \b Appendix 1\line \b0\expndtw-6 follows.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1947\margrsxn2340\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 1$: Smalltalk/ V 286 Environment    283}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1231\fi0\ri0\sb292\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1031\fs22  \lang1033 changeLog \b0 =}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi1199\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 \{textChunk\}.\line \expndtw9 <rule> textChunk =}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1231\fi364\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 sourceCode I classDefinition I expression I imageComment.\line \expndtw2\lang1031  \lang1033 sourceCode =}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1231\fi371\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 '!' \i class Name* methods\i0 !' \i \{method\i0\b "!'\} \b0 ' !'.\line \b\expndtw2\lang1031\fs22  \lang1033 classDefinition =}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1228\fi374\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 ' \b "define class" \b0 ' \i classDefinitionMessage\i0 '!'.\line \b\expndtw4\lang1031\fs22  \lang1033 expression =}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1235\fi367\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ' "evaluate" \b0 ' \i evaluatedText\i0 '!'.\line \b\expndtw2\lang1031  \lang1033 imageComment \b0 =}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi1202\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 \'ab\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'ab\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 *** \b saved image on: \b0 ' \i dateAndTime****" \i0 !'.\line \b\expndtw-1\fs20 where:    \b0\i className \i0 is}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1224\fi371\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 the name of a class.\line \i\expndtw-5\fs20 method \i0 is}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 a valid method as defined in \b Part \b0 2 of this manual with occurrences}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1217\fi385\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 of "!" replaced by "!!".\line \i\expndtw-5\fs20 classDefinitionMessage \i0 is}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1598\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 a valid class definition message to the class's superclass as defined}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1220\fi378\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 in \b Part 2 \b0 of this manual.\line \i\expndtw-7 evaluatedText \i0 is}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1595\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 the text evaluated by \i do it \i0 or \i show it \i0 with all occurrences of "!"}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1217\fi385\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 replaced by "!!".\line \i\expndtw-6 dateAndTime \i0 is}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1595\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 the character representation of the date and time the image was}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1598\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 saved.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \b change log \b0 is an ASCII text file which means that you can print it at any time. A\line \expndtw0 simple examination of the file should remove any questions regarding its format.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb349\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Importance of Saving the Image}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb209\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V \b0 is written entirely in Smalltalk. The image that is referred to in this manual\line \expndtw1 is the collection of all Smalltalk objects that make up the environment.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When you save an image, descriptions of all of these objects are written out on the \b image\line \b0\expndtw-2 file. When you start the system at a later time, all of the objects described in the \b image\line \b0\expndtw-5 file are recreated exactly as they were at the time the image was saved. Since these objects\line \expndtw-1 contain everything that makes up the \b Smalltalk/V \b0 environment, the system starts up\line exactly as you saved it.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As you use the system by writing Smalltalk code or using the environment for other\line purposes, new objects are created and existing objects have their contents changed. You\line \expndtw-3 must save the image to make your changes permanent. You can save the image by either}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1280\margrsxn1842\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-112\shptop7733\shpright-112\shpbottom9475\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-76\shptop8906\shpright-76\shpbottom9446\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 4445}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1166\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 284    Chapter 15.\dbch\af3\loch\af1\hich\f1\cchs0 \u9632\'3f\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  Smalltalk/ V 286 Environment}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1152\fi0\ri0\sb320\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 selecting the \i save image \i0 function from the system menu or by selecting the \i save image\line \i0\expndtw3 function as you are exiting the environment. If you do not save the image, the\line \expndtw1 environment will not have any of your changes the next time it is started.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1145\fi0\ri22\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If you forget to save the image, you can recover these changes. Items that were logged\line \expndtw-4 in the \b change log \b0 are still present, and you can install them again using the Disk Browser\line \expndtw-5 (see \b Surviving a System Crash \b0 later in this chapter). Not saving the image is useful when\line \expndtw-3 you are developing something new and you decide that you have made a major mistake\line \expndtw1 that you would like to discard.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb360\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs4 &\dbch\af2\loch\af0\hich\f0\cchs0 \u9632\'3f\u9632\'3f}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1148\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex83\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Compressing the Change Log}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex33\expndtw16\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 I*}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1148\fi0\ri32\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Compressing the \b change log \b0 reduces it to only the latest copy of each new or changed\line \expndtw-1 method. Class definitions are removed. A new image file is written automatically.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1145\fi0\ri32\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You should compress the \b change log \b0 when it starts to get large. There must be enough\line \expndtw0 space on the disk for both the new and the old \b change log \b0 at the same time, so make\line sure you compress the \b change log \b0\i before \i0 the disk gets too full.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1141\fi0\ri32\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To \i compress the change log, \i0 evaluate the following expression in any text pane (the System\line \expndtw-3 Transcript for example):}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1544\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk compressChanges}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li148\fi0\ri0\sb115\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs6 i,}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li180\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tx1145 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 j{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw2 If there is not enough room on the disk, delete some files and then try it again.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1145\fi0\ri0\sb295\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex83\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Compressing the Source File}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1138\fi0\ri29\sb202\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Compressing the \b source \b0 file creates a new \b source \b0 file for all of the methods currently in\line \expndtw-1 the system by taking the latest copy of the source code for each method from the old\line \b\expndtw-3 source \b0 rile or \b change log. \b0 A new \b image \b0 file and an empty \b change log \b0 are automatically\line \expndtw-5 written.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1130\fi0\ri32\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \b source \b0 file is written in a compressed format to preserve space on the disk. There are\line \expndtw-1 pointers from the compiled methods in the image to the \b source \b0 file and to the \b change\line \expndtw-3 log. \b0 For these reasons, it is \i imperative that \i0 the source file not be edited or altered except\line \expndtw-4 by \b Smalltalk/V. \b0 Copying the source is okay. You should always use the same \b image \b0 file\line \expndtw1 with the same \b source \b0 file (or a copy of it) and the same \b change log.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li1130\fi0\ri32\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 After you compress the sources, you will have a new \b image file \b0 and a new \b source \b0 file.\line \expndtw-3 The \b change log \b0 will be empty. Therefore, you should make backup copies of the \b source\line \b0\expndtw-4 file, the \b change log \b0 file, and the \b image \b0 file before compressing the sources so if anything\line \expndtw0 goes wrong, you can recover.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1991\margrsxn2294\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 15: Smalltalk/ V 286 Environment    285}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb306\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To compress the sources, evaluate the following expression in any text pane (the System\line \expndtw-3 Transcript, for example):}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk compressSources}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 After compressing the sources, you have a new base system. You should make a backup\line \expndtw-2 copy of the new \b source, image, \b0 and \b change log \b0 files. Maintaining backup copies will\line \expndtw1 allow you to recover from most, if not all, problems that might occur.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Retaining your old change logs gives you a record of all the changes you have made to\line the system. This will prove invaluable when you receive a new release of \b Smalltalk/V.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb349\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Surviving a System Crash}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V \b0 is very resilient to errors. Unfortunately, disasters can and will happen,\line \expndtw1 especially if you are making modifications to \b Smalltalk/V. \b0 The automatic logging\line feature of \b Smalltalk/V \b0 makes it possible to recover from most disasters. Disasters\line \expndtw-3 happen not because \b Smalltalk/V \b0 has bugs in it, but because it is a modifiable program\line development environment. \b Smalltalk/V \b0 will let you change anything, even if it destroys\line \expndtw-2 the environment.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 For example, if you make a mistake changing the method for the walkback window, or\line \expndtw-3 if the hardware fails, you may see the error message: \b doesNotUnderstand: is missing.\line \b0\expndtw3 This means that \b Smalltalk/V \b0 cannot find the code that displays the appropriate\line \expndtw-1 walkback message and has terminated.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This presents no problem since you can recover most, if not all, of your work very\line \expndtw-4 quickly. In fact, experimenting with the system is a good way to learn about it, but make\line \expndtw-1 sure you take the following precautions:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-227\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-21\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-227\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent227\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-21\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Always have a backup copy of the \b source \b0 file that you are currently using.\line \b\expndtw0 Smalltalk/V \b0 never modifies the \b source \b0 file and neither should you. When\line \expndtw-2 you compress the sources, as explained in \b Compressing the Source File, \b0 a\line \expndtw0 new \b source \b0 file is made. Make a backup copy of it immediately.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-227\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-20\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-227\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent227\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-20\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Always have a reliable backup copy of the \b image \b0 file and \b change log \b0 file.\line Remember that the \b image \b0 and the \b change log \b0 work together. If you use an\line \b image \b0 file with an older version of the \b change log, \b0 you may not be able to\line access the source code for some methods. After you compress the changes\line \expndtw1 (see \b Compressing the Change Log), \b0 the \b image \b0 and \b change log \b0 files are\line \expndtw2 both replaced. You cannot use an old \b image \b0 file with a new compressed\line \b\expndtw-1 change log \b0 file. You can use an old \b image \b0 with a later version of the same\line \b\expndtw2 change log \b0 if the \b change log \b0 has not been compressed between the time\line \expndtw-2 when the image was made and when you use the \b change log. \b0\i In short, back\line \expndtw0\fs20 up the image and change log together, and you shouldn't have any problems.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1154\margrsxn1968\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1181\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 286    Chapter 15: Smalltalk/ V 286 Environment}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1804\fi-238\ri0\sb328\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1804 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1804\fi-238\ri0\sb328\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1804 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart3\pnindent238\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You should save the \b image \b0 onto the disk (you can do this from the system\line menu) before trying something that might crash the system. Doing so will\line \expndtw3 preserve  most  of your  work.  If the  system  crashes,  you  can  restart\line \b\expndtw0 Smalltalk/V \b0 using this saved image. If you fail to save the \b image \b0 and the\line system crashes, you can use the Disk Browser to examine the \b change log.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1804\fi-238\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1804 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-15\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1804\fi-238\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1804 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart3\pnindent238\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-15\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If you have been experimenting with \b Smalltalk/V \b0 and you have any reason\line \expndtw-1 to believe you have damaged the system or if there are changes you do not\line \expndtw5 want to make permanent, \i do not \i0 save the \b image. \b0 Exit the \b Smalltalk/V\line \b0 environment using the \b forget image \b0 function. Remember, most of the}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li54\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1807 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-61\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 .-_\dbch\af3\loch\af1\hich\f1\cchs0 \u9632\'3f{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw2\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 changes you have made are in the \b change log \b0 and they can be reinstalled}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\tx1811 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ''{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-7 selectively.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1804\fi-238\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1804 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 5.{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw7 If \b Smalltalk/V \b0 crashes, don't panic. Make a copy of your \b image \b0 and\line \b\expndtw-4 change.log \b0 file. Usually, you can recover your work, and if you make a copy,\line \expndtw3 you can try repeatedly. Read the next section for information on how to\line \expndtw-6 recover.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1177\fi0\ri0\sb349\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Recovering From a System Crash}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1166\fi0\ri0\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If the system crashes you should make a copy of your \b image \b0 and \b change.log \b0 file before\line \expndtw0 proceeding. To recover your work, follow these steps:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li259\fi1321\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx1807 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-22\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 1.{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw1 Check if you have a good \b image \b0 file. To find out, try to start \b Smalltalk/V\line \b0\expndtw0 |{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw1 in the usual way using this file. If it does not start up, then you should get}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1800\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 your most recent backup of the \b image \b0 and \b change log \b0 and start \b Smal-\line \expndtw2 ltalk/V \b0 with that version.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1800\fi-238\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1800 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1800\fi-238\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1800 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent238\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now that you have \b Smalltalk/V \b0 running, use the Disk Browser to look at\line \expndtw5 the \b change.log \b0 file that you were using when the system crashed. The\line \b\expndtw8 change log \b0 has all of the modifications that you made and all of the\line \expndtw-2 expressions that you evaluated.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1800\fi-238\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1800 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1800\fi-238\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1800 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent238\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Find the point in the \b change log \b0 where you saved the image that you are\line \expndtw-2 currently running. Every time that the \b image \b0 is saved, \b Smalltalk/V \b0 writes a\line comment into the \b change log \b0 giving the time and date when the \b image \b0 was\line \expndtw0 saved. Scan the \b change log \b0 backwards, if you were using a recently saved\line \b\expndtw-4 image.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1800\fi-238\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1800 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-15\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1800\fi-238\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx1800 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart2\pnindent238\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-15\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Now that you have found the point at which you saved the image, you know\line \expndtw10 that your lost work is from this point to the end of the \b change log.\line \b0\expndtw-2 Remember that the \b change log \b0 is formatted as a series of chunks. The exact\line \expndtw6 format is given in the section \b Automatic Logging of Changes \b0 in this\line \expndtw-4 chapter. To restore your work, select one or more consecutive chunks of text.\line \expndtw-2 You must select complete chunks\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 watch the exclamation points (!). Next,}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1978\margrsxn2299\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 15: Smalltalk/ V 286 Environment    287}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li637\fi0\ri418\sb302\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 pop up the \i pane \i0 menu and select the \i install \i0 function. If a chunk is an\line \expndtw-3 expression, it will be evaluated. If a chunk is a method definition, it will be\line recompiled and installed into the system. Choose the chunks that you install\line \expndtw-4 carefully\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 one of them represents the error, and you don't want to put it back\line \expndtw-1 into the system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When you have finished recovering what you want, save the \b image.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb320\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Purging Unused Symbols}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri18\sb205\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Unused symbols tend to accumulate slowly over time in \b Smalltalk/V \b0 because instances\line \expndtw0 of class \b Symbol \b0 are not collected as garbage. Evaluate the following expression to\line remove all symbols that are no longer referenced.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk purgeUnusedSymbols}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri18\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The number of symbols recovered and the amount of space reclaimed is usually quite\line \expndtw-8 small.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb324\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Cloning}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri14\sb205\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Cloning is a process which builds a new \b image \b0 file from the current image. The Cloner\line does a complete memory walk of all of the objects in \b Smalltalk/V. \b0 Cloning does not in\line \expndtw-2 and of itself reclaim any space or objects. The image file is fully compacted every time\line the image is saved. Cloning does not remove unused symbols. Cloning the system is a\line \expndtw1 good way of verifying that all of the objects in the image are undamaged.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri18\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Cloner can also be used to build a version of the system that has many of the classes\line \expndtw-2 removed. In order to specify these classes and objects, you must edit the method\line \b initializeClones \b0 in file \b cloner.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri18\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Note that when you clone, the \b image \b0 file will be re-written. Therefore, you should back\line \expndtw0 up your \b image \b0 file before cloning.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri14\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Cloner classes are not resident in \b Smalltalk/V. \b0 To clone \b Smalltalk/V, \b0 an expression\line \expndtw0 must be evaluated to read and execute a file. The file installs several classes into\line \b\expndtw2 Smalltalk/V \b0 and then uses them to clone the system. The cloned system does not\line \expndtw-1 contain the cloning classes.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri18\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If the file \b cloner \b0 is in your disk directory, (the same directory as the \b image \b0 file), then\line \expndtw-1 evaluate the following expression in any \i text \i0 pane, (e.g. the System Transcript):}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (Disk file: 'cloner\b0 .st') \b fileln.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When cloning is finished, \b Smalltalk/V \b0 will exit to DOS. You should make a backup of\line your \b image \b0 file.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2305\margrsxn1961\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 288    Chapter 15: Smalltalk/ V 286 Environment}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb342\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 DOS Shell}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri18\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \b DOS Shell \b0 capability allows you to quickly exit \b Smalltalk/V \b0 to execute DOS\line \expndtw-2 commands and programs, and then return to \b Smalltalk/V \b0 when finished. For example,\line \expndtw2 you could use this capability to format a floppy disk or to run a word processing\line \expndtw1 program, and then return to \b Smalltalk/V.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Choosing \b dos shell \b0 from the system menu pops up another menu with several choices of\line \expndtw-1 single DOS commands you can execute. In addition, a \b to dos \b0 choice exits you to the\line \expndtw-2 DOS command processor \b COMMAND.COM \b0 from which you can run several DOS\line \expndtw1 commands and programs. To resume \b Smalltalk/V \b0 use the DOS \b EXIT \b0 command.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb360\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Reserving Space for DOS}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri18\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 In order to use this feature you must reserve space for DOS when starting up\line \b\expndtw-1 Smalltalk/V. \b0 The memory you reserve is not used by \b Smalltalk/V. \b0 You specify the\line \expndtw3 amount of space to reserve for DOS as the argument to the /d: parameter on the\line \expndtw-3 command line that invokes \b Smalltalk/V. \b0 For example, the following command invokes\line \b\expndtw0 Smalltalk/V \b0 and reserves 200K for DOS to use.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 v \b /d:200}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri18\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Notice that the argument is a decimal number and specifies the amount of memory to\line \expndtw-4 reserve in multiples of 1024 bytes. \b Appendix 3, Configuring Smalltalk/V 286, \b0 explains\line \expndtw1 all of the command line options in detail.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb356\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 How It Works}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri18\sb220\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The method \b executeCommands: \b0 in class \b ScreenDispatcher \b0 does all of the work. For\line \expndtw1 example, this expression displays text on the display screen using the DOS \b echo\line \b0\expndtw-5 command:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Scheduler systemDispatcher}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 executeCommands: #('ECHO hello from Smalltalk/V 'PAUSE')}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri18\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The argument \i is \i0 a collection of strings containing the DOS commands to execute, in this\line \expndtw-2 case an array with two strings containing the \b echo \b0 command and the \b pause \b0 command.\line \expndtw-3 These strings are written to the file \b smaltalk.bat, \b0 along with a few DOS commands to\line \expndtw-1 return to the appropriate directory so \b Smalltalk/V \b0 can resume properly. \b Smalltalk/V\line \b0\expndtw0 runs the DOS commands by executing this batch file.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri22\sb155\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As a final example, here is the method executed in \b ScreenDispatcher \b0 when you select \b go\line \expndtw-2 to DOS \b0 from the DOS menu; it exits to the DOS command processor \b COM-\line MAND.COM:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1980\margrsxn2298\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 15: Smalltalk/ V 286 Environment   289}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb302\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 gotoDOS}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "Exit to COMMAND.COM"\line \expndtw-5 self executeCommands:\line \expndtw11 #(ECHO OFF}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1260\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'ECHO TO resume SmaUtalk/V, enter EXIT'\line \expndtw4 CD V\line \expndtw3 COMMAND.COM)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb371\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Font and Cursor Shapes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb428\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Font}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 SmaUtalk/V \b0 represents characters in strings using their ASCII codes. In order to display\line \expndtw-2 them on the screen or printer in bitmapped graphics mode, these ASCII values must be\line \expndtw-6 converted into bitmap images. Class \b CharacterScanner \b0 (refer to Chapter 14) performs the\line \expndtw-3 conversion while instances of class \b Font \b0 provide the character bitmap image needed for\line \expndtw0 conversion, and information about how to retrieve the image.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V \b0 includes two fonts. This section provides you with the information about\line \expndtw1 how to install your own fonts.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The expression \b Font eightline \b0 returns a Font whose characters have a size of 8 by 8\line \expndtw-3 pixels, while the expression \b Font fourteenline \b0 returns a Font with characters of 8 by 14\line \expndtw-6 pixels.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa180 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The following global variables govern the fonts used by different parts of the system:}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2379\margrsxn4875\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1461\colsr1951\colno2\colw1242 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Global Variable}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb191\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 LabelFont\line \expndtw1 IistFont\line \expndtw-2 TextFont\line \expndtw-7 SysFont}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\ul\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Used by}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb191\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 window labels\line \expndtw-7 ListPane\line \expndtw-3 TextPane\line \expndtw-2 all others}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1980\margrsxn2298\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You can change the first three global variables to contain different fonts; when you open\line new windows, you'll see the new fonts being used. To change the \b SysFont \b0 to \b aFont, \b0 use\line \expndtw-2 the expression}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0\sl-403\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Font setSysFont: aFont.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-403\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The instance variables of \b Font \b0 are the following:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-403\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 charSize}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Contains a Point representing the width and height of each character.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2288\margrsxn2004\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 290    Chapter 15: Smalltalk/V 286 Environment}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb320\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 glyphs}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li796\fi0\ri4\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Contains a Form of bitmap images of all characters. The images are attached\line horizontally. For example, if the font contains 100 characters and charSize is 8\line \expndtw-4 by 14, then its glyphs Form will have a width of 800 (100 * 8) and height of 14.\line \expndtw0 Each Font can contain a maximum number of 256 characters.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 xTable}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li792\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Contains an Array of the \b x \b0 coordinate of the origin of each character image in\line \expndtw-1 the \b glyphs \b0 Form. It contains one entry more than the number of characters\line \expndtw1 contained in the Font.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 startChar, endChar}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li788\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Contain the ASCII value of the first and last character, respectively, in the Font.\line \expndtw-3 There cannot be any missing values between \b startChar \b0 and \b endChar. \b0 For\line \expndtw-4 characters with an ASCII value outside of the range \b startChar \b0 to \b endChar, \b0 the\line \expndtw-2 glyph for \b endChar \b0 is used.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 basePoint}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li796\fi0\ri7\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Contains a Point whose \b x \b0 value is always 0 and whose \b y \b0 value indicates the base\line \expndtw2 line of the font.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To install a fixed width new font, evaluate the following expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li799\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Font new}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1184\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 installFixedSize: glyphForm\line \expndtw-5 charSize: sizePoint\line \expndtw-8 startChar: x\line \expndtw-7 endChar: y\line \expndtw-3 basePoint: bPoint}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This initializes all the instance variables and automatically creates the \b xTable.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb356\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Cursor Shapes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb209\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V \b0 uses different cursor shapes to visually indicate system status. For example,\line the hourglass cursor shape is used to indicate a computation is in progress. Cursor shapes\line \expndtw0 are also a good way to convey status information about your applications.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri14\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V \b0 supports the Microsoft Mouse and its compatibles (e.g. PC Mouse,\line \expndtw-3 Logitech Mouse). It therefore has two interfaces to deal with cursors. When you have a\line \expndtw-4 mouse, the cursor is managed by class \b CursorManager. \b0 When you do not have a mouse,\line \expndtw-5 it is managed by class \b NoMouseCursor. CursorManager \b0 merely provides an interface\line \expndtw-3 between \b Smalltalk/V \b0 and the mouse driver supplied by the mouse manufacturer.\line \b\expndtw-2 NoMouseCursor, \b0 on the other hand, handles with Smalltalk code everything related to}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1971\margrsxn2321\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 13: Smalltalk/V 286 Environment    291}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb310\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 the cursor. \b Smalltalk/V \b0 automatically detects whether or not there is a mouse and sets\line \b\expndtw-1 a \b0 global variable, \b Cursor, \b0 to be an instance of the appropriate class. The user interface\line \expndtw0 to Cursor is identical for either class.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Normally when you display something that covers the cursor, you should first hide the\line \expndtw-2 cursor; otherwise, cursor writing by the mouse driver may interfere. To simplify this\line \expndtw-1 problem, all system primitives that can alter the contents of the screen currently make\line \expndtw1 sure that the cursor is hidden before the altering, and restored after.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 For the advanced user, the expression \b Cursor hide \b0 hides the cursor and \b Cursor display\line \b0\expndtw-1 displays it. These two messages work like parentheses: they must be balanced. For\line \expndtw0 example, suppose the cursor is currently being shown; if you hide it twice and then\line \expndtw-3 display it once, you must still display it again to show it. If you are not sure which level\line \expndtw0 the cursor is at, and it is not displayed, evaluate the following expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Cursor reset}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This forces a balance of hide and display and shows the cursor.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri14\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To change the cursor shape, simply send the message \b change \b0 to the cursor instance to\line \expndtw0 which you want to change. For example:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 CursorManager execute change}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri18\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 changes the cursor shape to an hour glass (indicated by \b execute). Smalltalk/V \b0 includes\line \expndtw-3 11 prebuilt cursor shapes:}\par \pard\plain\fs2\sa234\sl-1\slmult0\par\trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-209 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2045 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6257 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Message to}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-346 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2045 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6257 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 CursorManager}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li515\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Shape}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-324 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2045 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6257 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 normal}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li515\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 an arrow pointing north west}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-238 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2045 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6257 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 execute}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li518\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 an hour glass}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-245 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2045 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6257 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 origin}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li515\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 an angle bracket pointing north west}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-238 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2045 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6257 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 corner}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li515\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 an angle bracket pointing south east}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-245 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2045 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6257 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 downArrow}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li518\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 an arrow pointing down}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-238 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2045 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6257 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 upArrow}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li515\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 an arrow pointing up}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-238 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2045 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6257 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 leftArrow}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li511\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 an arrow pointing left}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-245 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2045 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6257 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 rightArrow}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li515\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 an arrow pointing right}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-209 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2045 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6257 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 crossHair}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li511\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 a cross}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-245 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2045 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6257 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 hand}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li511\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 a hand}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-252 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx2045 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6257 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 scroll}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li518\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 four arrows pointing out from the center}\cell \pard\intbl\row \pard\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To temporarily see a cursor shape on the screen, use the same expression followed by a\line \b\expndtw-4 Terminal read. \b0 For example:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2277\margrsxn2025\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 292     Chapter 15: Smalltalk/ V 286 Environment}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb313\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 CursorManager hand change. Terminal read}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 displays the hand cursor, and then returns to normal when you type anything.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Creating a cursor of your own is equally simple. First create \b a \b0 cursor form using the\line \b\expndtw-1 BitEditor \b0 included with \b Smalltalk/V. \b0 (The cursor form must have an extent of 16 @\line \expndtw-3 16.) Then evaluate the following expression:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 NewCursor : =}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li796\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 CursorManager new}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1188\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 initialize: cursorForm hotSpot: aPoint}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The argument \b cursorForm \b0 is the Form containing the cursor image. The hot spot \b aPoint\line \b0\expndtw0 is the point of the image that will be aligned with the actual cursor position. To make\line \expndtw-1 the new cursor the active cursor, evaluate:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 NewCursor change}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2017\margrsxn2256\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs32 16 SMALLTALK/V 286 STANDARD WINDOWS}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri0\sb446\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This section describes the special windows which \b Smalltalk/V \b0 provides for maintenance\line \expndtw-1 and debugging purposes.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \i Disk Browser \i0 displays the files on a given directory, and their contents. It also lets you\line \expndtw1 edit those contents.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri4\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \i Class Hierarchy Browser \i0 shows you the interrelationship of the classes within\line \b\expndtw1 Smalltalk/V, \b0 and lets you edit the code for each class.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Inspectors \i0 let you examine and edit objects. They serve as a low-level debugging aid.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Walkback \i0 and \i Debugger give \i0 views of the state of your program at the point of an error.\line \expndtw-2 They are high-level debugging aids.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Method Browsers \i0 let you browse and edit a list of methods.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Browsers use indexes to access information. Often the information is organized in a\line \expndtw-3 hierarchical manner. A browser is a window consisting of at least two panes, a \i list \i0 pane\line and a \i text \i0 pane. Selecting from the list displays related information in the text pane. This\line \expndtw-1 text may be modified, and eventually saved.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb367\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Disk Browser}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Disk Browser lets you browse the files on a disk device. To open a Disk Browser,\line \expndtw-3 select browse \b disk \b0 on the system menu. \b Smalltalk/V \b0 responds with a menu of available\line \expndtw-2 disk devices. Select the drive whose directory you want to see. \b Smalltalk\b0 /V then opens\line \expndtw-4 the new Disk Browser. You can have as many Disk Browsers open at a time as you want.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Disk Browser is divided into four panes, as shown in Figure 16.1.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \i directory hierarchy \i0 list appears in the upper-left pane. In this pane, the names of all the\line \expndtw0 directories on the disk are listed. You will see a backslash symbol (\\) in the top corner\line \expndtw-2 of the pane. This symbol stands for the root or parent dictionary of the entire disk. The\line names of the directories in the parent directory are indented and appear in hierarchical\line \expndtw-5 order. Initially only the first level of subdirectories are displayed and an ellipsis (...) is used\line \expndtw-3 to indicate that there may be subdirectories. By selecting \b hide/show \b0 from the pane menu\line \expndtw-1 or double-clicking on a directory name, the full subdirectory hierarchy will be shown.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \i file \i0 list is in the pane to the right of the directory hierarchy list. When a directory is\line \expndtw-1 selected in the directory hierarchy list pane, its files will appear in this pane.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \i contents \i0 pane shows the text of a selected file or further directory information if no\line file has been selected.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2329\margrsxn1922\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa418 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 294    Chapter 16: Smalltalk/ V 286 Standard Windows}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2704\margrsxn3928\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1162\colsr788\colno2\colw3326 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb2945\sa0\sl-220\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 16.1\line \expndtw-5 Disk Browser}\par \pard\plain\fs2\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\column\par\trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-223 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1123 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2167 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2275 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx3045 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx3326 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex93\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs14 Smalltalk/Of}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs14 Transcript}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs14 Z}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex72\expndtw-8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ipi[.}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-569 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1123 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1613 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2275 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx3326 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\