 \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 next menu}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-173 \clvmrg\clvertalt\clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1606 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2247 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx2456 \clvmrg\clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx3248 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx3608 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx4249 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx4926 {\par \cell }\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20\cell {\par \cell }\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20\cell \pard\intbl\row \pard\sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2284\margrsxn2007\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb302\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \i save as \i0 function allows the directory information to be written on a file. You can also\line \expndtw-2 edit the information in the contents pane (for example, adding file comments) before\line \expndtw0 writing it on the file.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The other functions of the file contents pane menus are the following:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Read \i0 //is used to read the entire contents of a large file. Normally, when the text of a file\line \expndtw-6 exceeds 10,000 bytes, the pane will only display the first 2,000 bytes, followed by the final\line \expndtw-4 8,000 bytes. Any editing changes performed before \b read it \b0 is selected are lost. After you\line \expndtw-1 select this function, the entire file is read into the pane. The pane menu will change to\line \expndtw1 the one for small files (one on the right). Now you can perform the save functions.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Save as is \i0 used to save edited material in a file different from the selected one \i \{save \i0 rewrites\line \expndtw-2 the selected file with the text in the pane). Selecting this function causes a Prompter to\line \expndtw1 appear which asks for the file name. You must respond with the name of a new or\line \expndtw-5 existing file.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Install \i0 compiles the selected text into the system. The selected text must be in the same\line \expndtw-4 format as the text in the \b change log \b0 or text in a \b class definition \b0 file. It is used to evaluate\line \expndtw0 Smalltalk expressions and to compile and install Smalltalk source code from files.\line \expndtw3 Typically, these would be from the \b change log \b0 or from a file containing a class\line \expndtw2 definition. This use of the \b change log \b0 is described in more detail in Chapter 15.\line Remember that the code you want to install must be in \b change log \b0 format.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb353\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Viewing Directory Contents}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The directory order pane is located immediately below the directory list pane. It allows\line \expndtw0 you to see the full information associated with each file in a directory, as well as to\line control the order in which this information is displayed.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2050\margrsxn2216\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 16: Smalltalk/ V 286 Standard Windows    291}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri18\sb302\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To \i display the directory \i0 in the contents pane, move the cursor into the directory order pane\line \expndtw-1 and press the \b select \b0 key. \b Smalltalk/V \b0 responds by displaying in the contents pane\line \expndtw-2 information about the files in the currently selected directory (the one selected in the\line \expndtw-3 directory list pane).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The directory order pane menu contains selections to determine how to sort the directory\line listing. To display the files in the directory in a different order, pop up the pane menu of\line \expndtw-1 the directory order pane by pressing the \b pane menu \b0 key. You now have a choice of\line \expndtw0 ordering by date, name, or size. After you choose, \b Smalltalk/V \b0 will reorder the files.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb378\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Class Hierarchy Browser}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri18\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Class Hierarchy Browser lets you examine the interrelationships of the classes within\line \b\expndtw1 Smalltalk/V, \b0 and to edit their contents.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb331\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Opening a Class Hierarchy Browser}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri25\sb76\sa302\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Select the \i browse classes \i0 function of the \i system \i0 menu to open a Class Hierarchy Browser.\line \expndtw-1 The Class Hierarchy Browser is divided into five panes as shown in Figure 16.3.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2384\margrsxn3480\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1332\colsr799\colno2\colw3913 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft1951\shptop0\shpright7084\shpbottom1555\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr5\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 75}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn pib}{\sv {\pict\picw9055\pich2743\picwgoal5133\pichgoal1555\jpegblip
}}}{\sp{\sn pictureGray}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn pWrapPolygonVertices}{\sv 8;13;(0,0);(0,21100);(727,21100);(727,21400);(9573,21400);(9573,21600);(21600,21600);(21600,21400);(21600,21400);(21600,21100);(21600,21100);(21600,0);(0,0)}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fIsBullet}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fAllowOverlap}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fEditedWrap}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fBehindDocument}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fPrint}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistLeft}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistRight}{\sv 0}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb2743\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 16.3\line \expndtw-7 Class Hierarchy\line \expndtw-5 Browser}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 panterText: aString font: aFont}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li508\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 "Urite aString whose center is at}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li245\fi331\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 the destination origin using aFont."\line \expndtw-2 CharacterScanner im}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li508\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 initialize: self clipRect font: aFont;\line \expndtw-6 setForeColor: halftone}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li508\fi266\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 backColor: halftone class Mhite;\line \expndtw-5 display: aString}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1040\fi-266\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 at: self location - (aFont width \loch\af16\hich\af16\dbch\f17\cchs0 \'ab\hich\af16\dbch\af17\loch\f16\cchs0  aString size\line 8 aFont height // 2)}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2050\margrsxn2216\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri18\sb259\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \i class hierarchy list \i0 appears in the upper-left pane. In this pane, the names of all of the\line \expndtw-4 classes in the system are presented in a hierarchical order. Notice that \b Object \b0 appears first\line in the list. Class \b Object \b0 is uppermost in the hierarchy. All other classes are subclasses to\line \b\expndtw-1 Object \b0 and therefore appear indented. Subclasses of these classes are indented further.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2347\margrsxn1915\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 298    Chapter 16: Smalltalk/ V 286 Standard Windows}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri18\sb320\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \i method list \i0 pane is the pane to the right of the class hierarchy list. In this pane, either\line \expndtw-1 a list of the instance methods or a list of the class methods is presented. The two small\line \expndtw-3 panes underneath the method list pane are used to select the desired list. You can choose\line \expndtw-5 one by moving the cursor over either the class pane or the instance pane and then pressing\line \expndtw-2 the \b select \b0 key or the left mouse button. There is no pane menu associated with the class\line \expndtw-1 or instance pane. If you press the \b pane menu \b0 key, \b Smalltalk/V \b0 will pop up the same\line \expndtw2 menu that the \b window menu \b0 key pops up.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Smalltalk code itself is seen in the \i contents \i0 pane which occupies the bottom half of\line \expndtw-5 the window. When you select a class in the hierarchy pane, the message which defines the\line class selected is displayed in this pane. Select a method and its code appears. The contents\line \expndtw2 pane is a text pane so it can be edited.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb313\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex81\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Browsing the Hierarchy}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri18\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To \i select a class to browse, \i0 move the cursor into the class hierarchy list pane, the top-left\line \expndtw-2 pane of the window, and select a class by pressing the \b select \b0 key. \b Smalltalk/V \b0 displays\line \expndtw-1 the class definition in the contents pane and a list of methods implemented by the class\line \expndtw-2 in the methods list pane. You can scroll the class hierarchy list pane, like all list panes,\line \expndtw3 with the vertical scrolling keys, Pg Up and Pg Dn, or the mouse.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri18\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The class hierarchy list pane menu contains functions which permit you to write the\line \expndtw0 definition of a class and all of its methods on a file, browse a particular class, add a\line \expndtw-1 subclass and remove a class.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri14\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 File out \i0 writes the class definition along with all of the instance and class messages of the\line selected class to a file. The file is in a special \b change log \b0 format (see \b Automatic Logging\line \expndtw-4 of Changes \b0 in Chapter 15 for more information). \b Smalltalk/V \b0 derives the file name from\line \expndtw2 the class name with the extension \b .els \b0 and places it in the directory from which\line \b\expndtw1 Smalltalk/V \b0 was invoked. Subclasses are not automatically filed out. The \b file out\line \b0 function does not affect the class in the system.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri18\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Update \i0 tells the Class Hierarchy Browser to recompute the class hierarchy list. You must\line \expndtw1 do this if you have removed a class or added a class using another browser.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri18\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Hide/show \i0 lets you hide or show the subclasses of the selected class. It is useful for\line \expndtw-3 shortening the list of classes by hiding the ones not currently of interest. If the subclasses\line \expndtw-5 of a class are hidden, an ellipsis (...) appears after the class name. Note that the same effect\line \expndtw0 can be achieved by simply double-clicking on the desired class.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb29\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Add subclass \i0 permits you to add a subclass to the selected class.\line \i\expndtw-5 Remove class \i0 lets you remove the selected class.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1987\margrsxn2300\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 16: Smalltalk/ V 286 Standard Windows    299}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb313\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Adding Classes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri7\sb205\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To \i add a subclass \i0 of a class, first select in the hierarchy list pane the class that will be the\line \expndtw-2 superclass of the new class. Now pop up the pane menu and select the \b add subclass\line \b0 function. A prompter with the selected class name in the label then asks for a subclass\line \expndtw-6 name.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When you reply to the prompter, a menu then appears asking you what type of subclass\line you want. The subclass type depends upon whether the objects belonging to the class are\line \expndtw-4 to contain named instance variables, indexed instance variables, or byte arrays. After you\line \expndtw-1 make a choice, the new subclass is built and the class hierarchy list is automatically\line \expndtw-4 updated. This subclass is already selected in order to allow you to define its instance and\line \expndtw0 class variables, add methods to it, or define subclasses of it.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb320\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Defining Classes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To define the instance variables, class variables, and pool dictionaries of a class, you must\line \expndtw-3 first select the class in the class hierarchy list pane. \b Smalltalk/V \b0 responds by displaying\line \expndtw-1 the current class definition in the contents pane. The definition includes the class's\line \expndtw-4 superclass, instance variables, class variables, and pool dictionaries. You change the class\line \expndtw-1 definition by editing the text in the contents pane and then selecting the save function\line \expndtw1 from the contents pane menu.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When you change a class definition, \b Smalltalk/V \b0 automatically recompiles all of the\line \expndtw-2 methods in the class and all of its subclasses. In addition, a message is written on the\line \expndtw0 system \b change log \b0 giving the new class definition.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Changes to a class definition take effect immediately so that all future instances of the\line \expndtw-4 class will have the new structure. You should therefore be very careful when modifying\line \expndtw-3 classes that are used by the \b Smalltalk/V \b0 environment because you are changing the\line \expndtw-4 environment. Until you are confident with these procedures, you should define subclasses\line \expndtw0 as opposed to modifying the structure of existing classes.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb317\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Removing Classes}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To \i remove a class, \i0 first select in the hierarchy list pane the class to be removed. Now pop\line up the pane menu and select remove class. The system will prompt you for confirmation.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 All of the methods in the class are automatically removed when the class is removed.\line \b\expndtw-2 Smalltalk/V \b0 will not let you remove a class if it has subclasses or if there are instances\line \expndtw-5 of the class anywhere in the environment. If either of these exists, you will get a walkback\line \expndtw3 window explaining the problem.  Remember that Smalltalk automatically  removes}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1333\margrsxn1921\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw720\colsr313\colno2\colw7621\linebetcol \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb11714\sa0\sl-295\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex41\expndtw-7\dn7\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs40 J)}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 300    Chapter 16: Smalltalk/ V 286 Standard Windows}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri4\sb320\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 (collects as garbage) any object that is not referred to by some other object in the system.\line \expndtw-5 To remove all instances of a class, you must change all references to instances of the class\line \expndtw0 to refer to something else.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You can send the message \b allReferences \b0 to an object to find out all objects that refer\line to the receiver object.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb360\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Browsing the Methods}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri0\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The method list pane is the top-right pane of the window. Remember that there are two\line \expndtw-1 kinds of methods in a class, \b instance \b0 methods and \b class \b0 methods. The list that appears\line \expndtw-3 is determined by the two panes directly beneath the method list pane, the class and the\line \expndtw-2 instance panes. You select the list by moving the cursor over the class or instance pane\line \expndtw1 and then pressing the \b select \b0 key or the right mouse button.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To \i select a method, \i0 move the cursor into the methods list pane and press the \b select \b0 key\line \expndtw-5 when the cursor is on top of the desired message selector you wish to select. \b Smalltalk/ V\line \b0\expndtw-3 responds by placing the source code for the method that implements the message in the\line \expndtw-2 contents pane below. You can move the cursor or scroll the methods list pane and then\line \expndtw1 select a different method by pressing the \b select \b0 key or the right mouse button.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The method list pane menu contains four functions:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri4\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To \i remove a method \i0 from the selected class, select the method, pop up the method list\line \expndtw0 menu, and select the \b remove \b0 function. The method list is immediately updated.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To \i addz. \i0 new method, you can use the \b new method \b0 function. A template that you can\line \expndtw0 edit appears. You can also edit the text of any existing method in the class. The name\line \expndtw-3 of the new method is taken from the first line of the text. In either case, you use the \b save\line \b0\expndtw-1 function to invoke the compiler and install the new method. Remember that there are\line \expndtw-2 two kinds of methods\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 class and instance methods. The kind of method you get is\line \expndtw0 determined by whether you have currently selected the class or instance pane.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If a compilation error is detected, you will see a message in your source in the pane at\line \expndtw-4 the point where the error was detected. The error message is selected (reversed). Edit the\line \expndtw1 text and use the \b save \b0 function again to recompile.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When the method is successfully compiled with no errors, \b Smalltalk/V \b0 installs it into the\line \expndtw-3 class automatically and all future invocations of this method use this new version. The\line \expndtw2 source code of the new or modified method is written onto the \b change log \b0 file.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Senders \i0 causes \b Smalltalk/V \b0 to search all of the methods in the environment for senders\line \expndtw-5 (callers) of the selected message selector. A \i senders \i0 window, which is a kind of Method\line \expndtw-3 Browser, pops up to display each method (and its class) that sends a message with the\line selected message selector.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2011\margrsxn2263\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 16: Smalltalk/ V 286 Standard Windows    301}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li18\fi0\ri0\sb299\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Implementors \i0 causes \b Smalltalk/V \b0 to search all of the classes in the environment for\line \expndtw-5 implementors (definers) of the selected message selector. An \i implementors \i0 window, which\line \expndtw1 is a kind of Method Browser, pops up to display the names of all the classes that\line \expndtw-1 implement methods for the selected message selector.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb346\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Modifying the Source Code for a Method}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri4\sb216\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To see the source code of a different method, move the cursor into the methods list pane\line \expndtw-4 and select another message selector. The contents pane is a text pane used for modifying\line \expndtw0 and adding methods.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri4\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 All of the functions on the contents pane menu behave exactly as described under \b Text\line \expndtw-3 Editor \b0 in Chapter 15.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To \i modify \i0 an existing method, you must first select it in the method list pane. The source\line currently being used by the system is displayed in the contents pane. Edit the source code\line \expndtw-3 in this pane. After editing the source text, you then use the save function on the contents\line \expndtw1 pane menu to invoke the compiler and install the new method in the environment.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb356\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Class Browser}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A Class Browser can be opened in either of two ways. You can send the message \b edit\line \b0\expndtw-2 to any class or you can select the \b browse \b0 function from the hierarchy pane menu of the\line Class Hierarchy Browser described previously in this chapter.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri11\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Move the cursor into any text pane. Type in the name of the class you want to browse\line \expndtw7 followed by \b edit \b0 and select it. Select the \b do it \b0 function from the pane menu.\line \b\expndtw0 Smalltalk\b0 /V then opens a Class Browser for the class.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri14\sb155\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 16.4 shows a Class Browser for the class \b DemoClass. \b0 Class Browsers have three\line \expndtw-3 panes: the \i dictionary list \i0 pane, the \i method list \i0 pane, and the \i contents \i0 pane.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri11\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The label of a Class Browser identifies the class you are browsing. In this window, only\line \expndtw1 the methods for the selected class can be browsed, added, or modified.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The dictionary list pane is the top-left pane that contains the two choices: \b class \b0 and\line \b instance. \b0 If you select \b instance, \b0 then the list of instance methods is displayed in the\line \expndtw-4 method list pane immediately below this pane. If you select \b class, \b0 then the list of class\line \expndtw-3 methods is displayed in the method list pane. There is no pane menu associated with the\line dictionary list pane. If you press the \b pane menu \b0 key, \b Smalltalk/V \b0 will pop up the same\line \expndtw2 menu that the \b window menu \b0 key pops up.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2332\margrsxn1927\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa346 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 302    Chapter 16: Smalltalk/ V 286 Standard Windows}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2703\margrsxn2222\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols3\colno1\colw1206\colsr1202\colno2\colw957\colsr94\colno3\colw3524 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft3337\shptop1181\shpright3337\shpbottom2657\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft7045\shptop691\shpright7045\shpbottom3225\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 13970}}}}{\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft1883\shptop0\shpright7045\shpbottom1210\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr5\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 75}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn pib}{\sv {\pict\picw9105\pich2133\picwgoal5162\pichgoal1209\jpegblip
}}}{\sp{\sn pictureGray}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn pWrapPolygonVertices}{\sv 8;17;(0,0);(0,16071);(0,16071);(0,19800);(0,19800);(0,21342);(0,21342);(0,21600);(2319,21600);(2319,21342);(6205,21342);(6205,19800);(6868,19800);(6868,16071);(21600,16071);(21600,0);(0,0)}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fIsBullet}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fAllowOverlap}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fEditedWrap}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fBehindDocument}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fPrint}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistLeft}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn dxWrapDistRight}{\sv 0}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb2963\sa0\sl-220\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 16.4\line Class Browser}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li43\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 bounceBall}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li43\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 dragon}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li43\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 dragon:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li43\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 Mandala}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li43\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 aultiEUipse}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li43\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\loch\af16\dbch\af17\hich\f16\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 \u9632\'3f\hich\af16\dbch\af17\loch\f16\cchs0 ultiHandala}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li43\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\loch\af16\dbch\af17\hich\f16\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 \u9632\'3f\hich\af16\dbch\af17\loch\f16\cchs0 ultiFantagon}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li47\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-12\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 au It\{Polygon:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li47\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 aultiSpiral}\par \pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb126\sa0{\pict\picw1689\pich952\picwgoal957\pichgoal540\jpegblip
}\par\column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 ilkLine}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li180\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 "Draw a rotating line."\line \expndtw-7 I incrK incrV nuaberOfLines boundX I\line \expndtw-5 Display fill: rectangle\line rule: Fora \i over}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li180\fi263\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\loch\af16\dbch\af17\hich\f16\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 \u9632\'3f\hich\af16\dbch\af17\loch\f16\cchs0 ask: (Display coapatibleHask color: 8).\line \expndtw-6 nuaberOfLines := 68.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li180\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 incrK := rectangle width \i // \i0 nuaberOfLines.\line \expndtw-7 incrV := rectangle height // nuaberOfLines.\line boundX : = incrK \loch\af16\hich\af16\dbch\f17\cchs0 \'ab\hich\af16\dbch\af17\loch\f16\cchs0  (nuaberOfLines \dbch\af17\loch\af16\hich\f16\cchs0 \u9830\'3f\hich\af16\dbch\af17\loch\f16\cchs0  1).\line \expndtw-6 1 to: nuaberOfLines \dbch\af17\loch\af16\hich\f16\cchs0 \u9830\'3f\hich\af16\dbch\af17\loch\f16\cchs0  1 do: [:i I\line \expndtw-5 pen}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1253\fi-544\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 aask: (Display coapatibleltask color:\line \expndtw-6 (Count := Count \dbch\af17\loch\af16\hich\f16\cchs0 \u9830\'3f\hich\af16\dbch\af17\loch\f16\cchs0  1)\line \expndtw-1 // 3 W 7):}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li716\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 dranFroa: i * incrX 0 8+ rectangle origin\line \expndtw-6 to: boundX - (i * incrX) 0 (i \loch\af16\hich\af16\dbch\f17\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af16\dbch\af17\loch\f16\cchs0  IncrV)}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2332\margrsxn1927\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb385\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The method list pane is immediately below the dictionary list pane. It displays the list of\line \b\expndtw-5 class \b0 or \b instance \b0 methods implemented by the class depending on what you choose (class\line \expndtw-2 or instance) in the dictionary list pane. This pane lets you choose a particular method\line implementation for viewing.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri22\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The contents pane is the large pane to the right of the two list panes. It displays the\line \expndtw-2 currently selected method in the method list pane. You can edit and recompile methods\line \expndtw0 in this text pane.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb360\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Browsing Method Lists}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri22\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The method list pane displays a list of the message selectors for either the \b class \b0 methods\line or the \b instance \b0 methods defined in the class. The choice you make in the dictionary list\line \expndtw-1 pane (immediately above this pane) determines which list you see.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri18\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The functions on the methods list pane menu let you remove methods and display the\line \expndtw-5 implementors (definers) and the senders (callers) of messages. Note that this is very similar\line \expndtw-2 to the method list pane described for the Class Hierarchy Browser. The items appearing\line in both menus function in the same manner. See \b Browsing the Methods \b0 for the Class\line \expndtw-3 Hierarchy Browser in this chapter for a description of how to select a method and for\line \expndtw0 explanations of the menu functions.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb353\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Modifying and Adding Methods}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The contents pane is a text pane used to modify and add methods to the class being\line \expndtw0 browsed. All of these functions behave exactly as described under \b Text Editor.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1976\margrsxn2290\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri18\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 16: Smalltalk/ V 286 Standard Windows    303}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri18\sb306\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The process of adding and modifying methods is the same as described for the Class\line Hierarchy Browser. The Class Browser, however, uses a dictionary list pane while the\line \expndtw-1 Class Hierarchy Browser has a class pane and an instance pane.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb353\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 The Inspector}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri22\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 16.5 shows an \i inspector \i0 window. Inspectors are used to examine and change objects\line \expndtw-1 in the system. They are a low-level debugging aid.}\par \pard \plain \li2293\ri310\sb403\sa0{\pict\picw8890\pich2908\picwgoal5040\pichgoal1648\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li313\fi0\ri0\sb1314\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 16.5\line \expndtw-4 Inspector Window}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri22\sb180\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 To open an Inspector, send the message \b inspect \b0 to any object. For example, to open an\line \expndtw0 inspector on the global variable \b Demo, \b0 evaluate the following text:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Demo inspect. \b0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'95}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri25\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Inspectors have two panes. The list pane on the left is the \i instance variable list \i0 pane. The\line pane on the right is the \i instance variable contents \i0 pane.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri22\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The instance variable list pane shows all of the instance variables of the object being\line inspected. The first item in the list is always the name \b self, \b0 which is the object being\line \expndtw0 inspected. The named instance variables, if any, are listed next. If the object being\line \expndtw-3 inspected has indexed instance variables, then they are listed last with numerical indices.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri22\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When you select one of the variables from the list, its current value is displayed in the\line \expndtw0 instance variable contents pane. If you select \b self, \b0 the current value of the inspected\line \expndtw-1 object is displayed.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The pane menu of the list pane has only one function, \b inspect. \b0 When you select this\line function, a new Inspector is opened on the currently selected instance variable. Also, if\line \expndtw1 you click on a selected item, an Inspector window is opened on that item.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2458\margrsxn1819\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 304    Chapter 16: Smalltalk/ V 286 Standard Windows}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri29\sb324\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The instance variable contents pane is a text pane. The pane menu has the normal text\line \expndtw-3 pane functions on it. You can use this pane to evaluate any type of expression that you\line \expndtw0 wish. There are two very important features of this pane:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-227\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-23\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-227\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent227\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-23\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Any expression that you evaluate is compiled in the scope of the object being\line \expndtw4 inspected. This means that you can use the names of all of the instance\line \expndtw-3 variables in your expressions.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-227\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-20\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-227\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent227\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-20\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If you select the save function from the pane menu, the entire contents of the\line \expndtw-1 text pane will be compiled and evaluated. The result of this expression will\line replace the current value of the selected instance variable in the list pane to\line \expndtw-3 the left. Similarly, if you select the \b restore \b0 function, \b Smalltalk/V \b0 places the\line \expndtw0 current value of the selected instance variable into this text pane.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb356\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Inspecting Dictionaries}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A \i Dictionary \i0 is an object which contains associations between keys and values. The\line \expndtw-4 Inspector created for objects which are dictionaries are special. The Dictionary Inspector\line \expndtw0\fs20 has two panes: an \i instance variable list \i0 pane and an \i instance variable contents pane, \i0 just like\line \expndtw-3\fs22 regular Inspectors. Dictionary Inspectors, however, list the keys in the dictionary in the\line instance variable list pane, rather than instance variable names and indices. When you\line \expndtw0 choose a key in the pane, the associated value is displayed in the instance variable\line \expndtw2 contents pane. Notice that \b self \b0 is not on the list pane of a dictionary inspector.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri11\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The dictionary inspector pane menu has three selections. Notice that there are two\line \expndtw1 functions not present on a normal inspector's pane menu.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li32\fi0\ri0\sb22\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Remove \i0 allows you to remove the association for a selected key from the Dictionary.\line \i\expndtw-1 Inspect \i0 opens an Inspector on the value associated with the currently selected key.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb122\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Add \i0 allows you to add a new element to the dictionary. The system pops up a Prompter\line asking for the new key. The associated value is nil until you select the new key, change\line \expndtw1 the nil value in the contents pane, and save it.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb378\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Debugger Windows}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There are two debugger windows: a \i walkback \i0 window and a \i debug \i0 window. A walkback\line \expndtw3 window pops up automatically when errors are detected. When you need more\line \expndtw0 information than provided in the walkback window, you explicitly request a debug\line window using the walkback window menu.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1988\margrsxn2295\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri18\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 16: Smalltalk/ V 286 Standard Windows    303}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb302\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex82\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Walkback Window}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri11\sb205\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You request a walkback window by sending the \b error: \b0 message to any object with a\line string describing the error as argument. For example,}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb126\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 self error: 'Index is outside of collection bounds'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb144\sa0\sl-245\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V \b0 also places a walkback window on the screen when any of the following\line \expndtw-4 occur.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0\tx616 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 \'95{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 The message \b halt \b0 is sent to any object, for example:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1192\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 self halt}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li616\fi-205\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx616 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li616\fi-205\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-234\slmult0\tx616 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The break key is typed at the same time that the control key is pressed,\line \expndtw-2 causing a \b control-break \b0 interrupt.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li616\fi-205\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx616 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li616\fi-205\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0\tx616 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent205\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The message-send nesting gets too deep, resulting in a stack overflow\line \expndtw-2 condition.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 16.6 shows a walkback window produced by evaluating the expression in the\line \expndtw-2 System Transcript. This expression attempts to access the eighth character in the string\line \b\expndtw0 'hello', \b0 an obvious error.}\par \pard \plain \li2315\ri652\sb353\sa0{\pict\picw8216\pich1879\picwgoal4658\pichgoal1065\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2855\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs14 itringCObject)\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 error:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2833\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs14 Str ing( I ndexsdCo 1 lect ion)\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 errorl nBounds:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2833\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs14 5tr ingCI ndexedCo 1 lect ion) \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 checkl ndax:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2833\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs14 String\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\'ab\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 t:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2833\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs14 Jndef inedObj ect\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'bb\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0 Do i t}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li306\fi0\ri0\sb1224\sa0\sl-220\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 16.6\line Walkback Window}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb241\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The label of the window describes the error. The text pane of the window contains a\line \i\expndtw-2 method walkback \i0 showing the incomplete message sends that led to the error. Each line\line in the text pane represents a single message send, with the most recent send listed first.\line \expndtw-3 On each line, the class of the receiver is given first. If the method used is defined in a\line \expndtw-2 superclass of the receiver, the class in which the method is defined appears next in\line \expndtw2 parentheses. The string on the right following ">>" is the message selector.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2464\margrsxn1828\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 306    Chapter 16: Smalltalk/V 286 Standard Windows}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li432\fi-414\ri0\sb198\sa0\sl-396\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Sometimes you will see lines of the form:\line \b\expndtw3 [] in ClassName \b0 >> \b methodName}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This indicates that an error occurred during the evaluation of a block in the method\line \expndtw-1 methodName of the class ClassName.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Whenever you get a walkback window, you generally do one of three things:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-227\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-21\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-227\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent227\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-21\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You can determine what the problem is from the information contained in\line \expndtw-4 the walkback window. In this case, you normally close the walkback window\line \expndtw2 and then go fix the problem.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-227\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-21\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-227\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent227\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-21\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You can resume execution at the point of interruption, provided that the\line \expndtw-3 walkback window occurred either as a result of a \b control-break \b0 interrupt, or\line because a \b halt \b0 message was sent. In these cases, there is nothing wrong with\line \expndtw-4 the program, so you can pop up the pane menu for the walkback window and\line \expndtw-1 select \b resume. \b0 The walkback window closes and execution continues.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-227\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-20\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-227\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent227\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-20\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You can decide that you need more information, and would like to use the\line \i\expndtw-5 debuggerto \i0 obtain it. In this case, you pop up the pane menu for the walkback\line \expndtw7 window and select \b debug. \b0 The walkback window closes and you are\line \expndtw1 prompted for the corners of the debugger window:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb346\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Debugger Window}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The debugger window gives you an expanded view of the walkback and allows\line \expndtw0 controlled execution of a process. The window has six panes. Figure 16.7 shows a\line \expndtw-2 debugger window.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn4840\margrsxn2008\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft5033\shptop1159\shpright5033\shpbottom3362\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}\pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb302\sa0{\pict\picw8928\pich3327\picwgoal5061\pichgoal1886\jpegblip
}\par\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li367\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 |at: anInteger}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li929\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 "Answer the character at position}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li662\fi335\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-133\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\loch\af16\hich\af16\dbch\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 \'ab\hich\af16\dbch\af17\loch\f16\cchs0 nInteger in the receiver string."\line \expndtw-11 <prii*itive: 63>}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx652\posnegy-43\absw0\absh-209\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 i"}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft648\shptop-11\shpright2390\shpbottom-11\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 25400}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li864\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs14 maiiuUMmai^miiuitusat-}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft648\shptop-40\shpright2390\shpbottom-40\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 31750}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li882\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs12 If priMitiveFailed}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2464\margrsxn1828\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li353\fi0\ri0\sb292\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure 16.7\line \expndtw-5 Debugger Window}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn1942\margrsxn2324\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 16: Smalltalk/ V 286 Standard Windows    307}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li29\fi0\ri0\sb306\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The top left list pane serves two purposes: presenting a walkback and listing breakpoints.\line If the pane labeled walkback is selected, the pane above has the same walkback list that\line \expndtw-4 appears in a walkback window. When you select a walkback line, the other panes contain\line \expndtw-2 related information.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li25\fi0\ri4\sb169\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If the pane labeled breakpoints is selected, the pane above lists the class name and\line \expndtw2 method selector for all methods which have breakpoints set. When you select a\line \expndtw-3 breakpoint line, the pane below displays the source code for the selected method. Setting\line \b a \b0 breakpoint in a method causes execution to stop when the method is entered, provided\line \expndtw-2 that execution is under control of the debugger (see the description of the \b hop, skip \b0 and\line \b\expndtw-1 jump \b0 buttons below).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li22\fi0\ri7\sb140\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The bottom pane displays the source code for the selected method and, if \b walkback \b0 is\line \expndtw-3 selected, the source code for the currently executing statement is highlighted. This pane\line \expndtw-4 also serves as a text editor so you can change the code as you do with the Class Hierarchy\line \expndtw1 Browser. You update a method by selecting the save entry on the pane menu. If a\line \expndtw0 selected walkback method is updated in this way, all walkback entries above the\line \expndtw-1 bottom-most occurrence of the method in the walkback list are discarded, because a\line \expndtw1 method they would return to has changed.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri11\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The two panes on the top right serve as an inspector for the receiver, arguments and\line \expndtw-4 temporary variables of the selected method. The variable name pane on the left of the two\line \expndtw-3 contains \b self, \b0 representing the receiver, and the names of all arguments and temporary\line \expndtw0 variables. The variable pane on the right displays the value of the selected variable.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri14\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The menu of the variable name pane contains a single entry: inspect which allows you\line to spawn another inspector on the selected object. For example, you can inspect the\line instance variables of the receiver by double-clicking on self.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri14\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The menu of the variable value pane is a standard text editing menu. When you select\line \b\expndtw-4 save, \b0 the value of the selected variable is changed to contain the results of evaluating the\line \expndtw-1 expression in the value pane.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri18\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The label bar of the debugger window contains three buttons related to debugging: \b hop,\line \expndtw0 skip \b0 and \b jump. \b0 These work as follows:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx623 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx623 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 hop \b0 - executes the next expression in the debugged process.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li623\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx623 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li623\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx623 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 skip \b0 - executes the next expression in the selected method or up to the next\line \expndtw5 breakpoint, whichever comes first. Note that skip may execute several\line \expndtw-3 expressions in lower-level methods.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx623 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx623 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 jump \b0 - executes up to the next breakpoint.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri22\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The menu of the walkback list pane contains the entries \b resume, restart, senders,\line \expndtw-2 implementors, add breakpoint \b0 and \b remove breakpoint, \b0 defined as follows:}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2561\margrsxn1665\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li47\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 308    Chapter 16: Smalltalk/ V 286 Standard Windows}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-209\ri0\sb324\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-209\ri0\sb324\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 resume \b0 - as in the walkback window, allows you to continue execution after\line \expndtw-3 a  \b halt   \b0 message   or  a  \b control-break   \b0 interrupt.  The   debugger   window\line \expndtw-1 disappears and execution continues from the point of interruption. You will\line \expndtw2 not be allowed to resume, however, if you have changed a method in the\line \expndtw-2 walkback list.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 restart \b0 - if a method is selected, the debugger window disappears and\line \expndtw1 execution is restarted by re-sending the message in the selected walkback\line \expndtw-4 entry.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 senders \b0 - as in the Class Hierarchy Browser, a Method Browser window is\line \expndtw5 popped up listing all methods in \b Smalltalk/V \b0 which send the message\line \expndtw1 corresponding to the selected method.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 implementors \b0 - as in the Class Hierarchy Browser, a Method Browser\line \expndtw0 window is popped up listing all methods in \b Smalltalk/V \b0 which implement\line \expndtw2 a method with the same name as the selected method.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 add breakpoint \b0 - two prompter windows appear, requesting the class and\line \expndtw3 selector of the method to add to the breakpoint list.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  \'95\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li637\fi-209\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx637 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnstart-1\pnindent209\pnf3{\pntxtb \'95}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 remove breakpoint \b0 - the selected breakpoint entry is removed from the\line \expndtw-1 breakpoint list.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li36\fi0\ri0\sb364\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Method Browser}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The Method Browser lets you browse and edit a list of methods. There are two ways to\line \expndtw1 open a Method Browser. Selecting \b senders \b0 in any of the list pane menus (e.g., the\line \expndtw-3 method list pane menu of the Class Hierarchy Browser) will open a Method Browser on\line \expndtw-2 the list of all methods in the system that send the selected message selector. Selecting\line \b\expndtw0 implementors \b0 in any of the list pane menus will open a Method Browser on the list of\line \expndtw-2 all methods that implement the selected message selector. These two windows are often\line \expndtw-6 referred to as the \i senders \i0 window and the \i implementors \i0 window respectively. Figure 16.8\line \expndtw1 shows a Method Browser on all senders of the message \b open.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri18\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Method Browsers have two panes. The list pane on the top is the \i method list \i0 pane, the\line \expndtw3 pane on the bottom is the \i text \i0 pane.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri18\sb140\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The method list pane shows a list of methods identified by the class and message selector.\line \expndtw-2 When you select a method in the list, the source code for the method is displayed in the\line \expndtw2 text pane. The pane menu of the list pane has two functions:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri14\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 senders \i0 causes \b Smalltalk/V \b0 to search all of the methods in the environment for methods\line that send (call) the selected message selector. A new Method Browser is opened on the\line \expndtw3 methods found.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 implementors \i0 causes \b Smalltalk/V \b0 to search all of the methods in the environment \b for\line \b0\expndtw-2 methods that implement (define) the selected message selector. A new Method Browser\line \expndtw3 is opened on the methods found.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2074\margrsxn2211\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li3794\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Chapter 16: Smalltalk/ V 286 Standard Windows    309}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn4651\margrsxn5821\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \li0\ri0\sb0\sa0\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx2833\posnegy1501\absw0\absh518\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\pict\picw1536\pich914\picwgoal871\pichgoal518\jpegblip
}\par{\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-263\shptop587\shpright4899\shpbottom1314\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr5\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 75}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn pib}{\sv {\pict\picw9105\pich1282\picwgoal5162\pichgoal727\jpegblip
