0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 Appendix 2: PRIMITIVE METHODS}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb684\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 How Primitive Methods Work}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Computing is done in a Smalltalk system by objects sending messages to each other. The\line useful work, however, is performed in \i primitive \i0 methods. Primitive methods perform\line \expndtw-3 low-level functions such as arithmetic operations, indexed instance variable access, and\line \expndtw-4 device access. They are also used for higher-level but performance-critical methods such\line \expndtw-1 as stream access and block transfers.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri4\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Primitive methods are identified with an integer primitive number enclosed in angle\line \expndtw1 brackets following the message pattern. For example, the implementation of the\line \expndtw-1 subscripting method \b at: \b0 in class \b Object \b0 is as follows:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 at: index}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li821\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ^primitive: 60 >\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-6 a \scaps0\b self primitiveFailed}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Primitive methods have two parts: (l) an assembly language part and (2) a Smalltalk part.\line \expndtw-3 The assembly language part is identified by the number following \b primitive: \b0 in angle\line \expndtw-1 brackets. The Smalltalk part follows the angle brackets.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The assembly language part of a primitive is executed first. It concludes by either\line \expndtw-1 succeeding (returning an object that is the method result) or failing. If the assembly\line \expndtw-4 language part fails, the Smalltalk part is executed to return the method result. This shared\line responsibility works efficiently because the assembly language code handles the most\line \expndtw1 common but simple cases. Since Smalltalk is much easier to write than assembly\line \expndtw0 language, the Smalltalk code handles the infrequent but complex cases.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb374\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Primitive Number Assignments}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb227\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The table below lists the primitive methods used by \b Smalltalk/V 286. \b0 For each primitive,\line \expndtw0 the primitive number, the method selectors, and the classes in which it is used are\line \expndtw-4 presented.}\par \pard\plain\fs2\sa220\sl-1\slmult0\par\trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-238 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1022 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx3542 \clvertalt\clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx6285 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Primitive}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li54\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Used in method:}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li623\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Implemented in class:}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-425 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlrtb\cellx1022 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15\cltxlr