ang1033\fs22 Time class}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-259 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx936 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4075 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6307 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li292\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 123}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li140\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 primitiveEndByte}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li104\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 FileHandle}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-230 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx936 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4075 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6307 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li292\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 124}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li144\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 openln:}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li101\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 FileHandle}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-245 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx936 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4075 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6307 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li292\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 125}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li140\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 readInto:atPage:}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li97\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 FileHandle}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-245 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx936 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4075 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6307 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li292\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 126}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li140\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 writeFrom:toPage:for:}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li101\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 FileHandle}\cell \pard\intbl\row \trowd\trgaph40 \trrh-230 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx936 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx4075 \clvertalt\cltxlrtb\cellx6307 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li288\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 127}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li137\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 primitiveClose}\cell \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li101\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \intbl \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 FileHandle}\cell \pard\intbl\row \pard\sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2179\margrsxn2097\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Appendix 2: Primitive Methods     499}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb349\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 User-Defined Primitive Methods}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri7\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 User-defined primitives are implemented as protected mode subroutines. These subrou-\line \expndtw-2 tines are collected into primitive modules that are loaded by \b Smalltalk/V 286 \b0 after the\line \expndtw0 image is loaded. The primitive modules are memory image files \b (.BIN \b0 format) and\line \expndtw-1 contain a table of the names and entry points of the primitives (subroutines) included.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb317\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex81\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Object Pointers}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri11\sb205\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V 286 \b0 uses 32-bit memory addresses as object pointers. Pointers with a\line \expndtw-5 segment value of 6 are \i positive \i0\b Smalllntegers. \b0 Pointers with a segment value of 116H are\line \i\expndtw-1 negative \i0\b Smalllntegers. \b0 The offset of a Smalllnteger is a 15 bit magnitude (always\line \expndtw-5 positive). This gives a range Of-32767 to +32767 for small integers. All other pointers are\line \expndtw1 the direct memory address of the object.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri11\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V 286 \b0 uses a generation scavenging garbage collector. When an object\line \expndtw-4 pointer is stored into an object, data structures internal to the memory manager may need\line \expndtw2 to be updated if cross generational references have been created or modified. An\line \expndtw-1 assembly language macro is provided for this purpose.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb320\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex81\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Accessing Objects within Primitives}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri14\sb205\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Certain object pointers may need to be referenced by a user primitive. These objects are\line \expndtw-4 at fixed addresses. The file \b fixdptrs.usr \b0 contains assembly language definitions for these\line \expndtw-3 fixed pointers.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri14\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 All objects have a twelve byte header. This is followed by the instance variables. Instance\line \expndtw-2 variables of an object are all of the same size. Objects that contain object pointers have\line \expndtw-4 32 bit instance variables; all others have eight bit instance variables. The order of instance\line \expndtw-3 variables in an object is the order in which the variables are defined by the class of the\line \expndtw-4 object (don't forget about inherited instance variables). Assembly language definitions for\line \expndtw1 the structure of the object header and some common objects is in file \b object.usr.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri22\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Often you need to check the class of an object to see if it is appropriate for the primitive.\line \expndtw-1 You can use the \b ClassPtrHash \b0 field in the object header for this purpose. The file\line \b\expndtw-3 object.usr \b0 also contains assembly language definitions of the fixed class hashes in the\line \expndtw-4 environment.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri22\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk methods cannot corrupt object memory because an object is not allowed to\line access outside itself. Primitive methods can access all of object memory, and therefore\line \expndtw-3 have the opportunity to corrupt object memory. The implementor of a primitive has the\line \expndtw-2 responsibility to guarantee that only the instance variables of the receiver object are\line \expndtw-5 changed. If you have been using (debugging) a primitive that may have stored erroneously\line \expndtw-1 in memory, discard the image.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2387\margrsxn1884\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 300    Appendix 2: Primitive Methods}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2387\margrsxn1884\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb353\sa0\tx7484 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-18\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Loading Primitives{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw0 j}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A module containing user primitives is loaded into \b Smalltalk/V 286 \b0 by sending the\line \expndtw-3 message \b loadPrimitivesFrom: \b0 to an instance of SystemDictionary. The argument is the\line \expndtw0 file path name where the file of primitives is located. For example:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk loadPrimitivesFrom: 'example.bin'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If you have several files containing \i primitives, \i0 then evaluate the above expression for each\line \expndtw2 file. These expressions are usually included in the \b go \b0 file to make the loading of\line \expndtw-3 primitives automatic.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb356\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Reserving Space for Primitives}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri11\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Primitives are loaded into memory in the address space of DOS (below 64OK). Unless you\line \expndtw-4 tell it otherwise, \b Smalltalk/V 286 \b0 will allocate all of memory to itself, therefore you must\line \expndtw-2 reserve memory for your primitives. You specify the amount of space to reserve for\line \expndtw0 primitives and DOS as the argument to the /d: parameter on the command line that\line \expndtw-1 invokes \b Smalltalk/V 286. \b0 For example, the following command invokes \b Smalltalk/V\line \b0\expndtw0 and reserves 200K for DOS and primitives to use:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw8\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 v /d:200}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri11\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You must specify enough memory to load all of your primitives. If you are also using the\line \expndtw-2 DOS shell feature you need to add that to your reservation request size. Notice that the\line \expndtw2 argument is a decimal number and specifies the amoung of memory to reserve in\line \expndtw-3 multiples of 1024 bytes. Appendix 3, \b Configuring Smalltalk/V 286, \b0 explains all of the\line \expndtw0 command line options in detail.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb356\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Macros}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri11\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Assembly language macros are provided to simplify writing user primitives. These handle\line \expndtw-1 all of the details of interfacing with the Smalltalk interpreter. The macros are contained\line in file access.usr. The following macros are provided.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 enterPrimitive}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li792\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This must be the first instruction in a user primitive. It saves the old stack frame\line \expndtw3 address and sets up \b BP \b0 to point to the arguments on the stack:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx785\posnegy695\absw832\absh-727\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b\i0\cf0\charscalex109\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 BP+14\line \expndtw-15 BP+18^\line \b0\i\expndtw-11 etc...}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phcol\posnegx1742\posnegy698\absw5483\absh-494\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 is the DWORD containing the second argument object point*\line \expndtw1 is the DWORD containing the third argument object pointer.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li878\fi0\ri0\sb234\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 BP+6] \b0 is the DWORD containing the receiver object pointer.\line \b\expndtw2 BP+10] \b0 is the \b DWORD \b0 containing the first argument object pointer.\line \b BP+14   \b0 is the DWORD containing the second argument object pointer.\line BP+18    is the DWORD containing the third argument ohiecr noinfer.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2653\margrsxn2020\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri7\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Appendix 2: Primitive Methods     301}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb299\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 exitWithSuccess}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li400\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This macro is used to exit the primitive when it has successfully computed an\line \expndtw1 object pointer as the method result. The 32 bit method result is placed in\line \b\expndtw0 DX,AX \b0 with DX containing the segment part and AX containing the offset\line \expndtw-4 part.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 exitWithFailure}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li407\fi0\ri7\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This macro is used to exit the primitive when it cannot compute the method\line \expndtw2 result. An example would be a floating point primitive that is passed an\line \expndtw1 argument that is not a floating point number.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 oldToNewUpdate}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li407\fi0\ri11\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This macro must be invoked after every store of an object pointer into an\line \expndtw-2 object. It updates several memory management data structures, if necessary.\line Failure to invoke this macro can lead to very bizarre and unpredictable results.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 getElementSize segReg ,offsetReg ,resultWordReg ,resultByteReg}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li396\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This macro returns the number of instance variables in the object referred to by\line \b segReg:OffsetReg. \b0 The high order 8 bits of the result is in \b resultByteRejf \b0 and\line \expndtw0 the low order 16 bits is in \b result WordReg. \b0 Note that this is not the same as\line \expndtw2 the size of the object in bytes.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 getByteSize seg Reg ,offsetReg ,result WordReg ,resultByteReg}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li400\fi0\ri11\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This macro returns the number of bytes, including the header, occupied by the\line \expndtw-1 object referred to by \b segReg-.OffsetReg. \b0 The high order 8 bits of the result is\line in \b resultByteReg \b0 and the low order 16 bits is in \b result WordReg. \b0 Note that\line \expndtw1 this is not the same as the number of instance variables.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb140\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 isPointerObject seg Reg,offset Reg}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li400\fi0\ri14\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This macro tests whether the object referred to by \b segReg-.OffsetReg \b0 contains\line \expndtw-4 object pointers or bytes. For example, \b Strings \b0 contain bytes and \b Arrays \b0 contain\line \expndtw-2 object pointers. It sets the condition code \b zero \b0 if it contains bytes and sets the\line \expndtw1 condition code \b non-zero \b0 if it contains object pointers.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb130\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 isIndexedObject segReg,offsetReg}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li400\fi0\ri14\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This macro tests whether the object referred to by \b segReg:OffsetReg \b0 contains\line \expndtw-4 indexed instance variables. For example, \b Strings \b0 and \b Arrays \b0 contain indexed\line \expndtw0 instance variables, and \b Dictionaries \b0 and \b Dates \b0 do not. It sets the condition\line \expndtw1 code \b zero \b0 if it does not contain indexable instance variables and sets the\line \expndtw0 condition code \b non-zero \b0 if does.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2207\margrsxn2063\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li54\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 502    Appendix 2: Primitive Methods}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li428\fi0\ri0\sb313\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 isSmallObject segReg,offsetReg}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li832\fi0\ri0\sb169\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This macro tests whether the object seqment referenced by \b segReg:OffsetReg\line \b0 is contained in a single 64K byte segment. It sets the condition code \b zero \b0 if it\line \expndtw-3 does not fit in a single segment and sets the condition code \b non-zero \b0 if it does.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li428\fi0\ri0\sb126\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 isSmallPosInt segmentExpression}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li828\fi0\ri4\sb169\sa0\sl-230\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This macro tests whehter the object segment referenced by \b segmentExpression\line \b0\expndtw-3 is a \i positive \i0\b Smalllnteger. \b0 It sets the condition code \b equal \b0 if it is and sets the\line \expndtw1 condition code \b non-equal \b0 if it is not.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li425\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 isSmallNeglnt segmentExpression}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li817\fi0\ri4\sb166\sa0\sl-230\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This macro tests whether the object segment referenced by \b segmentExpression\line \b0\expndtw-3 is a \i negative \i0\b Smalllnteger. \b0 It sets the condition code \b equal \b0 if it is and sets the\line \expndtw1 condition code \b non-equal \b0 if it is not.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 interruptVM}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li817\fi0\ri4\sb166\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This macro places the interrupt number contained in AL onto the interrupt\line \expndtw-3 queue of the interpreter. It is used by primitives that need to issue Smalltalk\line interrupts. This macro is used in protected mode primitives. There is another\line \expndtw0 macro for use in real mode interrupt service routines (described below).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb346\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Interrupt Service Routines}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri4\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V 286 \b0 lets you provide your own interrupt service routines, for example your\line \expndtw-2 own communications driver. Interrupt service routines are different from primitives in\line \expndtw-3 that they are not callable by the Smalltalk interpreter. Thy are entered in response to a\line \expndtw-4 machine interrupt. They also run in real mode instead of protected mode. This means that\line \expndtw-1 they do not have access to object memory. They do have access to all DOS and BIOS\line interrupts. They communicate with Smalltalk via virtual machine interrupts and via\line \expndtw-4 memory shared in the segment with Smalltalk primitives. It is for this reason that they are\line \expndtw-3 included in the same primitive module as the Smalltalk primitives that access the shared\line \expndtw-7 memory.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb173\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Interrupt service routines cannot store or refer to object pointers in any way. There is no\line \expndtw0 guarantee as to the state of the garbage collector at the time of the hardware interrupt,\line \expndtw1 so the pointers may not be valid.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri11\sb169\sa0\sl-234\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The following macro is included in file access.usr to allow interrupt service routines to\line issue Smalltalk virtual machine interrupts.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2039\margrsxn2245\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri18\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Appendix 2: Primitive Methods     503}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb299\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 ISVinterruptVM}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li796\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This macro places the interrupt number contained in AL onto the interrupt\line \expndtw-4 queue of the interpreter. It is used by interrupt service routines that wish to issue\line \expndtw-3 Smalltalk interrupts, for instance a communications driver. This macro is only\line \expndtw0 used by interrupt servcie routines and is only invokable from real mode.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb356\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Constructing Primitive Modules}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A primitive module is composed of four pieces that are assembled or linked into a single\line \expndtw1 memory image segment that starts at offset 0. These are the module header, the\line \expndtw-1 initialization routine, one or more primitive subroutines and interrupt service routines,\line and the entry point table. The file \b example.prm \b0 shows the assembly language source\line \expndtw-4 code for a complete primitive module. To build the primitive module, first assemble this\line \expndtw2 source file into the file \b example.obj. \b0 Then link it with the linker to produce the\line \expndtw-3 executable file \b example.exe.. \b0 Finally use the exe2bin program to turn it into the memory\line \expndtw-1 image file \b example.bin. \b0 Here are some sample DOS commands:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 masm example.prm;\line \expndtw-3 link example.obj;\line exe2bin example.exe}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The header must be the first 16 bytes of the segment when it is loaded into memory. The\line \expndtw1 format of the 16 byte module header is:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 0: module initialization entry point offset}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li410\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 2: reserved}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 4: reserved}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 6: physical segment address (filled in when loaded)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 8: offset of table of primitive entry point offsets}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 10: physical segment address of interpreter parameter area (filled in when loaded)}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx5558 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 12: reserved{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\i\expndtw0 "}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 14: reserved}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The installation routine for the primitive module is entered via a far call after the module\line \expndtw-1 is loaded. This allows any load time initialization to be done by the module before any\line of the primitives are called.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A primitive is entered via a far call when the associated Smalltalk method is invoked. The\line \expndtw-4 primitive must either succeed or fail. If it succeeds, the resultant object pointer is returned\line \expndtw0 in the \b DX,AX \b0 register pair, with DX holding the segment part and AX the offset part.\line \expndtw3 If it fails, \b DX \b0 and AX must be set to zero.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 User primitives are identified by names instead of numbers. The primitive entry point\line \expndtw-2 table contains the names and offset of all the primitives in the module. It does not have\line \expndtw2 any of the interrupt service routines since these are not callable from Smalltalk. The}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2435\margrsxn1857\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 504    Appendix 2: Primitive Methods}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb310\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 format of the entry point table is:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx972 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 DB{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-6 "name of primitive 1*}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx972 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 DB{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw0 0}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx972 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw25\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 DW{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw0 offset of entry point 1}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx972 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 DB{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw-1 'name of primitive 2'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx972 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 DB{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw0 0}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx972 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw23\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 DW{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\expndtw0 offset of entry point 2}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb450\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 DW   0 ;marks end of table}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li14\fi0\ri0\sb328\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex81\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Invoking User Primitives from Smalltalk}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb205\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Whenever a method is invoked that gives the user primitive as its implementation, the\line \expndtw-4 user primitive is entered via a far call. User primitives are referred to by the name given\line \expndtw-3 them in the containing primitive module. For example, the sample primitive is named\line \b\expndtw1 userStringAt: \b0 so the following method could be added to class \b String.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li400\fi0\ri0\sb133\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 examplePrimAt: index}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li821\fi382\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 "This method invokes the sample primitive in the file example.bin."\line \expndtw2 ^primitive: userStringAt: >\line \b0\scaps\expndtw-4 a \scaps0\b self error: 'user prim failed'}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 After the primitive module \b example.bin \b0 is loaded, the following expression would\line \expndtw-2 invoke the user primitive:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb137\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 'Now \b is the time' examplePrimAt: 5}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2230\margrsxn2047\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 Appendix 3: Configuring Smalltalk/V 286}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb454\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 This appendix presents information for configuring \b Smalltalk/V 286 \b0 for different\line \expndtw0 memory, hardware, and BIOS configurations. If you are having trouble starting the\line \expndtw-1 system up for the first time and suspect hardware or software incompatibilities, please\line \expndtw0 read the read.me file for up-to-date information.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri4\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V \b0 is configured by adding parameters to the DOS command line that invokes\line the Smalltalk environment. There are five possible configuration parameters: three deal\line \expndtw-5 with memory configurations and two deal with hardware and BIOS configuration. Briefly,\line \expndtw0 these parameters are (they are described in more detail in the following sections):}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 /d:nnn \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  Reserve \b nnn \b0 Kbytes (decimal) of memory for DOS shell and\line \expndtw0 primitives in the address space of DOS.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 /x:nnn \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  Reserve \b nun \b0 Kbytes (decimal) of memory in the beginning of\line \expndtw1 extended memory for other protected mode programs to use.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li407\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 /u:nnn \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  Use a maximum of \b nnn \b0 Kbytes (decimal) of extended memory for\line \expndtw-7 Smalltalk.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 /s:x where x is either s or h \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  Use hardware (h) or software (s) shutdown logic\line \expndtw2 to switch to real mode from protected mode.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li403\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 /t:x where x is either p or s \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\f3\cchs0 \'97\hich\af1\dbch\af3\loch\f1\cchs0  Use either primary (p) or secondary (s) startup\line \expndtw2 logic to start up in real mode after a shut down in protected mode.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri4\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When more than one parameter is specified they may be separated by blanks. Letters may\line \expndtw-3 be either upper or lower case. Numeric arguments are always decimal (base 10). There\line cannot be a space between the : and the argument in a parameter. The following are both\line \expndtw-2 valid command lines:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li396\fi0\ri0\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw6\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 v /D-.100 /s:h\line \expndtw4 V /t:P /U-.2048}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb364\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-11\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Memory Configuration}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Unless told otherwise, \b Smalltalk/V 286 \b0 will use all of available memory. Logically\line \expndtw0 speaking there are two kinds of memory available when the interpreter is invoked,\line \expndtw1 available DOS memory (memory accessable in real mode) and available extended\line \expndtw-1 memory (memory accessable in protected mode only).}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2364\margrsxn1903\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw5\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 .5\i 06    Appendix 3.Configuring Smalltalk/ V 286}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb342\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 DOS Memory}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri22\sb212\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If you are going to use the DOS shell or if you are going to load user primitive modules\line then you must reserve some of the DOS memory. The \b /d:nnn \b0 parameter specifies the\line amount of DOS memory to reserve in IK (1024) byte increments. The \b nnn \b0 is in decimal.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri0\sb155\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 When \b Smalltalk/V 286 \b0 is invoked, it allocates parts of itself in extended memory if\line \expndtw-3 needed to reserve the amount of space requested. However certain parts must be in real\line mode memory (approximately 100K).}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb342\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-14\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 Extended Memory}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri14\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/ V 286 \b0 allocates its extended memory starting at the end of available extended\line \expndtw-5 memory (high physical address) and going forward (towards low physical address). There\line \expndtw1 are two ways to control the amount of memory allocated in extended memory.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li14\fi0\ri14\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \b /u:nnn \b0 parameter specifies the maximum amount of extended memory that\line \b\expndtw-1 Smalltalk/V 286 \b0 is to allocate in IK byte increments. The \b nnn \b0 is in decimal. This sets\line \expndtw0 an upper bound. If less memory is available, less will be allocated.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li11\fi0\ri11\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \b /x:nnn \b0 parameter specifies the amount of extended memory not to allocate at the\line \expndtw-3 front (low physical address) of extended memory in IK byte increments- The nnn is in\line decimal. This reserves a portion of extended memory for other protected mode programs\line \expndtw0 to use.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Note that \b Smalltalk/V 286 \b0 detects the memory at the front of extended memory (low\line physical address) used by \b VDISK \b0 or multiple \b VDISKs \b0 and automatically treats the\line \expndtw-3 memory as not available. The parameters above only refer to available memory and not\line \b\expndtw-1 VDISK \b0 memory.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb353\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-14\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs24 An Example}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb187\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 As an example, let's take the following situation:}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx241 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-21\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  1.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx241 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent241\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-21\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 150K of DOS memory needs to be reserved for user primitives.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx241 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-21\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  2.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx241 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent241\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-21\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Two VDISKs of 256K each were created when the system was booted up.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi-241\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx241 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  3.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi-241\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx241 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent241\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 A one megabyte area of extended memory was reserved at the high end of extended\line \expndtw0 memory when the system was booted up.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi-241\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx241 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-14\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  4.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li241\fi-241\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx241 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent241\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-14\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 At least an additional one megabyte needs to be reserved for use after \b Smalltalk/V\line \b0\expndtw1 286 is started up; more would be nice.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx241 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  5.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx241 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent241\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-19\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 There are 8 megabytes of extended memory in the hardware.}\par {\pntext\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx241 \b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22  6.\tab}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx241 \f3\fs20{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent241\pnf3{\pntxta .}}\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-13\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The \b Smalltalk/V \b0 image we are going to run does not need more than two megabytes.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2191\margrsxn2090\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4306\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa187 \f3\fs20{\b0\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Appendix 3.-Configuring Smalltalk/ V 286    307}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2191\margrsxn2090\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw4014\colsr1451\colno2\colw2163 {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft5227\shptop1746\shpright5227\shpbottom4345\shpfhdr0\shpbxmargin\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 11430}}}}{\pard\plain \sb0\sa0\sl-1\slmult0\fs2\par}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx4903\posnegy1663\absw0\absh-209\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 9M}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-155\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx3175\posnegy1948\absw1811\absh-313\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 available for DOS shell\line \expndtw-2 and user primitives         \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\f2\cchs0 \'ab}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-144\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx3082\posnegy2740\absw1051\absh-291\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 used by\line \expndtw-5 Smalltalk/V 286}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx4802\posnegy2801\absw0\absh-209\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 6M}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-162\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx3085\posnegy3424\absw1130\absh-324\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 Smalltalk/V 286\line \expndtw-5 real mode kernel}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx4658\posnegy3575\absw0\absh-209\nowrap\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 2.5 M}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-158\slmult0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx3092\posnegy3859\absw1987\absh-519\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 DOS, BIOS and other  \i -\\ \i0 5 \scaps m\line \scaps0\expndtw-4 resident programs}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qr\li0\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20\pvpara\phmrg\posnegx3092\posnegy3859\absw1987\absh-519\dfrmtxtx40\dfrmtxty0 {\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 1 M}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li392\fi-392\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The command line would be:\line \expndtw4 v /d:15O /x:1024 /u:2O48}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-400\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 And the memory map is given in figure A3-1.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1339\fi0\ri0\sb18\sa0\sl-760\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-7\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 640 K\line 490 K}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1354\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-760\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-8\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 120 K}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1436\fi0\ri0\sb277\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 40 K}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1534\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-6\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs18 OK}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li414\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-10\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Figure A3-1\line \expndtw1 Smalltalk/V 286}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li418\fi0\ri0\sb7\sa0\sl-216\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Memory Map}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li2124\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 DOS Memory}\par \column \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1109\fi0\ri0\sb1858\sa0\sl-155\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 reserved at\line \expndtw-5 power on time}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1112\fi0\ri0\sb248\sa0\sl-155\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 used by\line Smalltalk/V 286}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1109\fi0\ri0\sb479\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-5\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 available}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li1109\fi0\ri0\sb299\sa0\sl-166\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 reserved by\line \expndtw-5 X parameter\line VDISK #2\line \expndtw-8 VDISK #1}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb421\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 Extended Memory}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2191\margrsxn2090\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb331\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs26 Hardware and BIOS Configuration}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb223\sa0\sl-238\slmult0\tx6746 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Smalltalk/V 286 dynamically determines the configuration of your hardware and the\line \expndtw2 type of BIOS installed when it is invoked. Due to the variety of hardware and\line \expndtw-3 manufacturers available, we included the following two parameters in case we have\line \expndtw-1 overlooked something. See file read.me for latest information about configuring for\line \expndtw-6 specific machines.{\charscalex100\expndtw0\tab }\super\expndtw0 #}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb353\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw3\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 80386 cpu vs 80286 cpu}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri14\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Since Smalltalk/V 286 runs in protected mode, and all of the BIOS and DOS facilities\line \expndtw-2 are accessible only from real mode, switching from protected to real mode is necessary.\line \expndtw0 If you have a 80386 cpu, this switching is done via 80386 software instructions.\line \expndtw1 Smalltalk/V detects the presence of the 80386 cpu automatically.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb151\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 If you have an 80286 cpu, then the switching between protected and real mode is\line \expndtw-4 accomplished by using the 80286 cpu to shutdown and restart in real mode. There are two\line \expndtw2 issues, how to accomplish the shutdown and how the cpu is to start up.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2422\margrsxn1865\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs16 508    Appendix \i0 3\i .Configuring Smalltalk/ V 286}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb364\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 80286 Shutdown}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri11\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The /s: parameter specifies the type of shutdown to use. There are two choices: shutdown\line \expndtw1 via hardware, /s:h, and via software, /s:s.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri7\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-3\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The hardware shutdown is done by intentionally causing what is commonly known as a\line triple fault condition in the 80286 cpu. This does require that the mother board cause an\line \expndtw-2 automatic restart condition when the 80286 enters the shutdown mode. All IBM PC/AT\line \expndtw1 machines and most clones do this.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri7\sb158\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The software shutdown is done by requesting a cpu reset via software. This involves\line \expndtw-3 sending commands to the keyboard processor on AT class machines and issuing port I/O\line \expndtw0 commands on some PS/2 machines.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb364\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw1\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 80286 Restart}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri7\sb209\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The /t: parameter specifies how the 80286 is to start up after a shutdown. This is done\line \expndtw-3 by writing a value into the CMOS memory (non-volatile mnemory) of the machine prior\line \expndtw0 to the shutdown. The BIOS boot up code in the ROM looks at this value to determine\line \expndtw-3 the type of restart to do. There are two choices: primary start up, /t:p, and secondary start\line \expndtw7 up /t:s.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li7\fi0\ri0\sb144\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The primary start up is done by using the documented BIOS function for starting up after\line \expndtw-3 switching from protected to real mode. For early PC/AT's and some clones, this function\line \expndtw-1 does not work properly. The secondary start up uses the BIOS block memory move\line \expndtw2 function to cause the real mode start up.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li11\fi0\ri0\sb364\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-9\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs28 Speed vs Space}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri4\sb220\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-1\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 The system menu contains a selection, speed/space, which lets you optimize Small-\line \expndtw0 talk/V for either speed (performance) or space (memory utilization). In speed mode,\line \expndtw-3 every window maintains a backup bitmap of itself for use in redrawing. In space mode,\line \expndtw-1 when a window is redrawn, it regenerates its screen image.}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li0\fi0\ri0\sb148\sa0\sl-241\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 You can tell which mode the system is in by looking at the system menu. If it says\line \expndtw2 speed/space, you are in speed mode. If it says space/speed, you are in space mode.}\par \sect\sectd \sbkpage\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2255\margrsxn2025\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols1\colsx60 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs30 Appendix 4: METHOD INDEX}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\qj\li4\fi0\ri0\sb454\sa169\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw7\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 This appendix is an index into \b Part 4: Encyclopedia of Classes. \b0 It is a complete index\line \expndtw5 of all the methods implemented in \b Smalltalk/\b0 V \b 286. \b0 The first column below contains the\line \expndtw3 selectors of all methods in sorted order. For each selector, the second column contains the\line \expndtw5 list of all classes that implement a method for that selector.}\par \sect\sectd \sbknone\pgwsxn11909\pghsxn16834\marglsxn2259\margrsxn2036\margtsxn1440\margbsxn720\cols2\colno1\colw1875\colsr983\colno2\colw4755 \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li4\fi0\ri0\sb11\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw2\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 Method Selector}\par {\shp {\*\shpinst \shpleft-4\shptop14\shpright1616\shpbottom14\shpfhdr0\shpbxcolumn\shpbypara\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt1\shpz0\shplid1{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn lineWidth}{\sv 15875}}}}\pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li0\fi0\ri0\sb180\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 &}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li22\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 *}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb774\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 /}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li18\fi0\ri0\sb4\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw10\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 //}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 <}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb227\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex141\expndtw-70\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 < =}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li25\fi0\ri0\sb1678\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw0\dn0\f2\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 >}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb281\sa0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex153\expndtw0\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 @}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li29\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex153\expndtw12\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs22 \\\\}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b\i0\cf0\charscalex75\expndtw-3\dn0\f17\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 abs\line \expndtw-6 accept\line \expndtw-14 accept :from:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb230\sa0\sl-238\slmult0 \f3\fs20{\b0\i0\cf0\charscalex100\expndtw-4\dn0\f3\cchs0\lang1033\fs20 acceptClass :from:}\par \pard \plain \cbpat0\ql\li7\fi0\ri0\sb0\sa0\sl-23